[{"slug":"amount-of-substance","number":1,"unit":1,"unitCode":"WCH11","unitName":"Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry","title":"Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance","subtitle":"The grammar of chemistry — moles, equations and the calculations everything else stands on.","summary":"Atoms, ions, molecules and the mole; writing and balancing full and ionic equations; reacting masses, gas volumes, solutions, ppm, yields and atom economy.","estHours":5.0,"sections":[{"id":"cmv1eoi290002jfqk6g41x5ba","sectionKey":"specification-map","number":"1.0","title":"Specification Map","blurb":"Every statement the specification assesses in this topic — use it as your revision checklist.","estMinutes":5,"spec":"1.1 – 1.12","specText":null,"outline":["The full list of assessment statements for Topic 1, worded exactly as the specification words them","Tick each point off once you can do it from memory"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Spec","Assessment statement"],"rows":[["1.1","know the terms 'atom', 'element', 'ion', 'molecule', 'compound', 'empirical formula' and 'molecular formula'"],["1.2","know that the mole (mol) is the unit for the amount of a substance and be able to perform calculations using the Avogadro constant L (6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)"],["1.3","write balanced full and ionic equations, including state symbols, for chemical reactions"],["1.4","understand the terms:\ni   'relative atomic mass' based on the ¹²C scale\nii   'relative molecular mass' and 'relative formula mass', including calculating these values from relative atomic masses. The term 'relative formula mass' should be used for compounds with giant structures.\niii   'molar mass' as the mass per mole of a substance in g mol⁻¹\niv   parts per million (ppm), including gases in the atmosphere"],["1.5","calculate the concentration of a solution in mol dm⁻³ and g dm⁻³\nTitration calculations are not required at this stage."],["1.6","be able to use experimental data to calculate empirical and molecular formulae"],["1.7","be able to use chemical equations to calculate reacting masses and vice versa, using the concepts of amount of substance and molar mass"],["1.8","be able to use chemical equations to calculate volumes of gases and vice versa, using:\ni   the concepts of amount of substance\nii   the molar volume of gases\niii   the expression pV = nRT for gases and volatile liquids"],["1.9","be able to calculate percentage yields and percentage atom economies (by mass) in laboratory and industrial processes, using chemical equations and experimental results\nAtom economy = (molar mass of the desired product ÷ sum of the molar masses of all products) × 100"],["1.10","be able to determine a formula or confirm an equation by experiment, including evaluation of the data"],["1.12","be able to relate ionic and full equations, with state symbols, to observations from simple test-tube experiments, to include:\ni   displacement reactions\nii   typical reactions of acids\niii   precipitation reactions"]]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2a0004jfqkcrkdhdv2","sectionKey":"atoms-elements-molecules","number":"1.1","title":"Atoms, Elements and Molecules","blurb":"The vocabulary of matter: atoms, elements, molecules, compounds and ions.","estMinutes":30,"spec":"1.1","specText":null,"outline":["Terms: atom, element, ion, molecule, compound, empirical formula, molecular formula","Subatomic particles — protons, neutrons, electrons — and their masses and charges","Mass number and atomic number; nuclide notation"],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Atom","text":"An atom is the smallest, electrically neutral particle of an element that can take part in a chemical change."},{"type":"list","items":["An atom comprises of subatomic particles **protons**, **neutrons** and **electrons**.","Protons and neutrons, together known as **nucleons**, are found in the nucleus which is the center of the atom.","Electrons orbit around the nucleus in definite energy levels called **shells**.","An atom gets its chemical properties from the number of electrons and arrangement of electrons in the atom."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/atom-structure.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/atom-structure-dark.png","alt":"Model of the atom showing the nucleus and electron shells","caption":"Nucleons (protons and neutrons) are found in the nucleus; electrons orbit the nucleus in shells."},{"type":"h3","text":"The masses and charges of the three sub atomic particles"},{"type":"table","headers":["Particle","Symbol","Charge / C","Relative charge","Mass / g","Relative mass"],"rows":[["Proton","p","+1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹","+1","1.67 × 10⁻²⁴","1"],["Neutron","n","0","0","1.67 × 10⁻²⁴","1"],["Electron","e⁻","−1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹","−1","9.11 × 10⁻²⁸","1 / 1840"]]},{"type":"list","items":["The mass of electrons are negligible, hence the mass of the atoms are determined by the mass of nucleons.","Atoms in an element are represented by using the following notation.","The number of protons is equal to the number of electrons; thus, an atom is electrically neutral."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/nuclide-notation.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/nuclide-notation-dark.png","alt":"Nuclide notation showing mass number A and atomic number Z beside the symbol X","caption":"Mass number = sum of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom · Atomic number = protons in the nucleus of an atom."},{"type":"def","term":"Element","text":"Elements are made up of only one type of atoms."},{"type":"list","items":["Elements are chemically the simplest substance, so they cannot be broken down using physical/ chemical means.","The atoms of an element all contain the same number of protons.","Can exist as either atoms (eg: sodium, argon) or molecules (eg: oxygen (O₂), nitrogen (N₂)).","Each element has its own characteristic properties.","Chemists use symbols to represent elements."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/element-symbols.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/element-symbols-dark.png","alt":"Tiles of chemical elements with their symbols and atomic numbers","caption":"Chemists use symbols to represent elements — each tile shows the atomic number, symbol, name and relative atomic mass."},{"type":"def","term":"Molecule","text":"A molecule is the smallest, electrically neutral particle, of an element or compound that can exist on its own. Example: oxygen molecules (O₂) — element; water (H₂O) — compound."},{"type":"list","items":["It is also the name given to the smallest part of those elements which do not exist as atoms in the free state i.e. hydrogen H₂, oxygen O₂, nitrogen N₂, fluorine F₂, chlorine Cl₂, bromine Br₂ and iodine I₂.","A molecule consists of two or more atoms of the same element or different elements, chemically combined.","Ionic compounds (e.g. sodium chloride) do not exist as molecules."]},{"type":"def","term":"Compound","text":"A compound is a substance formed when two or more elements chemically combined. Example: water, sugar, sodium chloride."},{"type":"list","items":["Have properties that are different from its component elements.","Can be broken down into simpler substances by chemical methods.","Chemical formulas are used to show the composition of compounds. Example: NaCl, H₂O."]},{"type":"def","term":"Ion","text":"An ion is an atom or a group of atoms (molecule) which carries an electrical charge."},{"type":"list","items":["**Negative ion** has more electron than protons. Example: Cl⁻ ion has 17 protons and 18 electrons.","A negative ion is obtained by adding electrons to a neutral atom.","**Positive ion** has fewer electrons than protons. Example: Na⁺ ion has 11 protons and 10 electrons.","A positive ion is obtained by removing electrons from a neutral atom."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/ion-formation.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/ion-formation-dark.png","alt":"Formation of the sodium ion and the chloride ion shown with electron shells","caption":"Sodium ion, Na⁺ [2,8]⁺ — formed by removing an electron · Chloride ion, Cl⁻ [2,8,8]⁻ — formed by adding an electron."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2b0006jfqkwk0txflt","sectionKey":"chemical-equations","number":"1.2","title":"Writing Chemical Equations","blurb":"Balanced full and ionic equations, with state symbols — done properly.","estMinutes":30,"spec":"1.3","specText":null,"outline":["What a chemical equation shows: formulae, physical states, stoichiometry","Balancing molecular equations — the five-step routine","Balancing ionic equations; spectator ions"],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Chemical equation","text":"Shows the formulae of the reactants and the products; shows the physical state in which each substance exists; and shows the relationship between the numbers of each substance involved — this is known as the **stoichiometry** of the reaction."},{"type":"p","text":"Equations can be written in words or using chemical formula."},{"type":"eq","code":"hydrogen + oxygen → water","caption":"Written in words"},{"type":"eq","code":"H₂ + O₂ → H₂O","caption":"Written using chemical formulae"},{"type":"list","items":["Chemical equations could be either **molecular equations** or **ionic equations**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Balancing molecular equations"},{"type":"list","items":["A balanced chemical equation must have the same number of each kind of atoms on the left-hand side (LHS) of the equation as on the right-hand side (RHS).","Net charge on both reactants and products side should be 0.","Simplest whole number ratios should be used to balance the equations."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Steps to follow in writing chemical equations"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Work out what has reacted and what has been formed. Write a word equation.","Get the correct formula for each species. Include the state symbols where necessary. When the correct formula of a species has been obtained, do not change the formula to balance the equation.","Having written out the initial equation, check to see if it is balanced. An equation balances if the same number of each type of atom appears on either side of the arrow.","Place whole numbers in front of any formula to indicate if more than one of it is required. This multiplies everything in the formula immediately behind it. Continue until the equation balances.","Finally, check the equation to see that you have balanced it correctly."]},{"type":"worked","title":"Balancing a full equation","problem":"sodium + water → sodium hydroxide + hydrogen","steps":["**Write the correct formulae**, with state symbols:\nNa(s) + H₂O(l) → NaOH(aq) + H₂(g)","**Count up the atoms:** LHS … 1 × Na, 2 × H, 1 × O — RHS … 1 × Na, 3 × H, 1 × O.\nThe equation doesn't balance; an extra H is needed on the LHS. However formulae should not be changed. One can only get extra H's by having two waters; multiply H₂O by two.","Na(s) + 2 H₂O(l) → NaOH(aq) + H₂(g)\nNow there are too many O's (2) and H's (4) on the LHS; multiplying the NaOH by two will solve this problem.\nNa(s) + 2 H₂O(l) → 2 NaOH(aq) + H₂(g)\nHowever, it creates yet another problem because it has introduced an extra Na on the RHS; multiply the Na on the LHS by two.","2 Na(s) + 2 H₂O(l) → 2 NaOH(aq) + H₂(g)","**Check** the equation to check whether it is balanced.\nLHS … 2 × Na, 4 × H, 2 × O — RHS … 2 × Na, 4 × H, 2 × O. It is balanced."],"answer":"2 Na(s) + 2 H₂O(l) → 2 NaOH(aq) + H₂(g)"},{"type":"h3","text":"Balancing ionic equations"},{"type":"list","items":["Many reactions involve ions in solution.","When substances react in this way an ionic equation is useful.","In ionic equations, only the **reacting particles** are included."]},{"type":"worked","title":"Writing a balanced ionic equation","problem":"Write down a balanced ionic equation for the following reaction.\nCaCO₃(s) + HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(l)","steps":["**Balance the molecular equation first**\nCaCO₃(s) + 2 HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(l)","**Separate the ions in solution** (write full molecular formula for solids, liquids and gases) and cancel out the **spectator ions**\nCaCO₃(s) + 2H⁺(aq) + 2Cl⁻(aq) → Ca²⁺(aq) + 2Cl⁻(aq) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(l)","**Write down the final ionic equation** with state symbols"],"answer":"CaCO₃(s) + 2 H⁺(aq) → Ca²⁺(aq) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(l)"}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2c0008jfqka2o3aqrd","sectionKey":"reactions-of-acids","number":"1.3","title":"Typical Reactions of Acids","blurb":"The three general equations every acid reaction follows.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"1.12(ii)","specText":null,"outline":["Acids with metals → salt + hydrogen","Acids with metal oxides / metal hydroxides → salt + water","Acids with metal carbonates → salt + water + carbon dioxide"],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Acids with metals"},{"type":"eq","code":"Acid + Metal → Salt + Hydrogen","caption":"General equation"},{"type":"h3","text":"Acids with metal oxides / metal hydroxides"},{"type":"eq","code":"Acid + Metal oxide/hydroxide → Salt + Water","caption":"General equation"},{"type":"h3","text":"Acids with carbonates"},{"type":"eq","code":"Acid + Metal carbonate → Salt + Water + Carbon dioxide","caption":"General equation"}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2d000ajfqkm73hoxs4","sectionKey":"displacement-reactions","number":"1.4","title":"Displacement Reactions","blurb":"One element replaces another — reactivity decides the winner.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"1.12(i)","specText":null,"outline":["A more reactive metal displaces a less reactive metal from its compound","Writing full equations for displacement reactions"],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Displacement reaction","text":"In simple, it is a reaction in which one element replaces another element in a compound."},{"type":"eq","code":"Mg(s) + CuSO₄(aq) → Cu(s) + MgSO₄(aq)","caption":"Example"},{"type":"eq","code":"2Al(s) + Fe₂O₃(s) → 2Fe(s) + Al₂O₃(s)","caption":"Example"},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Remember","text":"A more reactive metal displaces the less reactive metal from its compound."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2e000cjfqk8vewvsun","sectionKey":"precipitation-reactions","number":"1.5","title":"Precipitation Reactions","blurb":"Insoluble salts falling out of solution — and the rules that predict them.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"1.12(iii)","specText":null,"outline":["Formation of an insoluble salt (the precipitate) when two soluble salt solutions combine","The solubility rules for nitrates, sulfates, halides, carbonates and hydroxides"],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Precipitation reaction","text":"A precipitation reaction refers to the formation of an insoluble salt when two solutions containing soluble salts are combined."},{"type":"list","items":["The insoluble salt that falls out of solution is known as the **precipitate**."]},{"type":"eq","code":"Na₂SO₄(aq) + BaCl₂(aq) → BaSO₄(s) + 2 NaCl(aq)","caption":"Example"},{"type":"eq","code":"NaCl(aq) + AgNO₃(aq) → AgCl(s) + NaNO₃(aq)","caption":"Example"},{"type":"eq","code":"Ca(OH)₂(aq) + CO₂ → CaCO₃(s) + H₂O(l)","caption":"Example"},{"type":"h3","text":"Solubility rules"},{"type":"table","headers":["Soluble","Insoluble"],"rows":[["All nitrates","None"],["Most sulfates","Lead sulfate, barium sulfate and calcium sulfate"],["Most chlorides, bromides and iodides","Silver chloride, silver bromide, silver iodide, lead chloride, lead bromide, lead iodide"],["Sodium carbonate, potassium carbonate, ammonium carbonate","Most other carbonates"],["Sodium hydroxide, potassium hydroxide, ammonium hydroxide","Most other hydroxides"]]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2e000ejfqku169js24","sectionKey":"comparing-masses","number":"1.6","title":"Comparing Masses of Substances","blurb":"Relative masses on the ¹²C scale, and the mole that counts particles by weighing.","estMinutes":35,"spec":"1.1, 1.2 & 1.4","specText":null,"outline":["Atomic mass unit; relative atomic, molecular, isotopic and formula mass","The mole and the Avogadro constant L = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹","Molar mass in g mol⁻¹; molar volume of gases (24 dm³ at r.t.p.)"],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Relative masses of elements and compounds"},{"type":"list","items":["Measuring the mass of an atom or compound using a balance would be very difficult as they are very small.","Hence, to measure the mass of an atom or a compound the **atomic mass unit (amu)** is used as a standard reference.","The relative atomic mass does not have any units. They are measured compare to amu."]},{"type":"list","items":["**Atomic mass unit** is the mass of a carbon-12 atom divided by 12. This is used as the standard reference scale to measure mass of atoms and molecules.","**Relative atomic mass** of an element is the weighted average mass of an atom of that element divided by 1/12th the mass of a carbon-12 atom.","**Relative molecular mass** of a molecule or compound is the sum of the relative atomic masses of all the atoms in the chemical formula.","**Relative isotopic mass** is the mass of an atom of that isotope divided by 1/12th the mass of a carbon-12 atom."]},{"type":"eq","code":"Aᵣ = mean mass of an atom of an element ÷ (1/12 of the mass of an atom of ¹²C)","caption":"Relative atomic mass — a ratio on the ¹²C scale, with no units"},{"type":"h3","text":"The mole: amount of substance"},{"type":"list","items":["When chemists measure how much of a particular chemical reacts they measure the amount in grams or the volume of a gas. However, chemists find it convenient to use a unit called **mole**.","The mole is the amount of substance which contains the same number of particles (atoms, ions, molecules, formulae or electrons) as there are carbon atoms in 12 g of carbon-12.","The number of particles in a mole is known as **Avogadro's constant** which is equal to 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹."]},{"type":"eq","code":"1 mole = 6.02 × 10²³ particles (it can be atoms, ions, molecules)","caption":"The Avogadro constant, L"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/mole-comparison.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/mole-comparison-dark.png","alt":"One mole of helium, carbon dioxide and nitrate ions compared","caption":"Example: 6.02 × 10²³ helium atoms in 4 g of He · 6.02 × 10²³ carbon dioxide molecules in 44 g of CO₂ · 6.02 × 10²³ nitrate ions in 62 g of NO₃⁻."},{"type":"worked","title":"Counting particles","problem":"How many water molecules are there in 1.25 g of water?","steps":["Moles of water = 1.25 ÷ 18 = 0.0694 mol","1 mol of water → 6.02 × 10²³ molecules, so 0.0694 mol → x","x = 0.0694 × 6.02 × 10²³"],"answer":"x = 4.18 × 10²² water molecules"},{"type":"def","term":"Molar mass","text":"The molar mass of a substance is the mass, in grams, of one mole."},{"type":"def","term":"Molar volume of a gas","text":"The molar volume of a gas is the volume occupied by one mole at room temperature and pressure. It is equal to 24 dm³ at room temperature and pressure (r.t.p.)."},{"type":"eq","code":"Number of moles = mass in grams ÷ molar mass","caption":"n = m ÷ M"},{"type":"eq","code":"Number of moles of gas = volume of gas (dm³) ÷ molar volume (24 dm³)","caption":"n = V ÷ 24, at r.t.p."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/mole-map.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/mole-map-dark.png","alt":"The mole conversion map linking mass, particles, gas volume and solution concentration","caption":"The mole is the hub of every calculation in this topic: convert between mass, number of particles, gas volume and solution concentration."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2f000gjfqkxrcsk5sa","sectionKey":"reacting-masses","number":"1.7","title":"Calculations Using Reacting Masses","blurb":"The mole as the bridge between equations and the lab bench.","estMinutes":30,"spec":"1.7","specText":null,"outline":["Using chemical equations to calculate reacting masses and vice versa","Using amount of substance and molar mass to find and confirm equations"],"content":[{"type":"worked","title":"Mass of reactant needed","problem":"The equation for the reaction is\nSO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄\nWhat mass of sulfur trioxide is needed to form 75 g of sulfuric acid?","steps":["Molar masses: M(SO₃) = 32.1 + 48.0 = 80.1 g mol⁻¹; M(H₂SO₄) = 98.1 g mol⁻¹","Moles of H₂SO₄ = 75 ÷ 98.1 = 0.765 mol","Ratio SO₃ : H₂SO₄ = 1 : 1, so moles of SO₃ = 0.765 mol","Mass of SO₃ = 0.765 × 80.1"],"answer":"≈ 61.2 g of sulfur trioxide"},{"type":"worked","title":"Scaling with the equation ratio","problem":"The equation for the reaction is\n2NH₃ + H₂SO₄ → (NH₄)₂SO₄\nWhat mass of ammonia is needed to form 100 g of ammonium sulfate?","steps":["Molar masses: M((NH₄)₂SO₄) = 132.1 g mol⁻¹; M(NH₃) = 17.0 g mol⁻¹","Moles of (NH₄)₂SO₄ = 100 ÷ 132 = 0.758 mol","Ratio NH₃ : (NH₄)₂SO₄ = 2 : 1, so moles of NH₃ = 2 × 0.758 = 1.515 mol","Mass of NH₃ = 1.515 × 17.0"],"answer":"≈ 25.8 g of ammonia"},{"type":"worked","title":"Confirming the equation by experiment","problem":"A 16.7 g sample of sodium carbonate (Na₂CO₃·10H₂O) is heated at a constant temperature for a specified time until the reaction is complete. A mass of 3.15 g of water is obtained. What is the equation for the reaction occurring?","steps":["On heating, the crystals lose their water of crystallisation as steam.","Mole check: n(H₂O) = mass of water ÷ 18, and n(Na₂CO₃) = (mass of crystals − mass of water) ÷ 106. The ratio n(H₂O) : n(Na₂CO₃) gives the value of x in Na₂CO₃·xH₂O.","For the decahydrate x = 10: one mole of crystals (286 g) releases 10 moles of water (180 g) on complete heating."],"answer":"Na₂CO₃·10H₂O(s) → Na₂CO₃(s) + 10H₂O(g)"},{"type":"worked","title":"Identifying an oxide of copper","problem":"An oxide of copper is heated in a stream of hydrogen to constant mass. The masses of copper and water formed are 17.6 g and 2.56 g respectively. Write the equation for the reaction.","steps":["Moles of copper: n(Cu) = 17.6 ÷ 63.5 = 0.277 mol","Moles of water: n(H₂O) = 2.56 ÷ 18.0 = 0.142 mol","Ratio Cu : H₂O = 0.277 : 0.142 ≈ 2 : 1, so the oxide contains two copper atoms per oxygen atom — copper(I) oxide, Cu₂O","The equation is balanced with this 2 : 1 ratio of Cu to H₂O"],"answer":"Cu₂O(s) + H₂(g) → 2 Cu(s) + H₂O(g)"}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2h000ijfqk642povp3","sectionKey":"yield-atom-economy","number":"1.8","title":"Yield of a Reaction","blurb":"Percentage yield and atom economy — two measures of efficiency.","estMinutes":40,"spec":"1.9","specText":null,"outline":["Percentage yield: theoretical vs actual yield, and why yield is lost","Atom economy (by mass) and why industry cares about it"],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Percentage yield"},{"type":"p","text":"The efficiency of a reaction can be measured by the percentage yield. This gives the amount of product loss and how wasteful the process is."},{"type":"list","items":["The percentage yield of a reaction can be calculated using the formula:"]},{"type":"eq","code":"Percentage yield = (Actual (experimental) yield ÷ Theoretical yield) × 100"},{"type":"list","items":["**Theoretical yield** — the mass of the product that should be formed. It can be calculated using the mass of reactants and balanced chemical equation.","For any reaction the **actual mass of product formed (actual yield) will always be less than the theoretical yield**.","This is because:\n- all the reactants do not undergo complete reaction;\n- some product is lost during handling,\n- solution loss during filtration or during transfer between containers."]},{"type":"worked","title":"Theoretical yield of copper(II) oxide","problem":"Copper(II) carbonate is decomposed to obtain copper(II) oxide. The equation for the reaction is\nCuCO₃ → CuO + CO₂\nWhat is the theoretical yield of copper(II) oxide obtainable from 5.78 g of Copper(II) carbonate?","steps":["Molar masses: M(CuCO₃) = 63.5 + 12.0 + 48.0 = 123.5 g mol⁻¹; M(CuO) = 63.5 + 16.0 = 79.5 g mol⁻¹","Moles of CuCO₃ = 5.78 ÷ 123.5 = 0.0468 mol","Ratio CuCO₃ : CuO = 1 : 1, so moles of CuO = 0.0468 mol","Theoretical yield = 0.0468 × 79.5"],"answer":"3.72 g of copper(II) oxide"},{"type":"worked","title":"Theoretical yield of magnesium phosphate","problem":"Magnesium phosphate can be prepared from magnesium by reacting it with phosphoric acid. The equation for the reaction:\n3Mg + 2H₃PO₄ → Mg₃(PO₄)₂ + 3H₂\nWhat is the theoretical yield of magnesium phosphate obtainable from 5.62 g of magnesium?","steps":["Molar masses: M(Mg) = 24.3 g mol⁻¹; M(Mg₃(PO₄)₂) = (3 × 24.3) + (2 × 95.0) = 262.9 g mol⁻¹","Moles of Mg = 5.62 ÷ 24.3 = 0.2313 mol","Ratio Mg : Mg₃(PO₄)₂ = 3 : 1, so moles of Mg₃(PO₄)₂ = 0.2313 ÷ 3 = 0.0771 mol","Theoretical yield = 0.0771 × 262.9"],"answer":"≈ 20.3 g of magnesium phosphate"},{"type":"worked","title":"Percentage yield in industry","problem":"A manufacturer uses this reaction to obtain methanol from carbon monoxide and hydrogen:\nCO + 2 H₂ → CH₃OH\nThe manufacturer obtains 4.07 tonnes of methanol starting from 4.32 tonnes of carbon monoxide. What is the percentage yield?","steps":["Molar masses: M(CO) = 28.0 g mol⁻¹; M(CH₃OH) = 32.0 g mol⁻¹","Moles of CO = 4.32 × 10⁶ g ÷ 28.0 = 1.543 × 10⁵ mol","Ratio CO : CH₃OH = 1 : 1, so theoretical moles of CH₃OH = 1.543 × 10⁵ mol → theoretical yield = 1.543 × 10⁵ × 32.0 = 4.94 tonnes","Percentage yield = 4.07 ÷ 4.94 × 100"],"answer":"≈ 82.4%"},{"type":"h3","text":"Atom economy"},{"type":"list","items":["It is also a method used to measure the efficiency of a reaction.","The **atom economy** of a chemical reaction is a measure of the proportion of reactant atoms that become part of the desired products rather than by-products.","A reaction that has a very high yield could still be very wasteful if a lot of the atoms from the reactants end up in by-products.","**Inefficient, wasteful processes** have low atom economies.","**Efficient processes** have high atom economies, and are important for sustainable development, as they use fewer natural resources and create less waste.","The atom economy of a reaction can be calculated:"]},{"type":"eq","code":"% Atom economy = (Molecular mass of the desired product ÷ Sum of the molecular masses of all products) × 100"},{"type":"list","items":["**Addition reactions** have 100% atom economy since only one product will be formed and no atoms are wasted as by-products.","**Substitution reactions** have a lower atom economy than addition reactions. These reactions would form at least two products, where only one would be the desired product.","Companies in the chemical industry will often choose reactions with higher atom economies. Keeping high atom economy (greener chemical processes) has environmental and economic benefits.","Low atom economy leads to more waste. This makes separation of desired product and disposal of waste products costly.","Also the process itself becomes more costly and less efficient if most of the reactants end up as waste as more raw materials would be required to get sufficient amount of product."]},{"type":"worked","title":"Atom economy of the water–gas shift reaction","problem":"What is the atom economy for making hydrogen by reacting coal with steam?\nC(s) + 2 H₂O(g) → CO₂(g) + 2 H₂(g)","steps":["Desired product: 2 H₂ → relative molecular mass = 2 × 2.0 = 4.0","Sum of the molecular masses of all products: CO₂ (44.0) + 2 H₂ (4.0) = 48.0","Atom economy = 4.0 ÷ 48.0 × 100"],"answer":"≈ 8.3%"},{"type":"worked","title":"Atom economy of hydrazine manufacture","problem":"Hydrazine can be used as a rocket fuel and is manufactured using the reaction:\n2NH₃ + NaOCl → N₂H₄ + NaCl + H₂O\nWhat is the atom economy for this reaction?","steps":["Desired product: N₂H₄ → relative molecular mass = 32.0","Sum of the molecular masses of all products: N₂H₄ (32.0) + NaCl (58.5) + H₂O (18.0) = 108.5","Atom economy = 32.0 ÷ 108.5 × 100"],"answer":"≈ 29.5%"}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2h000kjfqkmzg8p379","sectionKey":"empirical-molecular-formula","number":"1.9","title":"Empirical and Molecular Formula","blurb":"Turning experimental numbers into formulae.","estMinutes":25,"spec":"1.1, 1.6 & 1.8(iii)","specText":null,"outline":["Using experimental mass / % composition data to find empirical formulae","Scaling up with molar mass to the molecular formula"],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Empirical formula","text":"Empirical formula gives the smallest whole number ratio of atoms in a compound."},{"type":"def","term":"Molecular formula","text":"Molecular formula gives the actual number of atoms in a molecule."},{"type":"list","items":["Sometimes, empirical and molecular formula are the same."]},{"type":"worked","title":"From masses to empirical formula","problem":"Calculate the empirical formula of a compound with C (1.8 g), O (0.48 g), H (0.3 g).","steps":["**Write out ratios by mass** — C: 1.8   H: 0.3   O: 0.48","**Divide by relative atomic mass** (this gives the molar ratio) — C: 1.8 ÷ 12 = 0.15   H: 0.3 ÷ 1 = 0.3   O: 0.48 ÷ 16 = 0.03","**If not whole numbers then scale up** — try dividing by the smallest value (0.03): C 5   H 10   O 1","**Express as a formula**"],"answer":"C₅H₁₀O"},{"type":"worked","title":"From empirical formula to molecular formula","problem":"Calculate the molecular formula of a compound of empirical formula CH₂ and relative molecular mass 84.","steps":["Calculate the empirical formula mass (CH₂ unit) = 14","Divide the molecular mass (84) by the empirical formula mass = 84 ÷ 14 = 6","Molecular formula = empirical formula (CH₂) × 6"],"answer":"C₆H₁₂"}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2i000mjfqkwjkix3yn","sectionKey":"ideal-gas-equation","number":"1.10","title":"The Ideal Gas Equation pV = nRT","blurb":"Gases and volatile liquids: moles from pressure, volume and temperature.","estMinutes":25,"spec":"1.8(iii)","specText":null,"outline":["Using pV = nRT for gases and volatile liquids, with SI units","Unit conversions: kPa, cm³, dm³, °C","Finding molar masses and molecular formulae from gas data"],"content":[{"type":"list","items":["**pV = nRT** is the ideal gas equation and can be used for gases (or volatile liquids above their boiling points) to find the amount of substance in moles.","If the mass of the substance is known, then the molar mass of the compound can be calculated.","This gives extra information needed to work out the empirical and molecular formula of the compound."]},{"type":"eq","code":"pV = nRT"},{"type":"h3","text":"SI units used in the ideal gas calculations"},{"type":"list","items":["p = pressure in pascals (Pa)","V = volume in cubic meters (m³)","T = temperature in kelvin (K)","n = amount of substance in moles (mol)","R = the gas constant (8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹)"]},{"type":"h3","text":"Conversion tips when doing questions"},{"type":"table","headers":["Conversion","How to do it"],"rows":[["kPa → Pa","Multiply by 10³"],["cm³ → m³","Divide by 10⁶"],["dm³ → m³","Divide by 10³"],["°C → K","Add 273"]]},{"type":"worked","title":"Molar mass of a gas","problem":"A 0.280 g sample of a gas has a volume of 58.5 cm³, measured at a pressure of 120 kPa and temperature of 70 °C. Calculate the molar mass of the gas.","steps":["Convert to SI units: p = 120 × 10³ = 120 000 Pa; V = 58.5 ÷ 10⁶ = 5.85 × 10⁻⁵ m³; T = 70 + 273 = 343 K","n = pV ÷ RT = (120 000 × 5.85 × 10⁻⁵) ÷ (8.31 × 343) = 7.02 ÷ 2850.3","n = 2.463 × 10⁻³ mol","Molar mass = mass ÷ moles = 0.280 ÷ (2.463 × 10⁻³)"],"answer":"≈ 114 g mol⁻¹"},{"type":"worked","title":"Molecular formula from gas data","problem":"A compound has the percentage composition by mass C = 52.2%, H = 13.0%, O = 34.8%. A sample containing 0.173 g of the compound had a volume of 95.0 cm³ when measured at 105 kPa and 45 °C. What is the molecular formula of the compound?","steps":["Empirical formula: C 52.2 ÷ 12 = 4.35   H 13.0 ÷ 1 = 13.0   O 34.8 ÷ 16 = 2.175 → divide through by 2.175 → C₂H₆O (empirical formula mass = 46)","Convert to SI units: p = 105 000 Pa; V = 95.0 ÷ 10⁶ = 9.50 × 10⁻⁵ m³; T = 45 + 273 = 318 K","n = pV ÷ RT = (105 000 × 9.50 × 10⁻⁵) ÷ (8.31 × 318) = 9.975 ÷ 2642.6 = 3.775 × 10⁻³ mol","Molar mass = 0.173 ÷ (3.775 × 10⁻³) = 45.8 ≈ 46 g mol⁻¹","Molecular formula = (empirical formula)ₙ with n = 46 ÷ 46 = 1"],"answer":"C₂H₆O"}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2j000ojfqk8ymln4bi","sectionKey":"molar-volume-solutions","number":"1.11","title":"Molar Volume: Solutions and PPM","blurb":"Molarity, g dm⁻³ and the ppm scale for solutions and gases.","estMinutes":35,"spec":"1.5, 1.8(ii) & 1.4(iv)","specText":null,"outline":["Concentration of a solution in mol dm⁻³ and g dm⁻³","Parts per million (ppm) for solutions and for gases in the atmosphere"],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Calculations involving chemicals in solutions"},{"type":"list","items":["Many chemical reactions occur in solution.","Concentration of a solution is measured in moles per cubic decimeter (mol dm⁻³)."]},{"type":"eq","code":"1 litre = 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³"},{"type":"p","text":"**Example**: Sodium chloride can be labeled as 1M NaCl. This means that each dm³ (1000 cm³) of the solution contains 1 mole (58.5 g) of NaCl, or its concentration is 1 mol dm⁻³."},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"How the solution is actually made up","text":"It doesn't mean that 58.5 g of NaCl have been added to 1000 cm³ of water as the volume of the mixture may no longer be 1000 cm³. The solution is made by dissolving 58.5 g of the solid in about 500 cm³ and then adding more water to make it up to 1000 cm³."},{"type":"list","items":["Concentration in mol dm⁻³ is called **molarity**."]},{"type":"eq","code":"Molarity = moles of solute ÷ volume of solution (dm³)"},{"type":"eq","code":"Concentration in g dm⁻³ = Molarity × Molar mass of the solute"},{"type":"h3","text":"Concentrations in PPM (parts per million)"},{"type":"list","items":["This unit is used when describing **very low concentrations**.","Percent means out of hundred, Parts per million means out of a million.","It usually describes something dissolved in water.","Parts per million (ppm) is often used for levels of pollutants in air or water.","One ppm is equivalent to one milligram of a solute per liter of water (mg/l) or one milligram of something per kg of solvent (mg/kg)."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Note","text":"Parts per billion and parts per trillion are also widely used when measuring polluting chemicals present in very low concentrations."},{"type":"eq","code":"Concentration in ppm = (mass of solute (g) × 1 000 000) ÷ mass of solution (g)","caption":"Solutions in ppm"},{"type":"worked","title":"Concentration of a solution in ppm","problem":"A solution contains 0.176 g of solute dissolved in 750 g of solvent. What is the concentration in ppm?","steps":["Mass of solution = 750 + 0.176 = 750.176 g","Concentration in ppm = (0.176 ÷ 750.176) × 1 000 000"],"answer":"≈ 235 ppm"},{"type":"worked","title":"A very dilute solution","problem":"A mass of 23 mg of sodium chloride is dissolved in 900 g of water. What is the concentration of sodium chloride in the solution in ppm?","steps":["23 mg = 0.023 g; mass of solution = 900 + 0.023 ≈ 900 g","Concentration in ppm = (0.023 ÷ 900) × 1 000 000"],"answer":"≈ 25.6 ppm"},{"type":"worked","title":"Working backwards from ppm","problem":"A sample of river water contains phosphate ions with a concentration of 17 ppm. What is the mass of the phosphate ions in 500 g of water?","steps":["Mass of solute = (concentration in ppm × mass of solution) ÷ 1 000 000","= (17 × 500) ÷ 1 000 000 = 0.0085 g"],"answer":"0.0085 g = 8.5 mg of phosphate ions"},{"type":"h3","text":"Gases in PPM"},{"type":"eq","code":"Concentration in ppm = (volume of gas × 1 000 000) ÷ volume of air","caption":"Gases in ppm — ppm by volume"},{"type":"worked","title":"Concentration of a gas in air","problem":"Some nitrogen dioxide gas, with a volume of 1.5 dm³, mixes with 10 000 dm³ of air. What is the concentration of nitrogen dioxide, in ppm, in air?","steps":["Concentration in ppm = (1.5 ÷ 10 000) × 1 000 000"],"answer":"150 ppm"},{"type":"worked","title":"Volume of gas from ppm","problem":"5000 dm³ of air is found to contain ozone with a concentration of 87 ppm. What volume of ozone is in the sample of air?","steps":["Volume of gas = (concentration in ppm × volume of air) ÷ 1 000 000","= (87 × 5000) ÷ 1 000 000 = 0.435 dm³"],"answer":"0.435 dm³ of ozone"},{"type":"worked","title":"Comparing two samples","problem":"Two samples of air containing sulfur dioxide were analysed. The results for sample 1 showed that 500 dm³ of air contained 37 cm³ of sulfur dioxide. The results of sample 2 showed that there were 1.4 dm³ of sulfur dioxide in 4000 dm³ of air. Show, by calculation, which sample has the higher concentration, in ppm, of sulfur dioxide.","steps":["Sample 1: 37 cm³ = 0.037 dm³ → (0.037 ÷ 500) × 1 000 000 = 74 ppm","Sample 2: (1.4 ÷ 4000) × 1 000 000 = 350 ppm","350 ppm > 74 ppm"],"answer":"Sample 2 has the higher concentration of sulfur dioxide"}]}],"cards":[{"id":"cmv1eoi2k000qjfqkh2k8btri","ord":1,"front":"Define the mole.","back":"The amount of substance containing as many elementary particles as there are atoms in exactly 12 g of carbon-12. Unit: mol. 1 mol = 6.02 × 10²³ particles (the Avogadro constant, L).","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi2m000sjfqk7e3ppl5g","ord":2,"front":"What is the Avogadro constant?","back":"L = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹ — the number of particles per mole of substance.","tag":"EQUATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi2m000ujfqkdknh9e4l","ord":3,"front":"Define relative atomic mass (Ar).","back":"The weighted mean mass of an atom of an element compared with one-twelfth of the mass of an atom of carbon-12.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi2n000wjfqkw7lrwthl","ord":4,"front":"Define relative molecular mass (Mr).","back":"The weighted mean mass of a molecule compared with one-twelfth of the mass of an atom of carbon-12. Use \"relative formula mass\" for giant structures.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi2n000yjfqkugjym2t6","ord":5,"front":"Empirical vs molecular formula?","back":"Empirical = simplest whole-number ratio of atoms of each element. Molecular = actual number of atoms of each element in one molecule. Molecular = (empirical)ₙ where n = Mr ÷ empirical mass.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi2p0010jfqk7e2hbgkf","ord":6,"front":"How do you calculate % yield?","back":"% yield = (actual moles (or mass) of product ÷ theoretical moles (or mass)) × 100","tag":"EQUATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi2p0012jfqkppyhtv65","ord":7,"front":"How do you calculate atom economy?","back":"Atom economy = (Mr of desired product ÷ total Mr of ALL products) × 100. Based on the balanced equation, not on lab yield.","tag":"EQUATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi2p0014jfqk9errp5vt","ord":8,"front":"What is ppm?","back":"Parts per million = (mass (or volume) of substance ÷ total mass (or volume)) × 10⁶. Used for trace concentrations, e.g. CO₂ ≈ 420 ppm by volume in air.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi2q0016jfqk3amc976d","ord":9,"front":"State the ideal molar gas volume at RTP (298 K, 100 kPa).","back":"24 dm³ mol⁻¹ (24 000 cm³ mol⁻¹). Volume of gas = moles × 24 dm³.","tag":"EQUATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi2q0018jfqkxi1tuufw","ord":10,"front":"What are the steps of a titration calculation?","back":"1) moles of the known solution = c × V (dm³). 2) scale by the stoichiometric ratio. 3) find unknown moles. 4) c = n ÷ V.","tag":"METHOD"}]},{"slug":"atomic-structure","number":2,"unit":1,"unitCode":"WCH11","unitName":"Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry","title":"Atomic Structure and the Periodic Table","subtitle":"Inside the atom — isotopes, electrons and the trends that organise the elements.","summary":"Subatomic particles and isotopes; the mass spectrometer; ionisation energies and their trends; electron configuration; periodicity across Period 3.","estHours":4.5,"sections":[{"id":"cmv1eoi2r001bjfqk0n477txl","sectionKey":"specification-map","number":"2.0","title":"Specification Map","blurb":"Every statement the specification assesses in this topic — use it as your revision checklist.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"2.1 – 2.18","specText":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:","outline":["The full list of assessment statements for Topic 2, worded exactly as the specification words them"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Spec","Assessment statement"],"rows":[["2.1","know the structure of an atom in terms of electrons, protons and neutrons"],["2.2","know the relative mass and charge of protons, neutrons and electrons"],["2.3","know what is meant by the terms 'atomic (proton) number' and 'mass number'"],["2.4","be able to use the atomic number and the mass number to determine the number of each type of subatomic particle in an atom or ion"],["2.5","understand the term 'isotope'"],["2.6","understand the basic principles of a mass spectrometer and be able to analyse and interpret mass spectra to:\ni   deduce the isotopic composition of a sample of an element\nii   calculate the relative atomic mass of an element from relative abundances of isotopes and vice versa\niii   determine the relative molecular mass of a molecule, and hence identify molecules in a sample\niv   understand that ions in a mass spectrometer may have a 2+ charge"],["2.7","be able to predict mass spectra, including relative peak heights, for diatomic molecules, including chlorine, given the isotopic abundances"],["2.8","be able to define first, second and third ionisation energies and understand that all ionisation energies are endothermic"],["2.9","know that an orbital is a region within an atom that can hold up to two electrons with opposite spins"],["2.10","understand how ionisation energies are influenced by the number of protons in the nucleus, the electron shielding and the sub-shell from which the electron is removed"],["2.11","know that ideas about electronic structure developed from:\ni   an understanding that successive ionisation energies provide evidence for the existence of quantum shells and the group to which the element belongs\nii   an understanding that the first ionisation energy of successive elements provides evidence for electron sub-shells"],["2.12","be able to describe the shapes of s and p orbitals"],["2.13","know that orbitals in sub-shells:\ni   each take a single electron before pairing up\nii   pair up with two electrons of opposite spin"],["2.14","be able to predict the electronic configuration of atoms of the elements from hydrogen to krypton inclusive and their ions, using s, p, d notation and electron-in-boxes notation"],["2.15","understand that electronic configuration determines the chemical properties of an element"],["2.16","know that the Periodic Table is divided into blocks, such as s, p and d, and know the number of electrons that can occupy s, p and d sub-shells in the first four quantum shells"],["2.17","be able to represent data, in a graphical form (including the use of logarithms of first ionisation energies on a graph) for elements 1 to 36 and hence explain the meaning of the term 'periodic property'"],["2.18","be able to explain:\ni   the trends in melting and boiling temperatures of the elements of Periods 2 and 3 of the Periodic Table in terms of the structure of the element and the bonding between its atoms or molecules\nii   the general increase and the specific trends in ionisation energy of the elements across Periods 2 and 3 of the Periodic Table\niii   the decrease in first ionisation energy down a group"]]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2s001djfqkbmne7vki","sectionKey":"atom-structure","number":"2.1","title":"Structure of the Atom","blurb":"Protons, neutrons and electrons — and where each one lives.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"2.1","specText":"know the structure of an atom in terms of electrons, protons and neutrons","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Our current understanding of the structure of atoms is influenced by the theories put forward by scientists such as **J.J Thomson** (discovered electrons in 1897), **Ernest Rutherford** (discovered proton in 1917) and **James Chadwick** (discovered the neutron in 1932)."},{"type":"def","term":"Atom","text":"An atom is the smallest, electrically neutral particle of an element that can take part in a chemical change."},{"type":"list","items":["An atom comprises of subatomic particles **protons**, **neutrons** and **electrons**.","Protons and neutrons, together known as **nucleons**, are found in the nucleus which is the center of the atom.","Electrons exist in energy levels surrounding the nuclei. The energy levels are called **quantum shells**.","An atom gets its chemical properties from the number of electrons and arrangement of electrons in the atom."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/atom-structure.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/atom-structure-dark.png","alt":"Model of the atom showing the nucleus and electron shells","caption":"Nucleons (protons and neutrons) are found in the nucleus; electrons exist in energy levels — quantum shells — around it."},{"type":"list","items":["The mass of electrons are negligible, hence the mass of the atoms are determined by the mass of nucleons.","The protons and neutrons in the nucleus give the atom its **mass**; the electrons around the nucleus give it its **chemical properties**."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2s001fjfqkmt4cs3mq","sectionKey":"relative-mass-charge","number":"2.2","title":"Relative Masses and Charges","blurb":"Weighing and charging the three subatomic particles against each other.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"2.2","specText":"know the relative mass and charge of protons, neutrons and electrons","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"The masses and charges of the three sub atomic particles"},{"type":"table","headers":["Particle","Symbol","Charge / C","Relative charge","Mass / g","Relative mass"],"rows":[["Proton","p","+1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹","+1","1.67 × 10⁻²⁴","1"],["Neutron","n","0","0","1.67 × 10⁻²⁴","1"],["Electron","e⁻","−1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹","−1","9.11 × 10⁻²⁸","1 / 1840"]]},{"type":"list","items":["Protons and neutrons have almost identical masses — each is given a **relative mass of 1** on this scale.","The electron is **1840 times lighter** than a proton, so its mass is taken as negligible (1/1840).","The proton carries a **relative charge of +1** and the electron a **relative charge of −1**; the neutron is **uncharged (0)**.","An atom is electrically neutral because the number of protons is equal to the number of electrons — the +1 and −1 charges cancel out."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2s001hjfqkdvezxpe0","sectionKey":"atomic-mass-number","number":"2.3","title":"Atomic Number and Mass Number","blurb":"The two numbers that label every atom, and the notation that carries them.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"2.3","specText":"know what is meant by the terms ‘atomic (proton) number’ and ‘mass number’","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Atomic (proton) number (Z)","text":"The number of protons in the nucleus of an atom of an element. The atomic number is unique to the element — it defines what the element is."},{"type":"def","term":"Mass number (A)","text":"The total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom."},{"type":"list","items":["Atoms are represented by the following notation, where the **mass number A** is written at the top left and the **atomic number Z** at the bottom left of the symbol.","Number of **neutrons = mass number − atomic number (A − Z)**.","In a neutral atom, the number of **electrons = the number of protons (Z)**."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/nuclide-notation.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/nuclide-notation-dark.png","alt":"Nuclide notation showing mass number A and atomic number Z beside the symbol X","caption":"Mass number = sum of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom · Atomic number = protons in the nucleus of an atom."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2t001jjfqkvgcw56z6","sectionKey":"counting-particles","number":"2.4","title":"Counting Subatomic Particles","blurb":"Using Z and A to count protons, neutrons and electrons in atoms and ions.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"2.4","specText":"be able to use the atomic number and the mass number to determine the number of each type of subatomic particle in an atom or ion","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"list","items":["The atomic number and the mass number are all you need to count every subatomic particle in an atom or an ion:","**Protons = Z** — the atomic number.","**Neutrons = A − Z** — the mass number minus the atomic number.","**Electrons in a neutral atom = Z**.","In a **negative ion** the atom has gained electrons: electrons = Z + (size of the charge). Example: Cl⁻ has 17 + 1 = 18 electrons.","In a **positive ion** the atom has lost electrons: electrons = Z − (size of the charge). Example: Ca²⁺ has 20 − 2 = 18 electrons.","Forming an ion changes **only the number of electrons** — the number of protons and neutrons stays the same."]},{"type":"worked","title":"Counting the particles in a calcium ion","problem":"Determine the number of protons, neutrons and electrons in the ion ⁴⁰₂₀Ca²⁺.","steps":["Protons = Z = 20","Neutrons = A − Z = 40 − 20 = 20","The 2+ charge means 2 electrons have been lost: electrons = 20 − 2 = 18"],"answer":"20 protons, 20 neutrons, 18 electrons"},{"type":"worked","title":"Counting the particles in a chloride ion","problem":"Determine the number of protons, neutrons and electrons in the ion ³⁵₁₇Cl⁻.","steps":["Protons = Z = 17","Neutrons = A − Z = 35 − 17 = 18","The − charge means 1 electron has been gained: electrons = 17 + 1 = 18"],"answer":"17 protons, 18 neutrons, 18 electrons"}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2u001ljfqk7jt1tiwj","sectionKey":"isotopes","number":"2.5","title":"Isotopes","blurb":"Same protons, different neutrons — why isotopes behave almost identically.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"2.5","specText":"understand the term ‘isotope’","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Isotope","text":"Isotopes are atoms of the same element having the same number of protons but different number of neutrons."},{"type":"list","items":["Example: Carbon has three isotopes, ¹²C, ¹³C and ¹⁴C. They all have the same number of protons but different number of neutrons."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/carbon-isotopes.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/carbon-isotopes-dark.png","alt":"The three isotopes of carbon shown with their protons, neutrons and mass numbers","caption":"The three isotopes of carbon — the atomic number stays 6 while the number of neutrons (and so the mass number) changes."},{"type":"table","headers":["","Protons","Neutrons","Mass number"],"rows":[["Carbon – 12","6","6","12"],["Carbon – 13","6","7","13"],["Carbon – 14","6","8","14"]]},{"type":"list","items":["Isotopes always have the same **chemical properties** because the number of electrons is the same.","They have slightly different **physical properties** as they have different number of neutrons.","Example: Isotopes with fewer neutrons will have lower masses, lower densities, faster rate of diffusion, lower melting and boiling points.","Relative atomic masses measured by chemical methods rarely produce whole numbers. This is because isotopes of the same element exist in different proportions in nature."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2v001njfqkgfyblo4e","sectionKey":"mass-spectrometer","number":"2.6","title":"The Mass Spectrometer","blurb":"The instrument that weighs atoms — and how to read the spectra it produces.","estMinutes":25,"spec":"2.6","specText":"understand the basic principles of a mass spectrometer and be able to analyse and interpret mass spectra to:","outline":["i   deduce the isotopic composition of a sample of an element","ii   calculate the relative atomic mass of an element from relative abundances of isotopes and vice versa","iii   determine the relative molecular mass of a molecule, and hence identify molecules in a sample","iv   understand that ions in a mass spectrometer may have a 2+ charge"],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Relative masses of elements or compounds"},{"type":"list","items":["Measuring the mass of an atom or compound using a balance would be very difficult as they are very small.","Hence, to measure the mass of an atom or a compound the **atomic mass unit (amu)** is used as a standard reference.","The relative atomic mass does not have any units. They are measured compare to amu."]},{"type":"list","items":["**Atomic mass unit** is the mass of a carbon-12 atom divided by 12. This is used as the standard reference scale to measure mass of atoms and molecules.","**Relative atomic mass** of an element is the weighted average mass of an atom of that element divided by 1/12th the mass of a carbon-12 atom.","**Relative molecular mass** of a molecule or compound is the sum of the relative atomic masses of all the atoms in the chemical formula.","**Relative isotopic mass** is the mass of an atom of that isotope divided by 1/12th the mass of a carbon-12 atom."]},{"type":"h3","text":"What is a mass spectrometer?"},{"type":"def","term":"Mass spectrometer","text":"An instrument that was invented by F.W. Aston in 1919. It can be used to find the relative masses of isotopes of atoms, molecules or fragments of molecules. This gave chemists an accurate method of comparing the relative masses of atoms and molecules, and to find the relative abundance of the isotopes in a sample of an element."},{"type":"list","items":["The mass spectrometer consists of an **ion source**, an **analyser** and a **detector**.","Inside the spectrometer there is a **high vacuum** which allows ionized atoms and molecules from the chemical being tested to be studied without interference from atoms and molecules in the air."]},{"type":"h3","text":"What can mass spectrometry be used for?"},{"type":"list","items":["**Carbon dating** — to measure the amount of C-14 in a substance and hence to determine the age of the substance.","**Pharmaceutical industry** — to identify the new compounds in possibly new drugs and to identify how long they would stay in the body.","**Testing athletes’ urine** for drugs or banned substances such as anabolic steroids.","**Studying the composition of the surface of Mars** — to look for molecules that might suggest that life existed on the planet, by sending probes to Mars."]},{"type":"h3","text":"How does a mass spectrometer work?"},{"type":"p","text":"There are five main processes occurring in a mass spectrometer. They are:"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["**Vapourisation:** the sample is turned into gas or vapourised using an electrical heater.","**Ionization:** gas particles are bombarded with a stream of high energy electrons from an electron gun, to ionize them. Electrons are knocked off the particles, producing uni-positively charged ions.","**Acceleration:** the positive ions are accelerated by an electric field towards the magnetic field, which provides the energy for the particles to accelerate.","**Deflection:** the positive ions’ path is altered with a magnetic field. For a given magnetic field, only ions with a particular mass/charge ratio will be detected. Lighter ions have less momentum and are deflected more than heavier ions. Ions with a large m/z ratio deflected less than ions with a smaller m/z ratio.","**Detection:** the ions are detected by the detector, which is linked to an amplifier and then to a recorder. This converts the current to a peak which is shown in a mass spectrum."]},{"type":"eq","code":"X(s) → X(g)      ·      X(l) → X(g)","caption":"Stage 1 — vapourisation"},{"type":"eq","code":"X(g) → X⁺(g) + e⁻","caption":"Stage 2 — ionization by the electron gun"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/mass-spectrometer.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/mass-spectrometer-dark.png","alt":"Schematic of a mass spectrometer showing the five stages","caption":"Vapourised sample → ionisation → acceleration → deflection by the electromagnet → detection → amplifier → chart recorder. A vacuum pump keeps the inside under high vacuum."},{"type":"h3","text":"The mass spectrum"},{"type":"list","items":["An early application was the demonstration by Aston (Nobel Prize, 1922) that naturally occurring neon consisted of three isotopes; ²⁰Ne, ²¹Ne and ²²Ne.","Position of peaks give **atomic mass**.","Peak intensity gives **relative abundance**.","The tallest peak represents the most abundant isotope."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/neon-spectrum.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/neon-spectrum-dark.png","alt":"Mass spectrum of neon with peaks at m/z 20, 21 and 22","caption":"The mass spectrum of neon — peaks at m/z 20, 21 and 22 with relative abundances 90.92%, 0.26% and 8.82%."},{"type":"h3","text":"Determining relative isotopic and atomic masses"},{"type":"p","text":"The mass spectrum could be used to calculate the relative atomic mass of an element."},{"type":"worked","title":"Relative atomic mass of neon","problem":"Calculate the average relative atomic mass of neon using the above information. Out of every 100 atoms 90.92 are ²⁰Ne, 0.26 are ²¹Ne and 8.82 are ²²Ne.","steps":["Out of every 100 atoms: 90.92 atoms of ²⁰Ne, 0.26 atoms of ²¹Ne and 8.82 atoms of ²²Ne","**Weighted mean:** mean mass = (90.92 × 20 + 0.26 × 21 + 8.82 × 22) ÷ 100","= (1818.4 + 5.46 + 194.04) ÷ 100 = 2017.9 ÷ 100"],"answer":"Relative atomic mass of neon = 20.18"},{"type":"worked","title":"Relative atomic mass of chlorine","problem":"What is the relative atomic mass of chlorine, if it exists in two isotopic forms: ³⁵Cl (relative isotopic mass 35, relative abundance 75) and ³⁷Cl (relative isotopic mass 37, relative abundance 25)?","steps":["**Weighted mean:** mean mass = (35 × 75 + 37 × 25) ÷ 100","= (2625 + 925) ÷ 100 = 3550 ÷ 100"],"answer":"Relative atomic mass of chlorine = 35.5"},{"type":"h3","text":"Determining relative molecular mass of diatomic molecules"},{"type":"list","items":["If an element exists as diatomic molecules, like chlorine, then the spectrum would contain peaks for the separate **atomic ions** as well as for the **molecular ions**.","Molecular ions are formed by the removal of an electron from the whole molecule.","Atomic ions are formed when the Cl—Cl bond is broken by a high energy beam of electron."]},{"type":"p","text":"If diatomic substances are analysed by mass spectrometry, you can obtain the relative molecular mass of the element or compound by observing the peaks with the **largest m/z ratios** (assuming a value of z = 1)."},{"type":"h3","text":"Determining relative molecular mass of a polyatomic molecule"},{"type":"p","text":"Mass spectrum of ethanol (C₂H₅OH):"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/ethanol-spectrum.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/ethanol-spectrum-dark.png","alt":"Mass spectrum of ethanol with the molecular ion peak at m/z 46","caption":"The mass spectrum of ethanol — the M peak at m/z 46 is the molecular ion peak, giving the relative molecular mass of the compound."},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Reading a molecular ion peak","text":"The peak with the highest m/z ratio is the **molecular ion peak**, which gives the RMM of the compound. RMM for ethanol = 46."},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Ions with a 2+ charge","text":"Ions in the spectrometer may carry a 2+ charge. A doubly-charged ion X²⁺ is recorded at **m/z = mass ÷ 2**, so a molecular ion of relative molecular mass M appears at m/z M/2 — remember this when reading spectra."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2w001pjfqk1e1grf4o","sectionKey":"diatomic-spectra","number":"2.7","title":"Mass Spectra of Diatomic Molecules","blurb":"Predicting chlorine’s five peaks and the 9 : 6 : 1 ratio from first principles.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"2.7","specText":"be able to predict mass spectra, including relative peak heights, for diatomic molecules, including chlorine, given the isotopic abundances","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Chlorine exists as two isotopes, ³⁵Cl (75%) and ³⁷Cl (25%). When chlorine vapour is analysed, the spectrum contains peaks for the atomic ions and for the molecular ions of the two isotopes combined:"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/chlorine-spectrum.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/chlorine-spectrum-dark.png","alt":"Mass spectrum of chlorine showing atomic ion peaks A and B and molecular ion peaks C, D and E","caption":"Mass spectrum of chlorine vapour — peaks A and B are the atomic ions (m/z 35 and 37); peaks C, D and E are the molecular ions (m/z 70, 72 and 74)."},{"type":"table","headers":["Peak","Peak height ratio","m/z ratio","Species"],"rows":[["A","3","35","³⁵Cl⁺"],["B","1","37","³⁷Cl⁺"],["C","9","70","³⁵Cl₂⁺  (two ³⁵Cl atoms)"],["D","6","72","³⁵Cl³⁷Cl⁺  (one of each)"],["E","1","74","³⁷Cl₂⁺  (two ³⁷Cl atoms)"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Explanation of peak height ratio"},{"type":"table","headers":["","³⁵Cl","³⁷Cl"],"rows":[["Abundance","75%","25%   = 100%"],["Chance of getting each isotope","75/100","25/100"],["Simplifying the ratio","3/4","1/4"]]},{"type":"list","items":["There is **3 in 4 chance** of selecting a ³⁵Cl from a sample of chlorine atom. This means total chance of two ³⁵Cl atoms combining together is ¾ × ¾ = **9/16**.","There is **1 in 4 chance** of selecting a ³⁷Cl atom. This means that the chance of ³⁷Cl combining with a ³⁵Cl atom is ¼ × ¾ = **3/16**.","The chance of a ³⁵Cl atom combining with a ³⁷Cl atom is also **3/16**.","This means that the total chance of ³⁷Cl and ³⁵Cl combining together in any order is 2 × 3/16 = **6/16**.","The chance of two ³⁷Cl atoms combining together is ¼ × ¼ = **1/16**.","9/16 : 6/16 : 1/16 in whole number ratio is **9 : 6 : 1**."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Predicting diatomic peak ratios","text":"For a diatomic molecule with isotopic abundances in the ratio **a : b**, the three molecular-ion peak heights follow the ratio **a² : 2ab : b²** — the expansion of (a + b)². For chlorine, a : b = 3 : 1, so the peaks are 3² : 2(3)(1) : 1² = 9 : 6 : 1."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2x001rjfqkbesdpw3g","sectionKey":"ie-definitions","number":"2.8","title":"Defining Ionisation Energies","blurb":"First, second and third ionisation energies — every one of them endothermic.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"2.8","specText":"be able to define first, second and third ionisation energies and understand that all ionisation energies are endothermic","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"**Ionization energy** is a measure of the energy required to remove electrons from an atom. There are as many ionization energies as there are electrons in the atom."},{"type":"h3","text":"First ionization energy"},{"type":"def","term":"First ionization energy","text":"The energy required to remove one mole of electrons from one mole of gaseous atoms to form one mole of gaseous uni-positive ions."},{"type":"eq","code":"Na(g) → Na⁺(g) + e⁻","caption":"Example"},{"type":"eq","code":"Mg(g) → Mg⁺(g) + e⁻","caption":"Example"},{"type":"callout","variant":"warning","title":"Always gaseous","text":"Ionisation energies are defined for gaseous atoms and ions — every species in the equation carries the state symbol (g)."},{"type":"list","items":["Its value gives an idea of how strongly the nucleus pulls on the electron being removed. The stronger the pull, the more energy needed to remove the electron."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Second ionization energy"},{"type":"def","term":"Second ionization energy","text":"The energy required to remove 1 mole of electrons from 1 mole of the gaseous uni-positive ions to form one mole of gaseous di-positive ions."},{"type":"eq","code":"Na⁺(g) → Na²⁺(g) + e⁻","caption":"Example"},{"type":"eq","code":"Mg⁺(g) → Mg²⁺(g) + e⁻","caption":"Example"},{"type":"h3","text":"Third ionization energy"},{"type":"def","term":"Third ionization energy","text":"The energy required to remove 1 mole of electrons from 1 mole of the gaseous di-positive ions to form one mole of gaseous tri-positive ions."},{"type":"eq","code":"Na²⁺(g) → Na³⁺(g) + e⁻","caption":"Example"},{"type":"eq","code":"Mg²⁺(g) → Mg³⁺(g) + e⁻","caption":"Example"},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"All ionisation energies are endothermic","text":"Every ionisation needs energy to overcome the attraction between the nucleus and the electron, so ionisation energies always have positive (endothermic) values."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2y001tjfqkyvgozhli","sectionKey":"shells-orbitals","number":"2.9","title":"Quantum Shells and Orbitals","blurb":"Shells, sub-shells and the regions that hold at most two electrons.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"2.9","specText":"know that an orbital is a region within an atom that can hold up to two electrons with opposite spins","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Quantum shells"},{"type":"p","text":"Max Planck first presented the quantum theory in 1900. We can use this theory to describe the arrangement of electrons around the nuclei. According to this theory electrons exist around the nucleus in certain regions of the atom called shells or energy levels. These well-defined energy levels are called **quantum shells**."},{"type":"list","items":["Each shell is described by a number known as the **principle quantum number (n)**. The larger the value of n, the further the electron is from the nucleus."]},{"type":"h3","text":"From shells to sub-shells to orbitals"},{"type":"list","items":["The energy levels or quantum shells (apart from the first one) are divided into **sub-shells** of slightly different energy levels. Each sub-shell is described by a letter s, p, d and f.","The energy of each sub-shell increases from s to p to d to f.","Each sub-shell is made up of **orbitals**."]},{"type":"def","term":"Orbital","text":"An orbital is a region within an atom that can hold up to two electrons with opposite spins."},{"type":"list","items":["An orbital is the region where the electrons are most likely to be found.","Orbitals are 3-dimensional statistical maps showing the likeliest places to find electrons — they come in different shapes and sizes.","Each orbital can accommodate a **maximum of two electrons** in which one electron spins clockwise and the other spins anti-clockwise."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2z001vjfqksvzhiyii","sectionKey":"ie-factors","number":"2.10","title":"Factors Affecting Ionisation Energy","blurb":"Nuclear charge, shielding, distance — and the sub-shell the electron leaves.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"2.10","specText":"understand how ionisation energies are influenced by the number of protons in the nucleus, the electron shielding and the sub-shell from which the electron is removed","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"list","items":["The value of ionization energy depends on the **distance of the electron from the nucleus**, and the **effective nuclear charge** (not the nuclear charge) of the atom."]},{"type":"def","term":"Nuclear charge (NC)","text":"The actual charge (relative) due to the protons in the nucleus."},{"type":"def","term":"Effective nuclear charge (ENC)","text":"The effectiveness of nuclear charge after passing through filled shells."},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Comparing effective nuclear charges","text":"A simple way to compare effective nuclear charges is to knock off a + for every electron in a filled inner level."},{"type":"h3","text":"What determines the size of the first ionisation energy?"},{"type":"p","text":"The amount of energy required to remove the first electron depends on the following factors:"},{"type":"list","items":["**The number of protons in the nucleus (nuclear charge)**\n- As the effective nuclear charge increases, force of attraction between the nucleus and the outer electrons increases.\n- Electrons are harder to remove (higher ionization energy).","**Electron shielding**\n- The electron in the outermost energy level experiences more inter-electron repulsion known as shielding.\n- The greater the number of the inner shell electrons the greater is the shielding effect (screening effect).\n- The force of attraction becomes weaker.\n- Less energy is required to remove the electron (lower ionization energy).","**Distance between the nucleus and the outermost electrons**\n- As the atoms get larger, the distance between the outer most electrons and the nucleus increases.\n- The outer electrons are held less strongly so easier to remove.\n- Less energy is required to remove the electron (lower ionization energy).","**The sub-shell from which the electron is removed**\n- Sub-shells sit at slightly different energies, so the electron removed is not always held equally tightly.\n- An electron in a higher-energy sub-shell is easier to remove: a 2p electron (boron) is easier to remove than a 2s electron (beryllium), and a 3p electron (aluminium) than a 3s electron (magnesium).\n- A **paired** electron is also easier to remove than an unpaired one — the two electrons sharing an orbital repel each other."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi2z001xjfqkh99txcwl","sectionKey":"ie-evidence","number":"2.11","title":"Ionisation Energies as Evidence","blurb":"Successive and first ionisation energies — the numbers that revealed shells and sub-shells.","estMinutes":25,"spec":"2.11","specText":"know that ideas about electronic structure developed from:","outline":["i   an understanding that successive ionisation energies provide evidence for the existence of quantum shells and the group to which the element belongs","ii   an understanding that the first ionisation energy of successive elements provides evidence for electron sub-shells"],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Successive ionization energies of an element"},{"type":"p","text":"The patterns in successive ionisation energies for an element give us important information about the electronic structure for that element."},{"type":"list","items":["When successive ionization energies of an element are listed, there are steady increases and big jumps occuring at defined places. This is one piece of evidence for the existence of **quantum shells**.","A sodium atom has 11 electrons. Its successive ionization energies are shown below — the values where the ionization energy has jumped significantly are marked in bold."]},{"type":"table","headers":["","1st","2nd","3rd","4th","5th","6th"],"rows":[["IE / kJ mol⁻¹","496","**4563**","6913","9544","13352","16611"]]},{"type":"table","headers":["","7th","8th","9th","10th","11th"],"rows":[["IE / kJ mol⁻¹","20115","25491","28934","**141367**","159079"]]},{"type":"p","text":"When a graph is plotted between the log of ionization energy values of an element against the number of electrons removed, the trends in successive ionization energies can be seen. There are two types of increases that can be observed."},{"type":"list","items":["**Successive ionization energies are always greater than the previous one** — this is due to increasing attraction of the nucleus on the remaining electrons, because the electrons are being removed from an increasingly positive ion.","**Large increases occur when there is a change of shell** — this is due to removal of electron from an inner main shell, which is closer to the nucleus on which the nuclear attraction is greater.","This can be used to predict the **group** of an unknown element. The group number would be given by counting the number of electrons removed before the first big jump. Electronic structure can also be found using successive ionization energies."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/sodium-ie.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/sodium-ie-dark.png","alt":"Graph of log ionisation energy against number of electrons removed for sodium","caption":"Successive ionization energies of sodium, plotted as log(IE) against the order of electron removed — the big jumps after the 1st and 9th electrons mark the changes of quantum shell."},{"type":"h3","text":"Why do the successive ionization energies of sodium increase?"},{"type":"list","items":["The first electron to be removed is in the 3s orbital, which is the third quantum shell. So less energy is needed to remove the electron. Therefore the first ionization energy is the lowest.","There is a large jump from 1st to the 2nd ionization energy. This is because the second electron to be removed is in the 2nd quantum shell which is lower in energy than the 3rd quantum shell.","There is steady increase in ionization energy from the 2nd to the 9th electron. This shows that the eight electrons exist within the same quantum shell. As each successive electron is removed from the same shell, the electron – electron repulsion within the shell decreases. As a result, there is steady increase in ionization energy from 2nd to 9th electron.","The large jump from 9th to 10th electron indicates that the electron is removed from first quantum shell (1s orbital)."]},{"type":"h3","text":"First ionisation energies of successive elements"},{"type":"list","items":["Plotting the **first ionisation energy of successive elements** against atomic number gives a repeating saw-tooth pattern: the value rises across each period to a peak at each noble gas, then falls steeply at the start of the next period.","The rise is not perfectly smooth — there are **dips after Group 2** (Be → B and Mg → Al) and **dips after Group 5** (N → O and P → S).","These dips are evidence for **electron sub-shells**: the electron removed from boron is a 2p electron — higher in energy than the 2s electron removed from beryllium — and the electron removed from oxygen is a paired 2p electron, which repels its partner.","Because the pattern **repeats in the same way across every period**, it is powerful evidence that the shells and sub-shells of every element are filled in the same order."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/first-ie-periods.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/first-ie-periods-dark.png","alt":"First ionization energies plotted against atomic number for the elements hydrogen to argon","caption":"First ionization energies for periods 1–3 — a periodic property: rising to a peak at each noble gas, with dips after Group 2 and Group 5."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi30001zjfqk067hnqmd","sectionKey":"orbital-shapes","number":"2.12","title":"Shapes of s and p Orbitals","blurb":"Spheres and dumbbells — the geometry of electron space.","estMinutes":5,"spec":"2.12","specText":"be able to describe the shapes of s and p orbitals","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"list","items":["The orbitals in different sub-levels have different shapes.","**s orbitals** are spherical — the electron density is spread evenly in all directions around the nucleus. Every shell contains one s orbital (1s, 2s, 3s …), each larger than the one before.","**p orbitals** are dumbbell-shaped, with two lobes either side of the nucleus. Each p sub-shell holds **three** p orbitals — pₓ, p_y and p_z — arranged at right angles along the x, y and z axes.","The five d orbitals have four-lobed shapes (their study is beyond the requirements of this specification)."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/orbitals.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/orbitals-dark.png","alt":"Shapes of s, p and d orbitals","caption":"Shapes of orbitals — s orbitals are spherical; each p sub-shell holds three dumbbell-shaped orbitals (pₓ, p_y, p_z) along the x, y and z axes; the five d orbitals have four-lobed shapes."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi310021jfqkjwhhoqv4","sectionKey":"filling-rules","number":"2.13","title":"Rules for Filling Orbitals","blurb":"Singly first, spins paired — Hund’s rule and the Pauli exclusion principle.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"2.13","specText":"know that orbitals in sub-shells:","outline":["i   each take a single electron before pairing up","ii   pair up with two electrons of opposite spin"],"content":[{"type":"list","items":["The orbitals in a sub-shell fill in a fixed pattern, described by two rules:"]},{"type":"def","term":"Hund's rule","text":"Electrons fill orbitals singly before they start pairing up."},{"type":"def","term":"Pauli Exclusion Principle","text":"Two electrons cannot occupy the same orbital unless they have opposite spin. This is due to the repulsion between electron pairs."},{"type":"list","items":["Each of the orbitals in a sub-shell **takes a single electron before any orbital gets a second electron** — for example, the three 2p orbitals of nitrogen fill as 2p¹ 2p¹ 2p¹, not 2p² 2p¹.","When a second electron does enter an orbital, it **pairs up with opposite spin** — one electron spins clockwise, the other anti-clockwise.","Electrons entering separate orbitals singly have **parallel spins**."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi310023jfqklrsfhb5o","sectionKey":"electronic-configuration","number":"2.14","title":"Electronic Configuration","blurb":"Writing configurations from hydrogen to krypton — spdf, boxes and the Cr/Cu exceptions.","estMinutes":25,"spec":"2.14","specText":"be able to predict the electronic configuration of atoms of the elements from hydrogen to krypton inclusive and their ions, using s, p, d notation and electron-in-boxes notation","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Electrons are added to the sub-shells according to the **Aufbau Principle**. The following rules must be applied when writing electronic configuration."},{"type":"def","term":"Aufbau principle","text":"Electrons fill up the lowest energy orbital first."},{"type":"list","items":["The sub-shells which are half filled or completely filled with electrons are more stable. Orbitals are not filled in numerical order.","For example, **4s is filled before 3d**. This is because the 4s orbital is at a lower energy level than a 3d orbital."]},{"type":"eq","code":"1s  2s  2p  3s  3p  4s  3d  4p  …","caption":"The order in which sub-shells fill"},{"type":"list","items":["When writing electronic configuration, the electronic structure of an atom can be represented in two ways: **s p d f notation** and **electron box notation**.","A shortened version of electronic structure can also be used, by writing the noble gas electronic configuration in square brackets."]},{"type":"worked","title":"Electronic configuration of carbon","problem":"Write down the electronic configuration of ₆C using spdf notation and box notation.","steps":["Carbon has 6 electrons. Fill the lowest energy orbitals first: 1s, then 2s, then 2p.","**spdf notation:** 1s² 2s² 2p²","**Box notation:** 1s [↑↓] · 2s [↑↓] · 2p [↑] [↑] [ ] — the two 2p electrons occupy separate orbitals singly, with parallel spins (Hund's rule)."],"answer":"1s² 2s² 2p²"},{"type":"worked","title":"Electronic configuration of sulfur","problem":"Write down the electronic configuration of ₁₆S using spdf notation and box notation.","steps":["Sulfur has 16 electrons: fill 1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, then 3p.","**spdf notation:** 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁴","**Box notation:** 1s [↑↓] · 2s [↑↓] · 2p [↑↓] [↑↓] [↑↓] · 3s [↑↓] · 3p [↑↓] [↑] [↑]","**Noble gas notation:** [Ne] 3s² 3p⁴"],"answer":"1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁴  — or  [Ne] 3s² 3p⁴"},{"type":"h3","text":"The exceptions: chromium and copper"},{"type":"list","items":["The electronic structure of **Cr** and **Cu** are exceptional. In both cases the outer subshells are either completely full or half full. This is achieved by donating one of their 4s electrons to the 3d sub shell, as this makes the d-sub shell to be half filled or completely filled.","Chromium is **1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁵ 4s¹** — not  1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁴ 4s².","Copper is **1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰ 4s¹** — not  1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁹ 4s².","Completely filled or half-filled sub-shell is a more stable electronic configuration."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Electronic configuration of ions"},{"type":"list","items":["When an atom loses or gains an electron to form ions, the electronic structure changes.","**Cations (positive ions)** — formed by loss of electrons.","**Anions (negative ions)** — formed by gain of electrons."]},{"type":"worked","title":"Sodium atom and sodium ion","problem":"Write down the electronic configuration of the sodium atom and the sodium ion using spdf notation.","steps":["Sodium atom, Na (Z = 11): 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹","The Na⁺ ion is formed by losing the single 3s electron."],"answer":"Na⁺: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ — the same configuration as neon"},{"type":"worked","title":"Chlorine atom and chloride ion","problem":"Write down the electronic configuration of the chlorine atom and the chloride ion using spdf notation.","steps":["Chlorine atom, Cl (Z = 17): 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁵","The Cl⁻ ion is formed by gaining one electron into the last 3p orbital."],"answer":"Cl⁻: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ — the same configuration as argon"}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi320025jfqkrlgr0wvv","sectionKey":"config-properties","number":"2.15","title":"Configuration and Chemical Properties","blurb":"Why an element’s electrons decide its chemistry and its place in the table.","estMinutes":5,"spec":"2.15","specText":"understand that electronic configuration determines the chemical properties of an element","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Atoms can be identified by the mass of their nucleons; however, it is their **electronic structure** which determines the behavior and chemical properties."},{"type":"list","items":["All the elements within a group have the **same number of electrons in their outer shell** and they have similar properties.","It is the outer (valence) electrons that take part in chemical reactions — so the configuration of the outer shell decides how an element reacts.","Position of an element in the periodic table is determined by its electronic structure. The physical and chemical properties depend on the electronic configuration."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi320027jfqk5v22m7zh","sectionKey":"periodic-blocks","number":"2.16","title":"Blocks of the Periodic Table","blurb":"The s, p, d and f blocks — and how many electrons each sub-shell holds.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"2.16","specText":"know that the Periodic Table is divided into blocks, such as s, p and d, and know the number of electrons that can occupy s, p and d sub-shells in the first four quantum shells","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"list","items":["In the modern periodic table the elements are arranged in order of their **atomic number**.","Vertical columns in the periodic table are called **groups** and the horizontal rows are called **periods**.","All the elements within a period have the same number of electron shells. Example: elements in period 2 have 2 electron shells."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Group","1","2","3","4","5","6","7","8"],"rows":[["Outer electronic configuration","ns¹","ns²","ns²np¹","ns²np²","ns²np³","ns²np⁴","ns²np⁵","ns²np⁶"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"The blocks of the periodic table"},{"type":"p","text":"The periodic table can be split into four blocks, based on the orbital in which the last electron gets filled."},{"type":"list","items":["**s – Block:** elements of group 1 and 2. Their outermost electrons are found in the s sub-shell.","**p – Block:** elements in groups 3 to 8. Outermost electrons are found in p sub-shell.","**d – Block:** transition elements and are found between group 2 and 3. Outermost electrons are found in d sub-shell.","**f – Block:** lanthanides and actinides, and they have outermost electrons in the f sub-shell."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/periodic-blocks.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/periodic-blocks-dark.png","alt":"The s, p, d and f blocks of the periodic table","caption":"The first four periods of the table — the block an element sits in tells you which sub-shell its last electron enters."},{"type":"h3","text":"Electrons in the first four quantum shells"},{"type":"table","headers":["Quantum shell or energy level","Sub-shells"],"rows":[["1","1s"],["2","2s   2p"],["3","3s   3p   3d"],["4","4s   4p   4d   4f"]]},{"type":"table","headers":["Sub-shell","Number of orbitals","Total number of electrons"],"rows":[["s","1  (1 × 2)","2"],["p","3  (3 × 2)","6"],["d","5  (5 × 2)","10"],["f","7  (7 × 2)","14"]]},{"type":"table","headers":["Shell","Principle quantum number (n)","Sub-shells","Total number of electrons"],"rows":[["1st","1","1s","2"],["2nd","2","2s   2p","8"],["3rd","3","3s   3p   3d","18"],["4th","4","4s   4p   4d   4f","32"]]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi330029jfqkvo8kdhmv","sectionKey":"periodic-graphs","number":"2.17","title":"Graphing Periodic Data","blurb":"Plotting first ionisation energies for elements 1–36 — and what ‘periodic property’ means.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"2.17","specText":"be able to represent data, in a graphical form (including the use of logarithms of first ionisation energies on a graph) for elements 1 to 36 and hence explain the meaning of the term ‘periodic property’","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Plotting the data"},{"type":"list","items":["Data for the elements can be represented in graphical form — for example the first ionisation energies of elements 1 to 36, plotted below using **logarithms of the ionisation energies**.","Logarithms are used because the ionisation energies span a wide range; taking log(IE) keeps the saw-tooth shape visible without the later periods dwarfing the first."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Z","Element","First IE / kJ mol⁻¹","Z","Element","First IE / kJ mol⁻¹"],"rows":[["1","H","1310","15","P","1060"],["2","He","2370","16","S","1000"],["3","Li","519","17","Cl","1260"],["4","Be","900","18","Ar","1520"],["5","B","799","19","K","418"],["6","C","1090","20","Ca","590"],["7","N","1400","21","Sc","632"],["8","O","1310","22","Ti","661"],["9","F","1680","23","V","648"],["10","Ne","2080","24","Cr","653"],["11","Na","494","25","Mn","716"],["12","Mg","736","26","Fe","762"],["13","Al","577","27","Co","757"],["14","Si","786","28","Ni","736"]]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/ie-1-36-log.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/ie-1-36-log-dark.png","alt":"Logarithm of first ionisation energy plotted against atomic number for elements 1 to 36","caption":"log(first ionisation energy) for the elements 1 to 36 — the same saw-tooth repeats across each period: rising to a peak at each noble gas (He, Ne, Ar, Kr), with small dips after Group 2 and Group 5, then a steep fall at each new period (Na, K)."},{"type":"h3","text":"What does ‘periodic property’ mean?"},{"type":"list","items":["A **periodic property** is any property of the elements whose values repeat in a regular pattern when the elements are arranged in order of atomic number.","First ionisation energy is a periodic property — the graph above shows the same pattern repeating across Period 1, Period 2, Period 3 and Period 4.","Other examples: atomic radius, ionic radius and melting and boiling temperatures — each rises and falls in the same repeating way across the periods."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Atomic radius"},{"type":"def","term":"Atomic radius","text":"The measurement of the size of an atom — the distance from the center of the nucleus to the boundary of the electron cloud. Since atoms does not have a well defined boundary, we can find the atomic radius by determining the distance between the two nuclei and dividing by 2."},{"type":"list","items":["The atomic radius generally **decreases across a period**.","Nuclear charge (proton number) increases across the period.","Shielding effect remains constant as the electrons are added to the same shell.","Therefore, attraction between the nucleus and the electrons increases.","Electrons are pulled closer to nucleus."]},{"type":"list","items":["The atomic radius generally **increases down the group**.","Electrons are added to new energy levels down the group.","Shielding effect increases.","The attraction between the electrons and the nucleus decreases."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Ionic radius"},{"type":"list","items":["The radius of a **positive ion is smaller** than the element’s atomic radius. This is because as the electrons are removed the remaining electrons are more strongly attracted to the positive nucleus.","The radius of a **negative ion is larger** than the element’s atomic radius. This is because as the electrons are gained, the number of electrons increases and therefore are bound less tightly to the nucleus."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/ionic-radius.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/ionic-radius-dark.png","alt":"Comparison of the radii of sodium and its ion, and chlorine and its ion","caption":"Na (11 protons, 11 electrons) shrinks to Na⁺ (11 protons, 10 electrons) · Cl (17 protons, 17 electrons) expands to Cl⁻ (17 protons, 18 electrons)."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi34002bjfqk8v99dl8a","sectionKey":"period-trends","number":"2.18","title":"Trends Across Periods and Down Groups","blurb":"Explaining melting points and ionisation energies across Periods 2 and 3, and down a group.","estMinutes":25,"spec":"2.18","specText":"be able to explain:","outline":["i   the trends in melting and boiling temperatures of the elements of Periods 2 and 3 of the Periodic Table in terms of the structure of the element and the bonding between its atoms or molecules","ii   the general increase and the specific trends in ionisation energy of the elements across Periods 2 and 3 of the Periodic Table","iii   the decrease in first ionisation energy down a group"],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Melting and boiling temperatures"},{"type":"p","text":"As you move from left to right the elements go from highly electropositive metals through metalloids with giant structures to the simple molecular structure of non-metals."},{"type":"list","items":["Both periods 2 and 3 show similar trends in their melting points and boiling points, which are linked to changes in structure and bond strength of these elements."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Period 2","Li","Be","B","C (diamond)","N","O","F"],"rows":[["Melting temperature / °C","181","1278","2300","3550","−210","−218","−220"],["Boiling temperature / °C","1342","2970","3927","4827","−196","−183","−188"],["Type of bonding","metallic","metallic","covalent","covalent","covalent","covalent","covalent"],["Structure","giant lattice","giant lattice","giant lattice","giant lattice","simple molecular","simple molecular","simple molecular"]]},{"type":"table","headers":["Period 3","Na","Mg","Al","Si","P","S","Cl"],"rows":[["Melting temperature / °C","98","649","660","1440","44","113","−101"],["Boiling temperature / °C","883","1107","2467","2355","280","445","−35"],["Type of bonding","metallic","metallic","metallic","covalent","covalent","covalent","covalent"],["Structure","giant lattice","giant lattice","giant lattice","giant lattice","simple molecular","simple molecular","simple molecular"]]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/melting-period3.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/melting-period3-dark.png","alt":"Melting points across Period 3 in kelvin","caption":"Melting points across Period 3 (in kelvin, from the table above) — rising to a maximum at silicon, then falling sharply for the simple molecular elements."},{"type":"h3","text":"Explaining the melting point trend"},{"type":"table","headers":["Element(s)","Structure and type of bonding","Trend in melting point"],"rows":[["Metals  (e.g. Na, Mg, Al)","Metallic bonding —\ngiant lattice structure","Melting point increases across the period:\n- ionic size decreases across the period\n- more number of electrons in the ‘sea’ of electrons\n- therefore strength of metallic bonding increases"],["Macromolecules  (e.g. Si)","Covalent bonding —\ngiant covalent structure","Melting point is very high:\n- they have strong covalent bonds linking all their atoms together\n- a lot of energy is needed to break the strong covalent bonds"],["Simple molecules  (e.g. P₄, S₈, Cl₂)","Covalent bonding —\nsimple molecular structure","Melting point is low:\n- melting point depends on the strength of intermolecular forces (London forces) between molecules\n- since London forces are weak they have a low melting point\n- London forces are stronger in S₈ as more electrons are present; P₄ contains more electrons than Cl₂, so London forces are stronger in P₄ than Cl₂"],["Noble gases  (e.g. Ar)","Simple atomic structure","They have the lowest melting and boiling points because they exist as individual atoms, resulting in very weak London forces between atoms"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Ionisation energies across a period"},{"type":"list","items":["In general the 1st ionization energy of the elements across the period **increases gradually**, because:","As the atomic size decreases, distance between the nucleus and the outer shell electrons decreases.","Nuclear attraction increases; shielding effect remains the same.","Therefore, the attraction between the outer electrons and the nucleus increases. Hence, more energy is required to remove the electrons."]},{"type":"h3","text":"The specific trends — explaining the deviations"},{"type":"list","items":["**Helium > hydrogen (He > H)**\n- Nuclear charge is greater — one extra proton in helium.\n- Extra electron has gone into the same energy level.\n- Increased attraction makes the electron harder to remove.","**Boron < beryllium (B < Be)** — the Group 2 → 3 deviation\n- Beryllium [4]: 1s² 2s²   ·   Boron [5]: 1s² 2s² 2p¹\n- Despite the increased nuclear charge, the outer electron is held less strongly.\n- The outermost electron of boron is in a 2p orbital, which is higher in energy than the 2s orbital of beryllium.\n- Nuclear attraction is less in boron because the 2p electrons in boron experiences greater electron-electron repulsion (greater shielding) than that in beryllium.\n- Less energy is required to remove an electron from boron.","**Aluminium < magnesium (Al < Mg)**\n- Magnesium [12]: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s²   ·   Aluminium [13]: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p¹\n- Despite the increased nuclear charge, the outer electron is held less strongly.\n- The outermost electron of aluminium is in a 3p orbital, which is higher in energy than the 3s orbital of magnesium.\n- Nuclear attraction is less in aluminium because the 3p electrons in aluminium experiences greater electron-electron repulsion (greater shielding) than that in magnesium.\n- Less energy is required to remove an electron from aluminium.","**Oxygen < nitrogen (O < N)** — the Group 5 → 6 deviation\n- Nitrogen [7]: 1s² 2s² 2p³   ·   Oxygen [8]: 1s² 2s² 2p⁴\n- Despite the increased nuclear charge the electron is easier to remove.\n- The 2p electron removed from oxygen is a paired electron, whereas the 2p electron from nitrogen is an unpaired electron.\n- When electrons are paired, the extra mutual repulsion results in less energy being required to remove an electron.\n- Hence less energy is required to remove an electron from oxygen.","**Sulfur < phosphorous (S < P)**\n- Phosphorous [15]: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p³   ·   Sulfur [16]: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁴\n- Despite the increased nuclear charge the electron is easier to remove.\n- The 3p electron removed from sulfur is a paired electron, whereas the 3p electron from phosphorous is an unpaired electron.\n- When electrons are paired, the extra mutual repulsion results in less energy being required to remove an electron.\n- Hence less energy is required to remove an electron from sulfur."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/paired-boxes.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/paired-boxes-dark.png","alt":"Box notation comparing the 2p orbitals of nitrogen and oxygen","caption":"Nitrogen’s three 2p electrons sit unpaired; oxygen’s fourth 2p electron must pair up — the extra mutual repulsion makes it easier to remove."},{"type":"h3","text":"Ionisation energies down the group"},{"type":"list","items":["Ionization energy **decreases** down the group. This is because:","Down the group, the atomic size increases and the number of shells increases.","Distance between the nucleus and the outer electrons increases.","The outer electrons are more shielded by the electrons in the inner shells.","The effect of the increase in nuclear charge is outweighed by the increase in shielding.","Therefore, the outer electrons are less attracted by the nucleus. Hence, less energy is required to remove the electrons."]}]}],"cards":[{"id":"cmv1eoi34002djfqk0p8jszxz","ord":1,"front":"Relative charge and mass of p, n, e⁻?","back":"Proton: +1, mass 1. Neutron: 0, mass 1. Electron: −1, mass 1/1836 (negligible).","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi35002fjfqkijb1b8nd","ord":2,"front":"Define isotope.","back":"Atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons (same Z, different A). Same chemical properties; different physical properties such as mass.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi36002hjfqk8w43d4nr","ord":3,"front":"Define first ionisation energy.","back":"The energy required to remove one electron from each atom in one mole of gaseous atoms to form one mole of gaseous 1+ ions. Always endothermic. X(g) → X⁺(g) + e⁻","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi36002jjfqkhplts5yq","ord":4,"front":"Why does first IE decrease down a group?","back":"Atomic radius increases, more inner shells so more shielding; the outer electron is further from the nucleus and less strongly attracted, so easier to remove. (Nuclear charge rises but is outweighed.)","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi37002ljfqk8f2n5oz7","ord":5,"front":"Why does first IE generally increase across a period?","back":"Nuclear charge increases with similar shielding (electrons enter the same shell); radius decreases, so attraction on the outer electron is stronger and more energy is needed.","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi38002njfqkahe8exxx","ord":6,"front":"Why do B and O dip below the general IE trend in Period 2?","back":"Be→B: the new electron enters 2p, slightly higher in energy / more shielded than 2s, so easier to remove. N→O: pairing begins in 2p; repulsion between the paired electrons makes one easier to remove.","tag":"TRAP"},{"id":"cmv1eoi39002pjfqkkm4ai7ex","ord":7,"front":"What does a mass spectrometer do (basic principle)?","back":"Ionises samples, then separates ions by their mass-to-charge ratio (m/z); a detector measures abundance. Used to find isotopic composition, Ar, and molecular mass (M⁺ peak).","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi39002rjfqkvoorkcf9","ord":8,"front":"How do you find Ar from a mass spectrum?","back":"Ar = Σ (isotopic mass × relative abundance) ÷ Σ abundances.","tag":"EQUATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3a002tjfqk81jhgqli","ord":9,"front":"State the Aufbau, Hund and Pauli principles.","back":"Aufbau: fill lowest energy sub-shells first. Hund: degenerate orbitals fill singly before pairing. Pauli: an orbital holds at most 2 electrons, with opposite spins.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3a002vjfqkv8m35g4q","ord":10,"front":"Write the full electron configuration of Fe (Z = 26).","back":"1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁶ 4s². (4s fills before 3d; 4s empties first when forming ions, e.g. Fe²⁺ = [Ar]3d⁶.)","tag":"CORE"}]},{"slug":"bonding-and-structure","number":3,"unit":1,"unitCode":"WCH11","unitName":"Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry","title":"Bonding and Structure","subtitle":"Ionic, covalent and metallic — the forces that decide how everything behaves.","summary":"Ionic, covalent and metallic bonding; dot-and-cross diagrams; shapes of molecules by VSEPR; polarity; and how bonding explains physical properties.","estHours":6.5,"sections":[{"id":"cmv1eoi3c002yjfqk02437jth","sectionKey":"specification-map","number":"3.0","title":"Specification Map","blurb":"Every statement the specification assesses in this topic — use it as your revision checklist.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"3.1 – 3.22","specText":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:","outline":["The full list of assessment statements for Topic 3, worded exactly as the specification words them","Sections run 3.1 → 3.22 through the four sub-sections: 3A Ionic bonding, 3B Covalent bonding, 3C Shapes of molecules and 3D Metallic bonding"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Spec","Assessment statement"],"rows":[["3.1","know and be able to interpret evidence for the existence of ions, limited to physical properties of ionic compounds, electron density maps and the migration of ions"],["3.2","be able to describe the formation of ions in terms of loss or gain of electrons"],["3.3","be able to draw dot-and-cross diagrams to show electrons in cations and anions"],["3.4","be able to describe ionic crystals as giant lattices of ions"],["3.5","know that ionic bonding is the result of strong net electrostatic attraction between ions"],["3.6","understand the effects of ionic radius and ionic charge on the strength of ionic bonding"],["3.7","understand reasons for the trends in ionic radii down a group in the Periodic Table, and for a set of isoelectronic ions, including N³⁻ to Al³⁺"],["3.8","understand the meaning of the term ‘polarisation’ as applied to ions"],["3.9","understand that the polarising power of a cation depends on its radius and charge, and the polarisability of an anion also depends on its radius and charge"],["3.10","understand that covalent bonding is the strong electrostatic attraction between two nuclei and the shared pair of electrons between them, based on the evidence:\ni   the physical properties of giant atomic structures\nii   electron density maps for simple molecules"],["3.11","be able to draw dot-and-cross diagrams to show electrons in covalent substances, including:\ni   molecules with single, double and triple bonds\nii   species with dative covalent (coordinate) bonds, including Al₂Cl₆ and the ammonium ion"],["3.12","be able to describe the different structures formed by giant lattices of carbon atoms, including graphite, diamond and graphene, and discuss the applications of each"],["3.13","understand the meaning of the term ‘electronegativity’ as applied to atoms in a covalent bond"],["3.14","know that ionic and covalent bonding are the extremes of a continuum of bonding type and be able to explain this in terms of electronegativity differences, leading to bond polarity in bonds and molecules, and to ionic bonding if the electronegativity is large enough"],["3.15","be able to distinguish between polar bonds and polar molecules and predict whether or not a given molecule is likely to be polar"],["3.16","understand the principles of the electron-pair repulsion theory, used to interpret and predict the shapes of simple molecules and ions"],["3.17","understand the terms ‘bond length’ and ‘bond angle’"],["3.18","know and be able to explain the shapes of, and bond angles in, BeCl₂, BCl₃, CH₄, NH₃, NH₄⁺, H₂O, CO₂, gaseous PCl₅, SF₆ and C₂H₄"],["3.19","be able to apply the electron-pair repulsion theory to predict the shapes of, and bond angles in, molecules and ions analogous to those in 3.18"],["3.20","understand that metals consist of giant lattices of metal ions in a sea of delocalised electrons"],["3.21","know that metallic bonding is the strong electrostatic attraction between metal ions and the delocalised electrons"],["3.22","be able to use the models in 3.20 and 3.21 to interpret simple properties of metals, including electrical conductivity and high melting temperature"]]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3c0030jfqkrpncg8n3","sectionKey":"evidence-for-ions","number":"3.1","title":"Evidence for Ions","blurb":"Physical properties, electron density maps and migrating ions — three independent proofs.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"3.1","specText":"know and be able to interpret evidence for the existence of ions, limited to physical properties of ionic compounds, electron density maps and the migration of ions","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Ions cannot be seen directly, so the evidence for their existence is indirect — and the specification limits it to three lines of attack: the physical properties of ionic compounds, electron density maps and the migration of ions. Each points to the same conclusion: charged particles are locked in a lattice when solid, and free to move when molten or dissolved."},{"type":"h3","text":"Evidence 1 — the physical properties of ionic compounds"},{"type":"list","items":["**High melting and boiling temperatures** indicate strong electrostatic forces acting throughout a three-dimensional lattice — a great deal of energy is needed to pull the ions apart.","Solid ionic compounds **do not conduct electricity**: the ions are held in fixed positions in the lattice, so there are no mobile charged particles to carry the current.","The same compound **conducts well when molten or in aqueous solution** — melting or dissolving frees the ions to move.","Taken together, these properties prove the existence of **charged particles (ions)** that are fixed in a lattice when solid but mobile when molten or dissolved."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Evidence 2 — electron density maps"},{"type":"list","items":["Electron density maps (obtained from **X-ray diffraction** experiments) show **discrete, spherical regions of high electron density** — the ions themselves.","Between a cation and an adjacent anion there is a **region of zero electron density** — the density does not bridge the gap between the two nuclei.","This is the signature of **complete electron transfer**: the electrons belong to one ion or the other, rather than being shared between the nuclei as they are in a covalent bond."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Compare with the covalent maps in 3.10","text":"In an ionic electron density map the density **falls to zero between** the cation and the anion. In a covalent map (Cl₂, HCl) the density is **highest between** the two nuclei. Same technique, opposite conclusion."},{"type":"h3","text":"Evidence 3 — the migration of ions"},{"type":"list","items":["Place a green solution of **copper(II) chromate(VI), CuCrO₄**, on moist filter paper (or in a U-tube) and apply a potential difference between two electrodes.","A **blue colour moves towards the cathode** (the negative electrode) — carried by the blue **Cu²⁺ cations**, which are attracted to the negative electrode.","A **yellow colour moves towards the anode** (the positive electrode) — carried by the yellow **CrO₄²⁻ anions**, which are attracted to the positive electrode.","The direction each coloured ion travels reveals its **charge** — and the fact that the colours move at all proves that **ions exist and are mobile** in solution."]},{"type":"eq","code":"CuCrO₄(aq) → Cu²⁺(aq) + CrO₄²⁻(aq)","caption":"Dissociation of copper(II) chromate(VI) — blue cation, yellow anion"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/evidence-ions.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/evidence-ions-dark.png","alt":"U-tube electrolysis of copper(II) chromate solution showing blue Cu²⁺ moving towards the cathode and yellow CrO₄²⁻ moving towards the anode","caption":"The migration of ions — blue Cu²⁺ moves towards the negative electrode while yellow CrO₄²⁻ moves towards the positive electrode."},{"type":"list","items":["The same evidence can be obtained with **potassium manganate(VII)** crystals on a moist filter paper or microscope slide: the purple **MnO₄⁻ anions** migrate towards the **anode**."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Interpret, don’t just recall","text":"The spec says **interpret** the evidence — so expect an observation and a conclusion to draw. Name the moving species, state its charge from the electrode it heads for, and finish with the conclusion: the particles are **ions**."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3d0032jfqkrqswxqeu","sectionKey":"formation-of-ions","number":"3.2","title":"Formation of Ions","blurb":"Metals lose, non-metals gain — the octet rule at work.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"3.2","specText":"be able to describe the formation of ions in terms of loss or gain of electrons","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Cation","text":"A positively charged ion, formed when an atom **loses** one or more of its outer-shell (valence) electrons."},{"type":"def","term":"Anion","text":"A negatively charged ion, formed when an atom **gains** one or more electrons into its outer shell."},{"type":"list","items":["**Metal atoms lose** their outer-shell electrons to achieve a stable **noble gas electronic configuration** — with more protons than electrons, the particle left behind is positive.","**Non-metal atoms gain** electrons into their outer shell to complete a full **octet** (eight outer electrons) — with more electrons than protons, the particle formed is negative.","The **nucleus never changes** during ion formation — an ion still has the same number of protons as the atom, so it is still the same element.","The **charge on the ion equals the number of electrons lost or gained**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Writing the equations"},{"type":"eq","code":"Na → Na⁺ + e⁻","caption":"Sodium (2,8,1) loses its single outer electron → 2,8"},{"type":"eq","code":"Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻","caption":"Magnesium (2,8,2) loses two outer electrons → 2,8"},{"type":"eq","code":"O + 2e⁻ → O²⁻","caption":"Oxygen (2,6) gains two electrons → 2,8"},{"type":"eq","code":"Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻","caption":"Chlorine (2,8,7) gains one electron → 2,8,8"},{"type":"list","items":["The charge follows the **Group number**: Group 1 → +1, Group 2 → +2, Group 13 → +3; Group 15 → 3−, Group 16 → 2−, Group 17 → 1−.","Transition metals form **more than one ion** (e.g. Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺, Cu⁺/Cu²⁺) — the charge is then shown in the name, as in iron(III) chloride."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Ions have noble gas configurations","text":"Na⁺ has the electron arrangement **2,8** (like neon); Cl⁻ has **2,8,8** (like argon). The drive towards a noble gas configuration is what makes the ion stable."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3d0034jfqkifuoy0vn","sectionKey":"dot-cross-ions","number":"3.3","title":"Dot-and-Cross Diagrams for Ions","blurb":"Square brackets, charges and transferred electrons — drawn the mark-scheme way.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"3.3","specText":"be able to draw dot-and-cross diagrams to show electrons in cations and anions","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Rules for ionic dot-and-cross diagrams"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Draw **outer-shell electrons only**, unless the question asks for all shells.","Put **square brackets** around each ion with the **overall charge at the top right** (e.g. [Na]⁺, [Cl]⁻).","Use **different symbols for different atoms** — dots for one, crosses for the other — so the **transferred electrons** can be traced back to the metal atom that donated them.","Make sure the **electron count in each ion is correct** — the anion’s outer shell is always full (8 electrons)."]},{"type":"worked","title":"Sodium chloride, NaCl","problem":"Draw the dot-and-cross diagram for sodium chloride.","steps":["Sodium (2,8,1) has **one outer electron**; chlorine (2,8,7) has **seven**.","Transfer **one electron** from sodium to chlorine: draw the transferred electron as a **cross** inside chlorine’s outer shell.","Enclose each ion in **square brackets** with its charge: [Na]⁺ and [Cl]⁻."],"answer":"Na⁺ with a full outer shell (2,8) and Cl⁻ with a full outer shell (2,8,8 — seven dots and one cross), each in square brackets with the charge shown."},{"type":"worked","title":"Magnesium oxide, MgO","problem":"Draw the dot-and-cross diagram for magnesium oxide.","steps":["Magnesium (2,8,2) must **lose two electrons**; oxygen (2,8,6) must **gain two**.","Transfer **two electrons** from magnesium to oxygen — both are drawn as crosses in oxygen’s outer shell.","The charges are **2+** and **2−** — one magnesium atom supplies one oxygen atom."],"answer":"[Mg]²⁺ and [O]²⁻, with the two transferred electrons shown as crosses inside oxygen’s shell."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/dot-cross-ions.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/dot-cross-ions-dark.png","alt":"Dot-and-cross diagrams showing electron transfer in NaCl and MgO with square brackets and charges","caption":"Electron transfer drawn the mark-scheme way — outer shells only, dots and crosses, square brackets with charges, and the transferred electrons visible inside the anion."},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"The formula follows from the charges","text":"In sodium oxide, **two** sodium atoms each donate one electron to the **same** oxygen atom — so the formula is Na₂O. Total electrons lost must equal total electrons gained: the compound is electrically neutral."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3e0036jfqkpvbgjnp0","sectionKey":"ionic-lattices","number":"3.4","title":"Giant Ionic Lattices","blurb":"Alternating ions in a regular 3D array — 6:6 coordination in sodium chloride.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"3.4","specText":"be able to describe ionic crystals as giant lattices of ions","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Giant ionic lattice","text":"A regular three-dimensional repeating array of alternating positive cations and negative anions, held together by strong electrostatic attraction extending in all directions."},{"type":"list","items":["The ions sit in a **regular, repeating 3D pattern** — the arrangement repeats through millions of ions in every direction, so ionic crystals are **giant** structures.","The electrostatic attraction is **non-directional**: each ion attracts oppositely charged ions **all around it**, which is what builds the lattice.","There are **no discrete molecules** in an ionic crystal — a formula such as NaCl is the **simplest ratio** of ions (an empirical formula), not a molecule.","The ratio keeps the crystal **electrically neutral** overall — equal amounts of positive and negative charge."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Coordination number in sodium chloride"},{"type":"def","term":"Coordination number","text":"The number of nearest-neighbour ions of **opposite charge** surrounding a given ion in a lattice."},{"type":"list","items":["In NaCl, each **Na⁺ is surrounded octahedrally by 6 Cl⁻**, and each **Cl⁻ is surrounded by 6 Na⁺** — described as **6:6 coordination**.","This arrangement lets every cation sit close to several anions, so the attractions on every ion outweigh its repulsions."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/ionic-lattice.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/ionic-lattice-dark.png","alt":"A giant ionic lattice of alternating sodium and chloride ions with one Na⁺ highlighted showing its six surrounding Cl⁻ ions","caption":"The sodium chloride lattice — alternating Na⁺ and Cl⁻ in all three dimensions. The highlighted Na⁺ touches six Cl⁻ ions: 6:6 coordination."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3e0038jfqkknpdhqap","sectionKey":"ionic-bonding","number":"3.5","title":"Ionic Bonding","blurb":"Strong net electrostatic attraction — the attractions outweigh the repulsions.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"3.5","specText":"know that ionic bonding is the result of strong net electrostatic attraction between ions","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Ionic bonding","text":"The strong **net** electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions in a giant lattice."},{"type":"list","items":["In the lattice, **attractions between oppositely charged ions** act alongside **repulsions between ions of like charge**.","The attractions **outweigh** the repulsions — the balance is a **net attraction**, and it is this net attraction that holds the lattice together.","Because the attraction is **non-directional**, the bonding acts **throughout the whole lattice**, in every direction — this is what makes the compound ‘giant’.","This one force explains the properties met in 3.1: high melting temperatures (much energy needed to overcome the attraction) and conduction only when the ions are free to move."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Forces inside the lattice","Between","Relative effect"],"rows":[["Attractive forces","Each cation and the surrounding anions","Dominate — hold the lattice together"],["Repulsive forces","Cation–cation and anion–anion neighbours","Present, but outweighed by the attractions"]]},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"The word ‘net’ earns the mark","text":"Definition questions want ‘strong **net** electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions’ — the ‘net’ acknowledges that repulsions exist but are outweighed."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3f003ajfqkxl529ria","sectionKey":"ionic-bond-strength","number":"3.6","title":"Ionic Radius, Charge and Bond Strength","blurb":"Why MgO out-melts NaCl — charge up, radius down.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"3.6","specText":"understand the effects of ionic radius and ionic charge on the strength of ionic bonding","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Two factors decide how strongly the ions in a lattice attract each other — and therefore how much energy it takes to melt the crystal."},{"type":"list","items":["**Higher ionic charge** — ions with larger charges exert a significantly stronger electrostatic attraction on each other (Mg²⁺ attracts more strongly than Na⁺; O²⁻ more than F⁻).","**Smaller ionic radius** — smaller ions allow the **centres of charge to sit closer together**, and electrostatic attraction grows rapidly as the separation falls.","The two factors combine into one idea: **charge density** — charge per unit volume. A small, highly charged ion packs a lot of charge into a small space."]},{"type":"worked","title":"Why does MgO melt over 2000 °C higher than NaCl?","problem":"Magnesium oxide melts at 2852 °C; sodium chloride melts at 801 °C. Explain the difference.","steps":["Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ are **doubly charged**; Na⁺ and Cl⁻ are singly charged — so the attractions in MgO are much stronger.","Mg²⁺ (72 pm) is **smaller** than Na⁺ (102 pm), and O²⁻ (140 pm) is **smaller** than Cl⁻ (181 pm) — the centres of charge in MgO sit closer together.","Both effects point the same way: much stronger ionic bonding in MgO."],"answer":"MgO’s ions are more highly charged and smaller than NaCl’s, so much more energy is needed to melt it (2852 °C vs 801 °C)."},{"type":"table","headers":["Compound","Ions","Melting point","Why"],"rows":[["NaCl","Na⁺ and Cl⁻ — both singly charged","801 °C","Baseline ionic lattice"],["NaF","Na⁺ and F⁻ — same charges as NaCl","993 °C","F⁻ is smaller than Cl⁻, so the attraction is stronger"],["MgO","Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ — both doubly charged","2852 °C","Higher charges and smaller ions — much stronger bonding"]]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/mp-comparison.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/mp-comparison-dark.png","alt":"Bar chart comparing the melting points of NaCl, NaF and MgO","caption":"Melting points climb as the charges rise and the radii fall — NaCl 801 °C, NaF 993 °C, MgO 2852 °C."},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Read melting points as bond strength","text":"For ionic compounds the melting point is a measure of the **strength of the ionic bonding** — the higher the melting point, the stronger the net electrostatic attraction in the lattice."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3f003cjfqk5wh85wfl","sectionKey":"ionic-radii-trends","number":"3.7","title":"Trends in Ionic Radii","blurb":"Down a group, and across an isoelectronic series from N³⁻ to Al³⁺.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"3.7","specText":"understand reasons for the trends in ionic radii down a group in the Periodic Table, and for a set of isoelectronic ions, including N³⁻ to Al³⁺","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Isoelectronic","text":"Describes species that share the **same number of electrons** — and therefore the same electronic configuration."},{"type":"h3","text":"Down a group"},{"type":"list","items":["Ionic radius **increases** down any group, because **extra quantum shells** of electrons are added with each new period.","The outer electrons sit in shells further and further from the nucleus — and the inner shells **shield** the outer electrons from the nuclear charge."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Across an isoelectronic series: N³⁻ to Al³⁺"},{"type":"list","items":["The ions **N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺, Mg²⁺, Al³⁺** all contain **10 electrons** (configuration 1s² 2s² 2p⁶) — they are isoelectronic.","The **proton number rises** from N (7 protons) to Al (13 protons) across the series.","The **same number of electrons** is pulled by an **increasing nuclear charge**, so the electron cloud is drawn in more and more tightly.","Therefore the ionic radius **decreases** from N³⁻ (the largest) to Al³⁺ (the smallest)."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Ion","Protons","Electrons","Radius (pm)"],"rows":[["N³⁻","7","10","171"],["O²⁻","8","10","140"],["F⁻","9","10","133"],["Na⁺","11","10","102"],["Mg²⁺","12","10","72"],["Al³⁺","13","10","54"]]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/isoelectronic-radii.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/isoelectronic-radii-dark.png","alt":"Bar chart of the radii of the isoelectronic ions from N³⁻ to Al³⁺, all with 10 electrons","caption":"One electron cloud (10 electrons), six different nuclei — as the proton count climbs from 7 to 13, the radius falls from 171 pm to 54 pm."},{"type":"list","items":["Compare ions with their parent atoms: a **cation is smaller** than its atom (its outer shell is lost), while an **anion is larger** (the extra electron increases repulsion within the shell)."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Same electrons? Count the protons","text":"Isoelectronic questions are solved with one line: **same number of electrons, more protons → smaller ion**. Order the species by proton number and you have the size order immediately."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3h003ejfqkpwozf36l","sectionKey":"polarisation","number":"3.8","title":"Polarisation of Ions","blurb":"When a cation distorts an anion — and the bond gains covalent character.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"3.8","specText":"understand the meaning of the term ‘polarisation’ as applied to ions","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Polarisation (of an anion)","text":"The distortion of the electron density cloud of an anion caused by the electrostatic attraction of a neighbouring cation."},{"type":"list","items":["In a perfectly ionic compound, each ion is an **undistorted sphere** of electron density — complete electron transfer, no sharing.","In reality, a neighbouring **cation pulls on the anion’s outer electrons**, dragging the anion’s cloud towards itself.","When the distortion is significant, electron density is pulled **into the region between the two nuclei** — precisely where the shared density of a covalent bond sits.","The bond therefore gains **covalent character**: pure ionic and pure covalent bonding are the **extremes of a continuum** (see 3.14), and polarisation is what shifts a bond along it."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/ion-polarisation.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/ion-polarisation-dark.png","alt":"A small highly charged cation distorting the electron cloud of a large anion, pulling electron density into the region between the nuclei","caption":"Polarisation — the cation’s attraction drags the anion’s electron cloud towards the region between the nuclei, introducing covalent character."},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Which compounds show it most?","text":"Look for a **small, highly charged cation** paired with a **large, highly charged anion** — e.g. AlCl₃ and Al₂O₃ show far more covalent character than NaCl. Section 3.9 turns this observation into a rule."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3h003gjfqkovv79yii","sectionKey":"polarising-power","number":"3.9","title":"Polarising Power and Polarisability","blurb":"Small, highly charged cations distort large, highly charged anions.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"3.9","specText":"understand that the polarising power of a cation depends on its radius and charge, and the polarisability of an anion also depends on its radius and charge","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Polarising power (of a cation)","text":"The ability of a cation to distort the electron cloud of a neighbouring anion — highest when the cation has a **high charge and a small ionic radius** (high charge density)."},{"type":"def","term":"Polarisability (of an anion)","text":"How easily an anion’s electron cloud is distorted — highest when the anion has a **large radius and a high negative charge**."},{"type":"list","items":["**Cations:** polarising power rises with **charge** (Al³⁺ > Mg²⁺ > Na⁺) and with **smaller radius** — Li⁺ is unusually polarising for a 1+ ion.","**Li⁺ and Al³⁺** are the classic high polarising power cations — small, with their charge concentrated in a small volume.","**Anions:** polarisability rises with **radius** (I⁻ > Br⁻ > Cl⁻ > F⁻) and with **more negative charge**.","**I⁻ is much more polarisable than F⁻**: its outer electrons are further from the nucleus and heavily shielded, so its electron cloud distorts easily."]},{"type":"worked","title":"Why does aluminium chloride show covalent character?","problem":"Aluminium is a metal and chlorine a non-metal, yet AlCl₃ behaves largely as a covalent, molecular substance. Explain why.","steps":["The **Al³⁺ cation is very small and carries a 3+ charge** — extremely high polarising power.","The **Cl⁻ anion is large** and therefore easily polarisable.","The chloride’s electron cloud is pulled strongly into the region between the two nuclei."],"answer":"Significant polarisation introduces substantial covalent character — which is why AlCl₃ is molecular (and dimerises to Al₂Cl₆, see 3.11) rather than truly ionic."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/polarising-power.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/polarising-power-dark.png","alt":"Cations ordered by increasing polarising power and anions ordered by increasing polarisability","caption":"Polarising power grows as cations get smaller and more highly charged; polarisability grows as anions get larger and more negatively charged."},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Extension: MgO vs Al₂O₃ melting points","text":"Al₂O₃ (2072 °C) melts **lower** than MgO (2852 °C) despite Al³⁺’s higher charge — the strong polarisation in Al₂O₃ introduces covalent character, weakening the purely ionic attraction. A classic application of 3.8 and 3.9."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3i003ijfqkdn6tuxt2","sectionKey":"covalent-bond","number":"3.10","title":"The Covalent Bond","blurb":"Shared pairs, sigma and pi bonds — and the evidence that proves them.","estMinutes":25,"spec":"3.10","specText":"understand that covalent bonding is the strong electrostatic attraction between two nuclei and the shared pair of electrons between them, based on the evidence:","outline":["i   the physical properties of giant atomic structures","ii   electron density maps for simple molecules"],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Covalent bond","text":"The strong electrostatic attraction between the two nuclei of the bonded atoms and the shared pair of electrons held between them."},{"type":"list","items":["When a covalent bond is formed, two atomic orbitals overlap and a **molecular orbital** is formed.","Like atomic orbitals, a molecular orbital can only contain **two electrons**.","Overlap of atomic orbitals is thus only possible if **both orbitals contain one electron each** (a normal covalent bond), or if **one orbital is full and the other is empty** (a dative covalent bond).","Atoms can share more than one pair of electrons and form double or triple covalent bonds:"]},{"type":"list","items":["**A single bond** results from one shared pair of electrons — e.g. in chlorine, Cl₂ (Cl—Cl).","**A double bond** results from two shared electron pairs — e.g. in oxygen, O₂ (O=O).","**A triple bond** results from three shared electron pairs — e.g. in nitrogen, N₂ (N≡N)."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Sigma (σ) bonds — the single covalent bond"},{"type":"list","items":["For a single covalent bond, an orbital containing one electron overlaps **linearly (head-on)** with an orbital containing one electron of the other atom.","There can only be **one sigma bond between two atoms**.","**s–s overlap** — formation of hydrogen, H₂: the two 1s orbitals overlap head-on.","**s–p overlap** — e.g. in hydrogen bromide, HBr: the hydrogen 1s orbital overlaps with the bromine p orbital containing the single electron.","**p–p overlap** — formation of fluorine, F₂: the half-filled p orbitals of the two atoms overlap head-on."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/sigma-bonds.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/sigma-bonds-dark.png","alt":"Head-on overlap forming sigma bonds: s–s in H₂, s–p in HBr and p–p in F₂","caption":"A sigma bond forms by linear (head-on) overlap of two half-filled orbitals — s–s (H₂), s–p (HBr) and p–p (F₂)."},{"type":"h3","text":"Pi (π) bonds — double and triple bonds"},{"type":"list","items":["When a double bond is formed between two atoms, one bond is a **sigma bond** and the other is a **pi bond**.","A pi bond is formed when two p orbitals overlap **laterally (sideways)**.","Each p orbital has two lobes — one above and one below the plane of the nuclei.","The bonding region of a pi bond lies **above and below the two nuclei**, not directly between them.","The sideways overlap of two p orbitals to form a pi bond is **less effective** than the direct, head-on overlap that forms a sigma bond — so a **pi bond is weaker than a sigma bond**.","**A double bond = one σ + one π** (e.g. ethene, C₂H₄); **a triple bond = one σ + two π** (e.g. ethyne, H—C≡C—H)."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/pi-bond.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/pi-bond-dark.png","alt":"Lateral overlap of two p orbitals forming a pi bond above and below the nuclear axis","caption":"A pi bond forms by lateral overlap of p orbitals — the shared density sits above and below the internuclear axis, making it weaker than a sigma bond."},{"type":"h3","text":"Bond length and bond strength"},{"type":"def","term":"Bond length","text":"The distance between the nuclei of two atoms that are covalently bonded together."},{"type":"list","items":["The strength of a covalent bond is measured by the **amount of energy required to break one mole of the bonds in the gaseous state**.","**Shorter bonds are stronger bonds** — the closer the nuclei, the greater the electrostatic attraction between the two nuclei and the electrons in the overlapping atomic orbitals."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Evidence 1 — the physical properties of giant atomic structures"},{"type":"list","items":["The physical properties of giant atomic structures (also called **giant molecular structures**), such as diamond and graphite, provide evidence for the strong electrostatic attraction in covalent bonding.","They are **very hard and have high melting temperatures** — the structures are made up of a large number of strong covalent bonds, all of which need to be broken if the atoms are to be separated.","They **do not conduct electricity** as they have no mobile ions or electrons — but **graphite conducts**: each atom uses only three of its outer-shell electrons for bonding, and the remaining electron can move through the layers, allowing conduction.","They are **insoluble** in water and other solvents."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Evidence 2 — electron density maps"},{"type":"list","items":["Electron density maps show **high electron density between atoms that are covalently bonded** — the shared pair of electrons sits between the two nuclei."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/electron-density.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/electron-density-dark.png","alt":"Electron density maps of Cl₂ and HCl showing electron density between the bonded atoms","caption":"Electron density maps for Cl₂ and HCl — the region of high electron density between the two nuclei is the shared pair that constitutes the covalent bond."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3i003kjfqksuaktrw4","sectionKey":"dot-cross-diagrams","number":"3.11","title":"Dot-and-Cross Diagrams","blurb":"Outer-shell bookkeeping: single, double, triple and dative covalent bonds on paper.","estMinutes":25,"spec":"3.11","specText":"be able to draw dot-and-cross diagrams to show electrons in covalent substances, including:","outline":["i   molecules with single, double and triple bonds","ii   species with dative covalent (coordinate) bonds, including Al₂Cl₆ and the ammonium ion"],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Dot-and-cross diagrams for discrete molecules"},{"type":"def","term":"Discrete molecule","text":"An electrically neutral group of two or more atoms held together by covalent bonds."},{"type":"list","items":["Use **dots** for the electrons from one of the atoms and a **cross** for the electrons from the other atom.","If there are more than two types of atom, use additional symbols such as a small circle or a small triangle.","Draw the **outer electrons in pairs** to emphasize the number of **bond pairs** and the number of **lone pairs** (non-bonded electrons)."]},{"type":"def","term":"Lone pair","text":"An electron pair that is part of an atom’s valence electron shell but is not involved in bonding."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/dot-cross-molecules.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/dot-cross-molecules-dark.png","alt":"Dot-and-cross diagrams for HCl, H₂O, CH₄, CO₂, O₂ and N₂","caption":"Outer-shell dot-and-cross diagrams — single bonds (HCl, H₂O, CH₄), a double bond (CO₂ and O₂, two shared pairs) and a triple bond (N₂, three shared pairs)."},{"type":"list","items":["A **displayed formula** (full structural formula) shows each bonding pair with a line drawn between the two atoms."]},{"type":"h3","text":"The octet rule"},{"type":"list","items":["In most cases, covalently bonded molecules have the same number of electrons in the outer shell as a noble gas — mostly **8 electrons**, referred to as the **octet rule**.","But this is not always true — some species are exceptions, like **BeCl₂, BCl₃, PCl₅ and SF₆**."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/octet-exceptions.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/octet-exceptions-dark.png","alt":"Dot-and-cross diagrams of BeCl₂, BCl₃, PCl₅ and SF₆","caption":"The four classic exceptions to the octet rule — beryllium and boron are electron-deficient; phosphorus and sulfur expand their octets."},{"type":"table","headers":["Compound","Electron pairs around the central atom","Electrons around the central atom"],"rows":[["Beryllium chloride, BeCl₂","2 bond pairs, 0 lone pairs","4"],["Boron trichloride, BCl₃","3 bond pairs, 0 lone pairs","6"],["Phosphorus(V) chloride, PCl₅","5 bond pairs, 0 lone pairs","10"],["Sulfur hexafluoride, SF₆","6 bond pairs, 0 lone pairs","12"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Dative covalent (coordinate) bonds"},{"type":"def","term":"Dative covalent (coordinate) bond","text":"A covalent bond formed when **both of the shared electrons come from just one of the atoms**."},{"type":"list","items":["It is formed when an orbital of one atom containing **two electrons (a lone pair)** overlaps with an **empty orbital** of the other atom.","The basic requirement: one atom with a **lone pair** of electrons (non-bonded) and the other atom having an orbital with **no electrons**.","The **ammonium ion, NH₄⁺** — the nitrogen of NH₃ donates its lone pair into the empty 1s orbital of an H⁺ ion.","The **hydroxonium ion, H₃O⁺** — the oxygen of H₂O donates its lone pair to an H⁺ ion.","The **aluminium chloride dimer, Al₂Cl₆** — two electron-deficient AlCl₃ molecules combine: a chlorine lone pair on each AlCl₃ donates into the empty orbital of the aluminium of the other molecule, giving two dative bonds."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/dative-bonds.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/dative-bonds-dark.png","alt":"Formation of the dative covalent bonds in NH₄⁺, H₃O⁺ and Al₂Cl₆","caption":"Dative covalent bonds — the lone pair of NH₃ completes the shell of H⁺ (NH₄⁺); the lone pair of H₂O does the same (H₃O⁺); in Al₂Cl₆ each bridging chlorine donates a lone pair to the opposite aluminium."},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Once formed, a dative bond is an ordinary covalent bond","text":"The arrow head only records **where the electrons came from**. A dative covalent bond has the same strength and the same length as any other covalent bond — in NH₄⁺ all four N—H bonds are identical."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3j003mjfqkg9sajb46","sectionKey":"giant-covalent-lattices","number":"3.12","title":"Giant Covalent Lattices","blurb":"Diamond, graphite and graphene — and how the four solid structures compare.","estMinutes":25,"spec":"3.12","specText":"be able to describe the different structures formed by giant lattices of carbon atoms, including graphite, diamond and graphene, and discuss the applications of each","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Giant covalent lattices"},{"type":"list","items":["Giant covalent lattices are sometimes called **network covalent lattices** — they consist of a giant network of atoms linked to each other by covalent bonds.","The most common giant covalent substances are **diamond**, **graphite** and **graphene**."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/carbon-lattices.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/carbon-lattices-dark.png","alt":"The giant lattices of diamond, graphite and graphene","caption":"The three giant lattices of carbon — diamond: a 3D tetrahedral network; graphite: layers of hexagons held by weak London forces; graphene: a single one-atom-thick honeycomb sheet."},{"type":"h3","text":"Diamond"},{"type":"list","items":["Composed of **interlocking tetrahedral** carbon atoms.","Each carbon atom forms **four sigma bonds** to four other carbon atoms in a giant three-dimensional tetrahedral arrangement.","Diamond is **extremely hard** because of the very strong C—C bonding throughout the structure.","It also has a **very high melting temperature**, because a great number of strong C—C bonds have to be broken in order to melt it — this requires a large amount of energy.","Its uses are based on its exceptional hardness: used in **drilling, cutting and grinding**, and as **bearings**.","It can be made artificially by subjecting graphite to **high temperatures and pressures**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Graphite"},{"type":"list","items":["Composed of **planes of trigonal carbon atoms** joined in a honeycomb pattern.","Each carbon atom is bonded to **three other carbon atoms at 120°**.","**Weak London forces (van der Waals forces)** exist between the layers.","Used as a **lubricant** — it is slippery as the layers slide over each other.","Used as the **‘lead’ in pencils**, as an **electrical conductor** and **electrode material** in dry cells, and in **nuclear reactors**.","It **can conduct electricity** as there are free electrons — one in each atom, since each carbon uses only three of its four outer electrons for bonding."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Graphene"},{"type":"list","items":["Graphene is pure carbon in the form of a **very thin sheet, one atom thick**.","The carbon atoms are arranged in a **honeycomb-like pattern**, bonded in exactly the same way as in graphite — it can therefore be described as a **one-atom-thick layer of graphite**.","It is **transparent, flexible and highly conductive**.","A sheet of graphene can be rolled into a ball to produce **fullerene molecules**, and into a cylinder to produce **carbon nanotubes**.","Graphene is **very strong**, so it can be used to enhance the strength of other materials — research has demonstrated that adding even a trace amount of graphene to plastics, metals or other materials can make these materials much stronger.","Graphene is **highly conductive**, hence it could be useful in microelectronics (for example, to make **LED lighting** more efficient and longer lasting) and also in larger applications — for example **thermal foils for mobile devices**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Structure and properties — the four types of solid structure"},{"type":"p","text":"The four types of solid structures covered in this topic are **giant metallic lattices**, **giant ionic lattices**, **giant covalent lattices** and **discrete (simple) molecular lattices**."},{"type":"list","items":["**Giant metallic lattices** — a regular arrangement of positive metal ions (cations) surrounded by a sea of mobile delocalised electrons. Fairly high to high melting and boiling temperatures; good thermal and electrical conductivity; malleable and ductile.","**Giant ionic lattices** — a regular arrangement of positive ions (cations) and negative ions (anions). Fairly high melting and boiling temperatures; brittle; poor electrical conductivity when solid but conducts when molten or aqueous; often soluble in water.","**Giant covalent lattices** — a giant network of atoms linked to each other by strong covalent bonds. High to very high melting and boiling temperatures; extremely hard; non-conductors (graphite is the exception); insoluble.","**Molecular solids** — collections of molecules held together by **intermolecular forces (IMFs)**. The solid structure is maintained by intermolecular forces rather than by bonds (metallic, ionic or covalent). Generally low melting and boiling temperatures: melting requires only the weak intermolecular forces to be broken, which is less than the energy needed to break covalent bonds."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Bonding","Structure","Examples"],"rows":[["Metallic","Giant lattice","Mg, Al, Cu, Zn, Na"],["Ionic","Giant lattice","NaCl, MgO, CsF"],["Covalent (including polar covalent)","Giant lattice","C (diamond), C (graphite), Si, SiO₂ (sand), BN"],["Covalent (including polar covalent)","Molecular","H₂O, I₂, P₄, S₈, C₆₀, C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ (sucrose)"],["Covalent (including polar covalent)","Macromolecular","Polymers e.g. polythene, proteins, DNA"]]},{"type":"table","headers":["","Giant metallic","Giant ionic","Giant covalent","Simple molecular"],"rows":[["Particles present","Positive ions and delocalised electrons","Positive and negative ions","Atoms","Molecules"],["Type of bonding","Metallic","Ionic","Covalent","Covalent (within the molecules)"],["Presence of intermolecular forces","No","No","No","Yes"],["Melting and boiling temperatures","Fairly high to high","Fairly high to high","High to very high","Generally low"],["Electrical conductivity","Good when solid and molten","Non-conductor when solid; conducts when molten and aqueous","Non-conductor (graphite conducts)","Non-conductor"],["Solubility in water","Insoluble unless the metal reacts with water (e.g. Na, Li)","Generally soluble — exceptions are AgCl, AgBr, AgI and BaSO₄","Insoluble","Generally insoluble, but may dissolve if hydrogen bonding is possible (e.g. sucrose) or if the substance reacts with water (e.g. Cl₂)"]]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3j003ojfqknkc7j515","sectionKey":"electronegativity","number":"3.13","title":"Electronegativity","blurb":"The tug-of-war for the bonding pair, measured on the Pauling scale.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"3.13","specText":"understand the meaning of the term ‘electronegativity’ as applied to atoms in a covalent bond","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Electronegativity","text":"The ability of an atom to attract the bonding pair of electrons in a covalent bond to itself."},{"type":"list","items":["Electronegativity is usually measured using the **Pauling scale**.","**Fluorine** is the most electronegative element according to this scale — it is given a value of **4.0**.","**Caesium** has the lowest value, **0.7**."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/electronegativity-trends.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/electronegativity-trends-dark.png","alt":"Pauling electronegativity values increasing across Period 3 and decreasing down Group 17","caption":"Pauling electronegativity values — rising across Period 3 (Na 0.9 → Cl 3.0) as nuclear charge increases, and falling down Group 17 (F 4.0 → I 2.5) as shielding increases."},{"type":"h3","text":"Trends in electronegativity"},{"type":"list","items":["**Electronegativity increases across a period**, because:","Nuclear charge increases across the period.","The outer electrons are in the same shell, so there is no extra shielding.","Therefore, there is more attraction between the nucleus and the shared pair of electrons."]},{"type":"list","items":["**Electronegativity decreases down a group**, because:","The outer electrons are further away from the nucleus.","There is increased shielding.","Therefore, there is less attraction between the nucleus and the shared pair of electrons."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3k003qjfqkq7yfrvm2","sectionKey":"bonding-continuum","number":"3.14","title":"The Bonding Continuum","blurb":"From pure covalent to ionic — the electronegativity difference decides where a bond sits.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"3.14","specText":"know that ionic and covalent bonding are the extremes of a continuum of bonding type and be able to explain this in terms of electronegativity differences, leading to bond polarity in bonds and molecules, and to ionic bonding if the electronegativity is large enough","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Most bonds are not purely ionic or purely covalent but somewhere in between — **intermediate bonding**. Electronegativity is the main factor which leads to this intermediate bonding."},{"type":"h3","text":"Distribution of electron density in a covalent bond"},{"type":"list","items":["**Non-polar bond** — similar atoms have the same electronegativity, so they both pull on the electrons to the same extent: the electrons are **equally shared**, the electron density is symmetrical, and the bond is a **non-polar (pure) covalent bond**.","**Polar covalent bond** — when the bonded atoms differ in electronegativity they share the electron pair **unequally**, giving a partial negative charge (**δ⁻**) on the more electronegative atom and a partial positive charge (**δ⁺**) on the less electronegative atom.","The atom with the higher electronegativity pulls the electron pair closer to its end, so the electron density is unsymmetrical.","A **dipole** is induced and the bond is said to be **polar** — the greater the electronegativity difference, the greater the bond polarity.","A polar covalent bond can be represented using an **arrow** to show the direction of electron drift (pointing towards the δ⁻ end)."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/bond-polarity.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/bond-polarity-dark.png","alt":"Symmetrical electron density in a non-polar bond versus shifted density, δ⁺ and δ⁻ charges and a dipole arrow in a polar bond","caption":"Equal sharing gives a non-polar bond; unequal sharing gives δ⁺/δ⁻ ends and a dipole — the arrow points in the direction of electron drift."},{"type":"h3","text":"Using electronegativity to classify bonds"},{"type":"list","items":["The ionic and covalent bondings are the **two extremes of bonding** — in practice, all degrees of intermediate bonding occur, having both ionic and covalent characteristics.","The degree of ionic and covalent character depends on the **electronegativity difference (ΔX)** between the two atoms.","It is somewhat artificial to classify bonds purely on ΔX, but these ranges are useful:"]},{"type":"table","headers":["Electronegativity difference ΔX","Bond type","Charges"],"rows":[["ΔX ≥ 1.7","Ionic — predominantly ionic with some covalent character","Full charges: A⁺ and Z⁻"],["0 < ΔX < 1.7","Polar covalent — predominantly covalent with some ionic character","Partial charges: Aδ⁺ and Zδ⁻"],["ΔX = 0","Pure covalent — the atoms share the electrons equally","No charges"]]},{"type":"worked","title":"Classifying bonds from electronegativity differences","problem":"Classify the bonding in NaCl, LiF, SrBr₂, H₂O, H₂ and Cl₂. (Pauling values: F 4.0, Cl 3.0, Br 2.8, O 3.0, Na 0.9, Li 1.0, Sr 1.0, H 2.1)","steps":["**NaCl:** ΔX = 3.0 − 0.9 = 2.1 → ΔX ≥ 1.7, so the bond is **ionic**.","**LiF:** ΔX = 4.0 − 1.0 = 3.0 → **ionic**.","**SrBr₂:** ΔX = 2.8 − 1.0 = 1.8 → **ionic**.","**H₂O:** ΔX = 3.0 − 2.1 = 0.9 → 0 < ΔX < 1.7, so the bonds are **polar covalent**.","**H₂ and Cl₂:** ΔX = 0 (identical atoms), so the bonds are **pure covalent**."],"answer":"NaCl, LiF and SrBr₂ are ionic (ΔX = 2.1, 3.0, 1.8); H₂O is polar covalent (ΔX = 0.9); H₂ and Cl₂ are pure covalent (ΔX = 0)."},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Water is neither purely ionic nor purely covalent","text":"Water does not contain positive and negative ions, and its electrons are not shared equally. Water is best described as a **polar compound**: one end (or pole) of the molecule carries a partial positive charge (δ⁺) and the other end a partial negative charge (δ⁻)."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/bonding-continuum.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/bonding-continuum-dark.png","alt":"The bonding continuum from pure covalent through polar covalent to ionic, indexed by electronegativity difference","caption":"The bonding continuum — ionic and covalent are the extremes; the electronegativity difference decides where a real bond sits between them."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3l003sjfqk2bdo0ry3","sectionKey":"polar-molecules","number":"3.15","title":"Polar Bonds and Polar Molecules","blurb":"Polar bonds don’t guarantee a polar molecule — symmetry decides.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"3.15","specText":"be able to distinguish between polar bonds and polar molecules and predict whether or not a given molecule is likely to be polar","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"list","items":["**Polarity is a vector quantity** — it has both magnitude and direction.","Some molecules that contain polar bonds are polar — the molecule has a **permanent dipole**.","Some molecules may **not** be polar even if they contain polar bonds.","Molecules will be polar if they have a **net dipole moment** — it is a bit like balanced forces:","In a **non-polar molecule**, the dipoles of the bonds within the molecule **cancel each other**.","In a **polar molecule**, the dipoles of the bonds within the molecule **do not cancel each other**."]},{"type":"list","items":["In **symmetrical molecules**, though the bonds are polar, the dipoles from each of the bonds cancel out, resulting in no overall polarity — these molecules are **non-polar**.","Therefore **linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral, trigonal bipyramidal and octahedral** molecules are non-polar (provided that similar atoms are bonded to the central atom)."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/molecular-polarity.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/molecular-polarity-dark.png","alt":"Bond dipoles cancelling in CCl₄ but adding to a net dipole in CHCl₃ and H₂O","caption":"CCl₄: four identical bond dipoles cancel — non-polar. CHCl₃ and H₂O: the dipoles cannot cancel, so the molecules are polar."},{"type":"h3","text":"Polar bonds vs polar molecules"},{"type":"list","items":["In a simple molecule like **HCl**, the H—Cl bond is polar because the electronegativity difference is large enough — and since HCl contains only that one bond, the HCl molecule is also described as polar.","In **tetrachloromethane, CCl₄**, each bond is polar — however, the molecule as a whole is **not polar**. In other words, it does not have an end (or a side) which is slightly negative and one which is slightly positive: there is no overall separation of charge from top to bottom, or from left to right.","In **trichloromethane, CHCl₃**, the hydrogen at the top of the molecule is less electronegative than carbon and so is slightly positive — the molecule now has a slightly positive ‘top’ and a slightly negative ‘bottom’, and so is overall a **polar molecule**.","The electrons in the trichloromethane molecule are attracted towards one end of the molecule — this distortion of the electron cloud means that the centre of positive charge and the centre of negative charge **do not occur in the same place** in the molecule."]},{"type":"list","items":["**Angular and pyramidal molecules are polar.**","**Example — water, H₂O:** oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, so it has a greater pull on the shared electrons; the oxygen is slightly negative (δ⁻) and the hydrogens are slightly positive (δ⁺). Because the molecule is not symmetrical, it has an overall dipole moment and is therefore a **polar molecule**."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"warning","title":"Exception — nitrogen trifluoride, NF₃","text":"NF₃ has the same geometry as NH₃, but the molecule has **very little polarity** (it is often treated as non-polar) because the lone pair cancels out the polarity of the N—F bonds."},{"type":"h3","text":"Experiment — determining the polarity of molecules"},{"type":"list","items":["Place a liquid in a burette and allow a narrow stream (jet) to run out.","Place a **charged rod** next to the flow of the liquid.","**Polar molecules** are attracted and deflected towards the charged rod; a jet of non-polar molecules continues straight down.","Determining the effect of an electrostatic force on jets of **water, ethanol and cyclohexane** therefore decides whether the molecules are polar or non-polar."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/liquid-jets.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/liquid-jets-dark.png","alt":"A polar jet of liquid deflected towards a charged rod while a non-polar jet falls straight","caption":"The electrostatic force on liquid jets — polar liquids (water, ethanol) are deflected towards a charged rod; non-polar liquids (cyclohexane) are not."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3l003ujfqkfo1tn3zf","sectionKey":"vsepr-theory","number":"3.16","title":"Electron-Pair Repulsion Theory","blurb":"Electron pairs repel — count them and the shape follows.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"3.16","specText":"understand the principles of the electron-pair repulsion theory, used to interpret and predict the shapes of simple molecules and ions","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion (VSEPR) theory","text":"The theory used to predict the shapes of simple molecules and ions by considering the repulsions between the pairs of electrons — bond pairs and lone pairs — in the valence shell of the central atom."},{"type":"list","items":["The shape of a molecule or ion is largely determined by the **total number of electron pairs in the valence shell of the central atom**.","The electron pairs arrange themselves **as far apart as possible** to minimize the repulsion (maximum separation).","The order of repulsive strength: **lone pair–lone pair > lone pair–bond pair > bond pair–bond pair**."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"The guiding principle","text":"The shape adopted is the one which keeps repulsive forces to a **minimum**."},{"type":"list","items":["To determine a shape, count up the number of **covalent bond pairs** and **lone pairs** around the central atom, and work out the shape which keeps the bonds as far apart as possible."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/vsepr-shapes.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/vsepr-shapes-dark.png","alt":"The five regular electron-pair arrangements: linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral, trigonal bipyramidal and octahedral","caption":"The five regular arrangements, with only bond pairs present — 2 pairs: linear 180°; 3: trigonal planar 120°; 4: tetrahedral 109.5°; 5: trigonal bipyramidal 90° and 120°; 6: octahedral 90°."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3m003wjfqkhzxstpyw","sectionKey":"bond-length-angle","number":"3.17","title":"Bond Length and Bond Angle","blurb":"The two measurements that describe every bond and every angle.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"3.17","specText":"understand the terms ‘bond length’ and ‘bond angle’","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Bond length","text":"The average distance between the centres of the nuclei of two bonded atoms in a molecule."},{"type":"def","term":"Bond angle","text":"In a molecule made up of three or more atoms, the average angle between the two covalent bonds meeting at the central atom (the angle between the bonded orbitals), written θ."},{"type":"list","items":["A bond angle is only defined where two bonds meet at the same atom — e.g. the H—O—H angle in water is 104.5° and the H—C—H angle in methane is 109.5°.","Bond length decides how close the two nuclei sit; bond angle decides the shape adopted by three or more atoms."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/bond-angle-def.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/bond-angle-def-dark.png","alt":"A water molecule with the bond length d and the H–O–H bond angle θ marked","caption":"The two measurements on one molecule — the bond length d (nucleus to nucleus) and the bond angle θ (between two bonds meeting at oxygen)."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3m003yjfqkoo9fxdhh","sectionKey":"shapes-bond-angles","number":"3.18","title":"Shapes and Bond Angles","blurb":"The ten species you must know — shapes, angles and the reasons behind them.","estMinutes":25,"spec":"3.18","specText":"know and be able to explain the shapes of, and bond angles in, BeCl₂, BCl₃, CH₄, NH₃, NH₄⁺, H₂O, CO₂, gaseous PCl₅, SF₆ and C₂H₄","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Species without lone pairs — the regular shapes"},{"type":"list","items":["Only bond-pair repulsions occur, so the basic shapes are **regular** — the shape of the molecule depends on the number of bond pairs around the central atom."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Species","Bond pairs","Lone pairs","Shape","Bond angle"],"rows":[["BeCl₂","2","0","Linear","180°"],["BCl₃","3","0","Trigonal planar","120°"],["CH₄","4","0","Tetrahedral","109.5°"],["NH₄⁺","4","0","Tetrahedral","109.5°"],["PCl₅ (gaseous)","5","0","Trigonal bipyramidal","90° and 120°"],["SF₆","6","0","Octahedral","90°"],["CO₂","2 double bonds (act like single bonds)","0","Linear","180°"],["C₂H₄","3 electron groups around each C (one is the C=C double bond)","0","Trigonal planar around each carbon — the whole molecule is planar","≈120°"],["NH₃","3","1","Trigonal pyramidal","107°"],["H₂O","2","2","Angular (bent)","104.5°"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Species with lone pairs"},{"type":"list","items":["Lone pairs of electrons have **greater repulsive power** than bond pairs, so their presence affects the bond angles — they push the bond pairs away.","The resulting configuration is based on the **total number of electron pairs**, but the actual shape **does not include the lone pairs** — they are ‘invisible’ when naming the shape.","A water molecule is **angular** despite having 4 electron pairs around the oxygen — two of the pairs are lone pairs.","Lone pairs distort the shape of the molecule and reduce the bond angle: **each lone pair decreases the bond angle by about 2.5°**."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/vsepr-lone-pairs.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/vsepr-lone-pairs-dark.png","alt":"Tetrahedral CH₄ (109.5°) compared with pyramidal NH₃ (107°) and angular H₂O (104.5°)","caption":"The lone-pair compression — four electron pairs give a tetrahedron; each lone pair squeezes the bond angle down by about 2.5°: 109.5° → 107° → 104.5°."},{"type":"worked","title":"Ammonia, NH₃","problem":"Determine the shape of, and the bond angle in, an ammonia molecule.","steps":["Nitrogen has **3 bond pairs and 1 lone pair** (total = 4 electron pairs).","The shape is based on a **tetrahedron**.","As the lone pair–bond pair repulsions are greater than the bond pair–bond pair repulsions, the H—N—H bond angle is reduced from 109.5°."],"answer":"The shape is trigonal pyramidal, with H—N—H = 107°."},{"type":"worked","title":"Water, H₂O","problem":"Determine the shape of, and the bond angle in, a water molecule.","steps":["Oxygen has **2 bond pairs and 2 lone pairs** (total = 4 electron pairs).","The shape is based on a **tetrahedron**.","The extra lone pair–lone pair repulsion pushes the H—O—H bond angle down further than in ammonia."],"answer":"The shape is angular (bent), with H—O—H = 104.5°."},{"type":"h3","text":"Species with double bonds"},{"type":"list","items":["For repulsive purposes, **double bonds act like single bonds** — each double bond is one region of electron density."]},{"type":"worked","title":"Carbon dioxide, CO₂","problem":"Determine the shape of, and the bond angle in, a carbon dioxide molecule.","steps":["Carbon has **2 double bond pairs and no lone pairs**.","Since double bonds act like single bonds, the shape is based on two regions of electron density repelling each other — they move as far apart as possible."],"answer":"The shape is linear, with O—C—O = 180°."},{"type":"worked","title":"Sulfur dioxide, SO₂","problem":"Determine the shape of, and the bond angle in, a sulfur dioxide molecule.","steps":["Sulfur has **2 double bond pairs and 1 lone pair**.","Since the double bonds act like single bonds, the shape is based on two bond pairs and one lone pair repelling each other — a trigonal planar arrangement with one corner occupied by the lone pair."],"answer":"The shape is angular (bent), with O—S—O = 117.5°."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3n0040jfqko4q6mdcw","sectionKey":"vsepr-analogues","number":"3.19","title":"Predicting Shapes by VSEPR","blurb":"Same method, new species — ions, analogues and organic molecules.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"3.19","specText":"be able to apply the electron-pair repulsion theory to predict the shapes of, and bond angles in, molecules and ions analogous to those in 3.18","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Shapes can be worked out for **ions** and for species analogous to those in 3.18 by the same method. It allows you to predict the shape — but, in some cases, not the true nature of the bonding."},{"type":"h3","text":"Shapes of simple ions"},{"type":"list","items":["Write the **outer electron configuration of the central atom**, adjust for the charge on the ion (a negative charge adds the extra electron to the central atom; a positive charge removes one), then count bond pairs and lone pairs exactly as before.","**AlH₄⁻** — aluminium has 3 outer electrons and the negative charge supplies one more: **4 bond pairs, 0 lone pairs** → tetrahedral, H—Al—H = 109.5°.","**ICl₂⁺** — iodine supplies 2 bond pairs and keeps **2 lone pairs** → angular, based on a tetrahedron, with the angle compressed below 109.5° by the two lone pairs.","**PF₆⁻** — 6 bond pairs, 0 lone pairs → octahedral, F—P—F = 90°.","**H₃O⁺** — the oxygen of water donates its lone pair to H⁺: **3 bond pairs, 1 lone pair** → trigonal pyramidal, like NH₃."]},{"type":"worked","title":"The hydroxonium ion, H₃O⁺","problem":"Predict the shape of, and the bond angle in, the hydroxonium ion H₃O⁺.","steps":["Oxygen (Group 16) has 6 outer electrons. Three are used to bond three hydrogen atoms, and the dative bond supplies nothing new to count — the ion has **3 bond pairs and 1 lone pair** (total = 4 electron pairs).","The arrangement is based on a **tetrahedron**, with one corner occupied by the lone pair.","The lone pair–bond pair repulsion compresses the angle below 109.5° — the same effect as in NH₃."],"answer":"Trigonal pyramidal, with H—O—H ≈ 107°."},{"type":"worked","title":"The tetrahydroaluminate ion, AlH₄⁻","problem":"Predict the shape of, and the bond angle in, the AlH₄⁻ ion.","steps":["Aluminium (Group 13) has 3 outer electrons; the negative charge supplies one more — all four form **4 bond pairs** with hydrogen, with **no lone pairs**.","Four bond pairs repel equally and arrange themselves in a tetrahedron."],"answer":"Tetrahedral, with H—Al—H = 109.5°."},{"type":"h3","text":"Shapes of organic molecules"},{"type":"list","items":["Organic molecules are not given a particular shape name — however, the **3D spatial arrangement** (and the bond angles at each atom) is expected.","Every **singly-bonded carbon with four electron groups** is tetrahedral, with bond angles of about **109.5°** — e.g. the H—C—H angle in ethane, methanol or chloromethane.","Every **carbon joined by a double bond (C=C)** is trigonal planar, with bond angles of about **120°** — e.g. the H—C—H angle in ethene, and the C—C=C angle in propene.","An **—O—H oxygen** carries two bond pairs and two lone pairs, so the C—O—H angle is about **104.5°**, like water."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Count electron groups, not bonds","text":"For VSEPR a **double bond counts as one group** of electron density. That is why the carbon of C₂H₄ — three groups: two C—H bonds plus one C=C — is trigonal planar (≈120°), while the carbon of CH₄ — four groups — is tetrahedral (109.5°)."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3n0042jfqk7m9h0j2o","sectionKey":"metallic-structure","number":"3.20","title":"Metallic Structure","blurb":"Cations in fixed layers, wrapped in a sea of delocalised electrons.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"3.20","specText":"understand that metals consist of giant lattices of metal ions in a sea of delocalised electrons","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Metallic structure","text":"A giant three-dimensional lattice of positive metal ions arranged in regular layers, surrounded by a sea of mobile, delocalised outer-shell electrons."},{"type":"list","items":["Each metal atom **donates its outer-shell electrons** into a shared ‘sea’ — sodium donates one per atom, magnesium two, aluminium three.","Having lost its outer electrons, each atom becomes a **positive ion (cation)**, fixed in position within the regular layered lattice.","The donated electrons are **delocalised** — they belong to the whole lattice, not to any particular ion — and are free to move throughout the structure.","The lattice is held together by the attraction between the fixed cations and this mobile sea of electrons — that attraction is **metallic bonding** (3.21)."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/metallic-lattice.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/metallic-lattice-dark.png","alt":"A giant metallic lattice showing layers of positive metal ions surrounded by a sea of delocalised electrons","caption":"The metallic model — fixed cations in regular layers, wrapped in a sea of delocalised electrons that moves freely through the whole lattice."},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"‘Delocalised’ is the key word","text":"The outer electrons are no longer attached to any single atom. This mobility is the source of every property explained in 3.22 — conductivity in particular."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3o0044jfqkkjukzirk","sectionKey":"metallic-bonding","number":"3.21","title":"Metallic Bonding","blurb":"The strong electrostatic attraction between metal ions and delocalised electrons.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"3.21","specText":"know that metallic bonding is the strong electrostatic attraction between metal ions and the delocalised electrons","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Metallic bonding","text":"The strong electrostatic attraction between the positive metal ions (cations) and the sea of delocalised electrons."},{"type":"list","items":["Every cation is attracted to **the whole shared sea of electrons**, and the attraction acts **in all directions** — there are no individual ‘bonds’ between pairs of atoms.","The strength of the attraction rises with the **charge of the cations** and falls with their **radius** — the same charge-density idea as ionic bonding (3.6).","It also rises with the **number of delocalised electrons donated per atom** — aluminium (three electrons per atom) has stronger metallic bonding than sodium (one).","This is why sodium melts at just 98 °C, while magnesium (650 °C) and aluminium (660 °C) need far more energy."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Spot the difference from ionic bonding","text":"Ionic: attraction between **oppositely charged ions**. Metallic: attraction between **cations and shared delocalised electrons**. The electron sea is not made of anions — the electrons have no fixed position at all."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi3o0046jfqkxjn0kw1a","sectionKey":"metallic-properties","number":"3.22","title":"Properties of Metals","blurb":"Conductivity, melting temperature and malleability — all explained by the model.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"3.22","specText":"be able to use the models in 3.20 and 3.21 to interpret simple properties of metals, including electrical conductivity and high melting temperature","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"The value of the metallic model is how neatly it explains the everyday behaviour of metals — the specification asks for electrical conductivity and high melting temperature in particular."},{"type":"h3","text":"High melting and boiling temperatures"},{"type":"list","items":["The strong electrostatic attraction between the cations and the delocalised electrons requires **large amounts of thermal energy** to overcome.","The melting temperature **increases with cation charge** — Al³⁺ > Mg²⁺ > Na⁺ — because more highly charged ions attract the sea more strongly.","It also increases as the ionic radius falls and as **more delocalised electrons are donated per ion**, both of which strengthen the bonding."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Metal","Ion","Electrons donated per atom","Melting point"],"rows":[["Sodium","Na⁺","1","98 °C"],["Magnesium","Mg²⁺","2","650 °C"],["Aluminium","Al³⁺","3","660 °C"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Electrical conductivity"},{"type":"list","items":["Metals are excellent conductors **in both the solid and the liquid state** — the delocalised electrons are mobile whatever the cations are doing.","When a potential difference is applied, the electron sea **flows through the structure**, carrying charge from one end to the other.","Contrast the ionic case (3.1): ionic solids cannot conduct because their ions are fixed — metals have **mobile electrons even in the solid**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Malleability and ductility"},{"type":"list","items":["The layers of cations can **slide over one another** when a force is applied — the shape changes instead of shattering.","The metallic bond survives the slide: the **non-directional sea of delocalised electrons adjusts** to the new arrangement and keeps holding the layers together.","This is why metals can be **hammered into sheets (malleable)** and **drawn into wires (ductile)**."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/metallic-slide.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/metallic-slide-dark.png","alt":"Layers of metal ions sliding over each other while the sea of delocalised electrons keeps the structure bonded","caption":"Why metals bend rather than break — the cation layers slide, and the non-directional electron sea simply adjusts to the new shape."},{"type":"worked","title":"Soft sodium, harder magnesium","problem":"Use the metallic bonding model to explain why sodium is soft with a melting point of 98 °C while magnesium is harder and melts at 650 °C.","steps":["Sodium: each atom donates **one electron**, and the Na⁺ ions carry a **single positive charge**.","Magnesium: each atom donates **two electrons**, and the Mg²⁺ ions are **doubly charged** and slightly smaller.","The attraction between cations and the electron sea is therefore much stronger in magnesium."],"answer":"Stronger metallic bonding in magnesium means more energy is needed to separate the ions — hence its higher melting point and greater hardness."},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Malleable metal vs brittle ionic crystal","text":"When a force makes layers slide in a **metal**, the electron sea adjusts and the structure stays intact. In an **ionic solid**, sliding brings like-charged ions face to face — the repulsion shatters the crystal. Same force, opposite outcome."}]}],"cards":[{"id":"cmv1eoi3p0048jfqkqa1os26q","ord":1,"front":"What holds an ionic lattice together?","back":"The net electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions, acting in all directions throughout the giant lattice.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3p004ajfqk52syrt6i","ord":2,"front":"How does ionic radius and charge affect melting point?","back":"Stronger attraction = higher melting point. Larger charge and smaller ionic radius → higher charge density → stronger electrostatic attraction (e.g. MgO > NaCl).","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3p004cjfqkc8x0dzoa","ord":3,"front":"Define dative (coordinate) covalent bond.","back":"A covalent bond in which both electrons of the shared pair are supplied by the same atom (e.g. NH₄⁺, H₃O⁺).","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3q004ejfqk7zco1wbs","ord":4,"front":"Predict the shape and angle: 4 bonding pairs, 0 lone pairs.","back":"Tetrahedral, 109.5° (e.g. CH₄). Electron pairs repel equally.","tag":"VSEPR"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3s004gjfqkbw8fzs61","ord":5,"front":"Predict the shape and angle of NH₃ and H₂O.","back":"NH₃: pyramidal, 107° (3 bonding + 1 lone pair). H₂O: non-linear/bent, 104.5° (2 bonding + 2 lone pairs). Lone pairs repel more, compressing angles.","tag":"VSEPR"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3s004ijfqk6t703goc","ord":6,"front":"Define electronegativity.","back":"The power of an atom to attract the pair of electrons in a covalent bond. Increases across a period, decreases down a group (F most electronegative).","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3t004kjfqk4ii7cojc","ord":7,"front":"When is a molecule non-polar despite polar bonds?","back":"When the molecule is symmetrical so the bond dipoles cancel — e.g. CO₂ (linear), CCl₄ and BF₃ (symmetrical). Asymmetrical molecules (H₂O, NH₃) are polar.","tag":"TRAP"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3t004mjfqkuqpfgy85","ord":8,"front":"Describe metallic bonding.","back":"Strong electrostatic attraction between positive metal cations (in a giant lattice) and a sea of delocalised electrons. Explains conductivity, malleability, high melting points.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3t004ojfqkthrb2yag","ord":9,"front":"Order these by melting point and explain: NaCl, SiO₂, Na, Ar.","back":"SiO₂ (giant covalent — strong covalent bonds must break) > NaCl (giant ionic) > Na (metallic, weaker) > Ar (simple molecular — only London forces between atoms).","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3u004qjfqkkhs5v2py","ord":10,"front":"What is polarisation of an anion?","back":"Distortion of the anion's electron cloud by a nearby cation. Small, highly charged cations have high polarising power; large, highly charged anions are more polarisable. Explains why some \"ionic\" compounds show covalent character.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3u004sjfqkfcn25x6r","ord":11,"front":"How do sigma (σ) and pi (π) bonds differ?","back":"σ: head-on overlap of orbitals, electron density between the nuclei; only one σ bond between two atoms. π: sideways (lateral) overlap of p orbitals, density above and below the nuclei; weaker than σ. Double bond = σ + π; triple bond = σ + 2π.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3v004ujfqkp0og0qzf","ord":12,"front":"Why is a π bond weaker than a σ bond?","back":"Sideways (lateral) overlap of p orbitals is less effective than direct, head-on overlap — the electron density lies above and below the nuclei rather than directly between them, so the attraction is weaker.","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3v004wjfqk7vh2ta0u","ord":13,"front":"Name four species that break the octet rule and give the electron count on the central atom.","back":"BeCl₂ (4 electrons), BCl₃ (6), PCl₅ (10), SF₆ (12). Be and B are electron-deficient; P and S have expanded octets.","tag":"TRAP"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3w004yjfqkqdgnvsax","ord":14,"front":"What is the evidence for covalent bonding (spec 3.10)?","back":"i) Physical properties of giant atomic structures — very hard, high melting temperatures (many strong covalent bonds to break), insoluble, non-conducting (except graphite). ii) Electron density maps — high electron density between the two bonded nuclei.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3w0050jfqkt6a3dfvw","ord":15,"front":"Describe the structures and uses of diamond, graphite and graphene.","back":"Diamond: tetrahedral network, 4 σ bonds per C — drilling/cutting/grinding, bearings. Graphite: hexagonal layers, 3 bonds per C at 120°, weak London forces between layers, free electron conducts — lubricant, pencils, electrodes. Graphene: one-atom-thick sheet, transparent/flexible/conductive/strong — composites, microelectronics, thermal foils.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3x0052jfqk4bmljbyx","ord":16,"front":"Classify a bond from its electronegativity difference ΔX.","back":"ΔX ≥ 1.7 → ionic. 0 < ΔX < 1.7 → polar covalent (δ⁺/δ⁻ ends). ΔX = 0 → pure covalent. E.g. NaCl ΔX = 2.1 (ionic); H₂O ΔX = 0.9 (polar covalent); Cl₂ ΔX = 0 (pure covalent).","tag":"EQUATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3y0054jfqkmsvimrvv","ord":17,"front":"Predict the shape and angle: PCl₅ (gaseous) and SF₆.","back":"PCl₅: 5 bond pairs, 0 lone pairs → trigonal bipyramidal, 90° and 120°. SF₆: 6 bond pairs → octahedral, 90°. Both are exceptions to the octet rule.","tag":"VSEPR"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3y0056jfqko179597l","ord":18,"front":"State the shapes of, and angles in, CO₂ and C₂H₄.","back":"CO₂: 2 double bonds (act like single bonds), 0 lone pairs → linear, 180°. C₂H₄: each carbon has 3 electron groups (1 C=C + 2 C–H) → trigonal planar around each C, angles ≈120°, molecule planar.","tag":"VSEPR"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3y0058jfqko6mufypp","ord":19,"front":"By roughly how much does each lone pair reduce the bond angle?","back":"About 2.5° — CH₄ (0 lone pairs) 109.5° → NH₃ (1 lone pair) 107° → H₂O (2 lone pairs) 104.5°. Repulsion order: LP–LP > LP–BP > BP–BP.","tag":"VSEPR"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3z005ajfqk8n3b4loy","ord":20,"front":"Why is CHCl₃ polar but CCl₄ non-polar, even though every C–Cl bond is polar in both?","back":"CCl₄ is symmetrical — the four identical bond dipoles cancel, so there is no net dipole. In CHCl₃ the H at the top is less electronegative than C, so the dipoles cannot cancel: slightly positive top, slightly negative bottom → polar.","tag":"TRAP"},{"id":"cmv1eoi3z005cjfqk4lqxtgrp","ord":21,"front":"Give the three pieces of evidence for the existence of ions (spec 3.1).","back":"i) Physical properties: high mp/bp (strong lattice forces); non-conducting solid but conducting molten/aqueous (ions fixed when solid, mobile when molten/dissolved). ii) Electron density maps: spherical high-density regions with zero density between ions — complete electron transfer. iii) Migration of ions: blue Cu²⁺ moves to the cathode, yellow CrO₄²⁻ to the anode in the copper(II) chromate experiment.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi42005ejfqkf3hhbwbc","ord":22,"front":"State the mark-scheme rules for ionic dot-and-cross diagrams.","back":"Outer-shell electrons only; square brackets around each ion with the charge top right; dots for one atom, crosses for the other so transferred electrons are traceable; anion outer shell full (8 electrons).","tag":"METHOD"},{"id":"cmv1eoi42005gjfqkdmhrmugv","ord":23,"front":"Describe the sodium chloride lattice (structure + coordination).","back":"A giant ionic lattice — a regular 3D array of alternating Na⁺ and Cl⁻ held by non-directional electrostatic attraction. Each Na⁺ is octahedrally surrounded by 6 Cl⁻ and each Cl⁻ by 6 Na⁺: 6:6 coordination. No discrete molecules — NaCl is an empirical formula.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi43005ijfqko5d2tp96","ord":24,"front":"Explain the trend in radius across the isoelectronic series N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺, Mg²⁺, Al³⁺.","back":"All have 10 electrons (1s²2s²2p⁶), but proton number rises from 7 (N) to 13 (Al). More protons pull the same electron cloud in more tightly, so radius decreases: N³⁻ (171 pm) > O²⁻ > F⁻ > Na⁺ > Mg²⁺ > Al³⁺ (54 pm).","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi45005kjfqkt58fmev2","ord":25,"front":"When does a cation have high polarising power, and an anion high polarisability?","back":"Cation polarising power: high charge + small radius (high charge density) — e.g. Li⁺, Al³⁺. Anion polarisability: large radius + high negative charge — e.g. I⁻ ≫ F⁻ (outer electrons far away and shielded, easily distorted).","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi45005mjfqkpngp52fi","ord":26,"front":"Why do metals conduct when solid, but ionic solids do not?","back":"Metals: the delocalised electrons are mobile even in the solid lattice, so they flow when a p.d. is applied. Ionic solids: the charged particles (ions) are fixed in the lattice — they only conduct when molten or dissolved.","tag":"TRAP"},{"id":"cmv1eoi46005ojfqkaugmlw47","ord":27,"front":"Why are metals malleable but ionic crystals brittle?","back":"Metal: layers of cations slide, and the non-directional electron sea adjusts to the new arrangement — the bond survives. Ionic: sliding brings like-charged ions face to face; the repulsion shatters the crystal.","tag":"EXAM"}]},{"slug":"organic-intro-and-alkanes","number":4,"unit":1,"unitCode":"WCH11","unitName":"Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry","title":"Introductory Organic Chemistry and Alkanes","subtitle":"Nomenclature, isomers, crude oil and the first reaction mechanism you will learn.","summary":"Functional groups and IUPAC naming; structural isomerism; crude oil, fractional distillation and cracking; combustion and pollutants; free-radical substitution.","estHours":6.0,"sections":[{"id":"cmv1eoi47005rjfqk1whp97rk","sectionKey":"specification-map","number":"4.0","title":"Specification Map","blurb":"Every statement the specification assesses in this topic — use it as your revision checklist.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"4.1 – 4.18","specText":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:","outline":["The full list of assessment statements for Topic 4, worded exactly as the specification words them","Sections run 4.1 → 4.18 through the two sub-sections: 4A Introduction to organic chemistry and 4B Alkanes"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Spec","Assessment statement"],"rows":[["4.1","understand the difference between hazard and risk"],["4.2","understand the hazards associated with organic compounds and why it is necessary to carry out risk assessments when dealing with potentially hazardous materials"],["4.3","be able to suggest ways in which risks can be reduced and reactions carried out safely, for example:\ni    working on a smaller scale\nii    taking precautions specific to the hazard\niii    using an alternative method that involves less hazardous substances"],["4.4","understand the concepts of homologous series and functional group"],["4.5","be able to apply the rules of International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC) nomenclature to:\ni    name compounds relevant to this specification\nii    draw these compounds, as they are encountered in the specification, using structural, displayed and skeletal formulae\nStudents will be expected to know prefixes for compounds up to C10"],["4.6","be able to classify reactions as addition, substitution, oxidation, reduction or polymerisation"],["4.7","understand that bond breaking can be:\ni    homolytic, to produce free radicals\nii    heterolytic, to produce ions"],["4.8","know definitions of the terms ‘free radical’ and ‘electrophile’"],["4.9","know the general formula of alkanes and cycloalkanes, and understand that they are hydrocarbons (compounds of carbon and hydrogen only) which are saturated (contain single bonds only)"],["4.10","understand the term ‘structural isomerism’ and be able to draw the structural isomers of organic molecules, given their molecular formula"],["4.11","be able to draw and name the structural isomers of alkanes and cycloalkanes with up to six carbon atoms"],["4.12","know that alkanes are used as fuels and obtained from the fractional distillation, cracking and reforming of crude oil, and be able to write equations for these reactions"],["4.13","know that pollutants, including carbon monoxide, oxides of nitrogen and sulfur, carbon particulates and unburned hydrocarbons, are emitted during the combustion of alkane fuels"],["4.14","understand the problems arising from pollutants from the combustion of alkane fuels, limited to the toxicity of carbon monoxide and why it is toxic, and the acidity of oxides of nitrogen and sulfur"],["4.15","be able to discuss the reasons for developing alternative fuels in terms of sustainability and reducing emissions, including the emission of CO₂ and its relationship to climate change"],["4.16","be able to apply the concept of carbon neutrality to different fuels, such as petrol, bioethanol and hydrogen"],["4.17","understand the reactions of alkanes with:\ni    oxygen in the air (combustion)\nii    halogens"],["4.18","understand the mechanism of the free radical substitution reaction between an alkane and a halogen:\ni    using free radicals, which are species with an unpaired electron, represented by a single dot\nii    showing the initiation step of the mechanism, with curly half-arrows for free radical formation\niii    showing the propagation and termination steps of the mechanism\niv    having limited use in synthesis because of further substitution reactions"]]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi47005tjfqkmuuvguv6","sectionKey":"hazard-vs-risk","number":"4.1","title":"Hazard and Risk","blurb":"The potential to cause harm versus the likelihood that it does.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"4.1","specText":"understand the difference between hazard and risk","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Hazard","text":"An inherent property of a chemical substance or procedure that has the **potential to cause harm**."},{"type":"def","term":"Risk","text":"The **probability or likelihood** that the harm will actually occur, under the specific conditions of use or exposure."},{"type":"list","items":["A hazard is a **property** — concentrated hydrochloric acid is corrosive whether you use one drop or one litre.","Risk is a **judgement about circumstances** — the risk of the same acid burns depends entirely on how the acid is handled: the scale, the equipment, the precautions.","Good working practice therefore does two things: it **identifies the hazards** first, then **reduces the risk** of each one (see 4.3)."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Two words, two different answers","text":"A classic one-mark question: ‘state the hazard and the risk of using…’. The hazard is what the substance **could do** (corrosive, toxic, flammable); the risk is the **chance it actually does it** in your procedure."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi48005vjfqk6hu6ynu5","sectionKey":"organic-hazards","number":"4.2","title":"Hazards of Organic Compounds","blurb":"Flammability, toxicity, volatility — and why we assess the risks first.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"4.2","specText":"understand the hazards associated with organic compounds and why it is necessary to carry out risk assessments when dealing with potentially hazardous materials","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Organic compounds carry their own characteristic hazards. Many burn readily, most evaporate into breathable vapours, and some are toxic or carcinogenic — which is exactly why a risk assessment is carried out **before** the experiment, not after the accident."},{"type":"list","items":["Organic compounds often present hazards such as **high flammability, volatility, toxicity or carcinogenicity**.","A **risk assessment** evaluates these hazards prior to an experiment to establish **safety controls** that minimise the risk of injury or exposure.","Volatility deserves special respect: a volatile, flammable liquid releases vapour that can ignite **some distance from the liquid itself**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Standard hazard symbols and meanings"},{"type":"table","headers":["Symbol","Appearance","Meaning","Examples"],"rows":[["Flammable","Flame","Easily catches fire","Alkanes, ethanol, propanone"],["Toxic","Skull and crossbones","Can cause death or serious illness if inhaled, swallowed or absorbed through the skin","Potassium cyanide, carbon monoxide"],["Corrosive","Corrosion of a surface and a hand","Causes severe skin burns and eye damage","Concentrated acids and alkalis"],["Irritant / health hazard","Exclamation mark","Causes redness, irritation or mild toxicity","Dilute acids, halogenoalkanes"],["Oxidising","Flame over a circle","Releases oxygen and can ignite or intensify fires","Concentrated nitric acid, potassium dichromate(VI)"],["Environmental hazard","Dead tree and dead fish","Toxic to aquatic organisms and ecosystems","Many solvents and fuel spills"]]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/hazard-symbols.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/hazard-symbols-dark.png","alt":"The six standard hazard pictograms: flammable, toxic, corrosive, irritant, oxidising and environmental hazard","caption":"The six pictograms worth knowing on sight — the appearance of the symbol is often itself the mark."},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Read the symbol, name the control","text":"Each symbol implies its own precautions: flame → no naked flames; skull → fume cupboard and gloves; corrosion symbol → goggles and gloves. Matching symbol to precaution is exactly what 4.3 tests."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi48005xjfqknjyyymd7","sectionKey":"reducing-risk","number":"4.3","title":"Reducing Risk","blurb":"Smaller scale, smarter precautions, safer reagents.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"4.3","specText":"be able to suggest ways in which risks can be reduced and reactions carried out safely, for example:","outline":["i    working on a smaller scale","ii    taking precautions specific to the hazard","iii    using an alternative method that involves less hazardous substances"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"The specification wants three families of risk reduction — smaller scale, hazard-specific precautions, and safer alternatives. In ‘suggest’ questions, any one of them earns credit provided it is matched to the hazard."},{"type":"list","items":["**i. Working on a smaller scale (microscale):** using smaller masses or volumes reduces the total amount of **toxic vapour released** and the **thermal energy produced** in exothermic reactions — a smaller spill or fire is also inherently safer.","**ii. Precautions specific to the hazard:** match the control to the symbol — flammable substances are heated with an **electric heating mantle or a hot water bath** instead of an open Bunsen flame; **toxic or volatile** chemicals are handled in a **functioning fume cupboard**; **corrosive** chemicals call for **nitrile gloves and safety goggles**.","**iii. Alternative methods:** replace hazardous reagents or routes — for example **replacing benzene with methylbenzene** (benzene is carcinogenic) or choosing **safer oxidising agents**."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Hazard","Precaution that targets it"],"rows":[["Flammable reagent","Electric heating mantle or hot water bath — never a naked flame"],["Toxic / volatile vapours","Work inside a functioning fume cupboard"],["Corrosive chemicals","Nitrile gloves and safety goggles"],["Large exotherm expected","Smaller scale; add slowly and control the temperature"]]},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Tie the precaution to the hazard","text":"‘Wear goggles’ on its own is a weak answer. ‘Wear goggles **because the concentrated acid is corrosive and can cause eye damage**’ names the hazard, the precaution and the consequence — full credit."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi49005zjfqk20e4i35a","sectionKey":"homologous-series","number":"4.4","title":"Homologous Series and Functional Groups","blurb":"Families of compounds — and the atom that decides how they react.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"4.4","specText":"understand the concepts of homologous series and functional group","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Homologous series","text":"A family of organic compounds with the **same functional group**, the **same general formula**, **similar chemical properties** and a **gradual trend in physical properties** — successive members differing by a **—CH₂—** unit."},{"type":"def","term":"Functional group","text":"The atom or group of atoms in an organic molecule that is **responsible for its characteristic chemical reactions**."},{"type":"list","items":["Members of a homologous series can be written with a **general formula** — an algebraic formula for the whole family (e.g. the alkanes: CₙH₂ₙ₊₂).","Because they share a functional group, members react **chemically in the same way** — learn one member’s chemistry and you have learned the family’s.","Physical properties change **gradually** along the series — e.g. boiling point rises as the chain length grows, because the intermolecular forces strengthen.","Each successive member differs by one **—CH₂—** unit, so the molar mass increases by **14 g mol⁻¹** per step."]},{"type":"worked","title":"Proving the alkanes are a homologous series","problem":"Show that methane (CH₄, Mr = 16), ethane (C₂H₆, Mr = 30) and propane (C₃H₈, Mr = 44) form a homologous series.","steps":["Each formula fits the **same general formula**, CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","The Mr values rise **16 → 30 → 44**: a constant step of **14 g mol⁻¹**, i.e. one —CH₂— unit each time.","All three contain only **C—C and C—H single bonds**, so they share the same chemistry (combustion, free-radical substitution)."],"answer":"Same general formula, a —CH₂— step of 14 g mol⁻¹ between members, and similar chemical properties — the three tests of a homologous series."},{"type":"table","headers":["Homologous series","Functional group","First member"],"rows":[["Alkanes","C—C and C—H single bonds only","Methane, CH₄"],["Alkenes","C=C double bond","Ethene, C₂H₄"],["Halogenoalkanes","—C—X (X = F, Cl, Br, I)","Chloromethane, CH₃Cl"],["Alcohols","—OH (hydroxyl) group","Methanol, CH₃OH"],["Aldehydes / ketones / carboxylic acids / esters","—CHO / C=O / —COOH / —COO—","Met later in the specification"]]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi490061jfqkudf5l2dt","sectionKey":"iupac-nomenclature","number":"4.5","title":"IUPAC Nomenclature and Formulae","blurb":"Names up to C10 — and the five ways to draw a molecule.","estMinutes":30,"spec":"4.5","specText":"be able to apply the rules of International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC) nomenclature to:","outline":["i    name compounds relevant to this specification","ii    draw these compounds, as they are encountered in the specification, using structural, displayed and skeletal formulae","Students will be expected to know prefixes for compounds up to C10"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Every organic name is built from the same parts: a **prefix** counting side-chains, a **stem** counting the main chain, and an **ending** showing the functional group. The stem comes from the carbon-chain prefixes — the specification expects you to know them up to C10."},{"type":"table","headers":["Carbon atoms","Prefix","Carbon atoms","Prefix"],"rows":[["1","Meth-","6","Hex-"],["2","Eth-","7","Hept-"],["3","Prop-","8","Oct-"],["4","But-","9","Non-"],["5","Pent-","10","Dec-"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"The IUPAC naming rules"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Identify the **longest continuous carbon chain containing the principal functional group** — this gives the stem name.","Number the chain from the end that gives substituents and functional groups the **lowest possible locant numbers**.","List side-chains / alkyl groups in **alphabetical order** (ethyl before methyl); the multiplying prefixes **di-, tri-, tetra- are ignored** when alphabetising.","Punctuation: **commas separate numbers from numbers; hyphens separate numbers from words** — e.g. 2,2-dimethylbutane."]},{"type":"worked","title":"Naming CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₂CH₃","problem":"Apply the IUPAC rules to name this compound.","steps":["The longest continuous chain has **five carbons** → pentane.","A **methyl** branch sits on carbon 2 (numbering from the nearer end gives the lowest locant)."],"answer":"2-methylpentane — stem ‘pentane’, substituent ‘2-methyl’, hyphen between the number and the word."},{"type":"h3","text":"The types of organic formulae"},{"type":"def","term":"Empirical formula","text":"The **simplest whole-number ratio** of the atoms of each element in a compound."},{"type":"def","term":"Molecular formula","text":"The **actual number of atoms of each element** in a single molecule (e.g. C₄H₁₀)."},{"type":"def","term":"General formula","text":"An algebraic formula representing a whole homologous series (e.g. the alkanes: CₙH₂ₙ₊₂)."},{"type":"def","term":"Structural formula","text":"Shows the **arrangement of atoms carbon by carbon**, without drawing every individual bond — e.g. CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃, or CH₃(CH₂)₂CH₃."},{"type":"def","term":"Displayed formula","text":"Shows **all atoms and all bonds** explicitly as lines."},{"type":"def","term":"Skeletal formula","text":"The carbon backbone drawn as a **zig-zag line**: each line end and each vertex is a carbon atom; hydrogens attached to carbons are omitted. **Heteroatoms (O, N, Cl…) and any H atoms attached to them must be drawn.**"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/formula-types.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/formula-types-dark.png","alt":"Butane drawn as its molecular, structural, displayed and skeletal formulae","caption":"One molecule, four conventions — butane as molecular (C₄H₁₀), structural, displayed and skeletal formulae."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/skeletal-heteroatom.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/skeletal-heteroatom-dark.png","alt":"The skeletal formula of butan-2-ol with the OH group drawn explicitly","caption":"Skeletal formulae never hide heteroatoms — in butan-2-ol the O—H is drawn out in full, while the carbon-bonded hydrogens are left invisible."},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Which formula is being asked for?","text":"Command words matter: ‘displayed’ means **every bond drawn**; ‘structural’ means the CH₃CH₂… text form; ‘skeletal’ means the zig-zag. Drawing the wrong one loses the mark even if the molecule is right."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi4a0063jfqk2ilvln2e","sectionKey":"reaction-types","number":"4.6","title":"Classifying Reactions","blurb":"Addition, substitution, oxidation, reduction or polymerisation.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"4.6","specText":"be able to classify reactions as addition, substitution, oxidation, reduction or polymerisation","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Five words cover every organic reaction you will meet. Classify a reaction by looking at **what happens to the molecules** — bonds formed or broken, atoms gained or lost, small units joined up."},{"type":"def","term":"Addition","text":"Two or more reactant molecules react to form a **single product** — nothing is left over, so the atom economy is 100%."},{"type":"def","term":"Substitution","text":"An atom or group of atoms in a molecule is **replaced by a different atom or group**."},{"type":"def","term":"Oxidation","text":"A reaction involving the **gain of oxygen**, the **loss of hydrogen** or an **increase in oxidation number**."},{"type":"def","term":"Reduction","text":"A reaction involving the **loss of oxygen**, the **gain of hydrogen** or a **decrease in oxidation number**."},{"type":"def","term":"Polymerisation","text":"The joining together of many small **monomer** units to form a long-chain macromolecule, a **polymer**."},{"type":"table","headers":["Type","Example equation","How to spot it"],"rows":[["Addition","C₂H₄ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br","Two molecules in, one product out"],["Substitution","CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl","Something swapped for something else"],["Oxidation","CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O (combustion)","Oxygen gained / hydrogen lost"],["Reduction","C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆","Hydrogen gained / oxygen lost"],["Polymerisation","n CH₂=CH₂ → (—CH₂—CH₂—)ₙ","Many monomers → one long chain"]]},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"One reaction can wear two labels","text":"Hydrogenating ethene (C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆) is **addition** AND **reduction** — hydrogen is gained across the double bond. Classify from the change, and mention both labels if both apply."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi4a0065jfqk5ygaph80","sectionKey":"bond-fission","number":"4.7","title":"Homolytic and Heterolytic Fission","blurb":"Two ways a covalent bond can break — radicals or ions.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"4.7","specText":"understand that bond breaking can be:","outline":["i    homolytic, to produce free radicals","ii    heterolytic, to produce ions"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"A covalent bond is a shared pair of electrons — so when the bond breaks, there are two ways to divide the pair. Which way it happens decides the whole course of the reaction."},{"type":"h3","text":"i. Homolytic fission — equal shares"},{"type":"def","term":"Homolytic fission","text":"The breaking of a covalent bond in which **each bonding atom retains one of the shared electrons**, forming two neutral **free radicals**."},{"type":"eq","code":"A—B → A• + B•","caption":"Homolytic fission — one electron goes with each fragment"},{"type":"h3","text":"ii. Heterolytic fission — one-sided shares"},{"type":"def","term":"Heterolytic fission","text":"The breaking of a covalent bond in which **one atom retains both of the shared electrons**, forming a positive cation and a negative anion."},{"type":"eq","code":"A—B → A⁺ + :B⁻","caption":"Heterolytic fission — both electrons go to one atom"},{"type":"list","items":["Homolytic fission produces **free radicals** — neutral, highly reactive, drawn with a **single dot** for the unpaired electron.","Heterolytic fission produces **ions** — the species that attack in electrophilic addition (Topic 5) and nucleophilic substitution (later in the course).","The arrow notation matters: a **full curly arrow** shows the movement of an **electron pair**; a **half-headed (fish-hook) arrow** shows the movement of a **single electron** — you will need fish-hooks for the initiation step of 4.18."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Which fission, and when?","text":"**Non-polar bonds** broken by heat or UV light tend to split evenly → homolytic (e.g. Cl₂ in sunlight). **Polar bonds** split unevenly, with both electrons going to the more electronegative atom → heterolytic (e.g. H—Br attacked by an alkene)."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi4a0067jfqku6ostcst","sectionKey":"radical-electrophile","number":"4.8","title":"Free Radicals and Electrophiles","blurb":"The two species types that drive organic reaction mechanisms.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"4.8","specText":"know definitions of the terms ‘free radical’ and ‘electrophile’","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Free radical","text":"A neutral chemical species possessing an **unpaired electron**, formed by **homolytic fission** — the unpaired electron is represented by a **single dot**."},{"type":"def","term":"Electrophile","text":"An **electron-pair acceptor** — a positively charged ion or electron-deficient species, attracted to regions of **high electron density**."},{"type":"list","items":["Radicals are **neutral** but intensely reactive — the unpaired electron seeks a partner: e.g. Cl•, •CH₃.","The dot is written **on the atom carrying the unpaired electron** — •CH₃ has the dot on the carbon, not the hydrogens.","Electrophiles are electron-poor: positive ions (H⁺) or the δ⁺ end of a polar bond (the H of H—Br) — they hunt **electron-rich** regions such as a C=C double bond.","These two definitions are the cast list for the two great organic storylines: **radicals** run the free-radical substitution of 4.18; **electrophiles** run the electrophilic addition of Topic 5."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Learn them word for word","text":"The spec says **know definitions** — so quote them exactly: ‘species with an unpaired electron’ and ‘electron-pair acceptor’. Vague versions (‘something reactive’) score nothing."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi4b0069jfqkbj535m60","sectionKey":"alkanes-cycloalkanes","number":"4.9","title":"Alkanes and Cycloalkanes","blurb":"Saturated hydrocarbons — open chains and rings.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"4.9","specText":"know the general formula of alkanes and cycloalkanes, and understand that they are hydrocarbons (compounds of carbon and hydrogen only) which are saturated (contain single bonds only)","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Hydrocarbon","text":"A compound containing **carbon and hydrogen atoms only**."},{"type":"def","term":"Saturated","text":"Containing **single carbon—carbon bonds only** — no C=C double or C≡C triple bonds."},{"type":"list","items":["Alkanes (straight-chain or branched) have the general formula **CₙH₂ₙ₊₂**.","**Cycloalkanes** are ring structures with the general formula **CₙH₂ₙ** — closing the ring costs two hydrogens.","Cycloalkanes are **structural isomers of alkenes**: both fit CₙH₂ₙ (e.g. cyclohexane and hex-1-ene are both C₆H₁₂).","A ring needs at least three carbons, so cycloalkane names start at **cyclopropane**."]},{"type":"table","headers":["n","Alkane CₙH₂ₙ₊₂","Cycloalkane CₙH₂ₙ"],"rows":[["1","Methane, CH₄","— (ring impossible)"],["2","Ethane, C₂H₆","— (ring impossible)"],["3","Propane, C₃H₈","Cyclopropane, C₃H₆"],["4","Butane, C₄H₁₀","Cyclobutane, C₄H₈"],["5","Pentane, C₅H₁₂","Cyclopentane, C₅H₁₀"],["6","Hexane, C₆H₁₄","Cyclohexane, C₆H₁₂"]]},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Saturated applies to rings too","text":"Cycloalkanes contain **only single bonds**, so they are saturated — even though their formula CₙH₂ₙ matches the unsaturated alkenes. Count bonds, not hydrogens, when judging saturation."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi4b006bjfqkx6rnim1g","sectionKey":"structural-isomerism","number":"4.10","title":"Structural Isomerism","blurb":"Same molecular formula, different structural formula — three ways.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"4.10","specText":"understand the term ‘structural isomerism’ and be able to draw the structural isomers of organic molecules, given their molecular formula","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Structural isomers","text":"Compounds with the **same molecular formula** but **different structural formulae** — the atoms are connected in a different order."},{"type":"list","items":["**Chain isomerism** — different carbon skeletons: a straight chain vs a branched chain (e.g. butane and 2-methylpropane, both C₄H₁₀).","**Position isomerism** — the same functional group attached at **different positions** on the same skeleton (e.g. 1-chloropropane and 2-chloropropane, both C₃H₇Cl).","**Functional group isomerism** — the atoms are rearranged into a **completely different functional group** (e.g. cyclohexane, an alkane ring, and hex-1-ene, an alkene — both C₆H₁₂).","Structural isomers have the **same molecular formula** but, because their structures differ, they have **different physical properties** (and often different chemistry)."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Isomer or same molecule redrawn?","text":"Rotating, flipping or renumbering a structure does **not** create an isomer — the connectivity must genuinely differ. Butane drawn upside-down is still butane."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi4c006djfqkybovidj1","sectionKey":"drawing-isomers","number":"4.11","title":"Drawing and Naming Isomers","blurb":"Every chain and ring isomer up to six carbons — systematically.","estMinutes":25,"spec":"4.11","specText":"be able to draw and name the structural isomers of alkanes and cycloalkanes with up to six carbon atoms","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Enumerating isomers is a systematic process, not a guessing game. Fix the longest chain, then move carbons and substituents one step at a time, checking every structure for duplicates."},{"type":"h3","text":"A reliable method for alkanes"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Draw the **straight chain** first.","Shorten the main chain by one carbon and place the spare carbon as a **methyl branch at every distinct position** along the shorter chain.","Shorten again and place **two methyl branches** in every distinct (non-equivalent) arrangement — including both on the same carbon.","Check each structure against the others — any duplicates must be crossed out, and every carbon still must show **four bonds**."]},{"type":"worked","title":"The three isomers of C₅H₁₂","problem":"Draw and name all the structural isomers of pentane, C₅H₁₂.","steps":["Straight chain: **pentane** — CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃.","Four-carbon chain + one methyl branch: **2-methylbutane** — CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ (a branch on C3 would be the same molecule numbered from the other end).","Three-carbon chain + two methyl branches on the middle carbon: **2,2-dimethylpropane** — CH₃C(CH₃)₂CH₃."],"answer":"Three isomers: pentane, 2-methylbutane and 2,2-dimethylpropane."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/alkane-isomers.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/alkane-isomers-dark.png","alt":"Skeletal formulae of the chain isomers of butane, pentane and hexane","caption":"Chain isomers up to C6 — butane has 2, pentane has 3 and hexane has 5 structural isomers."},{"type":"table","headers":["Molecular formula","Number of isomers","Names"],"rows":[["C₄H₁₀","2","butane; 2-methylpropane"],["C₅H₁₂","3","pentane; 2-methylbutane; 2,2-dimethylpropane"],["C₆H₁₄","5","hexane; 2-methylpentane; 3-methylpentane; 2,2-dimethylbutane; 2,3-dimethylbutane"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Cycloalkane isomers"},{"type":"list","items":["For rings, vary both the **ring size** and the **substituents** — a C₅H₁₀ skeleton can be a five-membered ring, a four-membered ring with a methyl, or a three-membered ring with one or two substituents.","Examples for **C₄H₈**: cyclobutane; methylcyclopropane.","Examples for **C₅H₁₀**: cyclopentane; methylcyclobutane; ethylcyclopropane; 1,1-dimethylcyclopropane; 1,2-dimethylcyclopropane.","Examples for **C₆H₁₂**: cyclohexane; methylcyclopentane; ethylcyclobutane — and further substituted smaller rings."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/cycloalkane-isomers.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/cycloalkane-isomers-dark.png","alt":"Skeletal formulae of cycloalkane isomers: cyclopropane, cyclobutane, methylcyclopropane, cyclopentane and methylcyclobutane","caption":"Ring isomers — vary the ring size, then move the substituents around the ring."},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Count the hydrogens every time","text":"Every isomer you draw must have **exactly the molecular formula asked for**. A structure with a carbon showing five bonds or the wrong H-count is not an isomer — it is not anything."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi4c006fjfqk2vczui4d","sectionKey":"fuels-crude-oil","number":"4.12","title":"Fuels from Crude Oil","blurb":"Fractional distillation, cracking and reforming — with the equations.","estMinutes":30,"spec":"4.12","specText":"know that alkanes are used as fuels and obtained from the fractional distillation, cracking and reforming of crude oil, and be able to write equations for these reactions","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Alkanes are the engine of the modern world — burned as fuels on a vast scale, and separated from crude oil by a three-stage industrial toolkit: fractional distillation, cracking and reforming."},{"type":"h3","text":"1. Fractional distillation — separating crude oil"},{"type":"list","items":["Crude oil is **heated and vaporised**, then passed into a **fractionating column** with a **temperature gradient** — hotter at the bottom, cooler at the top.","Hydrocarbons **condense at different heights** depending on their boiling temperatures.","**Longer chains** have higher boiling points (stronger London forces between molecules), so they condense **near the bottom**; shorter chains rise and condense **near the top**.","Each fraction is a **mixture of hydrocarbons with similar chain lengths** — refinery gas at the top, petrol, naphtha, kerosene, diesel and fuel oil descending, with the thick residue at the base."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/fractionating-column.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/fractionating-column-dark.png","alt":"A fractionating column with a temperature gradient showing fractions condensing at different heights by chain length","caption":"The fractionating column — long chains condense low where it is hot, short chains climb high before condensing."},{"type":"h3","text":"2. Cracking — cutting chains down to size"},{"type":"def","term":"Cracking","text":"The **thermal or catalytic breakdown** of longer-chain, less useful alkanes into shorter-chain alkanes and valuable **alkenes**."},{"type":"list","items":["Cracking exists because **demand** for short-chain alkanes and alkenes outstrips what distillation supplies from crude oil.","The products always include an **alkene** — the double bond makes the small molecules far more useful as chemical feedstock."]},{"type":"eq","code":"C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄","caption":"Cracking decane — a shorter alkane plus ethene"},{"type":"h3","text":"3. Reforming — reshaping chains into rings"},{"type":"list","items":["Reforming converts **straight-chain alkanes** into **branched-chain alkanes, cycloalkanes and aromatics**, using a **platinum catalyst** at elevated temperatures (of the order of 400–500 °C).","Branched and cyclic alkanes **burn more smoothly** in engine cylinders — they increase the **octane rating** of the fuel and reduce engine ‘knocking’.","Reforming releases hydrogen: closing a ring or building an aromatic ring means shedding H atoms."]},{"type":"eq","code":"C₆H₁₄ → C₆H₁₂ + H₂","caption":"Reforming hexane into cyclohexane"},{"type":"eq","code":"C₇H₁₆ → C₇H₈ + 4H₂","caption":"Reforming heptane into methylbenzene"},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Equations that must balance","text":"The spec says **write equations** — so check the atom count every time. In reforming, hydrogen gas is the giveaway coproduct: hexane loses H₂ to become cyclohexane; heptane loses 4H₂ to become methylbenzene."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi4d006hjfqk95bef7eh","sectionKey":"combustion-pollutants","number":"4.13","title":"Combustion and Pollutants","blurb":"Complete and incomplete combustion — and the pollutants each emits.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"4.13","specText":"know that pollutants, including carbon monoxide, oxides of nitrogen and sulfur, carbon particulates and unburned hydrocarbons, are emitted during the combustion of alkane fuels","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Burning an alkane fuel is just vigorous oxidation — but how much oxygen is available decides which pollutants leave the exhaust."},{"type":"h3","text":"Complete combustion — plenty of oxygen"},{"type":"eq","code":"C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O","caption":"Complete combustion of propane — carbon dioxide and water only"},{"type":"h3","text":"Incomplete combustion — limited oxygen"},{"type":"list","items":["With a limited oxygen supply the carbon is only partly oxidised, yielding **carbon monoxide** and/or **carbon particulates (soot)**.","The same fuel, the same flame — only the oxygen supply differs between clean and dirty combustion."]},{"type":"eq","code":"C₃H₈ + 3.5O₂ → 3CO + 4H₂O","caption":"Incomplete combustion — carbon monoxide formed"},{"type":"eq","code":"C₃H₈ + 2O₂ → 3C + 4H₂O","caption":"Even less oxygen — soot (carbon particulates)"},{"type":"h3","text":"The pollutants emitted from alkane fuels"},{"type":"table","headers":["Pollutant","How it forms"],"rows":[["Carbon monoxide, CO","Incomplete combustion — insufficient oxygen"],["Carbon particulates (soot, C)","Incomplete combustion — severe oxygen starvation"],["Unburned hydrocarbons","Fuel that escapes combustion entirely"],["Oxides of nitrogen, NOₓ (NO, NO₂)","Atmospheric N₂ and O₂ react at the high temperature and pressure inside engines: N₂ + O₂ → 2NO"],["Sulfur dioxide, SO₂","Sulfur impurities in the fuel burn: S + O₂ → SO₂"],["Carbon dioxide, CO₂","Complete combustion — not toxic, but the greenhouse gas behind climate change (4.15)"]]},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"NOₓ comes from the air, not the fuel","text":"A classic trap: nitrogen oxides form from **nitrogen in the air** reacting with oxygen in the hot engine — the fuel itself contains no nitrogen. Sulfur dioxide, by contrast, really does come from the fuel’s impurities."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi4e006jjfqk8bh5v4vk","sectionKey":"pollutant-problems","number":"4.14","title":"Problems from Pollutants","blurb":"Why CO poisons the blood and why NOₓ and SO₂ make acid rain.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"4.14","specText":"understand the problems arising from pollutants from the combustion of alkane fuels, limited to the toxicity of carbon monoxide and why it is toxic, and the acidity of oxides of nitrogen and sulfur","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Each pollutant has a distinct knock-on effect. The specification highlights two explanations in particular — why CO poisons you, and why NOₓ and SO₂ acidify the environment — with the others as supporting detail."},{"type":"h3","text":"Carbon monoxide — a silent poison"},{"type":"list","items":["CO is a **colourless, odourless, toxic gas** — undetectable by human senses.","It **binds preferentially to haemoglobin** in red blood cells — CO has a much greater affinity for haemoglobin than oxygen does — forming **carboxyhaemoglobin**.","With haemoglobin occupied, the blood can no longer **transport oxygen** around the body, leading to oxygen starvation (headaches, unconsciousness, death at high exposure)."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Oxides of sulfur and nitrogen — acid rain"},{"type":"list","items":["**SO₂** dissolves in atmospheric water droplets to form **sulfurous and sulfuric acid** — which falls as **acid rain**.","Acid rain **damages vegetation**, **acidifies lakes** (killing aquatic life) and **corrodes limestone buildings and statues**.","**NOₓ** reacts with water and oxygen in the atmosphere to form **nitric acid** — also acid rain — and contributes to **photochemical smog**."]},{"type":"eq","code":"SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃","caption":"Sulfur dioxide dissolving — the first step towards acid rain"},{"type":"h3","text":"Particulates and unburned hydrocarbons"},{"type":"list","items":["Carbon particulates **exacerbate respiratory problems** (such as asthma) and cause **global dimming**.","Unburned hydrocarbons contribute to **ground-level ozone formation and smog**."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/pollutants-map.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/pollutants-map-dark.png","alt":"A map linking each combustion pollutant to its origin and its environmental or health problem","caption":"Pollutant logic — where each species comes from and the problem it causes downstream."},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"The two explanations to memorise","text":"CO: binds to **haemoglobin** → **carboxyhaemoglobin** → oxygen transport blocked. SO₂/NOₓ: dissolve in atmospheric water → **acids** → **acid rain**. These two chains are the examinable core of 4.14."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi4e006ljfqkazplx09f","sectionKey":"alternative-fuels","number":"4.15","title":"Alternative Fuels","blurb":"Sustainability, emissions and the CO₂–climate change link.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"4.15","specText":"be able to discuss the reasons for developing alternative fuels in terms of sustainability and reducing emissions, including the emission of CO₂ and its relationship to climate change","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Two pressures drive the search for alternatives to fossil alkane fuels: the supply is finite, and every litre burned adds new CO₂ to the atmosphere."},{"type":"h3","text":"Sustainability"},{"type":"list","items":["Crude oil is a **finite, non-renewable** resource — it is being consumed far faster than it forms.","A sustainable fuel supply can be **replenished on a human timescale** (biofuels from crops, hydrogen from water using renewable electricity)."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Emissions and climate change"},{"type":"list","items":["Fossil fuel combustion releases carbon that has been **trapped underground for millions of years** — carbon that was safely out of the carbon cycle.","This raises the **atmospheric CO₂ concentration** and **enhances the greenhouse effect**, warming the planet — the mechanism behind **climate change**.","Alternative fuels aim to **reduce net CO₂ emissions** — ideally recycling carbon that is already in the biosphere (bioethanol) or emitting no carbon at all (hydrogen)."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"The two-part answer","text":"Questions on 4.15 want **both** drivers: fossil fuels are **finite (sustainability)**, and their combustion adds **new CO₂ that enhances the greenhouse effect (climate change)**."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi4w006njfqkjm80inm0","sectionKey":"carbon-neutrality","number":"4.16","title":"Carbon Neutrality","blurb":"Petrol, bioethanol and hydrogen — which fuels truly balance the books?","estMinutes":20,"spec":"4.16","specText":"be able to apply the concept of carbon neutrality to different fuels, such as petrol, bioethanol and hydrogen","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Carbon neutrality","text":"A fuel or process for which the **net CO₂ emitted during its combustion equals the amount of CO₂ absorbed during its formation** — e.g. by photosynthesis in the crop grown to make a biofuel."},{"type":"h3","text":"Applying the concept to three fuels"},{"type":"list","items":["**Petrol** — a fossil fuel: finite and non-renewable, and every molecule burns to release carbon that was previously locked underground. A clear **net positive CO₂ emitter** — not remotely carbon neutral.","**Bioethanol** — produced by the **fermentation of plant sugars**. The growing crop absorbs CO₂ by photosynthesis, so in principle the CO₂ released on burning is re-absorbed by the next crop — close to carbon neutral.","But **bioethanol is not 100% carbon neutral in practice**: fossil fuels are burned in the farming machinery, in transporting the crop, and in distilling the ethanol.","**Hydrogen** — burns to **water only** (2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O): **zero CO₂ at the point of use**. But most hydrogen is made using electricity generated from fossil fuels, and storing and transporting it under pressure is difficult."]},{"type":"eq","code":"C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂","caption":"Fermentation of glucose — the route to bioethanol"},{"type":"eq","code":"2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O","caption":"Hydrogen combustion — water is the only product"},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"The bioethanol caveat is the question","text":"‘Explain why bioethanol is not truly carbon neutral’ wants the **hidden fossil inputs** — farming machinery, transport and distillation all burn fossil fuels, tipping the carbon balance net positive."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi4x006pjfqk39qzscfe","sectionKey":"alkane-reactions","number":"4.17","title":"Reactions of Alkanes","blurb":"Combustion in oxygen, substitution with halogens in UV light.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"4.17","specText":"understand the reactions of alkanes with:","outline":["i    oxygen in the air (combustion)","ii    halogens"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Alkanes are famously unreactive — their C—C and C—H bonds are strong and almost non-polar. Their chemistry is essentially just two reactions: combustion, and substitution by halogens under UV light."},{"type":"h3","text":"i. Oxygen in the air — combustion"},{"type":"list","items":["Combustion is **exothermic** — it releases energy, which is exactly why alkanes are used as fuels.","With **plenty of oxygen** the products are CO₂ and H₂O (complete combustion).","With **limited oxygen** the products include toxic CO and soot (incomplete combustion — see 4.13)."]},{"type":"eq","code":"CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O","caption":"Complete combustion of methane"},{"type":"h3","text":"ii. Halogens — free-radical substitution"},{"type":"list","items":["Alkanes react with **chlorine or bromine in the presence of ultraviolet (UV) light** — bright sunlight supplies it.","The reaction is **free-radical substitution**: a halogen atom replaces a hydrogen of the alkane, producing a **halogenoalkane and a hydrogen halide**.","In the **dark there is no reaction** — the UV light provides the energy to break the Cl—Cl bond homolytically and start the chain (mechanism in 4.18)."]},{"type":"eq","code":"CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl","caption":"Chlorination of methane — UV light required"},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Alkane vs alkene with bromine","text":"Bromine water decolourises **instantly in the dark** with an alkene, but with an alkane it only fades **slowly in sunlight**. The contrast in conditions is the evidence: alkanes need UV radicals, alkenes react by electrophilic addition."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi4x006rjfqkbio0i4pk","sectionKey":"radical-substitution-mechanism","number":"4.18","title":"Free-Radical Substitution","blurb":"Initiation, propagation, termination — the first mechanism you will learn.","estMinutes":30,"spec":"4.18","specText":"understand the mechanism of the free radical substitution reaction between an alkane and a halogen:","outline":["i    using free radicals, which are species with an unpaired electron, represented by a single dot","ii    showing the initiation step of the mechanism, with curly half-arrows for free radical formation","iii    showing the propagation and termination steps of the mechanism","iv    having limited use in synthesis because of further substitution reactions"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"The chlorination of methane runs through three stages — initiation, propagation and termination. Learn the equations, the arrow conventions and the reason the reaction is of limited synthetic use."},{"type":"eq","code":"CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl   (UV light)","caption":"Overall: free-radical substitution"},{"type":"h3","text":"Step 1 — Initiation"},{"type":"list","items":["UV light provides the energy to split the Cl—Cl bond by **homolytic fission**.","**Curly half-arrows (fish-hooks)** show one electron going to each chlorine atom.","Two chlorine radicals are formed — the only step that creates radicals from nothing."]},{"type":"eq","code":"Cl—Cl → Cl• + Cl•","caption":"Initiation — homolytic fission driven by UV light"},{"type":"h3","text":"Step 2 — Propagation"},{"type":"list","items":["A chain reaction: each step **consumes one radical and regenerates another**, so the reaction sustains itself.","Step 1: the chlorine radical abstracts a hydrogen from methane.","Step 2: the methyl radical attacks a chlorine molecule — and hands the radical back to chlorine."]},{"type":"eq","code":"Cl• + CH₄ → •CH₃ + HCl","caption":"Propagation step 1"},{"type":"eq","code":"•CH₃ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl•","caption":"Propagation step 2 — the Cl• is regenerated: the chain continues"},{"type":"h3","text":"Step 3 — Termination"},{"type":"list","items":["Two radicals **collide and combine**, ending the chain — any two radicals will do.","Ethane among the products is **direct evidence for •CH₃ radicals** — two of them coupled."]},{"type":"eq","code":"Cl• + Cl• → Cl₂     •CH₃ + Cl• → CH₃Cl     •CH₃ + •CH₃ → C₂H₆","caption":"The three termination steps"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/mechanism-radical-substitution.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/mechanism-radical-substitution-dark.png","alt":"The three stages of the free-radical substitution mechanism: initiation by UV light, the two propagation steps and the three termination steps","caption":"The full mechanism at a glance — radicals are made at initiation, cycled at propagation and destroyed at termination."},{"type":"h3","text":"Mark-scheme conventions"},{"type":"list","items":["The **single dot sits on the correct atom** — •CH₃ carries the dot on the carbon.","**Fish-hook half-arrows** belong in the initiation step (and any single-electron movement) — full curly arrows mean a pair of electrons.","Propagation equations must **regenerate a radical**; termination equations must show **two radicals combining**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"iv. Limited use in synthesis"},{"type":"list","items":["With **excess chlorine**, substitution does not stop at CH₃Cl — further H atoms are replaced, giving **CH₂Cl₂, CHCl₃ and finally CCl₄**.","The result is a **mixture of products** at every stage (including termination by-products such as ethane), which are difficult to separate.","The reaction is therefore of **limited use in synthesis**: you cannot easily control substitution to just one product. Using **excess methane** favours the monosubstituted product, but never exclusively."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Count the chains","text":"A favourite question: ‘how many monochlorinated products form from butane?’ Answer: **two** — 1-chlorobutane and 2-chlorobutane — because butane’s hydrogens fall into two non-equivalent sets. Further substitution then multiplies the mixture."}]}],"cards":[{"id":"cmv1eoi4y006tjfqk7aoia4a3","ord":1,"front":"Define hazard and risk, and give three ways to reduce risk.","back":"Hazard = inherent property with potential to cause harm. Risk = probability the harm occurs under the conditions of use. Reduce by: i smaller scale, ii precautions specific to the hazard (mantle not Bunsen for flammables; fume cupboard for toxics; gloves/goggles for corrosives), iii alternative methods with less hazardous substances.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi4y006vjfqkb1zw49hv","ord":2,"front":"Name six hazard symbols and one example of each.","back":"Flammable (alkanes, ethanol, propanone); toxic — skull and crossbones (KCN, CO); corrosive (conc. acids/alkalis); irritant/health hazard — exclamation mark (dilute acids, halogenoalkanes); oxidising — flame over circle (conc. HNO₃, K₂Cr₂O₇); environmental hazard — dead tree and fish (toxic to aquatic life).","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi4z006xjfqkc8rewgxn","ord":3,"front":"Define homologous series and functional group.","back":"Homologous series: family with the same functional group, same general formula, similar chemical properties, gradual physical trend, successive members differing by —CH₂— (14 g mol⁻¹). Functional group: the atom/group responsible for the characteristic reactions.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi4z006zjfqkwg73epsq","ord":4,"front":"State the IUPAC naming rules.","back":"Longest chain containing the principal functional group gives the stem; number for the lowest locants; side-chains alphabetical (ignore di-/tri-/tetra-); commas between numbers, hyphens between numbers and words. Prefixes: meth, eth, prop, but, pent, hex, hept, oct, non, dec.","tag":"METHOD"},{"id":"cmv1eoi4z0071jfqkg7zc508c","ord":5,"front":"Distinguish the five types of organic formulae.","back":"Empirical: simplest ratio. Molecular: actual atom count (C₄H₁₀). General: algebraic, whole series (CₙH₂ₙ₊₂). Structural: carbon-by-carbon, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃. Displayed: all atoms AND all bonds. Skeletal: zig-zag, vertices = C, C-bonded H omitted, heteroatoms + their H drawn.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi500073jfqkat08m1mr","ord":6,"front":"Define the five reaction types with an example of each.","back":"Addition: two reactants → one product (C₂H₄ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br). Substitution: atom/group replaced (CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl). Oxidation: gain O / lose H (combustion). Reduction: lose O / gain H (C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆). Polymerisation: monomers → polymer (n CH₂=CH₂ → (—CH₂—CH₂—)ₙ).","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi500075jfqk4xvj0455","ord":7,"front":"Distinguish homolytic and heterolytic fission.","back":"Homolytic: each atom keeps one of the shared electrons → two free radicals (A—B → A• + B•), e.g. Cl₂ in UV. Heterolytic: one atom takes both → cation + anion (A—B → A⁺ + :B⁻), e.g. polar H—Br. Fish-hook arrows = single electron; full curly arrows = pair.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi510077jfqk9zepyvgb","ord":8,"front":"Define free radical and electrophile.","back":"Free radical: a neutral species with an unpaired electron (single dot on the atom), formed by homolytic fission — e.g. Cl•, •CH₃. Electrophile: an electron-pair acceptor — a positive ion or electron-deficient species attracted to regions of high electron density.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi510079jfqkcwurzze4","ord":9,"front":"State the general formulae of alkanes and cycloalkanes, and their relationship to alkenes.","back":"Alkanes CₙH₂ₙ₊₂; cycloalkanes CₙH₂ₙ (rings need n ≥ 3). Both are saturated hydrocarbons (single bonds only). Cycloalkanes are structural isomers of alkenes — same formula CₙH₂ₙ, different bonding.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi52007bjfqkue8ijy61","ord":10,"front":"Define structural isomerism and give the three sub-types.","back":"Same molecular formula, different structural formulae. Chain (butane vs 2-methylpropane), position (1- vs 2-chloropropane), functional group (cyclohexane vs hex-1-ene, both C₆H₁₂).","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi52007djfqkm2x6sdk6","ord":11,"front":"How many structural isomers do C₄H₁₀, C₅H₁₂ and C₆H₁₄ have?","back":"2, 3 and 5. C₄H₁₀: butane, 2-methylpropane. C₅H₁₂: pentane, 2-methylbutane, 2,2-dimethylpropane. C₆H₁₄: hexane, 2-methylpentane, 3-methylpentane, 2,2-dimethylbutane, 2,3-dimethylbutane.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi53007fjfqk3qeqroid","ord":12,"front":"Describe fractional distillation of crude oil.","back":"Crude oil is vaporised and enters a fractionating column with a temperature gradient (hot bottom, cool top). Fractions condense at different heights by boiling point: longer chains → higher bp → lower in the column. Each fraction is a mixture of similar chain lengths.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi54007hjfqkrrkrbgju","ord":13,"front":"What is cracking and reforming? Give one equation for each.","back":"Cracking: thermal/catalytic breakdown of long-chain alkanes into shorter alkanes + alkenes — C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄. Reforming: straight chains → branched/cyclic/aromatics over Pt at ~400–500 °C to burn smoothly (higher octane) — C₆H₁₄ → C₆H₁₂ + H₂; C₇H₁₆ → C₇H₈ + 4H₂.","tag":"EQUATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi55007jjfqk559sao9k","ord":14,"front":"Write the complete and incomplete combustion equations of propane.","back":"Complete (excess O₂): C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O. Incomplete: C₃H₈ + 3.5O₂ → 3CO + 4H₂O; C₃H₈ + 2O₂ → 3C + 4H₂O. Limited O₂ gives toxic CO and/or soot.","tag":"EQUATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi55007ljfqk25t0kn09","ord":15,"front":"Why is carbon monoxide toxic?","back":"It binds preferentially to haemoglobin (greater affinity than O₂), forming carboxyhaemoglobin — so the blood cannot transport oxygen. It is colourless and odourless, so it gives no warning.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi57007njfqk7vv641aw","ord":16,"front":"How do SO₂ and NOₓ cause acid rain, and where does each come from?","back":"SO₂: sulfur impurities burn (S + O₂ → SO₂), then dissolves in atmospheric water → sulfurous/sulfuric acid. NOₓ: N₂ + O₂ react in hot engines (N₂ + O₂ → 2NO), then form nitric acid. Both → acid rain: damages plants, acidifies lakes, corrodes limestone.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi58007pjfqkay3bvgpd","ord":17,"front":"Apply carbon neutrality to petrol, bioethanol and hydrogen.","back":"Petrol: fossil, net positive CO₂ — not neutral. Bioethanol: fermentation of plant sugars; the crop absorbs CO₂ by photosynthesis, but farming machinery, transport and distillation burn fossil fuels — not 100% neutral in practice. Hydrogen: burns to water only (2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O), but production often uses fossil-generated electricity and storage is difficult.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi59007rjfqk7gk7znh7","ord":18,"front":"Write the three stages of free-radical substitution for CH₄ + Cl₂.","back":"Initiation (UV, homolytic fission, fish-hook arrows): Cl₂ → Cl• + Cl•. Propagation: Cl• + CH₄ → •CH₃ + HCl; •CH₃ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl•. Termination: Cl• + Cl• → Cl₂; •CH₃ + Cl• → CH₃Cl; •CH₃ + •CH₃ → C₂H₆.","tag":"MECHANISM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi59007tjfqkwzcw2cal","ord":19,"front":"Why is free-radical substitution of limited use in synthesis?","back":"Further substitution with excess chlorine continues past CH₃Cl to CH₂Cl₂, CHCl₃ and CCl₄, and termination gives by-products (e.g. ethane) — an uncontrolled mixture that is hard to separate. Excess methane favours monosubstitution but never exclusively.","tag":"TRAP"},{"id":"cmv1eoi59007vjfqk1dhyur9b","ord":20,"front":"Alkane or alkene — what happens to bromine in each case?","back":"Alkene: decolourises bromine water immediately at room temperature, even in the dark (electrophilic addition across C=C). Alkane: bromine fades only slowly and needs UV light/sunlight (free-radical substitution); no reaction in the dark.","tag":"TRAP"}]},{"slug":"alkenes","number":5,"unit":1,"unitCode":"WCH11","unitName":"Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry","title":"Alkenes","subtitle":"The C=C double bond — reactivity, E–Z isomers and polymers.","summary":"The σ and π double bond; E–Z geometric isomerism; addition reactions of alkenes; the bromine water test; addition polymerisation and polymer waste.","estHours":3.5,"sections":[{"id":"cmv1eoi5a007yjfqk7setjf2n","sectionKey":"specification-map","number":"5.0","title":"Specification Map","blurb":"Every statement the specification assesses in this topic — use it as your revision checklist.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"5.1 – 5.8","specText":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:","outline":["The full list of assessment statements for Topic 5, worded exactly as the specification words them","Every statement 5.1 – 5.8 has its own section below"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Spec","Assessment statement"],"rows":[["5.1","know the general formula of alkenes and understand that alkenes and cycloalkenes are hydrocarbons which are unsaturated (have a carbon-carbon double bond which consists of a σ bond and a π bond)"],["5.2","be able to explain geometric isomerism in terms of restricted rotation around a C=C double bond and the nature of the substituents on the carbon atoms"],["5.3","understand the E–Z naming system for geometric isomers and why it is necessary to use this when the cis- and trans- naming system breaks down"],["5.4","be able to describe the reactions of alkenes, limited to:\ni   the addition of hydrogen, using a nickel catalyst, to form an alkane\nii   the addition of halogens to produce a di-substituted halogenoalkane\niii   the addition of hydrogen halides to produce mono-substituted halogenoalkanes\niv   the addition of steam, in the presence of an acid catalyst, to produce alcohols\nv   oxidation of the double bond by acidified potassium manganate(VII) to produce a diol"],["5.5","know the qualitative test for a C=C double bond using bromine or bromine water"],["5.6","be able to describe the mechanism (including diagrams), giving evidence where possible, of:\ni   the electrophilic addition of bromine and hydrogen bromide to ethene\nii   the electrophilic addition of hydrogen bromide to propene"],["5.7","be able to describe the addition polymerisation of alkenes and draw the repeat unit given the monomer, and vice versa"],["5.8","understand how chemists limit the problems caused by polymer disposal by:\ni   developing biodegradable polymers\nii   removing toxic waste gases produced by the incineration of polymers"]]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5b0080jfqkfmmlfh32","sectionKey":"general-formula","number":"5.1","title":"Alkenes and the Double Bond","blurb":"General formula, unsaturation, sp² hybridization and the σ + π makeup of C=C.","estMinutes":25,"spec":"5.1","specText":"know the general formula of alkenes and understand that alkenes and cycloalkenes are hydrocarbons which are unsaturated (have a carbon-carbon double bond which consists of a σ bond and a π bond)","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Alkenes","text":"Unsaturated hydrocarbons — they contain at least one carbon-carbon double bond (C=C)."},{"type":"list","items":["**General formula:** CₙH₂ₙ (non-cyclic alkenes only).","The C=C double bond consists of **one sigma bond and one pi bond**.","The bonds are arranged at **120°** around the carbon atoms of the C=C bond."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Hybridization in the two carbons of C=C"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/sp2-hybridization.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/sp2-hybridization-dark.png","alt":"Carbon orbitals: ground state 2s² 2p², promoted to 2s¹ 2p³, then hybridized into three sp² orbitals plus one unhybridized p orbital","caption":"One s and two p orbitals hybridize into three sp² orbitals; one p orbital stays unhybridized."},{"type":"list","items":["The three orbitals (an s and two p’s) combine or **hybridize** to give three new orbitals.","Therefore, the two carbons in C=C each have **three sp² hybrid orbitals and one normal p orbital**."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/ethene-bonding.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/ethene-bonding-dark.png","alt":"Ethene showing head-on sigma overlap along the C–H and C–C axes and the pi cloud above and below from unhybridized 2p orbitals","caption":"Ethene: every bond along the axes is σ (sp²–sp² head-on for C–C, sp²–1s for C–H); the unhybridized 2p orbitals overlap sideways to give the π cloud above and below."},{"type":"h3","text":"Nomenclature"},{"type":"p","text":"In the IUPAC system:"},{"type":"list","items":["Identify the **longest chain of carbon atoms containing the functional group, C=C**.","The principle chain is not always the longest chain.","Use prefixes according to the number of carbon atoms present in it.","Use **–ene** as the suffix.","The position of C=C is indicated by the **lower numbered carbon atom** on one end of the double bond. Count from one end to give the lowest number.","Position of side chain is based on the number allocated to the C=C bond."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Compound","Name"],"rows":[["CH₂=CH₂","ethene"],["CH₂=CHCH₃","propene"],["CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃","but-1-ene"],["CH₃CH=CHCH₃","but-2-ene"]]},{"type":"list","items":["Multiple bonds are named by adding an **‘a’** to the root word — e.g. **buta-1,3-diene**."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5d0082jfqks4zxv3c7","sectionKey":"geometric-isomerism","number":"5.2","title":"Geometric Isomerism","blurb":"The isomerism family tree — and why rotation about C=C is locked.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"5.2","specText":"be able to explain geometric isomerism in terms of restricted rotation around a C=C double bond and the nature of the substituents on the carbon atoms","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/isomerism-tree.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/isomerism-tree-dark.png","alt":"Tree of isomerism: structural isomerism (functional group, chain, position) and stereoisomerism (geometric, optical)","caption":"The isomerism family tree — alkenes sit in both major branches."},{"type":"def","term":"Stereoisomers","text":"Have the same structural formula but the bonds are arranged differently in 3D."},{"type":"list","items":["Alkenes exhibit **structural isomerism** and **stereoisomerism (geometric isomerism)**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Structural isomerism"},{"type":"list","items":["Can occur by **branching of the carbon chain** (carbon chain isomers). Example — for C₅H₁₀:"]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/structural-isomers.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/structural-isomers-dark.png","alt":"Chain isomers pent-1-ene and 3-methylbut-1-ene; positional isomers pent-1-ene and pent-2-ene","caption":"Chain isomerism branches the skeleton (pent-1-ene vs 3-methylbut-1-ene); positional isomerism moves the C=C along it (pent-1-ene vs pent-2-ene)."},{"type":"list","items":["Can also occur by **moving the carbon-carbon double bond to a different position** in the carbon chain (positional isomers). For example: pent-1-ene and pent-2-ene."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Geometric isomerism"},{"type":"list","items":["Occurs due to **restricted rotation** around the carbon-carbon double bond.","The C=C **would be broken if it were rotated**.","In order to exhibit geometric isomerism, the **carbons in the double bond should have a different atom or group of atoms attached to each carbon**.","Geometric isomers are named using the **cis–trans system** or the **E–Z system**."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/no-rotation.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/no-rotation-dark.png","alt":"But-2-ene with a crossed-out rotation arrow around the C=C bond","caption":"Rotation about C=C is locked — turning the molecule would destroy the sideways p-orbital overlap that holds the π bond together."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5d0084jfqkmvd8hepg","sectionKey":"ez-naming","number":"5.3","title":"The E–Z Naming System","blurb":"Cis–trans limits, CIP priorities and naming any alkene isomer.","estMinutes":30,"spec":"5.3","specText":"understand the E–Z naming system for geometric isomers and why it is necessary to use this when the cis- and trans- naming system breaks down","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Cis–trans isomers"},{"type":"list","items":["This naming system can be used if there are **two H’s and two non-hydrogen groups** attached to each carbon.","It can also be used when **two similar groups** are attached to the two carbons in C=C."]},{"type":"list","items":["**CIS:** similar groups on the **same side** of the double bond.","**TRANS:** similar groups are **across** the double bond."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/cis-trans.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/cis-trans-dark.png","alt":"cis-but-2-ene with both methyl groups on the same side, and trans-but-2-ene with methyl groups on opposite sides","caption":"But-2-ene: with the two hydrogens (and the two methyl groups) on the same side the name is cis-but-2-ene; on opposite sides it is trans-but-2-ene."},{"type":"h3","text":"Why the cis–trans naming system doesn’t work always"},{"type":"list","items":["For the cis–trans system to work, **two identical groups or atoms attached to the two carbons in C=C** are needed.","It doesn’t work for compounds where there are **four different atoms attached to the C=C**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"E–Z isomers"},{"type":"list","items":["In the E–Z system, the groups attached around the carbons in C=C are **ranked based on the atomic number**.","The atom with the highest atomic number has **highest priority**. For example, carbon (atomic number 6) has priority over hydrogen (atomic number 1).","If the two higher priority groups are on the **same side** of the C=C, they are **zusammen** (together) and this is the **Z-isomer**.","If the two higher priority groups are on the **opposite side** of the C=C, they are **entgegen** (opposite) and this is the **E-isomer**."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/ez-priority.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/ez-priority-dark.png","alt":"Z-isomer with both high-priority groups above the C=C, and E-isomer with them on opposite sides","caption":"Higher priority goes to the atom with the larger atomic number: Br (35) beats F (9) on the first carbon, Cl (17) beats H (1) on the second. Same side → Z; opposite sides → E."},{"type":"h3","text":"Naming an E/Z isomer in three steps"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["**Step 1 — Common name:** work out the common name for both isomers using IUPAC nomenclature.","**Step 2 — Priorities:** use the priority rules to decide which of the two atoms attached to the carbon on the right side of the C=C bond has the higher priority. Repeat the same for the other carbon of the C=C bond.","**Step 3 — Assign E or Z:** decide where the higher priority atoms are (above or below the C=C bond). If **both** are above or below, name the compound as the **Z-isomer**. If **one** is above and the other is below, name it as the **E-isomer**."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"warning","title":"When the first atoms tie","text":"If the atoms attached to the carbons in C=C are the same, then look at the **atoms attached to those atoms** — compare the next shell out until a difference appears."},{"type":"worked","title":"Naming with the E–Z system","problem":"Name the isomer CH₃C(C₂H₅)=C(Cl)H — the left-hand carbon of the C=C carries CH₃ and C₂H₅; the right-hand carbon carries Cl and H.","steps":["Apply the priority rules to the **right-hand C** of C=C: Cl (atomic number 17) has the higher priority over H (1).","Now look at the **left-hand C** of C=C. It has 2 carbon atoms attached to it — same priority at the first step.","Then check the other atoms attached to them: in the **CH₃ group** — H(1), H(1), (H); in the **C₂H₅ group** — C(6), H(1), H(1).","The bottom group (C₂H₅) has another C attached to it, which has a higher priority than the H in the upper group.","The higher priority atom/group is above on one C and below on the other — hence it is the E-isomer."],"answer":"E-1-chloro-2-methylbut-1-ene"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/ez-worked-example.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/ez-worked-example-dark.png","alt":"E-1-chloro-2-methylbut-1-ene with priority numbers marked: Cl priority 17 above, ethyl group higher than methyl below","caption":"The tie-break in action: both substituents on the left carbon start with carbon, so the comparison moves one shell out — C₂H₅ (C,H,H) beats CH₃ (H,H,H)."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5e0086jfqkhvyz9wcx","sectionKey":"alkene-reactions","number":"5.4","title":"Reactions of Alkenes","blurb":"Why the π bond reacts — and the five additions you must know.","estMinutes":35,"spec":"5.4","specText":"be able to describe the reactions of alkenes, limited to:","outline":["i   the addition of hydrogen, using a nickel catalyst, to form an alkane","ii   the addition of halogens to produce a di-substituted halogenoalkane","iii   the addition of hydrogen halides to produce mono-substituted halogenoalkanes","iv   the addition of steam, in the presence of an acid catalyst, to produce alcohols","v   oxidation of the double bond by acidified potassium manganate(VII) to produce a diol"],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Why alkenes are much more reactive than alkanes"},{"type":"list","items":["The **pi bond in alkenes is a region of high electron density**, which can be readily attacked by electrophiles.","The **pi bond is weaker than the sigma bonds**, therefore less energy is required to break the pi bond.","As a result, the most typical reaction of alkenes is an **addition reaction** in which the pi bond breaks to release electrons to form bonds with the reactant molecule.","In the presence of electrophiles, alkenes undergo **heterolytic electrophilic addition** reactions."]},{"type":"def","term":"Electrophile","text":"An electron-pair acceptor — a species attracted to the high electron density of the π bond (e.g. the δ+ hydrogen of H—Br)."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/reaction-map.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/reaction-map-dark.png","alt":"Radial map of the five reactions of alkenes: hydrogenation, halogenation, hydration, addition of hydrogen halides, oxidation by potassium manganate(VII)","caption":"The five reactions of alkenes — all additions across the same π bond, plus mild oxidation to a diol."},{"type":"h3","text":"1. Hydrogenation (addition of hydrogen)"},{"type":"list","items":["**Reagent:** hydrogen gas.","**Condition:** nickel catalyst and heat (140–180 °C).","**Type of reaction:** reduction of alkene.","**Product:** alkane."]},{"type":"eq","code":"C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆","caption":"Equation with ethene"},{"type":"h3","text":"2. Halogenation (addition of halogen)"},{"type":"list","items":["**Reagent:** halogen.","**Condition:** room temperature.","**Type of reaction:** addition.","**Product:** di-substituted halogenoalkane."]},{"type":"eq","code":"C₂H₄ + Cl₂ → CH₂ClCH₂Cl","caption":"Equation with ethene and chlorine"},{"type":"h3","text":"With bromine"},{"type":"list","items":["**Reagent:** bromine dissolved in hexane.","**Condition:** room temperature.","**Observation:** brown bromine solution turns colourless."]},{"type":"eq","code":"C₂H₄ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br","caption":"Equation with ethene"},{"type":"h3","text":"3. Hydration (addition of steam)"},{"type":"list","items":["**Reagent:** steam.","**Condition:** phosphoric acid catalyst and heat (300 °C).","**Type of reaction:** addition.","**Product:** alcohol."]},{"type":"eq","code":"C₂H₄ + H₂O → CH₃CH₂OH","caption":"Equation with ethene"},{"type":"h3","text":"4. Addition of hydrogen halides"},{"type":"list","items":["**Reagent:** hydrogen halide.","**Condition:** room temperature.","**Type of reaction:** addition.","**Product:** mono-substituted halogenoalkane."]},{"type":"eq","code":"C₂H₄ + HBr → CH₃CH₂Br","caption":"Equation with ethene and hydrogen bromide"},{"type":"h3","text":"5. Oxidation by potassium manganate(VII), KMnO₄"},{"type":"list","items":["**Reagent:** potassium manganate(VII) in dilute sodium hydroxide.","**Condition:** room temperature.","**Type of reaction:** oxidation of alkenes.","**Observation:** purple solution (MnO₄⁻) turns green (MnO₄²⁻).","**Product:** diol."]},{"type":"eq","code":"C₂H₄ + H₂O + [O] → CH₂(OH)CH₂(OH)","caption":"Equation with ethene — the [O] is oxygen supplied by the oxidising agent"},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"If the manganate is acidified","text":"The purple solution turns **colourless** instead of green if the potassium manganate(VII) is dissolved in acid — the alkene still becomes a diol either way."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5f0088jfqkceoqtewj","sectionKey":"double-bond-test","number":"5.5","title":"Testing for the Double Bond","blurb":"Bromine water, bromine in hexane and the manganate colour changes.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"5.5","specText":"know the qualitative test for a C=C double bond using bromine or bromine water","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"1. Reaction with bromine water"},{"type":"list","items":["Bromine water is **reddish brown** in colour.","When an alkene is added to bromine water, it becomes **colourless**. In other words, alkenes **decolorize bromine water**.","The double bond in the alkene is broken and **Br & OH are added** across the double bond."]},{"type":"eq","code":"C₂H₄ + Br₂ + H₂O → CH₂BrCH₂OH + HBr","caption":"With bromine water the products are a bromoalcohol and hydrogen bromide"},{"type":"h3","text":"Reaction with bromine in hexane"},{"type":"list","items":["Bromine in hexane is **reddish brown** in colour.","When an alkene is added to bromine, it becomes **colourless**.","The double bond in the alkene is broken and the **two Br atoms are added** across the double bond."]},{"type":"eq","code":"C₂H₄ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br","caption":"With bromine in hexane the only product is the dihalogenoalkane"},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Check the equation balances","text":"Adding Br₂ across C=C makes **only** CH₂BrCH₂Br — no HBr is formed in hexane. Count the atoms: C₂H₄ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br balances exactly; adding HBr would leave a hydrogen and a bromine unaccounted for."},{"type":"h3","text":"2. Reaction with potassium manganate(VII)"},{"type":"list","items":["Potassium manganate(VII) is a **purple** solution.","When an alkene is added, the purple solution turns **green** [if the potassium manganate(VII) is dissolved in alkali].","The purple solution turns **colourless** if the potassium manganate(VII) is dissolved in **acid**."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"A second colour test","text":"The oxidation itself — and its equation, C₂H₄ + H₂O + [O] → CH₂(OH)CH₂(OH) — is described in section 5.4, reaction 5. Here it works as a second way to reveal a C=C bond."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5h008ajfqk3mq8owyp","sectionKey":"electrophilic-addition","number":"5.6","title":"Electrophilic Addition Mechanisms","blurb":"Curly arrows, carbocations and why the major product wins.","estMinutes":35,"spec":"5.6","specText":"be able to describe the mechanism (including diagrams), giving evidence where possible, of:","outline":["i   the electrophilic addition of bromine and hydrogen bromide to ethene","ii   the electrophilic addition of hydrogen bromide to propene","Use of the curly arrow notation is expected – the curly arrows should start from either a bond or from a lone pair of electrons.","Knowledge of the relative stability of primary, secondary and tertiary carbocation intermediates is expected."],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Heterolytic electrophilic addition"},{"type":"p","text":"Alkenes undergo **electrophilic addition** reactions."},{"type":"h3","text":"Example 1: addition of HBr to ethene"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/mechanism-hbr-ethene.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/mechanism-hbr-ethene-dark.png","alt":"Two-step electrophilic addition of HBr to ethene: curly arrow from the pi bond to the delta-plus hydrogen, carbocation intermediate, then bromide attack","caption":"Step 1: the π pair bonds to the δ+ H and the H—Br pair goes to Br (heterolytic fission), leaving a carbocation. Step 2: Br⁻ donates a lone pair to C⁺."},{"type":"list","items":["As the HBr approaches the alkene, the C=C bond breaks and the pair of electrons bonds with the **δ+ hydrogen (electrophile)** of HBr.","A **carbocation** is formed.","At the same time, the H–Br bond breaks to form a bromide ion (**heterolytic fission**).","The bromide ion behaves as a **nucleophile** and attacks the carbocation, forming the product.","Overall, hydrogen bromide is added across the double bond."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Curly arrow rules","text":"The curly arrows used in this type of reaction must either: **start from a bond and move to an atom**, or **start from a lone pair of electrons and move to an atom**."},{"type":"h3","text":"Example 2: addition of Br₂ to ethene"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/mechanism-br2-ethene.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/mechanism-br2-ethene-dark.png","alt":"Electrophilic addition of bromine to ethene: induced dipole on Br-Br, carbocation intermediate with bromide ion, then attack to give 1,2-dibromoethane","caption":"Bromine is non-polar, but the π electrons repel the Br–Br bonding pair and induce a dipole (Brδ+—Brδ−). The δ+ end is attacked; the Br⁻ released then attacks the carbocation."},{"type":"list","items":["Bromine is **non-polar** but it can act as an electrophile.","This is because as the bromine molecule approaches the alkene, the electrons in the pi bond of the alkene **repel the electron pair in the bromine-bromine bond, thus inducing a dipole**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Example 3: addition of HBr to unsymmetrical alkenes (propene)"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/mechanism-hbr-propene.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/mechanism-hbr-propene-dark.png","alt":"HBr adding to propene by two routes: via the secondary carbocation to the major product 2-bromopropane, and via the primary carbocation to the minor product 1-bromopropane","caption":"Two isomeric products are possible. Route A goes through the secondary carbocation (2°) and dominates; route B through the primary carbocation (1°) gives the minor product."},{"type":"list","items":["Addition of HBr to unsymmetrical alkenes results in the formation of **two isomeric products**.","The **major product is formed via the more stable carbocation**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Stability of carbocations"},{"type":"def","term":"Carbocation","text":"The intermediate species formed in this reaction — an ion with a positively charged carbon atom."},{"type":"list","items":["Carbocations can be **primary, secondary or tertiary**, depending on the number of carbon atoms directly bonded to the positively charged carbon atom."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/carbocation-stability.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/carbocation-stability-dark.png","alt":"Primary, secondary and tertiary carbocations with an arrow showing stability increasing from primary to tertiary","caption":"Primary (1°), secondary (2°) and tertiary (3°) carbocations — stability increases as more alkyl groups surround the positive carbon."},{"type":"list","items":["These carbocations have different stabilities. The stability is **increased by the presence of alkyl groups**.","These groups are **electron releasing relative to hydrogen atoms**, and help to stabilize the positive charge on the carbocation.","As the number of alkyl groups increases, the stability of the carbocation increases, too. Thus, **primary carbocations are the least stable and tertiary carbocations are the most stable**."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5i008cjfqktvfnd6sc","sectionKey":"addition-polymerisation","number":"5.7","title":"Addition Polymerisation","blurb":"Monomer to polymer — drawing repeat units both ways.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"5.7","specText":"be able to describe the addition polymerisation of alkenes and draw the repeat unit given the monomer, and vice versa","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"list","items":["During polymerization, an alkene undergoes **addition reaction with itself**.","Many **monomers** are joined together to form a **polymer**.","Many polymers are prepared by a **free radical process** involving high pressure, high temperature and a catalyst.","The catalyst is usually a substance which readily breaks up to form **radicals** which initiate a chain reaction.","Long hydrocarbon chains are formed when alkenes are polymerized."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/polymerisation.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/polymerisation-dark.png","alt":"n molecules of ethene joining to form the poly(ethene) repeat unit shown in brackets with subscript n","caption":"Formation of poly(ethene): the monomer is ethene, the polymer is poly(ethene), and n is the number of repeat units joined together."},{"type":"eq","code":"n CH₂=CH₂ → (—CH₂—CH₂—)ₙ","caption":"Addition polymerisation of ethene"},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Inside the repeat unit","text":"Polymerisation uses up the π bond: the C=C of the monomer becomes a **C–C single bond** in the repeat unit, and the bonds continue out through both sides of the brackets."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5j008ejfqk3wv1aihh","sectionKey":"polymer-disposal","number":"5.8","title":"Polymer Disposal","blurb":"Landfill, incineration, recycling and biodegradable polymers.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"5.8","specText":"understand how chemists limit the problems caused by polymer disposal by:","outline":["i   developing biodegradable polymers","ii   removing toxic waste gases produced by the incineration of polymers"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Polymers derived from alkenes are very useful to modern society. However, the disposal of these polymers creates widespread problems."},{"type":"list","items":["They are **non-biodegradable**.","If they are just discarded they add to the **landfill problem**.","**Incineration** (burning of polymers) saves on landfill sites. However, if they are burned, **toxic fumes like HCl** are formed from chlorinated polymers.","They can be **recycled**. However, it is very expensive and it is difficult to separate all the different polymers."]},{"type":"h3","text":"How chemists limit the problems"},{"type":"h3","text":"Incineration"},{"type":"list","items":["An incinerator takes in polymer waste and converts it into **heat energy** which can be used in factories or used to generate electricity.","It produces **less solid waste**. However, **toxic gases** are formed and released to the atmosphere.","Over the years, chemists have made progress in finding better ways to **remove the toxic gases**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Biodegradable polymers"},{"type":"list","items":["They can be **broken down by microbes** in the environment.","Usually made from **plant material**, hence **more land is needed** to grow the plants."]}]}],"cards":[{"id":"cmv1eoi5j008gjfqk677bmw3u","ord":1,"front":"State the general formula of (non-cyclic) alkenes, and what makes them unsaturated.","back":"CₙH₂ₙ (non-cyclic alkenes only). They are unsaturated hydrocarbons — they contain at least one C=C bond, which consists of one σ bond and one π bond; bonds sit at 120° around the C=C carbons.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi5k008ijfqkhtvbra6y","ord":2,"front":"Describe the C=C double bond.","back":"Consists of a σ bond (direct overlap of orbitals along the axis) and a π bond (sideways overlap of unhybridized p orbitals above and below). The π electron cloud is a region of high electron density — attacked by electrophiles. Each C=C carbon has three sp² hybrid orbitals and one unhybridized p orbital.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi5k008kjfqk93h1e2fu","ord":3,"front":"Why is rotation restricted about C=C?","back":"Rotation would break the π overlap. Restricted rotation + a different atom/group on each carbon of the double bond → geometric (E/Z) isomerism.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi5l008mjfqkestzrggr","ord":4,"front":"When can the cis–trans naming system be used, and when does it break down?","back":"Usable when there are two H’s and two non-hydrogen groups (or two similar groups) on the C=C carbons: cis = similar groups on the same side, trans = across. It breaks down when four different atoms are attached to the C=C — then the E–Z system is needed.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi5l008ojfqkyb3c2q0m","ord":5,"front":"How do you assign E or Z to an alkene?","back":"Rank the two substituents on each C=C carbon by atomic number (higher Z = higher priority; if tied, compare the atoms attached to those atoms). Higher-priority groups on the same side → Z (zusammen, together); on opposite sides → E (entgegen, opposite).","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi5m008qjfqkwz9bxbuk","ord":6,"front":"Why are alkenes much more reactive than alkanes?","back":"The π bond is a region of high electron density, readily attacked by electrophiles; and the π bond is weaker than the σ bonds, so less energy is needed to break it. The typical reaction is addition across the π bond (heterolytic electrophilic addition).","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi5n008sjfqk63z0boug","ord":7,"front":"Give the reagent, condition and product for each of the five alkene reactions.","back":"i Hydrogenation: H₂ / Ni catalyst, 140–180 °C → alkane. ii Halogenation: Cl₂ or Br₂ (in hexane) / room temp → di-substituted halogenoalkane. iii Hydrogen halides: HBr / room temp → mono-substituted halogenoalkane. iv Hydration: steam / H₃PO₄ catalyst, 300 °C → alcohol. v Oxidation: KMnO₄ / room temp → diol.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi5n008ujfqkoinlyjpg","ord":8,"front":"What is observed when bromine water is shaken with an alkene?","back":"Reddish-brown bromine water decolourises to colourless — the positive test for a C=C double bond. Products with bromine water: a bromoalcohol + HBr (C₂H₄ + Br₂ + H₂O → CH₂BrCH₂OH + HBr).","tag":"TEST"},{"id":"cmv1eoi5n008wjfqkmsbua2oc","ord":9,"front":"Bromine in hexane vs bromine water — what is the difference in product?","back":"Bromine in hexane: only the two Br atoms add across C=C → dihalogenoalkane (C₂H₄ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br, balances exactly — no HBr formed). Bromine water: Br AND OH add → bromoalcohol + HBr.","tag":"TRAP"},{"id":"cmv1eoi5o008yjfqk0xp7sso3","ord":10,"front":"What is observed with alkaline KMnO₄, and with acidified KMnO₄?","back":"Purple solution turns GREEN in alkali (MnO₄⁻ → MnO₄²⁻); purple turns COLOURLESS in acid. Both oxidise the C=C to a diol: C₂H₄ + H₂O + [O] → CH₂(OH)CH₂(OH).","tag":"TEST"},{"id":"cmv1eoi5o0090jfqkal18z8am","ord":11,"front":"Describe the electrophilic addition of HBr to ethene.","back":"The π pair bonds to the δ+ H of H–Br; the H–Br pair moves onto Br (heterolytic fission) → carbocation + Br⁻. Br⁻ then acts as a nucleophile, donating a lone pair to C⁺ → bromoethane. Curly arrows start from a bond or a lone pair.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi5p0092jfqkxsg3fq44","ord":12,"front":"Why can non-polar bromine act as an electrophile?","back":"As Br₂ approaches the alkene, the π electrons repel the Br–Br bonding pair, inducing a dipole (Brδ+—Brδ−). The δ+ end is then attacked by the π bond.","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi5q0094jfqkkrpxdm6r","ord":13,"front":"HBr + propene: which product is major and why?","back":"2-bromopropane (major) forms via the SECONDARY carbocation; 1-bromopropane (minor) via the primary. Alkyl groups are electron-releasing relative to H and stabilise the positive charge, so stability increases 1° < 2° < 3° — the major product comes via the more stable carbocation.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi5s0096jfqkh1c1w2vq","ord":14,"front":"What happens during addition polymerisation of an alkene?","back":"The alkene undergoes addition reaction with itself: many monomers join (free-radical process — high pressure, high temperature, a catalyst that forms radicals) to give long saturated chains. The monomer’s C=C becomes a C–C single bond in the repeat unit, e.g. n CH₂=CH₂ → (—CH₂—CH₂—)ₙ.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi5t0098jfqkupjoe7mw","ord":15,"front":"Why are poly(alkene)s hard to dispose of, and how do chemists limit the problems?","back":"They are non-biodegradable (landfill), and recycling is expensive and hard to separate. Incineration saves landfill but releases toxic fumes (e.g. HCl from chlorinated polymers) — chemists improve toxic-gas removal and develop biodegradable polymers (made from plant material, broken down by microbes).","tag":"EXAM"}]},{"slug":"energetics","number":6,"unit":2,"unitCode":"WCH12","unitName":"Energetics, Group Chemistry, Halogenoalkanes and Alcohols","title":"Energetics","subtitle":"Enthalpy changes, calorimetry, Hess’s Law and bond enthalpies.","summary":"ΔH and standard conditions; exothermic vs endothermic; enthalpy level diagrams; the five standard enthalpy changes; calorimetry with q = mcΔT; Hess’s Law cycles; mean bond enthalpies.","estHours":5.0,"sections":[{"id":"cmv1eoi5u009bjfqkvd4kstg3","sectionKey":"specification-map","number":"6.0","title":"Specification Map","blurb":"Every statement the specification assesses in this topic — use it as your revision checklist.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"6.1 – 6.11","specText":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:","outline":["The full list of assessment statements for Topic 6, worded exactly as the specification words them","Every statement 6.1 – 6.11 has its own section below"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Spec","Assessment statement"],"rows":[["6.1","know that the enthalpy change, ΔH, is the heat energy change measured at constant pressure and that standard conditions are 100 kPa and a specified temperature, usually 298 K"],["6.2","know that, by convention, exothermic reactions have a negative enthalpy change and endothermic reactions have a positive enthalpy change"],["6.3","be able to construct and interpret enthalpy level diagrams, showing exothermic and endothermic enthalpy changes"],["6.4","know the definition of standard enthalpy change of:\ni   reaction, ΔrH\nii   formation, ΔfH\niii   combustion, ΔcH\niv   neutralisation, ΔneutH\nv   atomisation, ΔatH"],["6.5","be able to use experimental data to calculate:\ni   energy transferred in a reaction recalling and using the expression: energy transferred (J) = mass (g) × specific heat capacity (J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹) × temperature change (°C)\nii   enthalpy change of the reaction in kJ mol⁻¹"],["6.6","know Hess’s Law and be able to apply it to:\ni   constructing enthalpy cycles\nii   calculating enthalpy changes of reaction using data provided, or data selected from a table or obtained from experiments"],["6.7","CORE PRACTICAL 2 — Determination of the enthalpy change of a reaction using Hess’s Law."],["6.8","be able to evaluate the results obtained from experiments and comment on sources of error and uncertainty and any assumptions made in the experiments"],["6.9","understand the terms ‘bond enthalpy’ and ‘mean bond enthalpy’, and be able to use bond enthalpies to calculate enthalpy changes, understanding the limitations of this method"],["6.10","be able to calculate mean bond enthalpies from enthalpy changes of reaction"],["6.11","understand that bond enthalpy data gives some indication about which bond will break first in a reaction, how easy or difficult it is and therefore how rapidly a reaction will take place at room temperature"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Further suggested practicals:"},{"type":"list","items":["the enthalpy change for the decomposition of calcium carbonate using the enthalpy changes of reaction of calcium carbonate and calcium oxide with hydrochloric acid","the enthalpy change of combustion of an alcohol","the enthalpy change of the reaction between zinc and copper(II) sulfate solution","the enthalpy of hydration of anhydrous copper(II) sulfate"]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5u009djfqk0cssiwab","sectionKey":"enthalpy-change","number":"6.1","title":"Enthalpy Change and Standard Conditions","blurb":"ΔH is the heat energy change at constant pressure — and the conditions that make values comparable.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"6.1","specText":"know that the enthalpy change, ΔH, is the heat energy change measured at constant pressure and that standard conditions are 100 kPa and a specified temperature, usually 298 K","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Energetics","text":"The study of energy transfers between reacting chemicals and their surroundings — the energy changes in reactions reflect changes at a molecular level. The study of these energy transfers is also known as **thermodynamics**."},{"type":"list","items":["In chemical systems the major transfer of energy is by **heating**.","Every chemical reaction is accompanied by some form of energy change — sometimes obvious, in many cases unnoticed."]},{"type":"h3","text":"The First Law of Thermodynamics"},{"type":"list","items":["Energy can be **neither created nor destroyed** — but it can be converted from one form to another.","The energy transferred to the surroundings is not lost to the universe: it is **dissipated**, or spread through the surroundings.","This is the **principle of conservation of energy** — the total energy content of the universe is constant.","Two of the most important forms of energy are **chemical energy** and **heat energy**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Chemical energy and heat energy"},{"type":"list","items":["**Chemical energy** is made up of two components: **kinetic energy**, a measure of the motion of particles in a substance, and **potential energy**, a measure of how strongly the particles interact with one another.","**Heat energy** is the portion of the potential and kinetic energy of a substance that is responsible for its temperature — it is directly proportional to the absolute temperature."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Enthalpy and enthalpy change"},{"type":"def","term":"Enthalpy, H","text":"A measure of the **heat content** of a substance at constant pressure."},{"type":"list","items":["The enthalpy of a system **cannot be measured directly** — however, the **enthalpy change at constant pressure** can.","Enthalpy change is denoted by the symbol **ΔH** (‘delta H’) and is expressed in **kJ mol⁻¹**.","**Enthalpy change** is the amount of heat energy taken in or given out during any change in a system, provided the pressure is constant.","The enthalpy of a substance depends on its **physical state** (solid, liquid or gas), the **amount of substance**, and the **temperature and pressure**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Standard conditions"},{"type":"list","items":["Enthalpy changes for reactions vary slightly depending on the conditions under which the reaction is carried out.","Chemists use **standard conditions** so that all reactions and corresponding enthalpy changes are comparable — they are as close to normal lab conditions as possible.","Pressure of **100 kPa** and a specified temperature, usually **298 K (25 °C)**.","Solutions at a concentration of **1 mol dm⁻³**, and all substances in their **normal physical states** at standard temperature and pressure."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Part of the symbol","Meaning"],"rows":[["Δ","change in"],["H","enthalpy"],["°","standard conditions (100 kPa, usually 298 K)"]]},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Quote 100 kPa — not 1 atm","text":"Older sources sometimes quote 1 atm (101 kPa) as the standard pressure. The Edexcel specification values **100 kPa** exactly, and a specified temperature usually **298 K**. Use those values in definitions and exam answers."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5v009fjfqkpw1bq128","sectionKey":"exothermic-endothermic","number":"6.2","title":"Exothermic and Endothermic Reactions","blurb":"The sign convention — and what it means for the energy of the system.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"6.2","specText":"know that, by convention, exothermic reactions have a negative enthalpy change and endothermic reactions have a positive enthalpy change","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Exothermic reactions","text":"Reactions which **liberate heat energy to the surroundings**. By convention, ΔH is **negative**."},{"type":"list","items":["The enthalpy of the reactants is **greater than** the enthalpy of the products.","The energy content of the system has **decreased** — some energy has left the system and entered the surroundings as heat, so there is a net decrease in the potential energy of the system.","The reactants are therefore **thermodynamically unstable** compared with the products.","Many exothermic reactions are spontaneous. However, despite being thermodynamically unstable, some exothermic reactions might not occur due to **kinetic stability** — a high activation energy is required to start the reaction. The combustion of petrol is highly exothermic but occurs only when ignited."]},{"type":"def","term":"Endothermic reactions","text":"Reactions which **absorb heat energy from the surroundings**. By convention, ΔH is **positive**."},{"type":"list","items":["The enthalpy of the reactants is **less than** the enthalpy of the products.","The energy content of the system has **increased** — it has gained energy from its surroundings, so there is a net increase in the potential energy of the system.","The reactants are therefore **thermodynamically stable** compared with the products."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Bond making vs bond breaking","text":"A reaction is overall **exothermic** if the energy released for bond making is **greater** than the energy absorbed for bond breaking — and overall **endothermic** if the energy released for bond making is **less** than the energy absorbed for bond breaking."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5v009hjfqk4eel9p6v","sectionKey":"enthalpy-level-diagrams","number":"6.3","title":"Enthalpy Level Diagrams","blurb":"Constructing and reading the two diagrams every energetics question uses.","estMinutes":25,"spec":"6.3","specText":"be able to construct and interpret enthalpy level diagrams, showing exothermic and endothermic enthalpy changes","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Two diagrams carry this whole topic: the **enthalpy level diagram** — reactant and product levels joined by the ΔH arrow — and the **reaction profile**, which additionally shows the activation energy hump. You must be able to construct and interpret both for exothermic and endothermic changes."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/enthalpy-level-diagrams.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/enthalpy-level-diagrams-dark.png","alt":"Enthalpy level diagrams: exothermic with products below reactants and a downward ΔH arrow; endothermic with products above reactants and an upward ΔH arrow","caption":"Exothermic: products sit below the reactants, ΔH arrow points down (ΔH < 0). Endothermic: products sit above the reactants, ΔH arrow points up (ΔH > 0)."},{"type":"list","items":["The vertical axis is **enthalpy, H** — there is no zero and no scale; only the **difference** between the levels matters.","The arrow between the two levels is the enthalpy change of the reaction, with its **sign** shown by the direction."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/reaction-profiles.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/reaction-profiles-dark.png","alt":"Reaction profiles for exothermic and endothermic reactions showing the activation energy hump, Ea, and the ΔH arrow between reactants and products","caption":"Reaction profiles add the **activation energy, Ea** — the energy barrier that must be overcome before the reaction can proceed."},{"type":"list","items":["The **activation energy, Ea**, is measured from the reactant level up to the top of the hump.","A **high** activation energy explains kinetic stability: an exothermic reaction with a large Ea — like petrol combustion — simply waits for a spark."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5w009jjfqk3g2b9i3l","sectionKey":"standard-enthalpy-definitions","number":"6.4","title":"The Standard Enthalpy Changes","blurb":"Five definitions to know word-perfect — each with its equation.","estMinutes":35,"spec":"6.4","specText":"know the definition of standard enthalpy change of:","outline":["i   reaction, ΔrH","ii   formation, ΔfH","iii   combustion, ΔcH","iv   neutralisation, ΔneutH","v   atomisation, ΔatH"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"A **thermochemical equation** is a chemical equation that includes the value of ΔH — always with **state symbols**, since a change of physical state is itself accompanied by an enthalpy change. The standard symbol ΔH° carries the ° (standard conditions); the subscripted symbols below are all quoted per mole."},{"type":"eq","code":"C(s) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g)    ΔH = −393 kJ mol⁻¹","caption":"The correct thermochemical form — burning one mole of carbon releases 393 kJ"},{"type":"list","items":["When a thermochemical equation is **multiplied**, ΔH is multiplied too.","When a thermochemical equation is **reversed**, the **sign of ΔH changes** (conservation of energy)."]},{"type":"eq","code":"C₄₀H₈₂ + 60.5O₂ → 40CO₂ + 41H₂O    ΔH = −20 000 kJ"},{"type":"eq","code":"2C₄₀H₈₂ + 121O₂ → 80CO₂ + 82H₂O    ΔH = −40 000 kJ","caption":"Equation ×2 → ΔH ×2"},{"type":"eq","code":"40CO₂ + 41H₂O → C₄₀H₈₂ + 60.5O₂    ΔH = +20 000 kJ","caption":"Equation reversed → sign of ΔH reversed"},{"type":"h3","text":"i — Standard enthalpy change of reaction, ΔrH°"},{"type":"def","term":"ΔrH°","text":"The enthalpy change when the number of moles of reactants **as specified in the balanced equation** react together."},{"type":"eq","code":"N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g)    ΔrH° = −92 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"type":"eq","code":"½N₂(g) + 3⁄2H₂(g) → NH₃(g)    ΔrH° = −46 kJ mol⁻¹","caption":"The same reaction rescaled — half the equation, half the ΔH"},{"type":"callout","variant":"warning","title":"‘Per mole’ means per mole of equation","text":"ΔrH° refers to **one mole of the equation as written** — not to one mole of any particular reactant or product. Halve the equation and you halve ΔH."},{"type":"h3","text":"ii — Standard enthalpy change of formation, ΔfH°"},{"type":"def","term":"ΔfH°","text":"The enthalpy change that occurs when **one mole of a substance** is formed from its **elements in their standard states** under standard conditions."},{"type":"eq","code":"2C(s, graphite) + ½O₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → C₂H₅OH(l)","caption":"Standard enthalpy of formation of ethanol"},{"type":"list","items":["Elements in their standard states have **zero** enthalpy of formation — ΔfH° of H₂(g) = 0 and ΔfH° of O₂(g) = 0.","Carbon is usually taken as the **graphite** allotrope."]},{"type":"h3","text":"iii — Standard enthalpy change of combustion, ΔcH°"},{"type":"def","term":"ΔcH°","text":"The enthalpy change that occurs when **one mole** of a substance is **completely burnt in excess oxygen** under standard conditions, with all reactants and products in their standard states."},{"type":"list","items":["It is **always exothermic**."]},{"type":"eq","code":"C₂H₅OH(l) + 3O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g) + 3H₂O(l)","caption":"Standard enthalpy of combustion of ethanol"},{"type":"h3","text":"iv — Standard enthalpy change of neutralisation, ΔneutH°"},{"type":"def","term":"ΔneutH°","text":"The enthalpy change that occurs when **one mole of water** is formed in a reaction between an acid and an alkali under standard conditions."},{"type":"eq","code":"H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l)    ΔneutH° = −57.9 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"type":"list","items":["A value of about **−57 kJ mol⁻¹** is obtained when **strong** acids react with strong alkalis — strong acids and alkalis are fully ionised, so the reaction is the same in every case."]},{"type":"h3","text":"v — Standard enthalpy change of atomisation, ΔatH°"},{"type":"def","term":"ΔatH°","text":"The enthalpy change that occurs when **one mole of gaseous atoms** is formed from the element in its standard state under standard conditions."},{"type":"eq","code":"Na(s) → Na(g)        ½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g)"},{"type":"list","items":["It is **always endothermic**, as energy is required to break bonds."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Atomisation and bond enthalpy","text":"For a diatomic molecule the **bond enthalpy is twice the enthalpy of atomisation**: ½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g) is +121 kJ mol⁻¹, while Cl₂(g) → 2Cl(g) is +242 kJ mol⁻¹. The link is used in the bond-enthalpy work of 6.9."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5w009ljfqkp2rcisqb","sectionKey":"calorimetry-calculations","number":"6.5","title":"Calorimetry: from Temperature Change to ΔH","blurb":"q = mcΔT, then scale to one mole — solution and combustion experiments.","estMinutes":40,"spec":"6.5","specText":"be able to use experimental data to calculate:","outline":["i   energy transferred in a reaction recalling and using the expression: energy transferred (J) = mass (g) × specific heat capacity (J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹) × temperature change (°C)","ii   enthalpy change of the reaction in kJ mol⁻¹","This will be limited to experiments where substances are mixed in an insulated container and combustion experiments using a suitable calorimeter."],"content":[{"type":"eq","code":"energy transferred (J) = mass (g) × specific heat capacity (J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹) × temperature change (°C)","caption":"The specification expression — recall it and use it"},{"type":"eq","code":"Q = m × c × ΔT"},{"type":"table","headers":["Symbol","Meaning"],"rows":[["Q","heat energy transferred, in J"],["m","mass of the substance to which energy is transferred, in g"],["c","specific heat capacity, in J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹ — for water, 4.18 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹"],["ΔT","change in temperature, in °C (or K)"]]},{"type":"def","term":"Calorimeter","text":"An insulated reaction vessel which **minimises heat exchange** with the air and the surroundings. The simplest calorimeter is a **polystyrene cup**; there are also quite complex calorimeters."},{"type":"h3","text":"Reactions in solution (insulated container)"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/calorimetry-solution.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/calorimetry-solution-dark.png","alt":"Polystyrene cup calorimeter in a beaker with insulating lid, thermometer and reaction solution","caption":"The polystyrene cup calorimeter — the insulated container for mixing two solutions."},{"type":"list","items":["Place a measured volume of acid into the polystyrene cup and measure its temperature.","Measure the temperature of the alkali, add it to the acid, stir with the thermometer and record the **maximum temperature reached**.","The solution heated is the **total volume** of the two solutions — assume its density is 1 g cm⁻³ (so 1 cm³ = 1 g) and its specific heat capacity is that of water, 4.18 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹.","The alkali is often placed in slight **excess** to ensure all of the acid is neutralised."]},{"type":"worked","title":"Enthalpy change of neutralisation","problem":"25.0 cm³ of 1.00 mol dm⁻³ acid (18.6 °C) is mixed with 25.0 cm³ of 1.20 mol dm⁻³ alkali (18.8 °C) in a polystyrene cup; the maximum temperature reached is 25.4 °C. Calculate ΔneutH°.","steps":["Mean starting temperature = (18.6 + 18.8) ÷ 2 = 18.7 °C, so ΔT = 25.4 − 18.7 = +6.7 K.","Total volume of solution heated = 25 + 25 = 50 cm³ → mass = 50.0 g (density 1 g cm⁻³).","Q = mcΔT = 50 × 4.18 × 6.7 = 1400.3 J = 1.4003 kJ.","Amount of acid neutralised = amount of water formed = (1.00 × 25.0) ÷ 1000 = 0.025 mol.","ΔneutH° = −1.4003 ÷ 0.025 = −56 kJ mol⁻¹."],"answer":"ΔneutH° = −56 kJ mol⁻¹ — negative because neutralisation is exothermic"},{"type":"h3","text":"Combustion experiments (spirit burner)"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/calorimetry-combustion.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/calorimetry-combustion-dark.png","alt":"Combustion calorimetry: spirit burner with flame below a copper can of water on a tripod, inside a draught shield, with thermometer","caption":"Combustion calorimetry — the fuel burns beneath a known mass of water; the temperature rise of the water measures the energy released."},{"type":"list","items":["Weigh a **spirit burner** containing the fuel, place it below a beaker (or calorimeter) containing a known mass of water, and measure the water temperature.","Light the burner and **stir the water at regular intervals**.","When the temperature has risen by about 20 °C, put out the flame and **reweigh the burner** — the mass difference is the mass of fuel burned."]},{"type":"worked","title":"Enthalpy change of combustion of ethanol","problem":"A spirit burner burns ethanol beneath 100 cm³ of water, raising the water from 23.0 °C to 47.6 °C. The burner falls in mass from 52.76 g to 52.37 g. Calculate ΔcH° of ethanol.","steps":["Energy absorbed by the water: Q = mcΔT = 100 × 4.18 × (47.6 − 23.0) = 10 282 J = 10.28 kJ.","Moles of ethanol burned: mass = 52.76 − 52.37 = 0.39 g; n = mass ÷ molar mass = 0.39 ÷ 46 = 0.00847 mol.","Heat produced per mole: 10.28 ÷ 0.00847 = 1213.7 kJ mol⁻¹."],"answer":"ΔcH° of ethanol = −1213.7 kJ mol⁻¹ — the negative sign because combustion is exothermic"},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Sign discipline","text":"Q comes out of the calculation **positive** because the water gained energy. The reaction **released** that energy — so the enthalpy change you report is **negative**. Forgetting the sign is the most common mark lost in 6.5."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5x009njfqkdt3l9bhk","sectionKey":"hess-law","number":"6.6","title":"Hess’s Law and Enthalpy Cycles","blurb":"The route doesn’t matter — only the start and finish.","estMinutes":40,"spec":"6.6","specText":"know Hess’s Law and be able to apply it to:","outline":["i   constructing enthalpy cycles","ii   calculating enthalpy changes of reaction using data provided, or data selected from a table or obtained from experiments"],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Hess’s Law","text":"The enthalpy change of a chemical reaction is **independent of the path taken** in converting reactants into products, provided the initial and final conditions are the same."},{"type":"list","items":["The total energy for each route is the same — diagrams showing the alternative routes are called **enthalpy cycles** (Hess’s cycles).","Hess’s Law allows you to work out enthalpy changes that are **not possible to measure directly**, from other data.","It is used for calculating: enthalpy change of reaction **from bond enthalpy**; enthalpy change of reaction **from ΔcH° data**; enthalpy change of formation **from ΔfH° data**."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Why some ΔH values cannot be measured directly","text":"The reaction may have a **high activation energy**, be **very slow**, have **more than one reaction occurring at the same time**, involve a temperature change that is **difficult to measure** — or simply **not stop** when the stated products are formed."},{"type":"h3","text":"Route 1 — using combustion data (ΔcH°)"},{"type":"eq","code":"ΔH[reaction] = Σ ΔcH°[reactants] − Σ ΔcH°[products]"},{"type":"list","items":["When the products are burned, the amount of CO₂ and H₂O produced is the same as when the reactants are burned — that is what closes the cycle."]},{"type":"worked","title":"ΔfH° of carbon monoxide — Method 1 (subtract equations)","problem":"C(s, graphite) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g)  ΔcH° = −394 kJ mol⁻¹, and CO(g) + ½O₂(g) → CO₂(g)  ΔcH° = −283 kJ mol⁻¹. Find ΔfH° of CO(g).","steps":["The formation equation is C(s, graphite) + ½O₂(g) → CO(g). It cannot be measured directly because some carbon dioxide always forms as well.","Reverse the second equation: CO₂(g) → CO(g) + ½O₂(g) — its ΔH becomes +283 kJ mol⁻¹.","Add it to the first equation; the O₂ and CO₂ cancel to give C(s, graphite) + ½O₂(g) → CO(g).","ΔfH°[CO(g)] = −394 + 283 = −111 kJ mol⁻¹."],"answer":"ΔfH°[CO(g)] = −111 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"type":"worked","title":"ΔfH° of carbon monoxide — Method 2 (Hess’s cycle)","problem":"The same data, laid out as an enthalpy cycle that routes both carbon species through CO₂.","steps":["Direct route: C(s, graphite) + ½O₂(g) → CO(g) — ΔfH°.","Indirect route: C(s, graphite) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g) (−394), then CO₂(g) → CO(g) + ½O₂(g) (+283).","Cycle equation: ΔfH° + (−283) = −394."],"answer":"ΔfH° = −394 − (−283) = −111 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/hess-cycle-combustion.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/hess-cycle-combustion-dark.png","alt":"Hess cycle for the formation of methane from combustion data: elements and methane at the top linked by ΔfH, both routed down through the combustion products carbon dioxide and water","caption":"ΔfH° of methane from combustion data: ΔfH° + (−890) = (−394) + 2 × (−286), so ΔfH° = −76 kJ mol⁻¹."},{"type":"worked","title":"ΔfH° of methane from combustion data","problem":"ΔcH° data: C(s, graphite) −394; H₂(g) −286; CH₄(g) −890 kJ mol⁻¹. Calculate ΔfH° of CH₄(g).","steps":["Write the formation equation: C(s, graphite) + 2H₂(g) → CH₄(g).","Construct the energy cycle through the combustion products, CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l).","Apply Hess’s Law to the cycle: ΔfH° + (−890) = (−394) + 2 × (−286).","ΔfH° = [−394 + 2(−286)] − (−890) = −966 + 890."],"answer":"ΔfH°[CH₄(g)] = −76 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"type":"h3","text":"Route 2 — using formation data (ΔfH°)"},{"type":"eq","code":"ΔH[reaction] = Σ ΔfH°[products] − Σ ΔfH°[reactants]"},{"type":"list","items":["When enthalpies of formation for all the reactants and products are given, draw the Hess’s cycle through the **elements** required to form the reactants and products."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/hess-cycle-formation.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/hess-cycle-formation-dark.png","alt":"Hess cycle for the decomposition of calcium carbonate through the elements: calcium carbonate linked directly to calcium oxide and carbon dioxide by ΔrH, both routed through their elements with formation enthalpies","caption":"Decomposition of calcium carbonate from formation data: ΔrH° = [(−635) + (−394)] − (−1207) = +178 kJ mol⁻¹."},{"type":"worked","title":"Thermal decomposition of calcium carbonate","problem":"ΔfH° data: CaCO₃(s) −1207; CaO(s) −635; CO₂(g) −394 kJ mol⁻¹. Calculate ΔrH° for CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g).","steps":["Route both sides of the equation through their elements.","Apply Hess’s Law to the cycle: ΔrH° + (−1207) = (−635) + (−394).","ΔrH° = [(−635) + (−394)] − (−1207) = −1029 + 1207."],"answer":"ΔrH° = +178 kJ mol⁻¹"}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5y009pjfqkej169ylr","sectionKey":"core-practical-2","number":"6.7","title":"Core Practical 2: ΔH via Hess’s Law","blurb":"Two measurable reactions that bracket the one you can’t measure.","estMinutes":25,"spec":"6.7","specText":"CORE PRACTICAL 2 — Determination of the enthalpy change of a reaction using Hess’s Law.","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"**Core Practical 2** determines an enthalpy change of reaction that cannot be measured directly, by measuring the enthalpy changes of two reactions that **bracket** it and applying Hess’s Law. Each measurable reaction is carried out in a polystyrene cup calorimeter, and q = mcΔT converts the temperature change into an enthalpy change."},{"type":"worked","title":"Decomposition of calcium carbonate (a suggested practical)","problem":"Determine ΔrH° for CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g) — a decomposition that cannot be measured directly — given: CaCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)  ΔrH° = −17 kJ mol⁻¹; CaO(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l)  ΔrH° = −195 kJ mol⁻¹.","steps":["Both reactions share the same dissolved products, CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) — that is the common intermediate.","Reverse the second equation: CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) → CaO(s) + 2HCl(aq), ΔH = +195 kJ mol⁻¹.","Add it to the first equation; the 2HCl, CaCl₂ and H₂O cancel to give CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g).","ΔrH° = −17 + 195."],"answer":"ΔrH° = +178 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"type":"worked","title":"Hydration of anhydrous copper(II) sulfate (a suggested practical)","problem":"Determine ΔrH° for CuSO₄(s) + 5H₂O(l) → CuSO₄·5H₂O(s), given: CuSO₄·5H₂O(s) + aq → Cu²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) + 5H₂O(l)  ΔrH° = +11.3 kJ mol⁻¹; CuSO₄(s) + aq → Cu²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq)  ΔrH° = −67.0 kJ mol⁻¹.","steps":["Both measured reactions dissolve the solid to the **same aqueous ions** — ‘aq’ represents the water as solvent.","Reverse the first equation: Cu²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) + 5H₂O(l) → CuSO₄·5H₂O(s), ΔH = −11.3 kJ mol⁻¹.","Add it to the second: CuSO₄(s) + 5H₂O(l) → CuSO₄·5H₂O(s); ΔrH° = −67.0 − 11.3."],"answer":"ΔrH° = −78.3 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"The pattern to spot","text":"In both determinations the two measured reactions share a **common intermediate state** — the dissolved ions or the acid solution. Draw the Hess cycle through that shared state and the target enthalpy change is simply the **difference of the two measured values**."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5y009rjfqkgpl64qrp","sectionKey":"error-evaluation","number":"6.8","title":"Errors, Assumptions and Cooling Curves","blurb":"Evaluating the experiments — and drawing the graph that corrects for heat loss.","estMinutes":30,"spec":"6.8","specText":"be able to evaluate the results obtained from experiments and comment on sources of error and uncertainty and any assumptions made in the experiments","outline":["Students will need to consider experiments where substances are mixed in an insulated container and combustion experiments using, for example, a spirit burner and be able to draw suitable graphs and use cooling curve corrections."],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Every calorimetry result is an evaluation opportunity: the specification expects you to comment on **sources of error and uncertainty**, state the **assumptions** made, suggest **improvements** — and to draw suitable graphs using **cooling curve corrections**."},{"type":"h3","text":"Combustion experiments (spirit burner) — errors"},{"type":"list","items":["**Heat loss** to the surroundings.","**Incomplete combustion** of the fuel — black soot is deposited on the bottom of the beaker.","The conditions are **not standard**: water vapour is formed instead of liquid water.","Some of the heat released in burning heats up the **air**, not the water.","The **beaker and thermometer** absorb some of the heat produced."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Combustion experiments — assumptions"},{"type":"list","items":["1 cm³ of water is equal to **1 g**.","**No heat is lost** to the surroundings.","**All** the heat energy produced by the fuel is absorbed by the water."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Solution experiments (insulated container) — errors and uncertainties"},{"type":"list","items":["**Heat loss** to the surroundings.","The **polystyrene cup and thermometer** absorb some of the heat produced.","**Measurement uncertainties** involved with the use of the pipette and thermometer."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Solution experiments — assumptions"},{"type":"list","items":["The specific heat capacity of the resultant solution is equal to that of water — **4.18 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹**.","The density of the resultant solution is equal to that of water — **1 g cm⁻³**.","**No heat is lost** to the surroundings.","The total volume of the solution is equal to the **sum of the volumes** of the two solutions initially taken."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Improvements","text":"Use a **lid** on the polystyrene cup and add **lagging** around it — both cut heat loss to the surroundings, so the measured temperature change is closer to the true value. Shielding a spirit-burner experiment from draughts works the same way."},{"type":"h3","text":"Graphical method — cooling curves"},{"type":"list","items":["Take the temperature **every half minute** before mixing the reactants.","Mix the reactants after exactly **3 minutes** (say), then keep taking readings **every half minute** as the reaction mixture cools.","**Extrapolate** the two lines as shown and calculate the value of ΔT at the moment of mixing.","Extrapolating the graph **compensates for heat loss** to the surroundings — this method allows for the **cooling correction**."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/cooling-curve.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/cooling-curve-dark.png","alt":"Cooling curve correction: temperature against time, with readings before mixing, a rise after mixing at three minutes, a cooling line, and extrapolations back to the mixing time giving the corrected temperature change","caption":"The cooling correction: extrapolate the before-mixing and after-mixing lines back to the moment of mixing, and read ΔT there."},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"Why a series of readings?","text":"Recording the temperature every half minute — rather than just a starting and a maximum value — shows the **cooling trend** clearly, which is what justifies extrapolating both lines back to the mixing time to find the corrected ΔT."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5z009tjfqkem2i9ra4","sectionKey":"bond-enthalpy","number":"6.9","title":"Bond Enthalpy and Mean Bond Enthalpy","blurb":"Breaking bonds costs energy — calculating ΔH from bond enthalpies, and the limits of the method.","estMinutes":30,"spec":"6.9","specText":"understand the terms ‘bond enthalpy’ and ‘mean bond enthalpy’, and be able to use bond enthalpies to calculate enthalpy changes, understanding the limitations of this method","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Bond enthalpy, ΔBH","text":"The enthalpy change when **1 mol of a bond in the gaseous state** is broken: XY(g) → X(g) + Y(g)."},{"type":"eq","code":"Cl₂(g) → 2Cl(g)    ΔBH = +243 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"type":"eq","code":"HCl(g) → H(g) + Cl(g)    ΔBH = +432 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"type":"list","items":["For **polyatomic** molecules, each bond must be considered separately — for example, the four C–H bonds of methane:"]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/bond-dissociation-ch4.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/bond-dissociation-ch4-dark.png","alt":"Sequential dissociation of the four C-H bonds of methane: CH4 to CH3 to CH2 to CH to C, each step losing one hydrogen atom with bond enthalpies 423, 480, 425 and 335 kilojoules per mole","caption":"Each successive C–H bond in methane breaks with a different enthalpy — the strength of a bond depends on its environment, so **mean** values are quoted."},{"type":"def","term":"Mean bond enthalpy, E","text":"The **average** quantity of energy required to break **one mole** of that covalent bond in a **gaseous species** (at 100 kPa)."},{"type":"eq","code":"E(C–H) ≈ (423 + 480 + 425 + 335) ÷ 4 = +416 kJ mol⁻¹","caption":"The mean C–H bond enthalpy in methane — 415.75 ≈ +416 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"type":"list","items":["The C–H bond in different compounds has slightly different bond enthalpy values, so an **average value** is used.","The enthalpy change calculated from **standard enthalpy of formation or combustion data is more accurate** than one calculated from bond enthalpy values — because bond enthalpy values are means."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"warning","title":"Limitations of the bond-enthalpy method","text":"Bond enthalpies are measured in the **gaseous state** only, and the values are **not exact** — they are averages. Both effects make the calculated ΔH differ from the measured value."},{"type":"h3","text":"Calculating ΔH from bond enthalpies"},{"type":"eq","code":"ΔH[reaction] + Σ E[products] = Σ E[reactants]"},{"type":"eq","code":"ΔH[reaction] = Σ E[reactants] − Σ E[products]","caption":"Bonds broken (reactants) take energy in; bonds formed (products) release energy"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/bond-enthalpy-cycle.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/bond-enthalpy-cycle-dark.png","alt":"Bond enthalpy cycle for the formation of gaseous water: hydrogen plus half oxygen at the top, water at the top right, gaseous atoms below, with bond enthalpies 436, 249 and 924 kilojoules per mole","caption":"The cycle runs through **gaseous atoms**: break all the reactant bonds, then form all the product bonds."},{"type":"worked","title":"Formation of gaseous water","problem":"Use E(O–H) = +462, E(H–H) = +436 and E(O=O) = +498 kJ mol⁻¹ to find ΔHr for H₂(g) + ½O₂(g) → H₂O(g).","steps":["Apply the cycle: ΔHr + 2E[O–H] = E[H–H] + ½E[O=O].","ΔHr = (436) + (½ × 498) − (2 × 462).","ΔHr = 436 + 249 − 924."],"answer":"ΔHr = −239 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Link to atomisation (6.4 v)","text":"For a **diatomic** molecule, bond enthalpy = 2 × ΔatH°: Cl₂(g) → 2Cl(g) is +242 kJ mol⁻¹, while ½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g) is +121 kJ mol⁻¹."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi5z009vjfqkogp4bdzy","sectionKey":"mean-bond-enthalpy-calc","number":"6.10","title":"Finding Mean Bond Enthalpies from ΔH","blurb":"The bond-enthalpy cycle run in reverse.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"6.10","specText":"be able to calculate mean bond enthalpies from enthalpy changes of reaction","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"The bond-enthalpy cycle runs **both ways**. If the enthalpy change of a reaction is known — from experiment, or from ΔfH°/ΔcH° data — together with **all but one** of the bond enthalpies involved, the unknown **mean bond enthalpy** follows by rearrangement."},{"type":"eq","code":"ΔH[reaction] = Σ E[reactants] − Σ E[products]"},{"type":"eq","code":"unknown bond broken:  E(unknown) = ΔH[reaction] + Σ E[product bonds] − Σ E[other reactant bonds]"},{"type":"eq","code":"unknown bond formed:  E(unknown) = Σ E[reactant bonds] − ΔH[reaction] − Σ E[other product bonds]","caption":"The two rearrangements — depending on whether the bond you want is on the reactant (broken) or product (formed) side"},{"type":"list","items":["Write the equation, count **every** bond broken and formed (remember molecules like O₂ contain a double bond, counted as one O=O term per molecule).","Substitute the known values and make the unknown mean bond enthalpy the subject.","The value obtained is a **mean** for that bond — the same gaseous-state and mean-value limitations (6.9) apply to it."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"warning","title":"Watch the direction","text":"Bonds **broken** take energy **in**; bonds **formed** release energy. Check your answer against the sign of ΔH: for an exothermic reaction (ΔH negative) the energy released forming product bonds must exceed the energy absorbed breaking reactant bonds."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi60009xjfqk1w3jaovw","sectionKey":"bond-enthalpy-reactivity","number":"6.11","title":"Bond Enthalpy and Reactivity","blurb":"Which bond breaks first — and how fast the reaction goes at room temperature.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"6.11","specText":"understand that bond enthalpy data gives some indication about which bond will break first in a reaction, how easy or difficult it is and therefore how rapidly a reaction will take place at room temperature","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"list","items":["Bond enthalpy data give an indication of **which bond will break first** in a reaction — the bond with the **lower** bond enthalpy needs **less energy** to break, so it is the easier bond to break.","They also indicate **how easy or difficult** a reaction is: the larger the bond enthalpy, the more energy is needed to break that bond.","And therefore they indicate **how rapidly a reaction will take place at room temperature** — reactions whose first step breaks a high-enthalpy bond proceed only slowly without added energy."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"The methane values as evidence","text":"The four C–H bonds in CH₄ break with different enthalpies — +423, +480, +425 and +335 kJ mol⁻¹. The spread is exactly why mean values are quoted, and why a bond-enthalpy calculation only approximates the true ΔH of a reaction."},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Connecting to kinetic stability (6.2)","text":"The activation energy seen on a reaction profile is the energy needed to break bonds on the way to reaction — so strong bonds (high bond enthalpies) mean a difficult first step, which is what makes some exothermic reactions kinetically stable."}]}],"cards":[{"id":"cmv1eoi60009zjfqkgwlh9w62","ord":1,"front":"Define enthalpy change, ΔH.","back":"The heat energy change of a system measured at constant pressure. Units kJ mol⁻¹.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6000a1jfqktrwoiodz","ord":2,"front":"Standard conditions?","back":"100 kPa pressure and a stated temperature, usually 298 K. Standard states: the physical state of a substance under these conditions.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6100a3jfqka42cledl","ord":3,"front":"Exo vs endothermic — signs and temperature effects?","back":"Exothermic: ΔH negative, releases heat to surroundings, temperature rises. Endothermic: ΔH positive, absorbs heat, temperature falls. Energy profile products below/above reactants respectively.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6100a5jfqkl7ocoj8s","ord":4,"front":"The five standard enthalpy symbols — define each in one line.","back":"ΔrH°: the moles of reactants as specified in the balanced equation react together. ΔfH°: 1 mol of a substance formed from its elements in their standard states. ΔcH°: 1 mol completely burnt in excess oxygen. ΔneutH°: 1 mol of water formed from an acid + alkali. ΔatH°: 1 mol of gaseous atoms formed from the element in its standard state.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6200a7jfqkagqa9s9j","ord":5,"front":"Define standard enthalpy change of formation.","back":"The enthalpy change when one mole of a compound is formed from its elements in their standard states under standard conditions. ΔfH = 0 for elements in standard states.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6200a9jfqkl22xycwn","ord":6,"front":"Define standard enthalpy change of combustion.","back":"The enthalpy change when one mole of a substance is completely burned in oxygen under standard conditions.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6300abjfqkgtooo8o0","ord":7,"front":"Why is ΔneutH° about −57 kJ mol⁻¹ for every strong acid + strong alkali?","back":"Strong acids and alkalis are fully ionised, so the reaction is the same in every case: H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l), ΔneutH° = −57.9 kJ mol⁻¹.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6300adjfqktcwzo1zq","ord":8,"front":"The calorimetry equation?","back":"q = m × c × ΔT. m = mass of the substance being heated (usually the solution/water, in g), c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ for water, ΔT in °C or K. Then ΔH = −q ÷ moles.","tag":"EQUATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6300afjfqkgsg1ic0v","ord":9,"front":"Rules for thermochemical equations?","back":"Multiply the equation → multiply ΔH by the same factor. Reverse the equation → flip the sign of ΔH (conservation of energy). Always include state symbols — a change of physical state has its own enthalpy change.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6300ahjfqk6y8k8aom","ord":10,"front":"State Hess's Law.","back":"The enthalpy change of a reaction is independent of the route taken, provided initial and final conditions are the same.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6400ajjfqkf6ub69y4","ord":11,"front":"Give the two Hess formulas.","back":"Combustion data: ΔH[reaction] = Σ ΔcH°[reactants] − Σ ΔcH°[products]. Formation data: ΔH[reaction] = Σ ΔfH°[products] − Σ ΔfH°[reactants].","tag":"EQUATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6500aljfqktco1jh6f","ord":12,"front":"Why is measured ΔcH (e.g. of alcohols) less exothermic than the data book value?","back":"Heat loss to the surroundings and apparatus; incomplete combustion (soot on the beaker); water vapour forms instead of liquid water; some heat heats the air; the beaker and thermometer absorb heat. Not all heat transfers to the water.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6500anjfqk4r9slozs","ord":13,"front":"What is a cooling curve correction and why extrapolate?","back":"Temperature every half minute before mixing, mix (e.g. at 3.0 min), keep reading every half minute as the mixture cools. Extrapolate the before- and after-mixing lines back to the moment of mixing and read ΔT there — the extrapolation compensates for heat loss to the surroundings.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6600apjfqktp3qtntd","ord":14,"front":"Calculate ΔH from bond enthalpies.","back":"ΔH ≈ Σ(bond enthalpies broken) − Σ(bond enthalpies formed). Average bond enthalpies are mean values over many compounds, gas phase only — so results differ from measured ΔH.","tag":"EQUATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6600arjfqkg2862cv8","ord":15,"front":"Limitations of bond-enthalpy calculations?","back":"Bond enthalpies are measured in the gaseous state only, and the values are means (bond strength depends on its environment) — so the calculated ΔH differs from the true value. ΔH from ΔfH°/ΔcH° data is more accurate.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6700atjfqknqxu4f3n","ord":16,"front":"What does ΔatH° mean, and how does it relate to bond enthalpy?","back":"The enthalpy change when one mole of gaseous atoms forms from the element in its standard state — always endothermic. For a diatomic molecule, bond enthalpy = 2 × ΔatH°: ½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g) is +121, Cl₂(g) → 2Cl(g) is +242 kJ mol⁻¹.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6700avjfqkxn0f2gub","ord":17,"front":"How does Core Practical 2 find a ΔH you cannot measure directly?","back":"Measure two reactions that share a common intermediate and apply Hess’s Law: CaCO₃ decomposition via its HCl reactions (−17 and −195 → +178 kJ mol⁻¹); CuSO₄ hydration via the two solution enthalpies (−67.0 and +11.3 → −78.3 kJ mol⁻¹).","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6800axjfqkie7andgn","ord":18,"front":"What does bond enthalpy data indicate about a reaction?","back":"Which bond will break first (the lowest-enthalpy bond is easiest to break), how easy or difficult the reaction is — and therefore how rapidly it will take place at room temperature.","tag":"CORE"}]},{"slug":"intermolecular-forces","number":7,"unit":2,"unitCode":"WCH12","unitName":"Energetics, Group Chemistry, Halogenoalkanes and Alcohols","title":"Intermolecular Forces","subtitle":"London forces, dipoles and hydrogen bonds — the forces between molecules.","summary":"London forces and electron density; permanent dipole–dipole interactions; hydrogen bonding in H₂O, NH₃ and HF; anomalous properties of water; boiling trends of alkanes, alcohols and hydrogen halides; solvents and solubility.","estHours":3.5,"sections":[{"id":"cmv1eoi6900b0jfqkh5a3511v","sectionKey":"specification-map","number":"7.0","title":"Specification Map","blurb":"Every statement the specification assesses in this topic — use it as your revision checklist.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"7.1 – 7.6","specText":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:","outline":["The full list of assessment statements for Topic 7, worded exactly as the specification words them","Every statement 7.1 – 7.6 has its own section below"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Spec","Assessment statement"],"rows":[["7.1","understand the nature of the following intermolecular forces:\ni   London forces (instantaneous dipole–induced dipole)\nii   permanent dipole–permanent dipole interactions\niii   hydrogen bonds"],["7.2","understand the interactions in molecules, such as H₂O, liquid NH₃ and liquid HF, which give rise to hydrogen bonding"],["7.3","understand the following anomalous properties of water resulting from hydrogen bonding:\ni   its high melting and boiling temperature when compared with similar molecules\nii   the density of ice compared to that of water"],["7.4","be able to predict the presence of hydrogen bonding in molecules analogous to those mentioned in 7.2"],["7.5","understand, in terms of intermolecular forces, physical properties shown by substances, including:\ni   the trends in boiling temperatures of alkanes with increasing chain length\nii   the effect of branching in the carbon chain on the boiling temperatures of alkanes\niii   the relatively low volatility (higher boiling temperatures) of alcohols compared to alkanes with a similar number of electrons\niv   the trends in boiling temperatures of the hydrogen halides HF to HI"],["7.6","understand factors that influence the choice of solvents, including:\ni   water, to dissolve some ionic compounds, in terms of the hydration of the ions\nii   water, to dissolve simple alcohols, in terms of hydrogen bonding\niii   water, as a poor solvent for compounds (to include polar molecules such as halogenoalkane), in terms of inability to form hydrogen bonds\niv   non-aqueous solvents, for compounds that have similar intermolecular forces to those in the solvent"]]},{"type":"p","text":"This topic builds directly on the foundations of Topic 3: **electronegativity** (3.13), **bond polarity** (3.14) and the shapes of **polar molecules** (3.15). Here the polarity of bonds is used to explain the attractions **between** whole molecules — and those attractions turn out to decide how substances boil, melt, mix and dissolve."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6a00b2jfqkbsb8xkq3","sectionKey":"nature-of-forces","number":"7.1","title":"The Nature of Intermolecular Forces","blurb":"London forces, permanent dipole–dipole interactions and hydrogen bonds — the three non-bonded interactions.","estMinutes":40,"spec":"7.1","specText":"understand the nature of the following intermolecular forces:\ni   London forces (instantaneous dipole–induced dipole)\nii   permanent dipole–permanent dipole interactions\niii   hydrogen bonds","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Intermolecular forces","text":"The weak **electrostatic attractions between molecules** — described as **non-bonded interactions** because they occur *between* molecules, not inside them. They are considerably weaker than typical covalent and polar covalent bonds."},{"type":"list","items":["Intermolecular forces act **in addition to** the covalent bonds inside each molecule — they do not replace them.","Because they are weak, they are overcome easily: they decide **physical properties** such as melting temperature, boiling temperature, volatility and solubility."]},{"type":"h3","text":"The three types of intermolecular force"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["**Instantaneous dipole–induced dipole interactions** — also called **London forces**, temporary dipole forces or dispersion forces.","**Permanent dipole–permanent dipole interactions** — between molecules with a permanent separation of charge.","**Hydrogen bonds** — the strongest of the three; a special case of dipole–dipole attraction involving hydrogen."]},{"type":"h3","text":"London forces"},{"type":"def","term":"London forces","text":"Instantaneous dipole–induced dipole attractions. They occur between **all molecular substances and noble gases** — they do not occur in ionic substances."},{"type":"list","items":["In any molecule the electrons are moving **constantly and randomly**. As this happens the electron density can fluctuate, so parts of the molecule become more or less negative — small **temporary (transient) dipoles** form.","These temporary dipoles can cause dipoles to form in **neighbouring molecules** — these are called **induced dipoles**. The induced dipole is always the **opposite sign** to the original one on the facing sides.","The two dipoles between the molecules are arranged so that they interact favourably with one another — this favourable interaction is the **London force of attraction**.","London forces exist between **all** simple covalent molecules and the separate atoms in noble gases — no molecule can escape them."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/imf-london-origin.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/imf-london-origin-dark.png","alt":"An instantaneous dipole in molecule A inducing an opposite dipole in neighbouring molecule B, with attraction between the facing ends","caption":"The fluctuating electron density in molecule A creates an instantaneous dipole, which induces an opposite dipole in its neighbour B — the facing δ+ and δ− ends attract."},{"type":"h3","text":"Feature 1 — the size of the electron cloud"},{"type":"list","items":["The **more electrons** there are in a molecule, the greater the fluctuations in electron density and the larger the instantaneous and induced dipoles created.","This makes the London forces stronger between the molecules, so **more energy is needed to break them** — boiling temperatures will be greater.","This is demonstrated by the boiling temperatures of the **noble gases**: they are monatomic (single atoms), so London forces are the **only** force of attraction between the particles."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Gas","Helium","Neon","Argon","Krypton","Xenon","Radon"],"rows":[["Boiling temperature / K","4.3","27.1","87.4","121","165","211"]]},{"type":"list","items":["Down Group 18 the atoms get bigger and hold **more electrons**, so the London forces between the atoms increase — and the boiling temperature rises."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"The first rule of London forces","text":"As the **size of the electron cloud** increases, the **strength of London forces increases** — more electrons, bigger dipoles, higher boiling temperature."},{"type":"h3","text":"Feature 2 — the area of contact between molecules"},{"type":"list","items":["The **shape** of the molecule also affects the size of the London forces: the more **points of contact** there are between molecules, the greater the overall London force.","A long, straight chain lies alongside its neighbours over its whole length; a compact, branched molecule touches its neighbours at fewer points.","Pentane and 2,2-dimethylpropane share the same molecular formula C₅H₁₂ — the same electrons, but very different shapes — so the difference in their boiling temperatures is caused almost entirely by the **contact area**."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/imf-contact-area.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/imf-contact-area-dark.png","alt":"Straight-chain pentane molecules in close contact boiling at 36 °C, compared with compact 2,2-dimethylpropane molecules with fewer contact points boiling at 9.5 °C","caption":"More points of contact → stronger London forces → higher boiling temperature: pentane boils at 36 °C, its compact isomer 2,2-dimethylpropane at only 9.5 °C."},{"type":"h3","text":"Permanent dipole–dipole interactions"},{"type":"def","term":"Permanent dipole","text":"A **permanent separation of charge** across a molecule, produced by polar bonds arranged unsymmetrically — one end of the molecule is always δ−, the other always δ+."},{"type":"list","items":["Polar molecules are attracted towards one another by **permanent dipole–dipole interactions**: the negative end of one molecule attracts the positive end of another. For example, Hδ+—Clδ− molecules line up δ+→δ−.","These interactions occur between molecules containing permanent dipoles **but which cannot form hydrogen bonds**.","As a force *type*, permanent dipole–dipole interactions are weaker than hydrogen bonding but stronger than the weak van der Waals (London) forces."]},{"type":"list","items":["In a liquid, however, the **random movement** of the molecules means the dipoles are not always aligned to produce a favourable interaction — sometimes like ends meet and the pair repels.","As a result, **averaged out**, the interaction between permanent dipoles is usually **less** than the interaction between instantaneous and induced dipoles — the **London force is usually the more significant** interaction between molecules."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"warning","title":"Do not dismiss London forces","text":"It is tempting to assume the permanent dipole always dominates. In real liquids the permanent dipoles spend much of their time misaligned, so London forces — acting all the time, between all molecules — are usually the **bigger** contributor to the total attraction."},{"type":"h3","text":"Relative strengths of intermolecular forces"},{"type":"list","items":["Intermolecular forces are **much weaker than intramolecular forces** (the bonds *within* molecules): covalent bond strengths are typically between **200 to 500 kJ mol⁻¹**.","In comparison, the strength of **hydrogen bonds** is about **5 to 40 kJ mol⁻¹**.","**Permanent dipole–dipole interactions** are weaker than hydrogen bonds, and **London forces** are still weaker."]},{"type":"eq","code":"hydrogen bonding  >  permanent dipole–dipole interactions  >  London forces","caption":"The strength order to quote — as force types"},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Forces affect physical properties — not chemical properties","text":"Intermolecular forces are **not strong enough to influence chemical properties**, but they **can affect physical properties**. When a molecular substance boils, only the forces **between** molecules are overcome — the covalent bonds inside each molecule stay intact (7.5). Reacting breaks the bonds; boiling breaks the forces."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6b00b4jfqk58xt2qm7","sectionKey":"hydrogen-bonding","number":"7.2","title":"Hydrogen Bonding in Water, Ammonia and Hydrogen Fluoride","blurb":"The strongest intermolecular force — where it comes from, and how to draw it.","estMinutes":25,"spec":"7.2","specText":"understand the interactions in molecules, such as H₂O, liquid NH₃ and liquid HF, which give rise to hydrogen bonding","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Hydrogen bond","text":"The strong intermolecular attraction between a **lone pair** of electrons on a nitrogen, oxygen or fluorine atom of one molecule and a **hydrogen atom covalently bonded to N, O or F** in another molecule. It is the strongest type of intermolecular force."},{"type":"list","items":["Hydrogen bonding occurs in compounds that have a hydrogen atom covalently attached to one of the **three most electronegative atoms — nitrogen, oxygen and fluorine only**, which has a **lone pair** of electrons.","So hydrogen bonding is found where there is an **O–H**, **N–H** or **F–H** bond — there is a large electronegativity difference between the H and the O, N or F.","The classic examples are **water H₂O**, **ammonia NH₃** and **hydrogen fluoride HF**.","Bonds between hydrogen and these three elements are **extremely polar**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Why the hydrogen bond forms"},{"type":"list","items":["Due to the big difference in electronegativity between hydrogen and F, O or N, a substantial amount of electron density is drawn away from the hydrogen atom, leaving it with a **δ+ charge**.","The hydrogen atom **does not have an inner shell of electrons** — as a result, its nucleus is **exposed** by the shift in electron density and is not screened.","The **lone pair** of electrons on the F, O or N atom of another molecule is attracted to this positive hydrogen — leading to the formation of a **hydrogen bond**.","The interaction is **not just an extreme dipole–dipole attraction**: there is some **partial bond formation** using the lone pair of electrons on the O, N or F atom."]},{"type":"list","items":["Hydrogen bonds are **directional** — the angle between the three atoms involved (X–H···Y) is often **180° or close to it**, which is why hydrogen-bonded structures have definite geometries."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/imf-hbond-geometry.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/imf-hbond-geometry-dark.png","alt":"Hydrogen bonding in water, ammonia and hydrogen fluoride showing lone pairs, partial charges and the dashed hydrogen bond, including the zig-zag chains of solid HF","caption":"Drawing hydrogen bonds: lone pairs shown on N, O and F, the δ+/δ− charges marked, the H-bond dashed. Water offers two lone pairs and two δ+ hydrogens; ammonia offers one lone pair; HF forms zig-zag chains (HF)n in the solid state."},{"type":"h3","text":"Hydrogen bonding through oxygen"},{"type":"list","items":["All compounds containing an **–O–H group** form intermolecular hydrogen bonds.","In water, the hydrogen bond forms between the **oxygen atom** of one water molecule and the **hydrogen atom** of a second water molecule.","**Alcohols** also form intermolecular hydrogen bonds through their –O–H group."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Hydrogen bonding through nitrogen"},{"type":"list","items":["All compounds containing an **–N–H group** can form intermolecular hydrogen bonds.","Examples: **ammonia NH₃**, and organic compounds such as **primary amines**, which have the general formula **RNH₂**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Hydrogen bonding through fluorine"},{"type":"list","items":["The **only** fluorine compound with intermolecular hydrogen bonding is **hydrogen fluoride, HF**.","In the solid state HF molecules are held in **zig-zag chains**, written **(HF)n** — each molecule donates one hydrogen bond through its H and accepts one through a lone pair on its F."]},{"type":"h3","text":"How many hydrogen bonds per molecule?"},{"type":"table","headers":["Molecule","Lone pairs on N/O/F","δ+ H atoms on N/O/F","Hydrogen bonds per molecule"],"rows":[["H₂O","2","2","up to 4"],["NH₃","1","3","on average 2 — one through the lone pair, one through a δ+ hydrogen"],["HF","3","1","2 — one donated, one accepted, giving chains"]]},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"The HF vs H₂O paradox","text":"Hydrogen bond **strength** for HF is greater than for H₂O, yet HF boils lower (293 K vs 373 K). Why? **1)** HF forms two hydrogen bonds per molecule, whereas water can form up to **four** — hydrogen bonding in water is much more extensive. **2)** Not all the hydrogen bonds in HF are broken on vaporisation, since HF is substantially **polymerised even in the gas phase**."},{"type":"list","items":["**Ammonia has the lowest boiling temperature** of the three compounds. The amount of hydrogen bonding in ammonia is limited because each nitrogen atom has only **one lone pair** of electrons — on average an ammonia molecule can form one hydrogen bond using the lone pair on nitrogen, and one hydrogen bond involving one of its δ+ hydrogen atoms."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6c00b6jfqk03raf2aw","sectionKey":"anomalous-water","number":"7.3","title":"Anomalous Properties of Water","blurb":"Two famous quirks of water, both explained by hydrogen bonding.","estMinutes":30,"spec":"7.3","specText":"understand the following anomalous properties of water resulting from hydrogen bonding:\ni   its high melting and boiling temperature when compared with similar molecules\nii   the density of ice compared to that of water","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Water shows two **anomalous** (unexpected) properties, and both are consequences of hydrogen bonding:"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["It has a **relatively high melting and boiling temperature** for a molecule with so few electrons.","The **density of ice** at 0 °C is **less** than that of water at 0 °C."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Anomaly i — the high melting and boiling temperatures"},{"type":"list","items":["The hydrogen bonds between water molecules are relatively strong.","Water therefore has an abnormally **high melting temperature (0 °C, 273 K)** and **boiling temperature (100 °C, 373 K)** at 100 kPa pressure — far higher than the hydrides of the elements around oxygen in the periodic table."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/imf-hydrides-graph.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/imf-hydrides-graph-dark.png","alt":"Boiling temperatures of the hydrides of Groups 14, 15, 16 and 17 across periods 2 to 5, with the anomalously high points of H2O, NH3 and HF highlighted","caption":"The hydride boiling-point graph. Each Group 14–17 series rises steadily as the electron cloud grows — but H₂O, NH₃ and HF sit far above their trends because of hydrogen bonding. Water is the most extreme anomaly of all."},{"type":"list","items":["In **Group 14 (IV) hydrides** the boiling point increases as the electron cloud of the molecule increases and relative molecular mass increases — so the strength of the van der Waals (London) forces increases.","The **anomalously high boiling points of H₂O, NH₃ and HF** are caused by **hydrogen bonding** between these molecules **in addition to** their London forces. The additional forces require more energy to break, and so they have higher boiling points."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Why the anomaly matters so much","text":"Without hydrogen bonding, water would boil at roughly −80 °C by extrapolation of the Group 16 trend (H₂S 213 K → H₂Te 271 K). There would be **no liquid water on Earth** — and no aqueous chemistry, no oceans and no life as we know it."},{"type":"h3","text":"Anomaly ii — the density of ice"},{"type":"list","items":["The molecules in **ice** are arranged in **rings of six**, held together by hydrogen bonds.","In ice there are **large areas of open space inside the rings** — the open, honeycomb lattice holds the molecules further apart than they would prefer to be.","When ice melts, the ring structure is **destroyed** and the average distance between molecules **decreases**, causing an **increase in density**.","So ice is **less dense** than liquid water at the same temperature — which is why ice floats on water."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/imf-ice-lattice.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/imf-ice-lattice-dark.png","alt":"Ice as an open hexagonal lattice of rings of six water molecules with empty space inside the rings, compared with liquid water where the rings have collapsed and molecules sit closer together","caption":"In ice the hydrogen-bonded rings of six lock the molecules into an open lattice. On melting the rings collapse, the molecules pack closer — and the density rises."},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Why the ice anomaly matters","text":"Because ice floats, a freezing lake **cools from the top down**: the ice layer insulates the water beneath, so a pond freezes from the surface inwards and aquatic life survives in the denser 4 °C water below."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6c00b8jfqkm1lm8mpm","sectionKey":"predicting-hydrogen-bonding","number":"7.4","title":"Predicting Hydrogen Bonding","blurb":"A two-question test — then apply it to alcohols, amines, acids and ethers.","estMinutes":15,"spec":"7.4","specText":"be able to predict the presence of hydrogen bonding in molecules analogous to those mentioned in 7.2","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"The specification asks you to predict hydrogen bonding in molecules **analogous to** H₂O, NH₃ and HF. Apply a two-question test to any molecule:"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["**Does the molecule contain an O–H, N–H or F–H bond?** — a hydrogen atom covalently bonded to nitrogen, oxygen or fluorine, so the H carries a strong δ+ charge (a hydrogen-bond **donor**).","**Does the molecule (or its neighbour) carry a lone pair on N, O or F?** — the electron pair that is attracted to the exposed δ+ hydrogen (a hydrogen-bond **acceptor**)."]},{"type":"list","items":["If both are present, a hydrogen bond can form — with the δ+ hydrogen sitting on the lone pair, roughly in line with the bond to the atom it came from.","To hydrogen bond **with itself**, a molecule needs **both** features: a donor O–H/N–H/F–H **and** a lone pair on N, O or F."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Molecule","Key groups","Hydrogen bonds with itself?"],"rows":[["ethanol, CH₃CH₂OH","O–H; 2 lone pairs on O","Yes — O–H···O bonds between molecules"],["methoxymethane, CH₃OCH₃","C–O–C is polar; lone pairs on O — but no O–H","No — only permanent dipole–dipole and London forces between its own molecules"],["ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH","O–H; C=O with lone pairs","Yes — forms hydrogen-bonded **dimers**"],["methylamine, CH₃NH₂","N–H; lone pair on N","Yes — same pattern as ammonia"],["propanone, CH₃COCH₃","C=O with lone pairs — but no O–H","No — though it can accept hydrogen bonds **from water**"],["chloroethane, CH₃CH₂Cl","Polar C–Cl, but H is not bonded to N, O or F","No — chlorine lone pairs are too diffuse to hydrogen bond"]]},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Ethanol vs methoxymethane — same formula, 100 °C apart","text":"Both are C₂H₆O, so both have the same electrons and almost identical London forces. Ethanol boils at **78.5 °C**, methoxymethane at **−24.8 °C**. Ethanol contains an **O–H group**, so its molecules hydrogen bond to each other; methoxymethane’s oxygen is buried between two carbons with **no O–H bond**, so only dipole–dipole and London forces hold its molecules together. Over 100 °C of boiling temperature — all from one hydrogen bond."},{"type":"h3","text":"Dimers: hydrogen bonding between carboxylic acids"},{"type":"list","items":["Molecules of **ethanoic acid dimerise** through hydrogen bonding when dissolved in certain organic (non-aqueous) solvents.","The O–H hydrogen of one molecule sits on a lone pair of the **C=O oxygen** of the other — and vice versa — so **two** hydrogen bonds lock the pair into an eight-membered ring.","Measuring a relative molecular mass of about **120** instead of 60 in such a solvent is evidence for the dimer (CH₃COOH)₂."]},{"type":"eq","code":"2CH₃COOH  ⇌  (CH₃COOH)₂","caption":"Hydrogen-bonded dimer of ethanoic acid, formed in non-aqueous solvents"},{"type":"callout","variant":"warning","title":"Predicting — the examiner’s traps","text":"A polar molecule is **not** automatically hydrogen-bonded: the H must be bonded **directly to N, O or F**. HCl is very polar but cannot hydrogen bond with itself. Equally, a molecule with no O–H/N–H/F–H of its own can still accept hydrogen bonds **from water** — that is why methoxymethane and propanone are water-soluble even though they never hydrogen bond with themselves."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6d00bajfqk5v7jhqtm","sectionKey":"physical-properties","number":"7.5","title":"Intermolecular Forces and Physical Properties","blurb":"Boiling trends of alkanes, alcohols, noble gases and the hydrogen halides — all in terms of forces.","estMinutes":45,"spec":"7.5","specText":"understand, in terms of intermolecular forces, physical properties shown by substances, including:\ni   the trends in boiling temperatures of alkanes with increasing chain length\nii   the effect of branching in the carbon chain on the boiling temperatures of alkanes\niii   the relatively low volatility (higher boiling temperatures) of alcohols compared to alkanes with a similar number of electrons\niv   the trends in boiling temperatures of the hydrogen halides HF to HI","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"From 7.1: intermolecular forces are far too weak to influence **chemical** properties — but they govern the **physical** ones. Every explanation below has the same skeleton: *identify the forces present, compare their strengths, and match the result to the property.*"},{"type":"h3","text":"i — Boiling temperatures of unbranched alkanes"},{"type":"list","items":["The most significant intermolecular attraction between alkane molecules is the **London force**.","The boiling point of alkanes **increases as molecular mass increases**, because:"]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["when molecular mass increases, the **number of electrons per molecule increases** — hence the instantaneous and induced dipoles also increase;","when the chain length increases, the **number of points of contact** between adjacent molecules increases. The more points of contact there are, the greater the overall London force of attraction."]},{"type":"h3","text":"ii — The effect of branching"},{"type":"list","items":["**Branched-chain alkanes have lower boiling temperatures than their unbranched isomers.**","Branching lowers the melting and boiling temperature because **side chains interfere with the packing together of molecules** and reduce the formation of London forces."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Name of alkane","Condensed structural formula","Boiling temperature / K"],"rows":[["pentane","CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃","309"],["2-methylbutane","CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃","301"],["2,2-dimethylpropane","C(CH₃)₄","283"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Same molecular formula (C₅H₁₂), same electrons — the boiling temperature falls as the chain becomes more compact and the molecules make **fewer contact points**."},{"type":"h3","text":"iii — Alcohols compared with alkanes"},{"type":"list","items":["Alcohols contain an **–O–H group** and can therefore form intermolecular **hydrogen bonds** in addition to London forces.","Alcohols have a **higher boiling temperature (lower volatility)** than alkanes with the same number of carbons — or a similar number of electrons — because in alkanes only weak London forces are present, whereas alcohols hydrogen bond between their molecules."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Alkane","Boiling point / °C","Alcohol","Boiling point / °C"],"rows":[["methane","−164","methanol","65"],["ethane","−89","ethanol","79"],["propane","−42","propan-1-ol","97"],["butane","−0.5","butan-1-ol","117"],["pentane","36","pentan-1-ol","138"],["hexane","69","hexan-1-ol","156"]]},{"type":"table","headers":["Alcohol","Enthalpy change of vaporisation / kJ mol⁻¹"],"rows":[["CH₃CH₂OH","38.6"],["CH₃CH₂CH₂OH","47.5"],["CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH","52.4"],["CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂OH","57.0"],["CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂OH","61.6"]]},{"type":"table","headers":["Alkane","Enthalpy change of vaporisation / kJ mol⁻¹"],"rows":[["CH₃CH₂CH₃","15.7"],["CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃","21.0"],["CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃","26.4"],["CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃","31.6"],["CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃","36.6"]]},{"type":"list","items":["The enthalpy change of vaporisation is a measure of the amount of energy required to **completely separate the molecules of a liquid into a gas** at the same temperature — the greater the enthalpy change of vaporisation, the greater the force of attraction between the molecules.","These tables show that the predominant intermolecular force in the first few members of the alcohols is **hydrogen bonding**. However, **London forces predominate as the chain length increases** — the growing non-polar chain contributes more and more."]},{"type":"def","term":"Volatility","text":"A measure of **how easily molecules escape from liquids**. Volatile substances have a **low boiling temperature**."},{"type":"list","items":["The **viscosity** of alcohols also increases as the number of hydrogen bonds per molecule increases: propane-1,2-diol (two –OH groups) is more viscous than propan-1-ol (one), and propane-1,2,3-triol (glycerol, three –OH groups) is more viscous still."]},{"type":"eq","code":"CH₃CH₂CH₂OH  →  CH₃CH(OH)CH₂OH  →  CH₂(OH)CH(OH)CH₂OH","caption":"propan-1-ol → propane-1,2-diol → propane-1,2,3-triol — increasing viscosity as the hydrogen bonding increases"},{"type":"h3","text":"The noble gases"},{"type":"list","items":["Noble gases are **monatomic** — single atoms, with only weak van der Waals (London) forces between them.","From helium to radon, the size of the electron cloud increases. This increases the strength of the London forces between the atoms.","Therefore the boiling temperature **increases down the group**, as more energy is required to overcome the forces (see the table in 7.1: 4.3 K for helium → 211 K for radon)."]},{"type":"h3","text":"iv — Boiling temperatures of the hydrogen halides HF to HI"},{"type":"table","headers":["Hydrogen halide","HF","HCl","HBr","HI"],"rows":[["Boiling temperature / K","293","188","206","238"]]},{"type":"list","items":["**HF is the anomaly.** Its boiling temperature is far above the trend because its molecules are hydrogen bonded — the strongest intermolecular force in the set.","From HF to HCl there is a **large drop**: hydrogen bonding disappears, leaving only permanent dipole–dipole and London forces.","After the drop to HCl there is a **steady upward trend from HCl to HI**. This is because, despite the **decrease in permanent dipole–dipole forces** between the molecules, there is an **increase in the instantaneous dipole–induced dipole (London) forces** due to the increase in the size of the atoms (Br and I hold many more electrons than Cl).","Once again the London forces win out over the permanent dipole — the same conclusion reached in 7.1."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"exam","title":"The four-mark boiling-trend answer","text":"A complete explanation always names the forces and both factors: e.g. *HCl has permanent dipole–dipole forces and London forces; HI boils higher than HCl because I has many more electrons, so the instantaneous dipole–induced dipole forces are stronger — this outweighs the weaker permanent dipole in HI.* Naming only \"stronger intermolecular forces\" scores nothing."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6e00bcjfqkq0a5aweg","sectionKey":"solvents-solubility","number":"7.6","title":"Solvents and Solubility","blurb":"Hydration of ions and hydrogen-bonding alcohols — and why oil floats on water.","estMinutes":35,"spec":"7.6","specText":"understand factors that influence the choice of solvents, including:\ni   water, to dissolve some ionic compounds, in terms of the hydration of the ions\nii   water, to dissolve simple alcohols, in terms of hydrogen bonding\niii   water, as a poor solvent for compounds (to include polar molecules such as halogenoalkane), in terms of inability to form hydrogen bonds\niv   non-aqueous solvents, for compounds that have similar intermolecular forces to those in the solvent","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"What has to happen for a substance to dissolve"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["The **solute particles must be separated from each other**, and then become surrounded by **solvent particles**.","The force of attraction between solute and solvent particles must be **strong enough to overcome** the solvent–solvent forces and the solute–solute forces."]},{"type":"list","items":["So dissolving is a contest of attractions: the new solute–solvent attractions must pay for breaking the old ones."]},{"type":"h3","text":"i — Water dissolving ionic compounds: hydration of the ions"},{"type":"list","items":["In an ionic compound, the positive and negative ions are held in a **lattice structure** by strong electrostatic forces of attraction.","Energy is required to break down the lattice — this is the **lattice energy**.","**Water molecules are polar**: the hydrogen atoms carry a δ+ charge and the oxygen atoms carry a δ− charge.","Attractive forces between the water molecules and the ions in the lattice **break up the lattice** to form individual ions, which spread throughout the water.","The water molecules arrange themselves so that **positive ions are close to the negative oxygen** ends of water, and **negative ions are close to the positive hydrogen** ends."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/imf-nacl-hydration.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/imf-nacl-hydration-dark.png","alt":"Water molecules attacking a sodium chloride lattice; the liberated sodium ion surrounded by water molecules with oxygen ends facing it, and the chloride ion surrounded by water molecules with hydrogen ends facing it","caption":"Hydration of the ions: each Na⁺ is surrounded by water with its δ− oxygen facing in; each Cl⁻ with its δ+ hydrogens facing in. The ion–dipole attractions released as **hydration enthalpy** pay for breaking the lattice."},{"type":"list","items":["This process is known as **hydration**, and the energy released during it is the **hydration enthalpy**.","If the hydration enthalpy is **more than or equal to** the lattice enthalpy, the compound **will dissolve** in water. If the hydration enthalpy is **less than** the lattice enthalpy, the compound will **not dissolve** — the energy released is insufficient to break up the lattice."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"The solubility balance","text":"dissolves when **hydration enthalpy ≥ lattice enthalpy**. The same bookkeeping explains why some ionic compounds are insoluble: their lattices are too strong (or their ions too poorly hydrated) for the water to pay the bill."},{"type":"h3","text":"ii — Water dissolving simple alcohols: hydrogen bonding"},{"type":"list","items":["Alcohols with relatively **short carbon chains dissolve in water**, because the alcohol can form **hydrogen bonds with water** — stronger than the London forces between the carbon chains of the alcohol molecules.","The solubility **gradually decreases with increasing carbon chain length**: the **non-polar hydrocarbon part becomes bigger**, which increases the strength of the London forces between the carbon chains of the alcohol molecules.","More than one **–OH group** in the molecule increases solubility, since **more hydrogen bonds** can be formed between the alcohol and water — compare propane-1,2,3-triol (glycerol), which mixes with water in all proportions, with octan-1-ol, which barely dissolves.","**Carboxylic acids dissolve in a similar way to alcohols** — the –COOH group hydrogen bonds with water just as an –OH group does."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/imf-alcohol-solubility.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/imf-alcohol-solubility-dark.png","alt":"Methanol, butan-1-ol and octan-1-ol with the non-polar hydrocarbon tail shaded, showing solubility falling as the tail grows from one carbon to eight","caption":"The hydrophilic –OH head hydrogen bonds with water; the growing non-polar tail can only offer weak London forces. Methanol mixes with water in all proportions; octan-1-ol is essentially insoluble."},{"type":"h3","text":"iii — Water as a poor solvent: compounds that cannot hydrogen bond with it"},{"type":"list","items":["Non-polar molecules **do not dissolve in water**: the forces between non-polar molecules are much weaker than the hydrogen bonds in water, and the attraction between alkane and water molecules is **not strong enough to disrupt the hydrogen bonding** between water molecules.","So the two substances remain as **separate layers** — oil floats on water.","Many **polar compounds also have limited solubility** in water. Compounds like **halogenoalkanes** cannot disrupt the water structure even though the carbon–halogen bond is polar — because the **bulk of the molecule (the hydrocarbon chain) is not polar**.","**Ethoxyethane (CH₃CH₂OCH₂CH₃)** is polar, yet it is almost totally immiscible with water: the force of attraction between ethoxyethane and water molecules is not large enough to replace the relatively strong hydrogen bonding between water molecules."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"warning","title":"Polar ≠ water-soluble","text":"The specification deliberately includes polar molecules such as halogenoalkanes under \"poor solvents\". A single polar bond in a largely non-polar molecule is not enough — the molecule as a whole must offer attractions **at least as strong** as the water–water hydrogen bonds it must replace."},{"type":"h3","text":"iv — Non-aqueous solvents"},{"type":"list","items":["Two **non-polar liquids will mix completely** — for example hexane and octane.","Both liquids contain weak London forces, and these extend throughout the whole structure when they are mixed: there is no strong solvent–solvent force for the solute to fight."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Like dissolves like"},{"type":"list","items":["**Non-polar solutes** dissolve in **non-polar solvents**.","**Polar solutes** dissolve in **polar solvents** such as water (H₂O) or ethanol (C₂H₅OH).","**Ionic solutes** such as sodium chloride generally dissolve in **polar solvents** — but not in non-polar solvents, which cannot hydrate the ions."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"Choosing a solvent in practice","text":"Match the dominant force: ionic and hydrogen-bonding solutes → water; oily, non-polar solutes → hexane and other non-aqueous solvents; everything in between → ethanol, which offers both an –OH and a hydrocarbon chain. This is exactly how recrystallisation solvents are chosen in the laboratory."}]}],"cards":[{"id":"cmv1eoi6f00bejfqk0r8eymep","ord":1,"front":"How do London forces arise?","back":"Random electron movement creates an instantaneous dipole, which induces a dipole in a neighbouring molecule; the attractions between them are London (dispersion) forces. Present in ALL molecules.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6f00bgjfqkiw0d8qmp","ord":2,"front":"What increases the strength of London forces?","back":"More electrons (larger Mr), longer chain length, and larger surface contact area. Explains why unbranched alkanes boil higher than branched isomers.","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6g00bijfqk3h61u7xa","ord":3,"front":"When does hydrogen bonding occur?","back":"When H is bonded directly to N, O or F — the Hδ⁺ interacts with a lone pair on N/O/F of another molecule. Strongest intermolecular force.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6g00bkjfqkdgmw1cbt","ord":4,"front":"Why is water's boiling point so high compared with H₂S?","back":"Each H₂O can form up to 4 hydrogen bonds (2 H donors + 2 lone pairs); extra energy is needed to break these in addition to other forces. S is much less electronegative, H₂S has no H-bonds.","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6h00bmjfqkly0g9ewf","ord":5,"front":"Why is ice less dense than water?","back":"Hydrogen bonds hold water molecules in an open, hexagonal lattice with large gaps; on melting some H-bonds break and molecules pack closer.","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6h00bojfqkx1w7qu9g","ord":6,"front":"Why do alcohols boil far higher than alkanes of similar Mr?","back":"The O–H group allows hydrogen bonding between alcohol molecules, in addition to London forces; alkanes only have London forces.","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6i00bqjfqkzcwswqv2","ord":7,"front":"Explain the HF → HI boiling point trend.","back":"HF is highest (hydrogen bonding). HF → HCl: large drop as H-bonding disappears. HCl → HI: gradual rise as London forces grow with electron number.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6i00bsjfqkh3hxv27e","ord":8,"front":"What's the difference between bonds and intermolecular forces when a substance boils vs reacts?","back":"Boiling/melting overcomes only INTERMOLECULAR forces — covalent bonds stay intact. Reacting breaks INTRAMOLECULAR (covalent) bonds. Giant covalent substances are the exception: melting means breaking covalent bonds.","tag":"TRAP"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6j00bujfqkn0153akt","ord":9,"front":"Relative strengths: covalent bond vs H-bond, and the three IMF in order?","back":"Covalent bonds 200–500 kJ mol⁻¹; hydrogen bonds only 5–40 kJ mol⁻¹. As force types: hydrogen bonding > permanent dipole–dipole > London forces. BUT averaged over a liquid, London forces are usually the bigger contributor (dipoles misalign).","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6j00bwjfqkgv9ohzc6","ord":10,"front":"The two-question test for hydrogen bonding?","back":"1) Is there an O–H, N–H or F–H bond (δ+ H donor)? 2) Is there a lone pair on N, O or F (acceptor)? Both present → hydrogen bonding. For a substance to H-bond WITH ITSELF it needs both.","tag":"METHOD"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6l00byjfqkzaxxqsfh","ord":11,"front":"Why does HF have stronger H-bonds than water yet boil 80 K lower?","back":"HF forms 2 H-bonds per molecule vs up to 4 for H₂O — hydrogen bonding in water is far more extensive. Also HF is substantially polymerised even in the gas phase, so not all H-bonds break on boiling.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6m00c0jfqkaxfsgf27","ord":12,"front":"Why does NaCl dissolve in water? (energy bookkeeping)","back":"Water is polar: Oδ− faces Na⁺, Hδ+ faces Cl⁻. Ion–dipole attractions release HYDRATION ENTHALPY, which pays for the LATTICE ENERGY. Dissolves when hydration enthalpy ≥ lattice enthalpy.","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6n00c2jfqkdi65bau7","ord":13,"front":"Why is ethanol miscible with water but octan-1-ol barely soluble?","back":"The –OH head hydrogen bonds with water, but the non-polar tail only offers London forces. As the chain grows the tail dominates — octan-1-ol has 8 carbons of non-polar chain that water cannot compensate for.","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi6n00c4jfqkhgwi3srf","ord":14,"front":"State the like-dissolves-like rules.","back":"Non-polar solutes dissolve in non-polar solvents. Polar solutes dissolve in polar solvents (water, ethanol). Ionic solutes dissolve in polar solvents but not non-polar solvents — the ions must be hydrated.","tag":"CORE"}]},{"slug":"redox-and-groups","number":8,"unit":2,"unitCode":"WCH12","unitName":"Energetics, Group Chemistry, Halogenoalkanes and Alcohols","title":"Redox Chemistry and Groups 1, 2 and 7","subtitle":"Oxidation numbers, Group trends, and the halogens that prove them.","summary":"Oxidation numbers and their rules; electron transfer and redox; disproportionation; ionic half-equations; Group 1 and 2 reactivity, oxides, solubility and thermal stability; flame colours and ion tests; titrations and uncertainty; Group 7 oxidising power, displacement, halide reactions and predictions.","estHours":7.0,"sections":[{"id":"cmv1eoi6p00c7jfqksvwr0cc1","sectionKey":"specification-map","number":"8.0","title":"Specification Map","blurb":"Every statement the specification assesses in this topic — use it as your revision checklist.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"8.1 – 8.28","specText":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:","outline":["The full list of assessment statements for Topic 8, worded exactly as the specification words them","Every statement 8.1 – 8.28 has its own section below","Sources: Topic 8A Redox Chemistry, Topic 8B The Elements of Groups 1 and 2, Topic 8C Inorganic Chemistry of Group 7"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Spec","Assessment statement"],"rows":[["8.1","know what is meant by the term 'oxidation number' and understand the rules for assigning oxidation numbers"],["8.2","be able to calculate the oxidation number of elements in compounds and ions, including in peroxides and metal hydrides"],["8.3","be able to indicate the oxidation number of an element in a compound or an ion, using a Roman numeral"],["8.4","be able to write formulae given oxidation numbers"],["8.5","understand oxidation and reduction in terms of electron transfer and changes in oxidation number, and the application of these ideas to reactions of s-block and p-block elements"],["8.6","know that oxidising agents gain electrons and reducing agents lose electrons"],["8.7","understand that a disproportionation reaction involves an element in a single species being simultaneously oxidised and reduced"],["8.8","know that oxidation number is a useful concept in terms of the classification of reactions as redox and as disproportionation"],["8.9","understand that metals, in general, form positive ions by loss of electrons with an increase in oxidation number whereas non-metals, in general, form negative ions by gain of electrons with a decrease in oxidation number"],["8.10","be able to write ionic half-equations and use them to construct full ionic equations"],["8.11","understand reasons for the trend in ionisation energy down Groups 1 and 2"],["8.12","understand reasons for the trend in reactivity of the elements down Group 1 (Li to K) and Group 2 (Mg to Ba)"],["8.13","know the reactions of the elements of Group 1 (Li to K) and Group 2 (Mg to Ba) with oxygen, chlorine and water"],["8.14","know the reactions of:\ni   oxides of Group 1 and 2 elements with water and dilute acid\nii   hydroxides of Group 1 and 2 elements with dilute acid"],["8.15","know the trends in solubility of the hydroxides and sulfates of Group 2 elements"],["8.16","understand the reasons for the trends in thermal stability of the nitrates and the carbonates of the elements in Groups 1 and 2 in terms of the size and charge of the cations involved"],["8.17","understand the formation of characteristic flame colours by Group 1 and 2 compounds in terms of electron transitions"],["8.18","know experimental procedures to show:\ni   patterns in the thermal decomposition of Group 1 and 2 nitrates and carbonates\nii   flame colours in compounds of Group 1 and 2 elements"],["8.19","know reactions, including ionic equations where appropriate, for identifying:\ni   carbonate ions, CO₃²⁻, and hydrogencarbonate ions, HCO₃⁻, using an aqueous acid to form carbon dioxide (and testing the gas with limewater)\nii   sulfate ions, SO₄²⁻, using acidified barium chloride solution\niii   ammonium ions, NH₄⁺, using sodium hydroxide solution and warming to form ammonia (and testing with litmus and HCl fumes)"],["8.20","be able to calculate solution concentrations, in mol dm⁻³ and g dm⁻³, including simple acid–base titrations using the indicators methyl orange and phenolphthalein"],["8.21","CORE PRACTICAL 3 — finding the concentration of a solution of hydrochloric acid"],["8.22","understand how to minimize the sources of measurement uncertainty in volumetric analysis and estimate the overall uncertainty in the calculated result"],["8.23","CORE PRACTICAL 4 — preparation of a standard solution from a solid acid and use it to find the concentration of a solution of sodium hydroxide"],["8.24","understand reasons for the trends for Group 7 elements in:\ni   melting and boiling temperatures and physical state at room temperature\nii   electronegativity\niii   reactivity down the group"],["8.25","understand the trend in reactivity of Group 7 elements in terms of the redox reactions of Cl₂, Br₂ and I₂ with halide ions in aqueous solution"],["8.26","understand, in terms of changes in oxidation number, the reactions of the halogens with Group 1 and 2 metals, with water, with cold dilute aqueous sodium hydroxide and with hot alkali — and reactions analogous to these"],["8.27","understand the reactions of:\ni   solid Group 1 halides with concentrated sulfuric acid\nii   aqueous Cl⁻, Br⁻ and I⁻ with silver nitrate solution and nitric acid, and the solubility of the precipitates in aqueous ammonia\niii   hydrogen halides with ammonia gas and with water"],["8.28","be able to make predictions about fluorine and astatine and their compounds, in terms of knowledge of trends in halogen chemistry"]]},{"type":"p","text":"The topic is built from three sources: **8A Redox Chemistry** (8.1 – 8.10), **8B The Elements of Groups 1 and 2** (8.11 – 8.23) and **8C Inorganic Chemistry of Group 7** (8.24 – 8.28). Each assessment statement has its own section below, in specification order."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6q00c9jfqkyw6eqfpm","sectionKey":"oxidation-numbers","number":"8.1","title":"Oxidation Numbers and the Rules","blurb":"What an oxidation number is — and the rules for assigning one.","estMinutes":14,"spec":"8.1","specText":"know what is meant by the term 'oxidation number' and understand the rules for assigning oxidation numbers","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Oxidation number","text":"The charge on an atom of the element in a compound calculated assuming that all the atoms in the compound are simple monoatomic ions."},{"type":"p","text":"Oxidation numbers are assigned to elements in covalent compounds so that the oxidation number of an atom is the charge it would have if the electrons in each of its bonds belonged to the more electronegative atom."},{"type":"p","text":"Oxidation numbers are used to tell whether oxidation or reduction has taken place."},{"type":"list","items":["In **MgO** the oxidation number of magnesium is **+2** since the charge on the magnesium ion is 2+. Similarly the oxidation number of oxygen is **−2**.","In **SO₂** the oxidation number of sulfur is **+4** because if the compound were fully ionic the sulfur ion would have a charge of 4+. The oxidation number of oxygen is once again **−2**."]},{"type":"p","text":"Here are the rules for determining oxidation number:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Atom","Oxidation number","Example"],"rows":[["Uncombined element","0","O in O₂ = 0"],["Simple monoatomic ion","charge on ion","Na in Na⁺ = +1"],["Group 1, 2 & 3 element in a compound","+1, +2 & +3 respectively","Na = +1; Mg = +2"],["Fluorine in a compound","−1","F in BF₃ = −1"],["Hydrogen in a compound (except in metal hydrides, where it is −1)","+1","H in HCl = +1; H in NaH = −1"],["Oxygen in a compound (except in F₂O, peroxides and superoxides)","−2","O in MgO = −2; O in F₂O = +2; peroxide: −1 (e.g. K₂O₂); superoxide: −½ (e.g. KO₂)"],["Halogens","−1","Cl in HCl = −1"],["Other halogens with fluorine","+1","Cl in ClF = +1"],["Halogens (except fluorine) with oxygen","+1, +3, +5 & +7","Cl in ClO⁻ = +1; Cl in ClO₃⁻ = +5"],["Sum of the oxidation numbers of the elements in a compound or molecule","0","In CO₂ the oxidation number of C is +4 and of O is −2, i.e. +4 + (2 × −2) = 0"],["Sum of the oxidation numbers of the elements in a polyatomic ion","charge on the ion","In SO₄²⁻ the oxidation number of S is +6 and of O is −2, i.e. +6 + (4 × −2) = −2"]]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6r00cbjfqk60e4g6vh","sectionKey":"calculating-oxidation-numbers","number":"8.2","title":"Calculating Oxidation Numbers","blurb":"Calculating oxidation numbers in compounds and ions, including peroxides and metal hydrides.","estMinutes":14,"spec":"8.2","specText":"be able to calculate the oxidation number of elements in compounds and ions, including in peroxides and metal hydrides","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"To calculate an unknown oxidation number, fill in the elements whose oxidation numbers you know from the rules, then use the fact that the oxidation numbers add up to **0** in a compound, or to the **charge on the ion**."},{"type":"worked","title":"Oxidation numbers of chlorine","problem":"Deduce the oxidation number of chlorine in: (a) NaCl  (b) NaClO  (c) NaClO₃.","steps":["(a) Na is +1: +1 + x = 0, so **x = −1**.","(b) Na is +1 and O is −2: +1 + x + (−2) = 0, so **x = +1**.","(c) Na is +1 and 3 O at −2 give −6: +1 + x + (−6) = 0, so **x = +5**."],"answer":"Cl is −1 in NaCl, +1 in NaClO and +5 in NaClO₃."},{"type":"worked","title":"Oxidation numbers of nitrogen","problem":"Deduce the oxidation number of nitrogen in: (a) NH₃  (b) NO₂⁻  (c) NO₃⁻.","steps":["(a) H is +1: x + 3(+1) = 0, so **x = −3**.","(b) O is −2 and the charge on the ion is −1: x + 2(−2) = −1, so **x = +3**.","(c) x + 3(−2) = −1, so **x = +5**."],"answer":"N is −3 in NH₃, +3 in NO₂⁻ and +5 in NO₃⁻."},{"type":"h3","text":"Peroxides, superoxides, metal hydrides and F₂O"},{"type":"worked","title":"Calculating the exceptions","problem":"Find the oxidation number of: (a) O in H₂O₂  (b) O in K₂O₂  (c) O in KO₂  (d) H in NaH  (e) O in F₂O.","steps":["(a) H is +1: 2(+1) + 2x = 0, so **x = −1**.","(b) K is +1: 2(+1) + 2x = 0, so **x = −1** — in a **peroxide** oxygen is −1.","(c) K is +1: +1 + x = 0, so **x = −½** — in a **superoxide** oxygen is −½.","(d) Na is +1: +1 + x = 0, so **x = −1** — in a **metal hydride** hydrogen is −1.","(e) F is −1: x + 2(−1) = 0, so **x = +2**."],"answer":"O is −1 in the peroxides, −½ in the superoxide and +2 in F₂O; H is −1 in the metal hydride."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6r00cdjfqk18mmqxkt","sectionKey":"roman-numerals","number":"8.3","title":"Systematic Names and Roman Numerals","blurb":"Iron(II) or iron(III)? Manganate(VII) — the numbers in the brackets.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"8.3","specText":"be able to indicate the oxidation number of an element in a compound or an ion, using a Roman numeral","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Oxidation numbers are often included in naming compounds and ions that contain elements with varying oxidation numbers. This name is often referred to as a **systematic name**."},{"type":"list","items":["If the **cation** shows variable oxidation state, the Roman numeral is written in brackets following the name of the cation. **Manganese(IV) oxide** shows Mn is in the +4 oxidation state.","If the **anion** contains an element of variable oxidation state, the Roman numeral is written in brackets following the name of the anion. **Potassium manganate(VII)** shows Mn is in the +7 oxidation state.","Names of **oxoanions** end with the suffix **-ate**."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Formula of compound or ion","Relevant oxidation number","Systematic name of compound or ion"],"rows":[["FeCl₂","Fe +2","iron(II) chloride"],["FeCl₃","Fe +3","iron(III) chloride"],["KMnO₄","Mn +7","potassium manganate(VII)"],["K₂MnO₄","Mn +6","potassium manganate(VI)"],["CrO₄²⁻","Cr +6","chromate(VI)"],["Cr₂O₇²⁻","Cr +6","chromate(VI)"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Sodium and magnesium have only one oxidation number in their compounds (+1 and +2 respectively), so it is not necessary to refer to sodium chloride as sodium(I) chloride or magnesium oxide as magnesium(II) oxide."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6s00cfjfqkar6irva5","sectionKey":"formulae-from-oxidation-numbers","number":"8.4","title":"Writing Formulae from Oxidation Numbers","blurb":"Read the name, balance the charges, write the formula.","estMinutes":8,"spec":"8.4","specText":"be able to write formulae given oxidation numbers","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"It is equally important to be able to work out the formula of the compound or ion by looking at the systematic name with its oxidation number: take the oxidation numbers from the name, then choose the numbers of each ion that make the total charge zero."},{"type":"worked","title":"Formulae of the iron sulfates","problem":"Deduce the formula of (a) iron(II) sulfate  (b) iron(III) sulfate.","steps":["(a) Iron(II) is Fe²⁺ and sulfate is SO₄²⁻. One of each balances the charges (+2 and −2): **FeSO₄**.","(b) Iron(III) is Fe³⁺. Two Fe³⁺ (+6) balance three SO₄²⁻ (−6): **Fe₂(SO₄)₃**."],"answer":"FeSO₄ and Fe₂(SO₄)₃."},{"type":"table","headers":["Systematic name","Ions involved","Formula"],"rows":[["copper(I) oxide","Cu⁺ and O²⁻","Cu₂O"],["copper(II) oxide","Cu²⁺ and O²⁻","CuO"],["chromium(III) sulfate(VI)","Cr³⁺ and SO₄²⁻","Cr₂(SO₄)₃"],["lead(IV) iodide","Pb⁴⁺ and I⁻","PbI₄"],["cobalt(III) nitrate(V)","Co³⁺ and NO₃⁻","Co(NO₃)₃"]]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6t00chjfqkpl99vmnm","sectionKey":"electron-transfer","number":"8.5","title":"Oxidation, Reduction and Electron Transfer","blurb":"From oxygen transfer to electron transfer — and to changes in oxidation number.","estMinutes":14,"spec":"8.5","specText":"understand oxidation and reduction in terms of electron transfer and changes in oxidation number, and the application of these ideas to reactions of s-block and p-block elements","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Background to oxidation and reduction"},{"type":"p","text":"'Oxidation' is known as the addition of oxygen and 'reduction' is known as the removal of oxygen."},{"type":"p","text":"An example of such a reaction is the reaction between iron(III) oxide and carbon monoxide that takes place in the blast furnace."},{"type":"eq","code":"Fe₂O₃  +  3CO  →  2Fe  +  3CO₂","caption":"Iron(III) oxide loses oxygen (reduction); carbon monoxide gains oxygen (oxidation)."},{"type":"p","text":"Oxidation can also be described as removal of hydrogen and reduction can be described as addition of hydrogen."},{"type":"p","text":"An example of such a reaction is the reaction between chlorine and hydrogen sulfide."},{"type":"eq","code":"H₂S  +  Cl₂  →  S  +  2HCl","caption":"H₂S loses hydrogen (oxidation); Cl₂ gains hydrogen (reduction)."},{"type":"h3","text":"Oxidation and reduction in terms of electron loss and gain"},{"type":"p","text":"Oxidation and reduction involve the transfer of electrons."},{"type":"p","text":"When magnesium burns in oxygen it forms magnesium oxide."},{"type":"eq","code":"2Mg  +  O₂  →  2MgO","caption":"The reaction results in the formation of Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ ions."},{"type":"eq","code":"Mg  →  Mg²⁺  +  2e⁻","caption":"Each of the two magnesium atoms has lost two electrons."},{"type":"eq","code":"O₂  +  4e⁻  →  2O²⁻","caption":"The oxygen molecule has gained four electrons to become oxide ions."},{"type":"p","text":"The magnesium has been **oxidised** because it has **lost** electrons. Oxygen has been **reduced** because it has **gained** electrons."},{"type":"p","text":"We now have new definitions of oxidation and reduction:"},{"type":"def","term":"Oxidation","text":"Oxidation is the **loss** of electrons."},{"type":"def","term":"Reduction","text":"Reduction is the **gain** of electrons."},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"OIL RIG","text":"These definitions are easily remembered using the mnemonic **OIL RIG**: **O**xidation **I**s **L**oss (of electrons), **R**eduction **I**s **G**ain (of electrons)."},{"type":"h3","text":"Redox reaction"},{"type":"list","items":["A redox reaction is a chemical reaction in which oxidation and reduction occur at the same time.","Electrons are transferred in such reactions.","The reaction between zinc and copper(II) sulfate solution is an example of a redox reaction."]},{"type":"p","text":"In the following reaction, zinc metal gives up electrons:"},{"type":"eq","code":"Zn(s)  →  Zn²⁺(aq)  +  2e⁻","caption":"Oxidation"},{"type":"p","text":"The electrons are then taken up by the copper ions:"},{"type":"eq","code":"Cu²⁺(aq)  +  2e⁻  →  Cu(s)","caption":"Reduction"},{"type":"eq","code":"Zn(s)  +  Cu²⁺(aq)  →  Zn²⁺(aq)  +  Cu(s)","caption":"The two half-equations added together — the electrons cancel."},{"type":"p","text":"The overall equation for the reaction is:"},{"type":"eq","code":"Zn(s)  +  CuSO₄(aq)  →  ZnSO₄(aq)  +  Cu(s)","caption":""},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/redox-electron-transfer.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/redox-electron-transfer-dark.png","alt":"Zinc atoms transferring electrons to copper(II) ions, with the oxidation and reduction half-equations and the overall ionic equation","caption":"Electron transfer in the reaction between zinc and copper(II) sulfate solution."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6t00cjjfqkw2dh1rou","sectionKey":"oxidising-reducing-agents","number":"8.6","title":"Oxidising and Reducing Agents","blurb":"Oxidising agents gain electrons; reducing agents lose electrons.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"8.6","specText":"know that oxidising agents gain electrons and reducing agents lose electrons","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"list","items":["An **oxidising agent** is a species (atom, molecule or ion) that oxidises another species by removing one or more electrons and itself gets reduced.","Non-metals are oxidising agents, e.g. F₂, Cl₂, O₂.","A **reducing agent** is a species that reduces another species by giving it one or more electrons and itself gets oxidised.","Metals are reducing agents, e.g. Na, Fe, Zn."]},{"type":"def","term":"Oxidising agent","text":"It is an **electron acceptor**. It oxidises another substance. The oxidation number of the element in it **decreases**."},{"type":"def","term":"Reducing agent","text":"It is an **electron donor**. It reduces another substance. The oxidation number of the element in it **increases**."},{"type":"p","text":"In the reaction between chlorine and potassium bromide:"},{"type":"eq","code":"Cl₂  +  2KBr  →  2KCl  +  Br₂","caption":"Cl₂ is reduced (0 → −1) — the oxidising agent. Br⁻ is oxidised (−1 → 0) — the reducing agent."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/redox-agents-map.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/redox-agents-map-dark.png","alt":"The reaction of chlorine with potassium bromide labelled with oxidation numbers, showing chlorine as the oxidising agent and bromide as the reducing agent","caption":"Cl₂ gains the electrons (oxidising agent, itself reduced); Br⁻ gives the electrons (reducing agent, itself oxidised)."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6u00cljfqkipebxl7k","sectionKey":"disproportionation","number":"8.7","title":"Disproportionation","blurb":"One element, one species — oxidised and reduced at the same time.","estMinutes":12,"spec":"8.7","specText":"understand that a disproportionation reaction involves an element in a single species being simultaneously oxidised and reduced","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Disproportionation","text":"A disproportionation reaction takes place when the same element is both oxidised and reduced **simultaneously**."},{"type":"p","text":"Example: when chlorine gas is passed into aqueous sodium hydroxide, a disproportionation reaction takes place. One atom of chlorine is oxidised and the other is reduced."},{"type":"eq","code":"2NaOH  +  Cl₂  →  NaOCl  +  NaCl  +  H₂O","caption":"Cl: 0 → +1 in NaOCl (oxidised); Cl: 0 → −1 in NaCl (reduced)."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/redox-disproportionation.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/redox-disproportionation-dark.png","alt":"Disproportionation of chlorine in aqueous sodium hydroxide, with one chlorine atom oxidised from 0 to +1 and the other reduced from 0 to −1","caption":"One atom of chlorine is oxidised and the other is reduced."},{"type":"worked","title":"Heating potassium bromate(I)","problem":"When potassium bromate(I) is heated it decomposes: 3KOBr → 2KBr + KBrO₃. Using the oxidation numbers of the element involved, explain why this is a disproportionation reaction.","steps":["Br in KOBr is **+1**; Br in KBr is **−1**; Br in KBrO₃ is **+5**.","Bromine starts at +1 in the single species KOBr and finishes at −1 and +5.","Bromine has been reduced (+1 → −1) and oxidised (+1 → +5) at the same time."],"answer":"The same element (Br), in a single species (KOBr), is simultaneously oxidised and reduced — disproportionation."},{"type":"worked","title":"Decomposition of hydrogen peroxide","problem":"Is the decomposition 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂ a disproportionation reaction? Justify your answer.","steps":["O in H₂O₂ is **−1** (a peroxide); O in H₂O is **−2**; O in O₂ is **0**.","Oxygen starts at −1 in the single species H₂O₂ and finishes at −2 and 0.","Oxygen has been reduced (−1 → −2) and oxidised (−1 → 0) at the same time."],"answer":"Yes — oxygen in H₂O₂ is simultaneously oxidised and reduced, so the decomposition is a disproportionation reaction."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6v00cnjfqkq5maytop","sectionKey":"classifying-reactions","number":"8.8","title":"Classifying Reactions with Oxidation Numbers","blurb":"Assign the numbers, look for changes, name the reaction.","estMinutes":8,"spec":"8.8","specText":"know that oxidation number is a useful concept in terms of the classification of reactions as redox and as disproportionation","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Using oxidation numbers to classify reactions"},{"type":"p","text":"Consider the following ionic half-equations:"},{"type":"eq","code":"Zn(s)  →  Zn²⁺(aq)  +  2e⁻","caption":""},{"type":"eq","code":"Fe²⁺(aq)  →  Fe³⁺(aq)  +  e⁻","caption":""},{"type":"eq","code":"2I⁻(aq)  →  I₂(aq)  +  2e⁻","caption":""},{"type":"p","text":"Electrons have been lost, so the reactions are oxidations. The oxidation number of the element has increased in each case: Zn from **0 to +2**, Fe from **+2 to +3**, I from **−1 to 0**."},{"type":"p","text":"Another definition of oxidation and reduction:"},{"type":"list","items":["**Oxidation** occurs when there is an **increase** in oxidation number.","**Reduction** occurs when there is a **decrease** in oxidation number."]},{"type":"p","text":"Now consider the following ionic half-equations:"},{"type":"eq","code":"Cl₂(aq)  +  2e⁻  →  2Cl⁻(aq)","caption":"Cl changes from 0 to −1."},{"type":"eq","code":"MnO₄⁻(aq)  +  8H⁺(aq)  +  5e⁻  →  Mn²⁺(aq)  +  4H₂O(l)","caption":"Mn changes from +7 to +2."},{"type":"eq","code":"2H₂O(l)  +  2e⁻  →  2OH⁻(aq)  +  H₂(g)","caption":"H changes from +1 to 0."},{"type":"p","text":"In each case the oxidation number of one of the elements involved has decreased, so reduction has taken place."},{"type":"worked","title":"Redox or not?","problem":"Use oxidation numbers to show whether the following reactions are examples of redox reactions: (a) H₂S(g) + Cl₂(g) → 2HCl(g) + S(s)  (b) NaOH(aq) + HCl(aq) → NaCl(aq) + H₂O(l)  (c) 2NaOH(aq) + Cl₂(aq) → NaCl(aq) + NaClO(aq) + H₂O(l).","steps":["(a) Cl: 0 → −1 (decrease, reduced); S: −2 → 0 (increase, oxidised). Oxidation and reduction occur at the same time → **redox**.","(b) Na is +1, O is −2, H is +1, Cl is −1 — no element changes its oxidation number → **not redox**.","(c) Cl: 0 → −1 in NaCl and 0 → +1 in NaOCl. The same element is oxidised and reduced → **redox, and a disproportionation**."],"answer":"(a) redox; (b) not redox; (c) redox — and a disproportionation."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6v00cpjfqkt0ixczth","sectionKey":"metals-nonmetals","number":"8.9","title":"Metals, Non-metals and Oxidation Number","blurb":"Metals form positive ions by electron loss; non-metals form negative ions by electron gain.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"8.9","specText":"understand that metals, in general, form positive ions by loss of electrons with an increase in oxidation number whereas non-metals, in general, form negative ions by gain of electrons with a decrease in oxidation number","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Reactions of s-block elements"},{"type":"p","text":"Reactive metals in groups 1 and 2 lose electrons when they react."},{"type":"table","headers":["Element","Electrons lost","Half equation","Change in oxidation number"],"rows":[["Sodium (Na) — Group 1","1","Na → Na⁺ + e⁻","increase in oxidation number from 0 to +1"],["Magnesium (Mg) — Group 2","2","Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻","increase in oxidation number from 0 to +2"]]},{"type":"p","text":"All group 1 and 2 elements undergo oxidation."},{"type":"h3","text":"Reactions of p-block elements"},{"type":"p","text":"Predictions about the reactions of p-block elements can be made in terms of redox reactions. However it is not as straightforward as for s-block elements, because the p-block contains elements with different characteristics."},{"type":"list","items":["The group 3 element **aluminium** is a metal with reactivity nearly as high as magnesium, so it reacts in a similar way: Al → Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ — an **increase** in oxidation number from 0 to +3.","The group 7 element **fluorine** is the most reactive halogen: F₂ + 2e⁻ → 2F⁻ — a **decrease** in oxidation number from 0 to −1.","**Nitrogen** is a p-block element in group 5, with an electronegativity higher than aluminium but lower than fluorine. The change in oxidation number for nitrogen therefore depends on which element it reacts with."]},{"type":"p","text":"Nitrogen reacts with the group 1 metal sodium: 6Na + N₂ → 2Na₃N. In this redox reaction the oxidation number of sodium increases from 0 to +1 and that of nitrogen decreases from 0 to −3."},{"type":"p","text":"Now consider the reaction of nitrogen with fluorine: N₂ + 3F₂ → 2NF₃. The oxidation number of fluorine decreases from 0 to −1, but this time the oxidation number of nitrogen increases from 0 to +3."},{"type":"eq","code":"6Na  +  N₂  →  2Na₃N          N₂  +  3F₂  →  2NF₃","caption":"With sodium, nitrogen is reduced (0 → −3); with fluorine, nitrogen is oxidised (0 → +3)."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6w00crjfqkosls77ty","sectionKey":"half-equations","number":"8.10","title":"Ionic Half-Equations and Full Ionic Equations","blurb":"Balance atoms, balance charge with electrons, then combine — in acid or alkali.","estMinutes":30,"spec":"8.10","specText":"be able to write ionic half-equations and use them to construct full ionic equations","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"A half equation represents either an oxidation or a reduction."},{"type":"list","items":["**Oxidation** half equations have electrons on the **right hand side**: X → X⁺ + 1e⁻.","**Reduction** half equations have electrons on the **left hand side**: X + 1e⁻ → X⁻."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Balancing simple half equations"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Write down the formula of the reactants and products.","Balance the atoms.","Add electrons to the right hand side if it is oxidation, and to the left hand side if it is reduction.","Change the number of electrons to balance the charge on both sides of the equation."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Step","Iron(II) being oxidised to iron(III)","Chlorine being reduced to chloride"],"rows":[["1","Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺","Cl₂ → Cl⁻"],["2","Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ (atom already balanced)","Cl₂ → 2Cl⁻"],["3","Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻","Cl₂ + e⁻ → 2Cl⁻"],["4","Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻ (already balanced)","Cl₂ + 2e⁻ → 2Cl⁻"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Balancing half equations in acidic medium"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Write down the formula of the reactants and products.","Balance the atoms other than oxygen and hydrogen.","Balance oxygen atoms by adding H₂O to the side which requires oxygen atoms.","Balance hydrogen atoms by adding H⁺ to the side which requires hydrogen atoms.","Add electrons to balance the charge on both sides of the equation."]},{"type":"p","text":"Example: MnO₄⁻ being reduced to Mn²⁺ in acidic medium."},{"type":"table","headers":["Step","Half-equation"],"rows":[["1","MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺"],["2","MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺ (Mn balanced)"],["3","MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O"],["4","MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O"],["5","MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O — +7 on the left side and +2 on the right, so the charge is balanced by adding 5e⁻ to the left side"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Balancing half equations in alkaline medium"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Write down the formula of the reactants and products.","Balance the atoms other than oxygen and hydrogen.","Add OH⁻ to the side which requires oxygen atoms, and double the number of OH⁻ to the required number of oxygen atoms.","Balance hydrogen atoms by adding H₂O to the side which requires hydrogen atoms.","Add electrons to balance the charge on both sides of the equation."]},{"type":"p","text":"Example: MnO₄⁻ being reduced to Mn²⁺ in alkaline medium."},{"type":"table","headers":["Step","Half-equation"],"rows":[["1","MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺"],["2","MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺ (Mn balanced)"],["3","MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 8OH⁻ — 4 oxygen atoms on the left side, so 4 × 2 = 8 OH⁻"],["4","MnO₄⁻ + 4H₂O → Mn²⁺ + 8OH⁻"],["5","MnO₄⁻ + 4H₂O + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 8OH⁻ — −1 on the left side and −6 on the right, so the charge is balanced by adding 5e⁻ to the left side"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Writing balanced overall equations"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Write the balanced half equation for oxidation and for reduction.","Multiply the equations so that the number of electrons in each is the same.","Add the equations and cancel out the electrons on either side of the equation.","If necessary, cancel out any other species which appear on both sides."]},{"type":"p","text":"Example: the reaction between manganate(VII) and iron(II)."},{"type":"table","headers":["Step","Equations"],"rows":[["1","Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻ (oxidation) · MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O (reduction)"],["2","5Fe²⁺ → 5Fe³⁺ + 5e⁻ (multiply by 5) · MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O (multiply by 1)"],["3","5Fe²⁺ + MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → 5Fe³⁺ + 5e⁻ + Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O (add the equations, then cancel the electrons)"]]},{"type":"eq","code":"5Fe²⁺  +  MnO₄⁻  +  8H⁺  →  5Fe³⁺  +  Mn²⁺  +  4H₂O","caption":"The overall equation — the electrons have cancelled."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6x00ctjfqkbcwdugg6","sectionKey":"group1-2-ionisation-energy","number":"8.11","title":"Ionisation Energy Trends in Groups 1 and 2","blurb":"The Group 1 and 2 elements, their configurations, atomic radii and the fall in ionisation energy down the group.","estMinutes":12,"spec":"8.11","specText":"understand reasons for the trend in ionisation energy down Groups 1 and 2","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Introduction to the Group 1 and 2 elements"},{"type":"list","items":["The **alkali metals** and **alkaline-earth metals** are members of Group I and Group II, respectively, in the periodic table.","They are **solids at room temperature**.","The pure elements in these groups (except Francium and Radium) appear as **bright silvery solids**. However, when exposed to air, they combine with oxygen to form oxides as surface layers, which makes them appear dull.","They are **s-block elements** since their valence electrons are in s orbitals.","All Group I metals have **one outermost s-electron**, whereas Group II metals have **two** outermost s-electrons. These electrons are situated much further from the nucleus than all other electrons in the metals, so they are only weakly held by the positive nucleus. As a result, Group I and II elements readily lose their outermost s-electrons to form stable ions with **noble gas structures**."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Group I — The alkali metals","Group II — The alkaline-earth metals"],"rows":[["Lithium Li","Beryllium Be"],["Sodium Na","Magnesium Mg"],["Potassium K","Calcium Ca"],["Rubidium Rb","Strontium Sr"],["Caesium Cs","Barium Ba"]]},{"type":"table","headers":["Group I","Electronic configuration","Group II","Electronic configuration"],"rows":[["Li Lithium","[He]2s¹","Be Beryllium","[He]2s²"],["Na Sodium","[Ne]3s¹","Mg Magnesium","[Ne]3s²"],["K Potassium","[Ar]4s¹","Ca Calcium","[Ar]4s²"],["Rb Rubidium","[Kr]5s¹","Sr Strontium","[Kr]5s²"],["Cs Caesium","[Xe]6s¹","Ba Barium","[Xe]6s²"],["*Fr Francium","[Rn]7s¹","*Ra Radium","[Rn]7s²"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Atomic radius"},{"type":"list","items":["Atomic radius **increases** down the Group I and II as the outer most electrons are further away from the nuclei.","Atomic radius of Group II < Group I."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Group 1 element","Atomic radius / nm","Group II element","Atomic radius / nm"],"rows":[["Li","0.152","Be","0.112"],["Na","0.186","Mg","0.160"],["K","0.231","Ca","0.197"],["Rb","0.244","Sr","0.215"],["Cs","0.262","Ba","0.224"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Ionisation energy"},{"type":"list","items":["**Ionisation energy decreases** down the Group I and II.","Down the group, the atomic size increases and the number of shells increases.","Distance between the nucleus and the outer electrons increases.","The outer electrons are more shielded by the electrons in the inner shells.","The effect of the increase in nuclear charge is outweighed by the increase in shielding.","Therefore, the outer electrons are less attracted by the nucleus.","Hence, less energy is required to remove the electrons."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Group I element","1st IE / kJ mol⁻¹","2nd IE / kJ mol⁻¹","Group II element","1st IE / kJ mol⁻¹","2nd IE / kJ mol⁻¹","1st and 2nd IE / kJ mol⁻¹"],"rows":[["Li","519","7 300","Be","900","1 757","2 657"],["Na","494","4 560","Mg","738","1 451","2 189"],["K","418","3 070","Ca","590","1 145","1 735"],["Rb","402","2 370","Sr","550","1 064","1 614"],["Cs","376","2 420","Ba","503","965","1 468"]]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/group12-trends.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/group12-trends-dark.png","alt":"Paired trend arrows for Groups 1 and 2: atomic radius and reactivity increasing, ionisation energy decreasing down the group, with first-ionisation-energy data anchors","caption":"Down the group the outer electron is held more weakly — radius and reactivity rise, ionisation energy falls."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6x00cvjfqkymqj5lmc","sectionKey":"group1-2-reactivity","number":"8.12","title":"Reactivity Trends in Groups 1 and 2","blurb":"Reactivity increases down the group — and Group I is more reactive than Group II.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"8.12","specText":"understand reasons for the trend in reactivity of the elements down Group 1 (Li to K) and Group 2 (Mg to Ba)","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Reactivity"},{"type":"list","items":["The reactivity of elements **increases down the group** because of the tendency of elements to lose electron increases down the group. This is because ionization energy decreases, meaning **less energy is required to form the cations**.","**Group I elements are more reactive than Group II** because Group I elements can lose electrons easily and are more metallic."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6y00cxjfqkr492wnhz","sectionKey":"group1-2-reactions","number":"8.13","title":"Reactions with Oxygen, Chlorine and Water","blurb":"Reactions of the Group 1 and 2 elements with oxygen, chlorine and water, with the observations for each metal.","estMinutes":24,"spec":"8.13","specText":"know the reactions of the elements of Group 1 (Li to K) and Group 2 (Mg to Ba) with oxygen, chlorine and water","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Oxygen"},{"type":"list","items":["S-block elements react readily with oxygen to form **solid metal oxides**."]},{"type":"table","headers":["","Group I metal with oxygen","Group II metal with oxygen"],"rows":[["General equation","4M(s) + O₂(g) → 2M₂O(s)","2M(s) + O₂(g) → 2MO(s)"],["Products","products are oxides containing M⁺ and O²⁻ ions","products are oxides containing M²⁺ and O²⁻ ions"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Chlorine"},{"type":"list","items":["All s-block elements react with chlorine to form **ionic chlorides** except beryllium.","The ionic chlorides of Group I and II are **soluble in water**."]},{"type":"table","headers":["","Group I metal with chlorine","Group II metal with chlorine"],"rows":[["General equation","2M(s) + Cl₂(g) → 2MCl(s)","M(s) + Cl₂(g) → MCl₂(s)"],["Products","products are chlorides containing M⁺ and Cl⁻ ions","products are chlorides containing M²⁺ and Cl⁻ ions"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Water"},{"type":"list","items":["S-block elements react with water to produce **alkaline metal hydroxide** and **hydrogen**.","**Group 1:** the general equation is"]},{"type":"eq","code":"2M(s)  +  2H₂O(l)  →  2MOH(aq)  +  H₂(g)","caption":"Group 1 metals with water"},{"type":"h3","text":"Lithium"},{"type":"list","items":["When a small piece of lithium is added to a beaker or trough of water, it **floats** on the surface of the water (density is half of that of water), gently fizzing and giving off hydrogen.","It gradually reacts and disappears forming a colorless solution of lithium hydroxide."]},{"type":"eq","code":"2Li(s)  +  2H₂O(l)  →  2LiOH(aq)  +  H₂(g)","caption":"Lithium with cold water"},{"type":"h3","text":"Sodium"},{"type":"list","items":["When sodium is added to water, the sodium **melts to form a ball** that moves around on the surface. It fizzes rapidly, and the hydrogen produced may burn with an **orange flame** (the colour is due to the contamination of hydrogen flame with sodium compounds) before the sodium disappears.","The flame is caused by the heat of the reaction igniting the hydrogen."]},{"type":"eq","code":"2Na(s)  +  2H₂O(l)  →  2NaOH(aq)  +  H₂(g)","caption":"Sodium with cold water"},{"type":"h3","text":"Potassium"},{"type":"list","items":["Potassium behaves rather like sodium except that the reaction is **faster** and enough heat is given off to set light to the hydrogen.","When potassium is added to water, the metal melts and floats. It moves around very quickly on the surface of the water. The hydrogen ignites instantly. The metal is also set on fire, with sparks and a **lilac flame**."]},{"type":"eq","code":"2K(s)  +  2H₂O(l)  →  2KOH(aq)  +  H₂(g)","caption":"Potassium with cold water"},{"type":"list","items":["**Group II:** the general equation for all these reactions is"]},{"type":"eq","code":"M(s)  +  2H₂O(l)  →  M(OH)₂(aq)  +  H₂(g)","caption":"Group 2 metals with cold water"},{"type":"h3","text":"Magnesium"},{"type":"list","items":["Reacts **very slowly** with cold water to form magnesium hydroxide and hydrogen gas."]},{"type":"eq","code":"Mg(s)  +  2H₂O(l)  →  Mg(OH)₂(s)  +  H₂(g)","caption":"Magnesium with cold water"},{"type":"h3","text":"Magnesium and steam"},{"type":"list","items":["Magnesium reacts differently when heated in steam.","It rapidly forms magnesium oxide (a white solid) and hydrogen gas in a **vigorous** reaction. The equation for the reaction is"]},{"type":"eq","code":"Mg(s)  +  H₂O(g)  →  MgO(s)  +  H₂(g)","caption":"Magnesium with steam"},{"type":"list","items":["The hydrogen formed is burned as it leaves the tube. This is for safety reasons to prevent the escape of highly flammable gas into the laboratory."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Calcium, Strontium and Barium"},{"type":"list","items":["React **rapidly** with cold water to form metal hydroxide and hydrogen gas."]},{"type":"eq","code":"Ca(s)  +  2H₂O(l)  →  Ca(OH)₂(s)  +  H₂(g)","caption":"Calcium with cold water"},{"type":"eq","code":"Sr(s)  +  2H₂O(l)  →  Sr(OH)₂(aq)  +  H₂(g)          Ba(s)  +  2H₂O(l)  →  Ba(OH)₂(aq)  +  H₂(g)","caption":"Strontium and barium with cold water"}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6z00czjfqk781ajzry","sectionKey":"oxides-hydroxides-acids","number":"8.14","title":"Oxides and Hydroxides with Water and Acids","blurb":"Basic oxides (beryllium oxide is amphoteric) and their reactions with water and with dilute acids.","estMinutes":14,"spec":"8.14","specText":"know the reactions of:\ni   oxides of Group 1 and 2 elements with water and dilute acid\nii   hydroxides of Group 1 and 2 elements with dilute acid","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Oxides"},{"type":"list","items":["Group one and two oxides are classified as **basic oxides** (beryllium oxide/hydroxide is **amphoteric**).","They react with water to form **alkaline hydroxides**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Reactions of Group I oxides with water"},{"type":"table","headers":["Group I oxide","Nature of oxide","Reaction with water"],"rows":[["M₂O (in general)","Basic","M₂O(s) + H₂O(l) → 2MOH(aq)"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Reactions of Group II oxides with water"},{"type":"table","headers":["Group II oxide","Nature of oxide","Reaction with water"],"rows":[["MO (in general)","Basic","MO(s) + H₂O(l) → M(OH)₂(aq)"],["MgO(s)","Basic","Reacts very slowly and incompletely to form a slightly alkaline suspension of Mg(OH)₂. MgO(s) + H₂O(l) → Mg(OH)₂(s) (white ppt)"],["CaO(s)","Basic","CaO(s) + H₂O(l) → Ca(OH)₂(s) (cloudy suspension)"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Hydroxides and oxides with acids"},{"type":"list","items":["All of the **Group 1 and 2 oxides and hydroxides** behave as **bases** and **react with acids** to form **salts and water**.","These reactions can be described as **neutralization** reactions.","During the reactions, the only observations to be made are that a **white solid** reacts to form a **colourless solution**.","The reactions are highly **exothermic**."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi6z00d1jfqkxydsrui3","sectionKey":"group2-solubility","number":"8.15","title":"Solubility Trends of Group 2 Hydroxides and Sulfates","blurb":"Solubility of Group 2 hydroxides increases down the group; sulfates decreases.","estMinutes":12,"spec":"8.15","specText":"know the trends in solubility of the hydroxides and sulfates of Group 2 elements","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Solubility of Group II hydroxides and sulphates"},{"type":"list","items":["Solubility of **group 2 hydroxides increases** down the group.","Solubility of **group 2 sulphates decreases** down the group."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Solubility of hydroxides","","Solubility of sulphates",""],"rows":[["Be(OH)₂","insoluble","BeSO₄","very soluble"],["Mg(OH)₂","sparingly soluble","MgSO₄","quite soluble"],["Ca(OH)₂","slightly soluble","CaSO₄","slightly soluble"],["Sr(OH)₂","quite soluble","SrSO₄","sparingly soluble"],["Ba(OH)₂","very soluble","BaSO₄","insoluble"]]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/group2-solubility.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/group2-solubility-dark.png","alt":"Opposing solubility trends for Group 2 hydroxides and sulfates from beryllium to barium","caption":"Hydroxide solubility increases Be(OH)₂ → Ba(OH)₂ down the group; sulfate solubility decreases BeSO₄ → BaSO₄."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi7000d3jfqkmoeianr5","sectionKey":"thermal-stability","number":"8.16","title":"Thermal Stability of Nitrates and Carbonates","blurb":"How cation size and charge control the thermal stability of the nitrates and carbonates.","estMinutes":22,"spec":"8.16","specText":"understand the reasons for the trends in thermal stability of the nitrates and the carbonates of the elements in Groups 1 and 2 in terms of the size and charge of the cations involved","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Thermal stability"},{"type":"list","items":["**Thermal stability** is a term that indicates how stable a compound is when it is heated. If it does not decompose at all, it is very thermally stable. If decompose as much as possible then it is not thermally stable.","The stabilities of the nitrate and carbonate anions are influenced by the **charge and size of the cations** present. Smaller and more highly charged cations affect these anions more.","In general:","Cations with small size and high charge has a higher **polarizing power**.","So they can **polarize the anion** more effectively, which weakens the bonds in anions.","This assists decomposition and makes the compound **less stable** to heat."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Trends in thermal stability of Group I and II nitrates and carbonates"},{"type":"list","items":["**Thermal stability increases down the group.** This is because:","Size of the cation increases and charge density decreases.","So polarizing power decreases.","The cation cannot polarize the anion effectively.","So the bonds in the anion are not weakened.","As the degree of covalency decreases, the strength of the ionic lattice increases.","Therefore, more energy is required to break the lattice structure.","**Thermal stability decreases across the period from Group I to II.** This is because:","Group 2 cations are smaller in size than group 1 cations.","Charge on group 2 cations are higher.","So, polarizing power of group 2 cations are larger.","They can polarize the anion more effectively, weakening the bonds in it.","So less energy is required to break the bonds."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Group 1 element","Charge on ion","Ionic radius / nm","Group 2 element","Charge on ion","Ionic radius / nm"],"rows":[["Lithium","+1","0.074","Beryllium","+2","0.027"],["Sodium","+1","0.102","Magnesium","+2","0.072"],["Potassium","+1","0.138","Calcium","+2","0.100"],["Rubidium","+1","0.149","Strontium","+2","0.113"],["Caesium","+1","0.170","Barium","+2","0.136"]]},{"type":"p","text":"The cation with the greatest influence (biggest charge and smaller size) on an anion is **Be²⁺**, and the one with the least influence (smallest charge and largest size) is **Cs⁺**."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/thermal-stability.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/thermal-stability-dark.png","alt":"A small highly charged cation polarising a nitrate electron cloud, weakening one bond until decomposition occurs, contrasted with a large cation leaving the anion undistorted","caption":"The bond is weakened due to polarisation of the electron cloud by the cation — decomposition of the Group II nitrate occurs."},{"type":"h3","text":"Decomposition of Group I and II nitrates"},{"type":"table","headers":["","Decomposition of Group I nitrates","Decomposition of Group II nitrates"],"rows":[["General equation","2XNO₃(s) → 2XNO₂(s) + O₂(g)","2X(NO₃)₂(s) → 2XO(s) + 4NO₂(g) + O₂(g)"],["Pattern","All Group I nitrates, except LiNO₃, decomposes to form **metal nitrites**, XNO₂ (pale yellow solid) and **oxygen gas**. LiNO₃ decomposes to form lithium oxide, nitrogen dioxide and oxygen.","All Group II nitrates decompose to form **metal oxide**, **nitrogen dioxide** (brown gas) and **oxygen**."],["Description of nitrates","Group I nitrates are white solids.","Group II nitrates are white solids."],["Equations","4LiNO₃(s) → 2Li₂O(s) + 4NO₂(g) + O₂(g) (Greater decomposition); 2NaNO₃(s) → 2NaNO₂(s) + O₂(g) (Lesser decomposition)","2Be(NO₃)₂(s) → 2BeO(s) + 4NO₂(g) + O₂(g) (Greater decomposition)"],["Observation","4LiNO₃(s) → Brown fumes; 2NaNO₃(s), 2KNO₃(s), 2RbNO₃(s) and 2CsNO₃(s) → No brown fumes","2Be(NO₃)₂(s), 2Mg(NO₃)₂(s), 2Ca(NO₃)₂(s), 2Sr(NO₃)₂(s) and 2Ba(NO₃)₂(s) → Brown fumes"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Decomposition of Group I and II carbonates"},{"type":"list","items":["Group 1 carbonates are **stable to heat** except lithium carbonate, which decomposes in a similar manner to group 2 carbonates.","Lithium carbonate decompose when heated because the **Li⁺ cation is very small** and polarizes the O–C bond in the CO₃²⁻ ion sufficiently for it to break and form an O²⁻ ion."]},{"type":"table","headers":["","Decomposition of Group I carbonates","Decomposition of Group II carbonates"],"rows":[["General equation","X₂CO₃(s) → X₂O(s) + CO₂(g)","XCO₃(s) → XO(s) + CO₂(g)"],["Pattern","All carbonates except lithium carbonate do not decompose at Bunsen temperature. Lithium carbonate decomposes to form lithium oxide and carbon dioxide.","All carbonates except barium carbonate decomposes to give metal oxide and carbon dioxide."],["Description of carbonates","Group I carbonates are white solids.","Group II carbonates are white solids."]]},{"type":"eq","code":"Li₂CO₃(s)  →  Li₂O(s)  +  CO₂(g)","caption":"Lithium carbonate decomposes on heating — the Group I exception"},{"type":"list","items":["Na₂CO₃, K₂CO₃, Rb₂CO₃ and Cs₂CO₃ **do not decompose at Bunsen temperatures**.","BaCO₃ **does not decompose at Bunsen temperatures**; BeCO₃, MgCO₃, CaCO₃ and SrCO₃ decompose to give the metal oxide and carbon dioxide."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi7100d5jfqkf12rqunp","sectionKey":"flame-colours","number":"8.17","title":"Flame Colours and Electron Transitions","blurb":"Excitation and relaxation of electrons — and the flame colours of the Group 1 and 2 cations.","estMinutes":14,"spec":"8.17","specText":"understand the formation of characteristic flame colours by Group 1 and 2 compounds in terms of electron transitions\nStudents will be expected to know the flame colours for Group 1 and 2 compounds.","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Flame test"},{"type":"list","items":["It is an important test to identify the **metal cation** present in a compound."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Origin of flame colours"},{"type":"list","items":["Metal cations of the metals in Groups I and II give **characteristic colours**.","This arises due to **electronic transitions** within the cation.","When a compound is heated, electrons gain energy and move to higher energy levels. This is commonly referred as **excitation** of electrons.","Excited electrons in higher energy levels are not stable. So, they fall back to the ground state, emitting energy in the form of light.","The light emitted is of a characteristic frequency and hence colour depends on the energy level difference between the two shells."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/flame-origins.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/flame-origins-dark.png","alt":"An energy level diagram showing excitation from the ground state and relaxation with photon emission, beside the flame colour chart for Group 1 and 2 cations","caption":"Heat promotes the electron to a higher energy level (excitation); it falls back to the ground state, emitting light of a characteristic frequency — the colour depends on the energy level difference between the two shells."},{"type":"h3","text":"Flame colours of Group I and II metal cations"},{"type":"table","headers":["Metal cation","Formula","Colour"],"rows":[["Lithium","Li⁺","red"],["Sodium","Na⁺","yellow/orange"],["Potassium","K⁺","lilac"],["Rubidium","Rb⁺","red/purple"],["Caesium","Cs⁺","blue/violet"],["Beryllium","Be²⁺","(no colour)"],["Magnesium","Mg²⁺","(no colour)"],["Calcium","Ca²⁺","(brick) red"],["Strontium","Sr²⁺","(crimson) red"],["Barium","Ba²⁺","(apple) green"]]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi7100d7jfqk14kndq3k","sectionKey":"thermal-decomposition-experiment","number":"8.18","title":"Experimental Procedures: Decomposition and Flame Tests","blurb":"The thermal decomposition experiment and the flame test procedure.","estMinutes":12,"spec":"8.18","specText":"know experimental procedures to show:\ni   patterns in the thermal decomposition of Group 1 and 2 nitrates and carbonates\nii   flame colours in compounds of Group 1 and 2 elements\nStudents will be expected to know tests for carbon dioxide and oxygen; and to recognise nitrogen dioxide by its colour and acidic pH.","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Experiment to study thermal decomposition"},{"type":"list","items":["The relative ease of decomposition of group 2 carbonates and nitrates can be studied by the following experiment."]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Place the same amount of each of the salts in a series of hard glass test tubes.","Fix a delivery tube to the test tube and clamp in a stand.","Light the burner and measure the time taken for the gas evolved to reach the mark on the test tube in the water bath.","Repeat the experiments by changing the salts."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Flame test procedure"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Wear a safety glasses and a lab coat. Within a fume cupboard light a Bunsen burner.","Using a dropper, add a few drops of concentrated hydrochloric acid to the solid and mix together so that the metal compound begins to dissolve. (One reason for using hydrochloric acid is to convert any metal compound to a chloride — chlorides are more volatile than other salts so are more likely to give better results.)","Dip a clean metal wire (platinum or nichrome) or silica rod into the mixture to obtain a sample of the compound.","Hold the end of wire or rod in the flame and observe the colour."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi7200d9jfqkxntq5uf4","sectionKey":"tests-for-ions","number":"8.19","title":"Tests for Carbonate, Sulfate and Ammonium Ions","blurb":"Tests for ammonium, carbonate and hydrogencarbonate, and sulfate ions — with observations and ionic equations.","estMinutes":16,"spec":"8.19","specText":"know reactions, including ionic equations where appropriate, for identifying:\ni   carbonate ions, CO₃²⁻, and hydrogencarbonate ions, HCO₃⁻, using an aqueous acid to form carbon dioxide (and testing the gas with limewater)\nii   sulfate ions, SO₄²⁻, using acidified barium chloride solution\niii   ammonium ions, NH₄⁺, using sodium hydroxide solution and warming to form ammonia (and testing with litmus and HCl fumes)","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Test for ions"},{"type":"table","headers":["Ion","Test","Observations","Equations"],"rows":[["Ammonium NH₄⁺","Add aqueous sodium hydroxide and warm","pungent smell (NH₃); damp litmus paper turns blue; or dense white smoke is formed (NH₄Cl) when passed over hydrogen chloride gas","NH₄⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → NH₃(g) + H₂O(l); NH₃(g) + HCl(g) → NH₄Cl(s)"],["Carbonate or hydrogen carbonate CO₃²⁻ or HCO₃⁻","Add dilute acid (HCl, H₂SO₄, HNO₃)","Effervescence (CO₂); gas turns limewater (Ca(OH)₂) milky (CaCO₃)","CO₃²⁻(aq) + 2H⁺(aq) → CO₂(g) + H₂O(l); CO₂(g) + Ca(OH)₂(aq) → CaCO₃(s) + H₂O(l)"],["Sulphate SO₄²⁻","(To a solution of a compound) add aqueous barium chloride followed by dilute hydrochloric acid or nitric acid","White ppt (BaSO₄); insoluble in hydrochloric acid","Ba²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) → BaSO₄(s)"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Note: there are other ions that could form white precipitate with barium ions, especially carbonate ions, so in the test there must be H⁺ ions present to prevent barium carbonate from forming as a white precipitate. Dilute nitric acid or dilute hydrochloric acid is therefore added as part of the test."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi7o00dbjfqknt4i1h7p","sectionKey":"solution-concentrations","number":"8.20","title":"Solution Concentrations and Titrations","blurb":"Volumetric analysis: apparatus, standard solutions, concentration calculations, indicators and the mean titre.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"8.20","specText":"be able to calculate solution concentrations, in mol dm⁻³ and g dm⁻³, including simple acid–base titrations using the indicators methyl orange and phenolphthalein","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Volumetric analysis"},{"type":"list","items":["The quantitative investigation of chemical reactions by comparing reacting volumes is known as **volumetric analysis**.","The procedure by which reacting volumes are determined is known as a **titration**. One substance needed in a titration is a **standard solution**.","Volumetric analysis can be used to determine:","Concentration of a solution","Formula of a compound","Number of moles of a substance","Percentage yield of a substance","Percentage purity of a substance","Water of crystallization of a substance"]},{"type":"h3","text":"Apparatus used"},{"type":"table","headers":["Apparatus","Use"],"rows":[["Pipette","designed to deliver a fixed volume accurately"],["Pipette filler","used to fill the pipette"],["Burette","designed to deliver definite but variable volumes of liquid"],["Conical flask","used to mix solutions"],["Volumetric flask","used to prepare standard solutions"],["Beaker","used to measure solutions approximately"],["Funnel","used to transfer solutions without spilling"],["Dropper","used to deliver small volumes of solution"],["White tile","used to show the colour changes clearly at the end point"],["Stand","used to attach the burette"]]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/titration-setup.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/titration-setup-dark.png","alt":"A titration bench setup with stand, burette, stopcock, conical flask on a white tile, and a meniscus reading guide","caption":"Burette above the conical flask on a white tile; the burette reading is taken from the lowest part of the meniscus."},{"type":"h3","text":"Standard solution"},{"type":"list","items":["A **standard solution** is a solution whose concentration is **accurately known**. One way to prepare a standard solution is to take a known mass of a substance (primary standards) and dissolve it in water to make a known volume of solution.","These substances are known as **primary standards**. Ideally primary standards should:","Be solids with high molar masses","Be available in a high degree of purity","Be chemically stable","Not absorb water from the atmosphere","Be soluble in water","React rapidly and completely with other substances when used in titrations"]},{"type":"h3","text":"Concentration"},{"type":"list","items":["**Concentration** of a solution is usually expressed as the amount of solute dissolved in a given amount of solvent or solution.","It is usually measured in **moles of solute per dm³ of solution** or in **grams of solute per dm³ of solution**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Calculations in titration"},{"type":"list","items":["Write a balanced chemical equation.","Calculate the amount/moles of the reagent of known concentration: **amount of substance = concentration × volume (n = cV)**.","Calculate the number of moles of the unknown substance, using the ratio given in the balanced chemical equation.","Calculate the concentration of the unknown solution: **concentration = moles/volume (c = n/V)**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Indicator"},{"type":"list","items":["**Indicators** are substances whose solutions change color due to changes in pH (by adding acid or alkali).","Two common indicators are **methyl orange** and **phenolphthalein**."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Indicator","Colour in acid","Colour in alkali","Acid–base combination"],"rows":[["methyl orange","red","yellow","strong acid – weak base and strong acid – strong base"],["phenolphthalein","colourless","pink","weak acid – strong base and strong acid – strong base"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Mean titre"},{"type":"list","items":["Mean is the set of values added to give a total, which is then divided by the number of values.","Only **concordant values** must be selected to calculate the mean titre."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Titration number","1","2","3","4"],"rows":[["final burette reading / cm³","24.15","25.30","24.60","23.25"],["initial burette reading / cm³","1.20","2.70","1.90","0.60"],["titre / cm³","22.95","22.60","22.70","22.65"],["concordant titres","✗","✓","✓","✓"]]},{"type":"list","items":["Titre 2, 3 and 4 are all within 0.20 cm³ of each other, so are concordant."]},{"type":"eq","code":"Mean  =  (22.60 + 22.70 + 22.65) ÷ 3  =  22.65 cm³","caption":"Only the concordant titres are averaged"}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi7p00ddjfqkfxprbgjf","sectionKey":"core-practical-3","number":"8.21","title":"Core Practical 3: Concentration of Hydrochloric Acid","blurb":"Core Practical 3: the titration method for finding the concentration of an acid solution.","estMinutes":14,"spec":"8.21","specText":"CORE PRACTICAL 3\nFinding the concentration of a solution of hydrochloric acid.","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Titration"},{"type":"list","items":["A **titration** is a practical method with the aim of measuring the volumes of two solutions that react together, and using the results to calculate the concentration of one of the solutions.","It involves having one solution in a **burette** and pipetting a known volume of the other solution (**standard solution**) into a **conical flask**.","An **indicator** is then added to the solution in the conical flask and is titrated from the burette until the indicator changes colour."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Key vocabulary"},{"type":"list","items":["**Equivalence point**: the point at which there are exactly the right amounts of substances to complete the reaction.","**End point**: the point at which the indicator just changes colour; ideally the end point should coincide with the equivalence point.","**Meniscus**: the curving of the upper surface of the liquid in a container; the lowest (horizontal) part of the meniscus should be read.","**Titre**: the volume added from the burette during a titration.","**Concordant titres**: titres that are close together (usually within 0.20 cm³ of each other)."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Outline of the titration method"},{"type":"list","items":["Add the acid to the alkali or vice versa until the equivalence point of the titration and the end point of the indicator is reached.","Record the burette reading using the lowest part of the meniscus.","Calculate the titre.","Repeat the titration until concordant titres are obtained."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Titration method (sulfamic acid and sodium hydroxide)"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Rinse the conical flask with deionized water and place it on a white tile.","Using a pipette filler, rinse the pipette with deionized water and then with some of the sodium hydroxide solution.","Use the pipette to transfer 25.0 cm³ of the sodium hydroxide solution to the conical flask.","Add about 3 drops of methyl orange indicator.","Rinse the burette with deionized water and then with sulfamic acid solution.","Fill the burette with sulfamic acid solution and set it up in the stand above the conical flask.","Record the burette reading.","Add the sulfamic acid solution to the conical flask until the indicator just changes colour, and again record the burette reading.","Empty and rinse the conical flask with deionized water, and repeat the titration until concordant titres have been obtained."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi7q00dfjfqk3kawqfqu","sectionKey":"measurement-uncertainty","number":"8.22","title":"Mistakes, Errors and Measurement Uncertainty","blurb":"Mistakes, errors, accuracy and precision, measurement uncertainty and overall percentage uncertainty.","estMinutes":16,"spec":"8.22","specText":"understand how to minimize the sources of measurement uncertainty in volumetric analysis and estimate the overall uncertainty in the calculated result.","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Mistake"},{"type":"list","items":["A **mistake** is something that a skilled operator can avoid by being careful.","Some examples of mistakes:","A chemist weighs a beaker on a balance without making sure the balance is tared (set to zero) beforehand. The reading on the balance could be very different from the actual mass of the beaker.","A student sees a burette reading of 27.35 cm³ but writes it down as 23.75 cm³. This is the students mistake, and nothing to do with the apparatus."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Error"},{"type":"list","items":["**Error** is something that even a skilled operator would find difficult to avoid, and is a consequence of the way the apparatus has been constructed and how readings can be made using it.","Measurement errors can be divided into **random errors** and **systematic errors**:"]},{"type":"table","headers":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["Random error","This is an error caused by unpredictable changes in conditions such as temperature or pressure, or by a difference in recording that is difficult to get exactly right."],["Systematic error","This is an error caused by the apparatus, and leads to the recorded value being either too low or too high."]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Accuracy"},{"type":"list","items":["**Accuracy** is a measure of how close values are to the **accepted or correct value**.","If the pipette is wrongly calibrated, it is not **accurate** even if it is precise. It delivers the same amount of liquid but not the amount it claims to have delivered."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Precision"},{"type":"list","items":["**Precision** is a measure of how close values are to **each other**.","**Burette** is a **precise** device to deliver variable amounts of a liquid measured to the nearest 0.05 cm³.","**Pipette** is a **precise** device to deliver a fixed amount of a liquid, usually 25.0 cm³ or 10.0 cm³."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Measurement uncertainty"},{"type":"list","items":["It is the **potential error** involved when using a piece of apparatus to make a measurement.","The size of measurement uncertainty is determined by the **precision of the apparatus**.","Digital balances can have various degree of precision. A balance that measures to three decimal places is more precise than one that measures to only one decimal place.","The greater the degree of precision of the balance, the smaller the measurement uncertainty in the recorded mass."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Number of decimal places","Measurement uncertainty","Example"],"rows":[["1","±0.05 g","A reading of 17.1 g could be between 17.05 and 17.15 g"],["2","±0.005 g","A reading of 17.10 g could be between 17.095 g and 17.105 g"],["3","±0.0005 g","A reading of 17.100 g could be between 17.0995 g and 17.1005 g"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Examples of measurement uncertainties in glassware"},{"type":"table","headers":["Apparatus","Capacity","Measurement uncertainty"],"rows":[["burette","50 cm³","±0.05 cm³ (but the burette is read twice in a titration, so the total measurement uncertainty is ±0.10 cm³)"],["pipette","25 cm³","±0.06 cm³"],["volumetric flask","250 cm³","±0.3 cm³"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Percentage uncertainty"},{"type":"list","items":["Percentage uncertainty is the actual measurement uncertainty in an experiment multiplied by 100 and divided by the value recorded."]},{"type":"eq","code":"% uncertainty  =  (actual measurement uncertainty in an experiment ÷ value recorded)  ×  100","caption":"Percentage uncertainty"},{"type":"h3","text":"Percentage uncertainty of burette, pipette and volumetric flask"},{"type":"table","headers":["Apparatus","Capacity","Uncertainty","Percentage uncertainty"],"rows":[["burette","50 cm³","±0.05 cm³","Note that two burette values are read in a titration, so the total measurement uncertainty is ±0.10 cm³. If the titre is 22.50 cm³, then the percentage uncertainty is: ±0.10 × 100/22.50 = **±0.44%**"],["pipette","25 cm³","±0.06 cm³","The reading is taken only once, and for the same volume each time, so the percentage uncertainty is always: ±0.06 × 100/25 = **±0.24%**"],["volumetric flask","250 cm³","±0.3 cm³","The reading is taken only once, and for the same volume each time, so the percentage uncertainty is always: ±0.3 × 100/250 = **±0.12%**"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Percentage uncertainty of balance"},{"type":"list","items":["The percentage uncertainty using a balance depends on:","The precision of the balance","The mass being weighed"]},{"type":"worked","title":"Worked example","problem":"Mass of marble chips = 3.57 g. The measurement uncertainty in a two decimal place balance is ±0.005 g. We have to count this uncertainty twice — there is a ±0.005 g uncertainty when calibrating the balance to zero, as well as a ±0.005 g uncertainty when measuring mass.","steps":["Total uncertainty = 2 × 0.005 g.","So the percentage uncertainty is ±(2 × 0.005 × 100) ÷ 3.57."],"answer":"±0.28%"},{"type":"h3","text":"Total measurement uncertainty"},{"type":"list","items":["**Total measurement uncertainty** is obtained by **adding together the individual uncertainties**.","For example, if a concentration of 0.118 mol dm⁻³ has been calculated from a titration that has involved balance and glassware uncertainties, then the uncertainties might be:"]},{"type":"table","headers":["","Percentage uncertainty"],"rows":[["Balance","±0.09%"],["Volumetric flask","±0.12%"],["Pipette","±0.24%"],["Burette","±0.47%"],["Overall percentage uncertainty","±0.92%"]]},{"type":"list","items":["This means there is an overall uncertainty in the concentration of 0.92% of 0.118, which is about ±0.001, so the final value can be quoted like this:"]},{"type":"eq","code":"Concentration  =  0.118  ±  0.001 mol dm⁻³","caption":"The exact value is in range 0.117 – 0.119 mol dm⁻³"},{"type":"h3","text":"Minimizing uncertainty and errors"},{"type":"list","items":["Percentage uncertainty can be minimized by using a **higher value** rather than a lower one.","For example, in using a balance, weighing sample of 5 g will lead to a much lower percentage uncertainty than weighing a 0.5 g sample.","**Minimizing random errors:** for example, in a thermochemistry experiment, **minimizing heat loss** will reduce random errors in the measurement of temperature change."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi7r00dhjfqk55swbnw3","sectionKey":"core-practical-4","number":"8.23","title":"Core Practical 4: Standard Solution from a Solid Acid","blurb":"Core Practical 4: preparation of a standard solution from a solid acid.","estMinutes":12,"spec":"8.23","specText":"CORE PRACTICAL 4\nPreparation of a standard solution from a solid acid and use it to find the concentration of a solution of sodium hydroxide.","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Preparation of a standard solution"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["**Weigh** accurately the required amount of material (see section 3.2).","**Transfer** the material to a beaker and dissolve in a small amount of solvent (usually deionised water). Ensure all the solid has dissolved.","Using a clean glass funnel, **transfer the solution quantitatively** into a clean volumetric flask. Wash out the beaker with the solvent a number of times and transfer the washings to the flask. Hint: pouring the liquid down a glass rod held in the spout of the beaker can help prevent liquid running down the side of the beaker.","**Make sure the flask and contents are at ambient temperature.** Carefully add solvent to the flask. Use a pasteur pipette to slowly add solvent until the bottom of the meniscus touches the calibration mark on the neck of the volumetric flask (see section 3.1 for information on the correct use of volumetric flasks).","**Stopper** the volumetric flask and shake to ensure the solution is thoroughly mixed."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi7r00djjfqkxhbvgcgl","sectionKey":"group7-trends","number":"8.24","title":"Trends in Group 7","blurb":"The halogens: appearance, changes of state, atomic radius, melting and boiling temperatures, electronegativity and reactivity — with the reason for each trend.","estMinutes":16,"spec":"8.24","specText":"understand reasons for the trends for Group 7 elements in:\ni   melting and boiling temperatures and physical state at room temperature\nii   electronegativity\niii   reactivity down the group","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Introduction to the Group 7 elements"},{"type":"list","items":["The elements in group VII are known as **halogens** — fluorine, chlorine, bromine, iodine and astatine.","The term **halogen** is derived from Greek meaning **salt producing**. Halogens are very reactive and readily form salts called **halides**.","All have the electronic configuration **ns² np⁵**.","They exist as separate **diatomic molecules** (F₂, Cl₂, Br₂ and I₂)."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Appearance"},{"type":"table","headers":["","F₂","Cl₂","Br₂","I₂"],"rows":[["Colour","Pale yellow","Green","Red-brown","Shiny greyish black"],["State (at RTP)","gas","gas","liquid","solid"],["In water","very pale yellow","Pale green","Reddish brown","Pale brown"],["In hydrocarbon solvents","—","Pale green","Reddish brown","Violet"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Changes of state"},{"type":"list","items":["When bromine is left at room temperature it gives off **brown vapour**: Br₂(l) → Br₂(g).","When iodine is left at room temperature, it gives off brown vapour **without melting**. This change is called **sublimation**: I₂(s) → I₂(g)."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Atomic radius"},{"type":"list","items":["Atomic radius **increases** down the Group 7 as the outer most electrons are further away from the nuclei."]},{"type":"table","headers":["F","Cl","Br","I"],"rows":[["Atomic radius / nm","0.064","0.099","0.111","0.128"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Melting and Boiling temperature"},{"type":"list","items":["Melting and boiling temperatures **increase** down the group.","As the size of the halogen increases, the number of electrons also increases.","This increases the strength of **London forces** (instantaneous dipole-induced dipole attraction).","Therefore, more energy is required to overcome the forces between molecules."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Electronegativity"},{"type":"def","term":"Electronegativity","text":"The ability of an atom to attract the shared pair of electrons in a covalent bond."},{"type":"list","items":["**Electronegativity decreases** down the group. The electronegativity of fluorine is the highest of all elements.","As the size of the atom increases, the number of shells increases.","This increases the shielding of the nucleus by the inner shell electrons.","As a result, the ability of the nucleus to attract electron pair is reduced."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Element","State at room temperature","Melting temperature / °C","Boiling temperature / °C","Electronegativity"],"rows":[["Fluorine","gas","−220","−188","4.0"],["Chlorine","gas","−101","−35","3.0"],["Bromine","liquid","−7","59","2.8"],["Iodine","solid","114","184","2.5"],["Astatine","solid","302","337","2.2"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Reactivity"},{"type":"list","items":["The reactivity of halogens **decreases down the group**. F₂ is most reactive and I₂ is least reactive.","The decrease in reactivity can be explained with reference to the same factors used to explain the decrease in electronegativity down the group.","**Electron affinity** also decreases down the group, therefore it is less easy to form anions."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi7t00dljfqkbu8ew9ui","sectionKey":"halogen-displacement","number":"8.25","title":"Displacement Reactions and Oxidising Power","blurb":"Oxidising power decreases F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂ — shown by the displacement reactions with halide ions in aqueous solution.","estMinutes":16,"spec":"8.25","specText":"understand the trend in reactivity of Group 7 elements in terms of the redox reactions of Cl₂, Br₂ and I₂ with halide ions in aqueous solution.\nStudents are expected to know the colours of the elements in standard conditions, in aqueous solution and in a non-polar organic solvent.","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Displacement reactions of halogen/halide"},{"type":"list","items":["Halogens are strong **oxidizing agents** — as they need an electron to complete their outer shell, they accept electrons and get reduced.","The oxidizing power of the halogen **decreases** as we move down the group from F to I:"]},{"type":"eq","code":"F₂  >  Cl₂  >  Br₂  >  I₂","caption":"Oxidising power of the halogens"},{"type":"list","items":["As the size of the atoms increases down a group, **shielding increases**.","Therefore, the ability of the atoms to accept electrons decreases (form −1 ions) and hence their oxidising power decreases.","**Chlorine** will displace **bromide and iodide** ions from solution.","**Bromine** will displace **iodide** ions from solution, but not chloride ions.","**Iodine** cannot displace either bromide or chloride ions from solution."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Worked example"},{"type":"table","headers":["Halogen added","Observation","Potassium chloride(aq)","Potassium bromide(aq)","Potassium iodide(aq)"],"rows":[["chlorine(aq)","Appearance of aqueous solution","Very pale green","Reddish brown","Pale brown"],["chlorine(aq)","Appearance of cyclohexane","Very pale green","Reddish brown","Violet"]]},{"type":"eq","code":"Cl₂(aq)  +  2Br⁻(aq)  →  2Cl⁻(aq)  +  Br₂(aq)          Cl₂(aq)  +  2I⁻(aq)  →  2Cl⁻(aq)  +  I₂(aq)          Br₂(aq)  +  2I⁻(aq)  →  2Br⁻(aq)  +  I₂(aq)","caption":"The ionic equations for the three displacements described above"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/displacement-grid.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/displacement-grid-dark.png","alt":"The halogen displacement grid showing which halogens displace which halide ions, with the three ionic equations","caption":"Displacement = oxidising power: each halogen oxidises (and displaces) the halide ions of any halogen below it in the group."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi7t00dnjfqkk27ag3nh","sectionKey":"chlorine-reactions","number":"8.26","title":"Chlorine with Water and Alkali","blurb":"Group 1 and 2 metals with halogens; chlorine with water, with cold dilute alkali and with hot alkali — disproportionation in terms of oxidation numbers.","estMinutes":14,"spec":"8.26","specText":"understand, in terms of changes in oxidation number, the following reactions of the halogens:\ni   oxidation reactions with Group 1 and 2 metals\nii   the disproportionation reaction of chlorine with water and the use of chlorine in water treatment.\niii   the disproportionation reaction of chlorine with cold, dilute aqueous sodium hydroxide to form bleach\niv   the disproportionation reaction of chlorine with hot alkali\nv   reactions analogous to those specified above","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Metals of Group 1 and 2"},{"type":"list","items":["Metals react with halogens on heating to form **halides** or white ionic salts.","Reactions are most vigorous between elements at the bottom of Group 1 and 2, and elements at the top of Group 7. The most vigorous reaction should be between **caesium (or francium) and fluorine**, and the least vigorous between **beryllium and iodine (or astatine)**.","These reactions are **redox reactions** in which the halogen acts as an **oxidizing agent**.","The oxidation number of the halogen **decreases from 0 to −1** and the oxidation number of the metal **increases from 0 to +1 or +2**, depending on the group."]},{"type":"table","headers":["","Group I metal","Group II metal"],"rows":[["General equation","2M + X₂ → 2MX","M + X₂ → MX₂"]]},{"type":"eq","code":"2Li  +  Cl₂  →  2LiCl          2Na  +  Br₂  →  2NaBr","caption":"Group I metal examples"},{"type":"eq","code":"Mg  +  Cl₂  →  MgCl₂          Ca  +  Cl₂  →  CaCl₂          Sr  +  Br₂  →  SrBr₂","caption":"Group II metal examples"},{"type":"h3","text":"Disproportionation reactions of chlorine"},{"type":"def","term":"Disproportionation","text":"One element undergoes both oxidation and reduction at the same time."},{"type":"h3","text":"Chlorine with water"},{"type":"list","items":["When chlorine is added to water, it dissolves to form **chlorine water**.","Some of the dissolved chlorine also reacts to form a mixture of two acids, **hydrochloric acid** and **chloric(I) acid** (commonly called **hypochlorous acid**):"]},{"type":"eq","code":"Cl₂  +  H₂O  →  HCl  +  HClO","caption":"Cl: 0 → −1 (HCl) · 0 → +1 (HClO)"},{"type":"list","items":["**HClO is a mild oxidising agent** and effective at killing bacteria without being harmful to humans. For this reason, a small amount of chlorine dissolved in water will sterilize the water, and chlorine is widely used in **swimming pools** and in **water treatment**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Chlorine with COLD alkali"},{"type":"list","items":["When chlorine is added to **COLD dilute aqueous sodium hydroxide**, it reacts to form the salts of the acids.","These salts are **sodium chloride** and **sodium chlorate(I)**, which is also known as **sodium hypochlorite**.","The disproportionation that occurs can be shown using the oxidation numbers:"]},{"type":"eq","code":"2NaOH(aq)  +  Cl₂(g)  →  NaCl(aq)  +  NaOCl(aq)  +  H₂O(l)","caption":"Cl: 0 → −1 (NaCl) · 0 → +1 (NaOCl)"},{"type":"list","items":["The sodium chlorate(I) formed is also a disinfectant, but it is mainly known for its **bleaching action**. It is used extensively in industry and is the **active ingredient in household bleach**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Chlorine with HOT alkali"},{"type":"list","items":["When chlorine is added to **HOT concentrated sodium hydroxide** solution, it reacts to form sodium chloride and a different product, **sodium chlorate(V)**:"]},{"type":"eq","code":"6NaOH(aq)  +  3Cl₂(aq)  →  5NaCl(aq)  +  NaClO₃(aq)  +  3H₂O(l)","caption":"Cl: 0 → −1 (NaCl) · 0 → +5 (NaClO₃)"},{"type":"list","items":["Chlorine is oxidized to the **+5 state**.","The sodium chlorate(V) formed is also used in bleaching, and as a **weed killer**.","**Bromine and iodine react in similar ways.**"]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi7u00dpjfqkyqppjhne","sectionKey":"halide-reactions","number":"8.27","title":"Reactions of the Halides","blurb":"Halide ions as reducing agents — the sodium halides with concentrated sulfuric acid, testing for halide ions with silver nitrate and ammonia, and the hydrogen halides with water and ammonia.","estMinutes":26,"spec":"8.27","specText":"understand the following reactions:\ni   solid Group 1 halides with concentrated sulfuric acid, to illustrate the trend in reducing ability of the hydrogen halides\nii   precipitation reactions of the aqueous anions Cl⁻, Br⁻ and I⁻ with aqueous silver nitrate solution and nitric acid, and the solubility of the precipitates in aqueous ammonia solution\niii   hydrogen halides with ammonia gas (to produce ammonium halides) and with water (to produce acids)","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Reducing agent"},{"type":"list","items":["The halide ions are **reducing agents**, as they can lose electrons and get oxidized.","Reducing power of halide ions **increases down the group**. **Iodide ions are the most reducing**, followed by bromide ions. Fluoride ions have **no significant reducing properties**.","As the number of shells in the ion increases, there is more **shielding** of the nucleus and the outer electrons become less strongly held.","These electrons are thus **lost more easily** and the halide ion is **more readily oxidised**."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Oxidising power","Halogen","Halide","Reducing power"],"rows":[["High","fluorine F₂","fluoride F⁻","Low"],["↓","chlorine Cl₂","chloride Cl⁻","↑"],["↓","bromine Br₂","bromide Br⁻","↑"],["↓","iodine I₂","iodide I⁻","↑"],["Low","astatine At₂","astatide At⁻","High"]]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/halide-power-ladder.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/halide-power-ladder-dark.png","alt":"Opposing ladders showing halogen oxidising power increasing towards fluorine while halide reducing power increases towards astatide","caption":"One trend, two directions: oxidising power of the halogens rises towards fluorine; reducing power of the halide ions rises towards astatide."},{"type":"h3","text":"Reaction of halide ions with concentrated sulfuric acid"},{"type":"list","items":["The variation in reducing strength of the halides can be clearly seen in the reaction of the **sodium halides with concentrated sulfuric acid**.","Concentrated sulfuric acid can act as an **oxidizing agent** and as well as an **acid**.","When it acts as an oxidizing agent, it is reduced, but the extent of its reduction and the products formed depends on the species being oxidized.","The three possible reduction products are **sulfur dioxide**, **sulfur** and **hydrogen sulfide**."]},{"type":"table","headers":["NaX","Observation"],"rows":[["NaCl(s)","Misty fumes (HCl)"],["NaBr(s)","Misty fumes (HBr)"],["NaBr(s)","Brown fumes Br₂"],["NaBr(s)","Colourless gas with choking odour (SO₂)"],["NaI(s)","Misty fumes (HI)"],["NaI(s)","Purple fumes + black ppt (I₂)"],["NaI(s)","Colourless gas with choking odour (SO₂)"],["NaI(s)","Yellow ppt (S)"],["NaI(s)","Colourless gas with rotten egg smell (H₂S)"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Testing for halide ions in solutions"},{"type":"list","items":["The analysis of the halide ions is based on the **solubilities of the silver halides**.","The reagent is **silver nitrate** solution, but dilute **nitric acid** is added first to make sure that any other anions (especially carbonate ions) are removed, as they would also form precipitates.","If a precipitate is obtained, **dilute or concentrated ammonia** is added to further confirm the halide ion."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Halide","Add dilute nitric acid, then silver nitrate solution","To distinguish the precipitate, add dilute ammonia and concentrated ammonia solution"],"rows":[["Chloride","White precipitate of AgCl(s)","Precipitate soluble in both"],["Chloride","Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s)","AgCl(s) + 2NH₃(aq) → [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq)"],["Bromide","Cream precipitate of AgBr(s)","Precipitate slightly soluble in dilute ammonia and soluble in concentrated ammonia"],["Bromide","Ag⁺(aq) + Br⁻(aq) → AgBr(s)","AgBr(s) + 2NH₃(aq) → [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺(aq) + Br⁻(aq)"],["Iodide","Yellow precipitate of AgI(s)","Precipitate insoluble in both"],["Iodide","Ag⁺(aq) + I⁻(aq) → AgI(s)","—"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Hydrogen halides"},{"type":"list","items":["Hydrogen halides are **colourless gases** and exist as **polar diatomic molecules**.","They are formed when hydrogen reacts with halogens."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Reactions with water"},{"type":"list","items":["The hydrogen halides readily react with water to form **acidic solutions**, all of which are colourless."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Hydrogen halide","Acid formed","Equation"],"rows":[["Hydrogen fluoride","hydrofluoric acid","HF + H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + F⁻"],["Hydrogen chloride","hydrochloric acid","HCl + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻"],["Hydrogen bromide","hydrobromic acid","HBr + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + Br⁻"],["Hydrogen iodide","hydroiodic acid","HI + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + I⁻"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Reaction with ammonia"},{"type":"list","items":["Hydrogen halides react with ammonia gas to form salts, all of which are **white ionic solids**:"]},{"type":"eq","code":"HCl(g)  +  NH₃(g)  →  NH₄Cl(s)","caption":"Example — ammonium chloride, a white ionic solid"}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi7v00drjfqk94bfpa86","sectionKey":"halogen-predictions","number":"8.28","title":"Predicting Fluorine and Astatine","blurb":"Predicting reactions of fluorides and astatides using the trends in halogen chemistry.","estMinutes":8,"spec":"8.28","specText":"be able to make predictions about fluorine and astatine and their compounds, in terms of knowledge of trends in halogen chemistry","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"If you are asked to predict reactions of **fluorides and astatides** that you are not familiar with, you can use information in this topic to help you."},{"type":"h3","text":"Fluorides and Astatides"},{"type":"list","items":["For example, you could write an equation to show the formation of an acid when **hydrogen astatide** is added to water, based on the equation for hydrogen iodide:"]},{"type":"eq","code":"HI  +  H₂O  →  H₃O⁺  +  I⁻          HAt  +  H₂O  →  H₃O⁺  +  At⁻","caption":"The hydrogen astatide prediction, based on the equation for hydrogen iodide"},{"type":"h3","text":"Where fluorine and astatine already appear in this topic"},{"type":"list","items":["**Appearance and physical state:** fluorine is a pale yellow gas; astatine is a solid at room temperature.","**Melting and boiling temperatures:** astatine continues the increase down the group — melting temperature 302 °C, boiling temperature 337 °C.","**Electronegativity:** fluorine is the highest of all elements (4.0); astatine continues the decrease (2.2).","**Reactivity and oxidising power:** fluorine is the most reactive halogen, at the top of the order F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂; astatine sits at the low end of the oxidising-power ladder, so astatide At⁻ sits at the high end of the reducing-power ladder.","**Reactions with metals:** the least vigorous reaction of all is between beryllium and iodine (or astatine)."]}]}],"cards":[{"id":"cmv1eoi7v00dtjfqkhm0km855","ord":1,"front":"Define oxidation and reduction (two ways).","back":"In terms of electrons: oxidation = loss of electrons, reduction = gain (OIL RIG). In terms of oxidation number: oxidation = increase, reduction = decrease.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi7w00dvjfqk4nq0fbj9","ord":2,"front":"Define a disproportionation reaction.","back":"A redox reaction in which the same element in one species is simultaneously oxidised and reduced (e.g. 2H₂O + Cl₂ ⇌ HCl + HClO; Cl goes 0 → −1 and 0 → +1).","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi7w00dxjfqkgv9nubif","ord":3,"front":"Oxidation number of O in H₂O₂ and H in CaH₂?","back":"O = −1 in peroxides; H = −1 in metal hydrides. These are the classic exceptions.","tag":"TRAP"},{"id":"cmv1eoi7x00dzjfqk96nbsh8l","ord":4,"front":"Explain why Group 1 reactivity increases down the group.","back":"Atomic radius and shielding increase; the outer electron is further from the nucleus and less strongly held, so lost more easily (lower first IE).","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi7x00e1jfqku8dh90xb","ord":5,"front":"Equation for calcium with water?","back":"Ca(s) + 2H₂O(l) → Ca(OH)₂(s) + H₂(g) — steady fizzing and a cloudy white **suspension** of Ca(OH)₂. (Mg is slower still with cold water and needs steam for MgO: Mg + H₂O(g) → MgO + H₂.)","tag":"EQUATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi7y00e3jfqky1dnp34z","ord":6,"front":"Trend in solubility of Group 2 hydroxides and sulfates?","back":"Hydroxides: more soluble down the group (Mg(OH)₂ insoluble → Ba(OH)₂ soluble). Sulfates: less soluble down the group (MgSO₄ soluble → BaSO₄ insoluble — basis of the sulfate test).","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi7y00e5jfqky4os6thz","ord":7,"front":"Trend in oxidising power of halogens down Group 7?","back":"Decreases: Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂. Atomic radius/shielding increase, so the incoming electron is attracted less strongly. Shown by displacement: Cl₂ displaces Br⁻ and I⁻; Br₂ displaces I⁻ only.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi7y00e7jfqkcz1mcfbi","ord":8,"front":"Halide test colours with AgNO₃.","back":"Cl⁻: white ppt (dissolves in dilute NH₃). Br⁻: cream ppt (dissolves in concentrated NH₃). I⁻: yellow ppt (insoluble in NH₃).","tag":"TEST"},{"id":"cmv1eoi7z00e9jfqkpuez0mgq","ord":9,"front":"Trend in reducing power of halide ions?","back":"Increases down the group (I⁻ strongest): larger ions lose electrons more easily as outer shell is further/shielded. Shown with conc. H₂SO₄: Cl⁻ gives HCl only; Br⁻ gives SO₂ (orange vapour Br₂); I⁻ gives H₂S (rotten eggs) + black I₂.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8000ebjfqkuadzn5vh","ord":10,"front":"Why is chlorine added to water, and what's the equation in NaOH?","back":"Cl₂ disproportionates, killing bacteria: Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HCl + HClO. With cold dilute NaOH: Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O (household bleach).","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8000edjfqkkkaldnx4","ord":11,"front":"Name KMnO₄ and K₂MnO₄ using Roman numerals.","back":"KMnO₄: potassium manganate(VII) (Mn = +7). K₂MnO₄: potassium manganate(VI) (Mn = +6). The numeral is the oxidation number of the variable element in the anion.","tag":"NOMENCLATURE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8000efjfqkfg2gnghg","ord":12,"front":"Formula of iron(III) sulfate?","back":"Fe₂(SO₄)₃ — two Fe³⁺ (+6) balance three SO₄²⁻ (−6). Polyatomic ions get brackets when multiplied.","tag":"SKILL"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8100ehjfqkkoo6pt2j","ord":13,"front":"Balanced half-equation for MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺ in acidic solution?","back":"MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O. Balance O with H₂O, H with H⁺, charge with electrons (+7 left → add 5e⁻).","tag":"EQUATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8100ejjfqkqvhh8i3z","ord":14,"front":"Trend and explanation for thermal stability of Group 1 and 2 nitrates/carbonates?","back":"Increases down each group, decreases from Group 1 to Group 2: bigger cations polarise the anion less (lower charge density), anion bonds stay strong, so more energy is needed to decompose. Be²⁺ polarises most, Cs⁺ least.","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8200eljfqkzashsk7e","ord":15,"front":"Which titres go into the mean titre?","back":"Only **concordant** ones — titres within 0.20 cm³ of each other. E.g. titres 22.95, 22.60, 22.70, 22.65 → discard 22.95, mean = 22.65 cm³.","tag":"SKILL"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8200enjfqkxzs8vo8d","ord":16,"front":"Why is the burette uncertainty ±0.10 cm³ and not ±0.05?","back":"The burette is **read twice** (initial and final), so ±0.05 cm³ counts twice. A titre of 22.50 cm³ then carries ±0.10 ÷ 22.50 × 100 = ±0.44%.","tag":"TRAP"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8300epjfqkm8g9meum","ord":17,"front":"Flame colours: Na, K, Ca, Sr, Ba (and the two no-colours)?","back":"Na yellow/orange; K lilac; Ca (brick) red; Sr (crimson) red; Ba (apple) green. Be²⁺ and Mg²⁺ give no visible colour — their transitions emit in the ultraviolet.","tag":"TEST"}]},{"slug":"kinetics-and-equilibria","number":9,"unit":2,"unitCode":"WCH12","unitName":"Energetics, Group Chemistry, Halogenoalkanes and Alcohols","title":"Introduction to Kinetics and Equilibria","subtitle":"Collision theory, Maxwell–Boltzmann, catalysts — and reversible reactions at equilibrium.","summary":"Collision theory and rate factors; rates from graphs and tangents; Maxwell–Boltzmann distributions; catalysts and reaction profiles. 9B: reversible reactions and dynamic equilibrium; effects of temperature, pressure and concentration on the position of equilibrium; compromise conditions in the Haber and Contact processes.","estHours":5.0,"sections":[{"id":"cmv1eoi8400esjfqk78q0fgsh","sectionKey":"specification-map","number":"9.0","title":"Specification Map","blurb":"Every statement the specification assesses in this topic — use it as your revision checklist.","estMinutes":8,"spec":"9.1 – 9.11","specText":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:","outline":["The full list of assessment statements for Topic 9, worded exactly as the specification words them","Every statement 9.1 – 9.11 has its own section below, in specification order"],"content":[{"type":"p","text":"Students will be assessed on their ability to:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Spec","Assessment statement"],"rows":[["9.1","understand, in terms of the collision theory, the effect of changes in concentration, temperature, pressure and surface area on the rate of a chemical reaction"],["9.2","understand that reactions take place only when collisions have sufficient energy, known as the activation energy"],["9.3","be able to calculate the rate of a reaction from:\ni   the time taken for a reaction, using rate = 1/time\nii   the gradient of suitable graph, by drawing a tangent, either for initial rate, or at a time, t"],["9.4","understand qualitatively, in terms of the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution of molecular energies, how changes in temperature affect the rate of a reaction"],["9.5","understand the role of catalysts in providing alternative reaction routes of lower activation energy"],["9.6","be able to draw the reaction profiles for uncatalysed and catalysed reactions, including the energy level of the intermediate formed with the catalyst"],["9.7","understand the use of catalysts in industry to make processes more sustainable by using less energy and/or higher atom economy"],["9.8","be able to interpret the action of a catalyst in terms of a qualitative understanding of the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution of molecular energies"],["9.9","know that many reactions are readily reversible and that they can reach a state of dynamic equilibrium in which:\ni   the rate of the forward reaction is equal to the rate of the backward reaction\nii   the concentrations of the reactants and the products remain constant"],["9.10","be able to predict and justify the qualitative effects of changes of temperature, pressure and concentration on the position of equilibrium in a homogeneous system"],["9.11","evaluate data to explain the necessity, for many industrial processes, to reach a compromise between the yield and the rate of reaction"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Further suggested practical — experiments to demonstrate the factors that influence the rate of chemical reactions, including the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide, reaction of marble chips with acid, reaction of thiosulfate ions with acid."},{"type":"p","text":"Further suggested practical (9B) — demonstrate the effect of a change of temperature, pressure and concentration on a system at equilibrium: chlorine reacting with iodine to form iodine(I) chloride, which then reacts with chlorine to form iodine(III) chloride; and the equilibrium system between nitrogen dioxide (NO₂) and dinitrogen tetroxide (N₂O₄)."},{"type":"p","text":"The topic is built in two parts: **9A Kinetics** (9.1 – 9.8) and **9B Equilibria** (9.9 – 9.11). Each assessment statement has its own section below, in specification order."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi8500eujfqkhy1muibw","sectionKey":"collision-theory-rate-factors","number":"9.1","title":"Collision Theory and Rate Factors","blurb":"React only when they collide — hard enough, the right way, and more often when conditions change.","estMinutes":30,"spec":"9.1","specText":"understand, in terms of the collision theory, the effect of changes in concentration, temperature, pressure and surface area on the rate of a chemical reaction","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Collision theory"},{"type":"p","text":"According to the theory, for a reaction to occur —"},{"type":"list","items":["The reacting particles must **collide** before a reaction takes place. However, not all collisions will lead to a reaction.","During collision, the reacting particles must approach each other in the correct orientation.","The reacting particles must have a certain minimum amount of energy referred to as the activation energy (Ea)."]},{"type":"def","term":"Effective — successful — collisions","text":"A reaction occurs when the reactants collide each other in the correct orientation with energy equal to or greater than activation energy. Such collisions are effective or successful collisions."},{"type":"h3","text":"Orientation (steric effect)"},{"type":"p","text":"Collisions will result in reaction only if the molecules collide in the proper geometry."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/kinetics-orientation.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/kinetics-orientation-dark.png","alt":"Three collisions between ethene and hydrogen bromide in different orientations — only the H-end-first vertical collision can react","caption":"Collision 1 — possible reaction · collisions 2 and 3 — no reaction."},{"type":"h3","text":"Effect of concentration, pressure and surface area on rate of reaction"},{"type":"list","items":["According to collision theory, reactant particles must collide with sufficient energy before they can react.","Hence rate of reaction can be increased by increasing the frequency of collision with sufficient energy between reacting particles.","Collisions that result in a reaction are called successful collisions."]},{"type":"h3","text":"The effect of concentration"},{"type":"p","text":"Increase in the concentration of the reactants in solution increases the rate of the reaction. This is because"},{"type":"list","items":["Reactant particles per unit volume increases.","Frequency of collisions increases.","So frequency of effective collisions increases."]},{"type":"p","text":"Decreasing the concentration of the reactants in solution decreases the rate of the reaction because"},{"type":"list","items":["Reactant particles per unit volume decreases.","Frequency of collisions decreases.","So frequency of effective collisions decreases."]},{"type":"p","text":"**Rate of reaction:** reactions start off at their fastest then slow as the reactant concentration drops."},{"type":"p","text":"eg In the reaction **CaCO₃(s) + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + CO₂(g)**, the change in volume of carbon dioxide is measured with time when the concentration of HCl acid is increased. The following graph shows the volume of carbon dioxide collected:"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/kinetics-concentration-effect.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/kinetics-concentration-effect-dark.png","alt":"Volume of carbon dioxide against time — steeper curve A levels off quickly, curve B rises more slowly to the same final volume","caption":"Curve A is steeper — rate of reaction is faster than curve B, and the reaction completes faster."},{"type":"list","items":["Curve A is steeper therefore rate of reaction is faster than curve B.","Gradient of curve A is greater than gradient of curve B.","Curve A levels off quickly compared to curve B indicating faster completion of reaction."]},{"type":"h3","text":"The effect of pressure (only for gases)"},{"type":"p","text":"Increasing the pressure of gaseous reacting particles or decreasing the volume of the reaction vessel increases the rate of the reaction. This is because"},{"type":"list","items":["Same number of gaseous molecules in smaller space or number of gaseous molecules per unit volume increases.","Increasing the pressure makes gas particles get closer together.","This increases the frequency of collisions.","So frequency of effective collisions increases."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/kinetics-pressure-effect.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/kinetics-pressure-effect-dark.png","alt":"The same gas molecules before and after the pressure is increased — smaller volume, particles closer together","caption":"The molecules have less space to move in and are more likely to collide."},{"type":"p","text":"Changing the pressure has almost no effect on reactions in solid or liquid phase. This is because the volume of solids and liquids changes very little when pressurized. So their particles do not move close together."},{"type":"h3","text":"The effect of surface area"},{"type":"p","text":"For heterogeneous reactions increasing surface area leads to more chances of a collision."},{"type":"p","text":"Increasing the surface area of solid reactants increases the rate of the reaction. This is because"},{"type":"list","items":["Greater surface area of the solid is exposed for collision.","Frequency of collisions increases.","So frequency of effective collisions increases."]},{"type":"p","text":"Catalysts (in catalytic converters) are used in a finely divided form for this reason."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/kinetics-surface-area.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/kinetics-surface-area-dark.png","alt":"One large solid compared with the same volume as powder, and magnesium reacting with dilute acid as a lump and as a powder","caption":"More surface is exposed for reaction — only collisions with magnesium atoms on the surface result in a reaction, so powdered magnesium gives off hydrogen more quickly."},{"type":"h3","text":"How changes in temperature can affect the rate of a reaction"},{"type":"p","text":"Increasing the temperature increases the rate of a reaction. This is because"},{"type":"list","items":["Average kinetic energy of the particles increases.","More particles have energy equal to or greater than activation energy.","Speed of the particles also increases.","So the frequency of effective collisions increases."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Collisions in solution"},{"type":"list","items":["In solution the molecules are much more closely packed together, so there is not much space between them.","In low to medium concentrations, most of the solution is solvent, so solute particles are surrounded by solvent molecules and are said to be trapped in a **solvent cage**.","There are a number of solute molecules that get trapped in the same solvent cage. Hence increasing the collision rate between solute particles and if collisions are sufficiently energetic they may react just as in the gas phase."]},{"type":"p","text":"There are two distinct stages to reaction in solution:"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Molecules have to come together, by a process of diffusion, in the same solvent cage.","They have to react."]},{"type":"p","text":"For simple reactions involving two species, the chance of them meeting each other in solution is proportional to their concentrations, same as in gas phase."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi8500ewjfqkrmpkq158","sectionKey":"activation-energy","number":"9.2","title":"Activation Energy, Ea","blurb":"Collisions need enough energy to break bonds — otherwise they simply bounce apart.","estMinutes":12,"spec":"9.2","specText":"understand that reactions take place only when collisions have sufficient energy, known as the activation energy","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"def","term":"Activation energy, Ea","text":"Activation energy is the minimum energy that colliding particles must possess for a reaction to occur."},{"type":"list","items":["If particles collide with less energy than activation energy then they simply bounce apart and no reaction occurs.","Only those collisions that have energies equal to or greater than activation energy result in a reaction.","Chemical reaction results in the breaking of some bonds (needing energy) and making new bonds (releasing energy). Activation energy is involved in breaking some of the original bonds.","If collisions are relatively gentle, there is not enough energy available to break the bonds and so particles do not react."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi8600eyjfqkcs6lr188","sectionKey":"calculating-rate","number":"9.3","title":"Calculating the Rate of Reaction","blurb":"Rate from concentration–time graphs: tangents, gradients, initial rate.","estMinutes":24,"spec":"9.3","specText":"be able to calculate the rate of a reaction from:\ni   the time taken for a reaction, using rate = 1/time\nii   the gradient of suitable graph, by drawing a tangent, either for initial rate, or at a time, t","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Rate of reaction"},{"type":"p","text":"Chemical kinetics is concerned with the dynamics of chemical reactions such as the way reactions take place and the rate (speed) of the process."},{"type":"p","text":"Rate of reaction can be defined as"},{"type":"list","items":["The **decrease** in the concentration of any one of the reactants per unit time","**OR** the **increase** in the concentration of any one of the product per unit time"]},{"type":"eq","code":"Rate of reaction = change in concentration / time for change to happen"},{"type":"p","text":"In a chemical reaction, the concentration of the reactants decreases or the concentration of the products increases."},{"type":"p","text":"A graph can be drawn by plotting the concentration of the reactants or products against time; the slope of the graph measures the rate of the reaction."},{"type":"p","text":"To measure rate of a reaction, we need to find out:"},{"type":"list","items":["How fast one of the reactants is used up","How fast one of the products is formed"]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/kinetics-rate-graphs.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/kinetics-rate-graphs-dark.png","alt":"Two concentration–time graphs: a falling reactant curve with a tangent drawn at point A and its gradient triangle, and a rising product curve with the initial-rate tangent, the tangent at time t and a zero gradient at the end","caption":"fig A — with respect to reactants: tangent at point A, gradient zero when the reaction is complete · fig B — with respect to products: initial rate, rate at time t, rate at end = zero."},{"type":"h3","text":"Rate of reaction with respect to reactants"},{"type":"list","items":["The gradient decreases as the rate decreases and becomes zero when all reactants are used up.","Rate of reaction is determined by drawing a tangent at a point (eg point A) and measuring its gradient."]},{"type":"eq","code":"Gradient = y / x","caption":"Y = change of concentration (mol dm⁻³) · X = change in time (seconds)"},{"type":"eq","code":"Unit of rate = mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹"},{"type":"h3","text":"Rate of reaction with respect to products"},{"type":"list","items":["Rate is also measured by the change in concentration of products over a period of time.","The slope at the start of the reaction will give the **initial rate**.","The slope gets less (showing the rate is slowing down) as the reaction proceeds."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi8600f0jfqkgnim35jc","sectionKey":"maxwell-boltzmann-temperature","number":"9.4","title":"The Maxwell–Boltzmann Distribution","blurb":"One curve explains why a small temperature rise gives a large rate increase.","estMinutes":28,"spec":"9.4","specText":"understand qualitatively, in terms of the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution of molecular energies, how changes in temperature affect the rate of a reaction","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution curves"},{"type":"list","items":["The molecules in a sample of gas have a wide range of energies. Due to the number of collisions taking place, there is a spread of molecular velocities and energies."]},{"type":"p","text":"Experiments indicated that …"},{"type":"list","items":["No particles have zero velocity (all the particles have some amount of energy).","Some have very low and some have very high velocities.","Most have intermediate velocities.","Area under the curve represents the total number of particles in the system."]},{"type":"p","text":"This curve shows the distribution of energies of the molecules in a gas (but can also be applied for particles in a solution)."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/kinetics-maxwell-boltzmann.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/kinetics-maxwell-boltzmann-dark.png","alt":"Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution curve starting from the origin, peaking at the most probable energy, with the activation energy marked and the small shaded tail beyond it","caption":"Curve starts from origin · no molecule has zero energy · few molecules have extremely high energies · the curve should not touch the X-axis."},{"type":"list","items":["**Ea** corresponds to the activation energy.","The area under the curve beyond this Ea value is proportional to the number of particles with energy greater than activation energy."]},{"type":"p","text":"If the **activation energy is high**, the number of colliding particles that have this energy at room temperature is extremely small (only about 1 in 10¹² molecules possesses this energy), so the reaction does not take place. The system is said to be **kinetically stable**."},{"type":"p","text":"Such reactions occur only if the temperature is increased."},{"type":"h3","text":"Effect of an increase in temperature on the Maxwell-Boltzmann curve"},{"type":"p","text":"Increasing the temperature alters the distribution:"},{"type":"list","items":["Curve shifts to the **right** — higher energies/velocities.","Curve gets **broader and flatter** due to the greater spread of values.","Area under the curve stays constant — corresponds to the total no. of particles."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/kinetics-mb-temperature.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/kinetics-mb-temperature-dark.png","alt":"Two Maxwell-Boltzmann curves at temperatures T1 and T2 where T2 is higher — the T2 peak is lower and further right, and the shaded area beyond Ea grows","caption":"TEMPERATURE T₂ > T₁ — the shaded area is the extra molecules with sufficient energy to overcome the energy barrier."},{"type":"list","items":["Increasing the temperature gives more particles with energy greater than activation energy (Ea).","More reactants can overcome the energy barrier associated and form products.","Therefore, more effective collisions can take place, increasing the rate of reaction.","A small rise in temperature can lead to a large increase in rate."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Points to note about the Maxwell-Boltzmann graph"},{"type":"list","items":["Neither curve is symmetrical.","Both curves start at the origin and finish by approaching the X axis and do not touch the X axis.","The area under both curves is the same.","The peak of the T₂ distribution is to the right and lower than the peak of the T₁ distribution."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi8700f2jfqk56jahq9w","sectionKey":"catalysts-alternative-routes","number":"9.5","title":"Catalysts: Alternative Routes of Lower Ea","blurb":"Catalysts alter the rate without undergoing any permanent chemical change.","estMinutes":10,"spec":"9.5","specText":"understand the role of catalysts in providing alternative reaction routes of lower activation energy","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"The effect of catalysts"},{"type":"list","items":["A catalyst alters rate of reaction by providing an alternative path with lower activation energy — **but** they do not undergo any permanent chemical change.","Catalysts are specific to reactions and they cause the reaction rate to increase."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi8800f4jfqkf1wlggtw","sectionKey":"reaction-profiles-catalysed","number":"9.6","title":"Reaction Profiles: Catalysed and Uncatalysed","blurb":"Profiles with Ea, ΔHr — and the intermediate formed with the catalyst.","estMinutes":22,"spec":"9.6","specText":"be able to draw the reaction profiles for uncatalysed and catalysed reactions, including the energy level of the intermediate formed with the catalyst","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Reaction profile diagrams"},{"type":"p","text":"A reaction profile diagram shows the energy levels of the reactants and the products of the reaction and of the transition state that the reactants go through. The activation energy (Ea) and the enthalpy change (ΔHr) are also shown on the diagram."},{"type":"p","text":"The energy changes during the course of an exothermic and endothermic reaction are shown in the reaction profile diagram."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/kinetics-reaction-profiles.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/kinetics-reaction-profiles-dark.png","alt":"Two reaction profile diagrams against the reaction co-ordinate — exothermic with products below reactants and endothermic with products above reactants, each showing Ea and ΔH","caption":"EXOTHERMIC REACTION — products lower than reactants · ENDOTHERMIC REACTION — products higher than reactants · Ea and ΔH shown on both."},{"type":"p","text":"A catalyzed reaction takes place using a different route from the uncatalyzed reaction."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/kinetics-catalysed-profile.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/kinetics-catalysed-profile-dark.png","alt":"Energy profile with a tall uncatalysed hump and a shorter catalysed hump between the same reactants and products, with both activation energies marked","caption":"Activation energy without catalyst is higher; activation energy with catalyst is lower."},{"type":"p","text":"If an intermediate is formed between the reactant and the catalyst then the reaction takes place in at least two steps as shown below."},{"type":"p","text":"Example: the reaction between peroxydisulphate ions and iodide ions, catalyzed by Fe²⁺ ions."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/kinetics-catalyst-intermediate.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/kinetics-catalyst-intermediate-dark.png","alt":"Enthalpy profile with a single tall uncatalysed hump and a catalysed two-step curve dipping at an intermediate level between the two humps","caption":"Enthalpy profile diagram for a catalyzed reaction involving the formation of an intermediate."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi8800f6jfqkiofcnzda","sectionKey":"catalysts-industry","number":"9.7","title":"Catalysts in Industry","blurb":"Homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts — less energy, higher atom economy.","estMinutes":24,"spec":"9.7","specText":"understand the use of catalysts in industry to make processes more sustainable by using less energy and/or higher atom economy","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Catalysts in industry"},{"type":"p","text":"Catalysts have been increasingly used in the chemical industry. The development of catalysts was mostly for economic reasons, but now development of catalysts has been for political and environmental reasons as well."},{"type":"p","text":"Two major economic advantages of the use of catalysts are:"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Increase the rate of chemical reaction — more of the desired product can be made in a given period of time.","Reactions can take place at lower temperatures, resulting in a decrease in the energy costs to the manufacturer."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Homogeneous catalyst"},{"type":"def","term":"Homogeneous catalyst","text":"A homogeneous catalyst is one that is in the same phase (physical state) as the reactants."},{"type":"p","text":"Example: Fe²⁺ ion catalyst in oxidation of iodide ions I⁻ by persulphate ions S₂O₈²⁻."},{"type":"eq","code":"S₂O₈²⁻(aq)  +  2I⁻(aq)  —Fe²⁺(aq)→  2SO₄²⁻(aq)  +  I₂(s)","caption":"Both the reactants and the iron(II) catalyst are in the aqueous phase."},{"type":"h3","text":"Heterogeneous catalyst"},{"type":"p","text":"Most catalysts used in industry are heterogeneous catalysts."},{"type":"list","items":["A heterogeneous catalyst is one that is in a different phase from the reactants.","Solids are commonly used as heterogeneous catalysts for reactions involving gases."]},{"type":"p","text":"Example: many reactions between gases are catalyzed using a solid catalyst."},{"type":"eq","code":"N₂(g)  +  3H₂(g)  ⇌  2NH₃(g)","caption":"Iron powder as a catalyst — the reactants are in the gaseous state, but the catalyst is in the solid state."},{"type":"h3","text":"How heterogeneous catalysts work"},{"type":"p","text":"Heterogeneous catalysis takes place at active sites on the surface of a solid (e.g. a metal). Gases are adsorbed onto the surface and form weak bonds with metal atoms. This form of catalysis is thought to work in three stages as follows:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Stage","What happens"],"rows":[["1. Adsorption","Formation of bonds with the metal may use some of the electrons from bonds within the gas molecules thus weakening these bonds and making a subsequent reaction easier."],["2. Reaction","Adsorbed gas molecules may be held on the surface of the metal in just the right orientation for a reaction to occur. This increases the chances of favourable collisions taking place."],["3. Desorption","The products are then released from the active sites."]]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/kinetics-heterogeneous-catalyst.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/kinetics-heterogeneous-catalyst-dark.png","alt":"Three panels showing gas molecules adsorbing onto a metal surface, reacting in the right orientation, and the products desorbing","caption":"In the example, H₂ and C₂H₄ adsorb on the metal surface, the H–H bond breaks and H atoms migrate along the surface, and ethane (C₂H₆) desorbs."},{"type":"h3","text":"Catalysts and atom economy in industry"},{"type":"list","items":["In the manufacture of chemicals, waste is produced when products are formed.","Use of catalysts can lead to reactions with little or no co-product (waste). It may also reduce side reactions producing unwanted products, making separation easier.","Example: phenol can be manufactured by the oxidation of dinitrogen monoxide (N₂O) using a zeolite catalyst, giving nitrogen as the only by-product.","Manufacture of phenol using benzene via cumene has a very low atom economy."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Advantages of using catalysts"},{"type":"list","items":["Fewer steps are involved in the manufacture, hence the energy costs are likely to be less.","The percentage yield of each step may be higher (and fewer steps).","Less product is lost in the separation after each step, or separation may not be required until the end.","The whole process may be quicker so that more product is obtained in a given time."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi8a00f8jfqk4iumgk7c","sectionKey":"maxwell-boltzmann-catalyst","number":"9.8","title":"Catalysts on the Maxwell–Boltzmann Curve","blurb":"Lower the Ea line and more particles can react.","estMinutes":12,"spec":"9.8","specText":"be able to interpret the action of a catalyst in terms of a qualitative understanding of the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution of molecular energies","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution curve for catalysed reactions"},{"type":"list","items":["Catalysts provide an alternative pathway with lower activation energy.","Since activation energy is reduced, more particles have sufficient energy for effective collisions.","Therefore, the number of effective collisions increases, thus increasing the rate of reaction.","Using catalysts avoids the need to supply extra heat — it is safer and cheaper.","Catalysts remain chemically unchanged at the end of the reaction."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/kinetics-mb-catalyst.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/kinetics-mb-catalyst-dark.png","alt":"Maxwell-Boltzmann curve with two dashed activation-energy lines — a lower one with the catalyst and a higher one without it, with the shaded area reaching the lower line","caption":"The Ea line with a catalyst is lower — the shaded area of particles with sufficient energy is larger, so the rate increases."}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi8b00fajfqk1s6hl3bc","sectionKey":"reversible-dynamic-equilibrium","number":"9.9","title":"Reversible Reactions and Dynamic Equilibrium","blurb":"Reversible reactions, the ⇌ symbol — and how forward and backward rates become equal in a closed container.","estMinutes":20,"spec":"9.9","specText":"know that many reactions are readily reversible and that they can reach a state of dynamic equilibrium in which:\ni   the rate of the forward reaction is equal to the rate of the backward reaction\nii   the concentrations of the reactants and the products remain constant","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Irreversible and reversible reactions"},{"type":"p","text":"When a mixture of hydrogen and oxygen in a 2:1 ratio is ignited with a spark, water is produced and no uncombined hydrogen and oxygen is left. This is an example of a complete or irreversible reaction."},{"type":"eq","code":"2H₂(g)  +  O₂(g)    →    2H₂O(l)"},{"type":"p","text":"But many reactions do not go to completion. For example, a mixture of hydrogen and iodine in a 1:1 molar ratio is heated to 300 °C in a closed vessel, only about 90% of hydrogen and iodine reacts. This is an example of a **reversible reaction**."},{"type":"eq","code":"H₂(g)  +  I₂(g)    ⇌    2HI(g)"},{"type":"list","items":["A reversible reaction is one that goes in both directions at the same temperature.","The symbol ⇌ is used to indicate reactions that are reversible.","Many reactions go to completion if they are performed in an open container."]},{"type":"p","text":"Example: CaCO₃(s) would completely decompose into CaO(s) and CO₂(g) on heating at 900 °C in an open container according to the equation;"},{"type":"eq","code":"CaCO₃(s)    →    CaO(s)    +    CO₂(g)"},{"type":"list","items":["However, if the above reaction was performed in a closed container, only a small portion of CaCO₃ decomposes, no matter how long the temperature was maintained at 900 °C.","The reason for this is that the products CaO and CO₂ can also react together at this temperature to form the reactants CaCO₃. The reaction is reversible."]},{"type":"eq","code":"CaCO₃(s)    ⇌    CaO(s)    +    CO₂(g)"},{"type":"p","text":"In a reversible reaction, there are actually two reactions: one from the reactant side to the product side (left-hand side to right-hand side), known as the **forward reaction**, and the other one from the right-hand side to the left-hand side, the **reverse (backward) reaction**."},{"type":"h3","text":"How is equilibrium established?"},{"type":"list","items":["In a reversible reaction, as the reaction continues the concentration of the reactants decreases and the rate of the forward reaction also decreases.","The following reaction proceeds in the forward direction because no products are yet present."]},{"type":"eq","code":"A  +  B    →"},{"type":"p","text":"As the reaction proceeds, the products formed react together to re-form the reactants. The rate of forward reaction decreases with time."},{"type":"eq","code":"A  +  B    ⇌    C  +  D"},{"type":"p","text":"Since the concentration of the product is initially low, the rate of the backward reaction is also slow. As the concentration of the product increases the rate of the backward reaction increases."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/eq-established-rates.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/eq-established-rates-dark.png","alt":"Two rate–time graphs — the rate of the A + B reaction falls with time, while the rate of the C + D reaction increases with time","caption":"Rate of A + B reaction falls with time · rate of C + D reaction increases with time."},{"type":"p","text":"Eventually the rates of the forward reaction and backward reaction become equal. At this point a dynamic equilibrium has been reached."},{"type":"h3","text":"Dynamic equilibrium"},{"type":"p","text":"Two conditions must be met for dynamic equilibrium to be established;"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["The reaction must be reversible.","The reaction mixture must be in a closed container."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/eq-dynamic-equilibrium.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/eq-dynamic-equilibrium-dark.png","alt":"Rate of reaction against time — the forward reaction slows, the backward reaction speeds up, and both settle at the same rate labelled equilibrium established","caption":"fig A — Dynamic equilibrium."},{"type":"p","text":"Three important features of a system in dynamic equilibrium:"},{"type":"list","items":["The reactions continue to occur in both directions.","The concentration of the reactants and products remain constant.","Rate of the forward reaction is equal to the rate of the backward reaction."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi8c00fcjfqkgv3ekddk","sectionKey":"conditions-equilibrium-position","number":"9.10","title":"Changing Conditions and the Position of Equilibrium","blurb":"Concentration, pressure, temperature and catalysts — predicting and justifying how the position of equilibrium shifts.","estMinutes":34,"spec":"9.10","specText":"be able to predict and justify the qualitative effects of changes of temperature, pressure and concentration on the position of equilibrium in a homogeneous system","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Types of equilibria"},{"type":"p","text":"**Homogeneous equilibrium:** all reactants and products are in the same physical state (e.g. all gases or all liquids)."},{"type":"eq","code":"2SO₂(g)  +  O₂(g)    ⇌    2SO₃(g)","caption":"All reactants and products in gaseous state."},{"type":"eq","code":"CH₃COOH(l)  +  CH₃CH₂OH(l)    ⇌    CH₃COOCH₂CH₃(l)  +  H₂O(l)","caption":"All reactants and products in liquid state."},{"type":"p","text":"**Heterogeneous equilibrium:** all reactants and products are in different physical states."},{"type":"eq","code":"H₂O(g)  +  C(s)    ⇌    H₂(g)  +  CO(g)"},{"type":"eq","code":"Cu(s)  +  2Ag⁺(aq)    ⇌    Cu²⁺(aq)  +  2Ag(s)","caption":"All reactants and products in solid and aqueous state."},{"type":"h3","text":"Changing the composition of an equilibrium mixture"},{"type":"list","items":["When a reaction mixture reaches a position of equilibrium, the concentration of each component will not alter as long as the conditions remain constant.","However, if a condition is changed by adding more of or removing one of the reactants or products, then the composition of the components may change.","This is referred to as \"changing the position of equilibrium\" and we can refer to the position being moved to the right, to the left or unchanged."]},{"type":"p","text":"For example: reaction between chromate(VI), CrO₄²⁻ ions with acid turns orange. This is because of an increase of dichromate(VI) ions, Cr₂O₇²⁻."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/eq-chromate-dichromate.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/eq-chromate-dichromate-dark.png","alt":"Two beakers — a yellow solution of chromate(VI) ions and an orange solution of dichromate(VI) ions, with the equilibrium 2CrO₄²⁻ + 2H⁺ ⇌ Cr₂O₇²⁻ + H₂O between them","caption":"CrO₄²⁻ — yellow · Cr₂O₇²⁻ — orange."},{"type":"list","items":["If acid (H⁺) is added to the system, equilibrium moves to the right and solution becomes orange.","If alkali (OH⁻) is added to the system, equilibrium moves to the left and solution becomes yellow."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Four factors that may affect the position of equilibrium"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Concentration of component.","Pressure of the system.","Temperature of the system.","Addition of a catalyst."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Effect of changes in concentration on the position of equilibrium"},{"type":"list","items":["Increasing concentration of reactants drives the forward reaction. Therefore, the equilibrium shifts to the right.","Increasing concentration of products drives the backward reaction. Therefore, the equilibrium shifts to the left.","As the concentration of the products increases, the rate of backward reaction increases and a new equilibrium is established.","The position of equilibrium has moved to the right, with slightly more products than at the original position of equilibrium."]},{"type":"p","text":"**Summary**"},{"type":"table","headers":["Concentration change","Effect on equilibrium"],"rows":[["Reactants increase","Moves to right (rate of forward reaction increases to decrease the concentration of reactants)"],["Reactants decrease","Moves to left (rate of backward reaction increases to increase the concentration of the reactants)"],["Products increase","Moves to left (rate of backward reaction increases to decrease the concentration of the products)"],["Products decrease","Moves to right (rate of forward reaction increases to increase the concentration of the products)"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Effect of changes of pressure on the position of equilibrium"},{"type":"list","items":["Effect of pressure only applies to equilibrium reactions involving gases.","If there is no change in the number of moles of gas in a gaseous reaction, changes of pressure have no effect on the composition of equilibrium mixture."]},{"type":"eq","code":"H₂(g)  +  I₂(g)    ⇌    2HI(g)","caption":"2 moles ⇌ 2 moles"},{"type":"p","text":"Applies only to systems where the total number of moles of gas on the reactant side is different from that on the product side. This is because the number of moles of a gas is proportional to the pressure."},{"type":"eq","code":"2SO₂(g)  +  O₂(g)    ⇌    2SO₃(g)","caption":"3 moles ⇌ 2 moles"},{"type":"list","items":["If the pressure of an equilibrium mixture is increased at constant temperature, the position of the equilibrium will shift towards the side with fewer number of gas molecules, so that the total pressure is decreased.","The pressure of an equilibrium system can be increased by reducing the volume, or adding more gas into the same volume.","If the pressure of an equilibrium mixture is decreased at constant temperature, the position of equilibrium will shift towards the side with greater number of gas molecules, so that the total pressure is increased.","Position of equilibrium moves to restore equilibrium."]},{"type":"p","text":"**Summary**"},{"type":"table","headers":["Pressure change","Effect on equilibrium"],"rows":[["INCREASE","Moves to side with fewer gaseous molecules"],["DECREASE","Moves to side with more gaseous molecules"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Consider the reaction between nitrogen and hydrogen to form ammonia. The following graph shows the effect of pressure changes using different amounts of nitrogen and hydrogen to form ammonia:"},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/eq-ammonia-pressure.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/eq-ammonia-pressure-dark.png","alt":"Percentage of ammonia in the equilibrium mixture against pressure — the curve rises steeply at first and levels off towards 600 atm","caption":"At high pressure more ammonia is produced at equilibrium — high pressure shifts equilibrium to the side having fewer moles of gas."},{"type":"eq","code":"N₂(g)  +  3H₂(g)    ⇌    2NH₃(g)","caption":"4 moles ⇌ 2 moles"},{"type":"h3","text":"Effect of changes of temperature on the position of equilibrium"},{"type":"eq","code":"Reactants    ⇌    Products","caption":"Forward reaction: ΔH = −ve · Backward reaction: ΔH = +ve"},{"type":"list","items":["The direction of change in equilibrium position depends upon whether the reaction is exothermic or endothermic.","If the temperature of an equilibrium system is increased, the position of equilibrium shifts towards the endothermic direction.","If the temperature of an equilibrium system is decreased, the position of equilibrium shifts towards the exothermic direction.","Position of equilibrium moves to establish a new equilibrium."]},{"type":"p","text":"**Summary**"},{"type":"table","headers":["Type of forward reaction","ΔH","Increase temperature","Decrease temperature"],"rows":[["Exothermic","−","Moves to LEFT","Moves to RIGHT"],["Endothermic","+","Moves to RIGHT","Moves to LEFT"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Effect of the addition of a catalyst"},{"type":"list","items":["A catalyst works by providing an alternative route that has lower activation energy than that of uncatalysed reaction.","Therefore rate of a reaction with a catalyst is much faster than the rate without the catalyst.","A catalyst has **NO EFFECT** on the position of equilibrium of a reversible reaction."]},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Catalyst","text":"Catalyst speeds up the forward and backward reactions equally, so equilibrium is reached sooner than without catalyst."},{"type":"h3","text":"Limitations of making qualitative predictions"},{"type":"list","items":["The qualitative predictions made about the effect of concentration, pressure and temperature on the position of equilibrium are just predictions. The arguments are not explanations of why changes sometimes occur.","There are occasions when it is impossible to predict the direction of change or indeed when predictions turn out to be incorrect."]},{"type":"p","text":"Example: the equilibrium mixture of NO₂(g) (brown) and N₂O₄(g) (colourless) in a closed container is placed into a beaker of hot water at room temperature — both the temperature and the pressure of the gaseous mixture will rise."},{"type":"eq","code":"2NO₂(g)    ⇌    N₂O₄(g)    ΔH = −57.2 kJ mol⁻¹","caption":"brown ⇌ colourless"},{"type":"list","items":["Forward reaction is exothermic so we would predict an increase in temperature would shift equilibrium to the left. But since there are fewer moles on the right-hand side, we would predict that an increase in pressure would shift the equilibrium to the right.","We do not know which effect is greater, so we cannot make a prediction of which way equilibrium will shift.","In practice the mixture becomes darker in colour, so the temperature effect must be greater than the pressure effect. The equilibrium must have shifted to the left to produce more NO₂(g)."]}]},{"id":"cmv1eoi8d00fejfqk5ip0dcva","sectionKey":"reversible-reactions-industry","number":"9.11","title":"Reversible Reactions in Industry","blurb":"Yield versus rate — compromise conditions in the Haber process and the Contact process.","estMinutes":26,"spec":"9.11","specText":"evaluate data to explain the necessity, for many industrial processes, to reach a compromise between the yield and the rate of reaction","outline":[],"content":[{"type":"h3","text":"Applying the principles of reaction rates and reversibility to industrial processes"},{"type":"list","items":["The principles of reaction rates and reversibility are important in the design and conditions of many industrial processes.","To maximize profits, it is important to convert reactants to products (1) as quickly as possible and (2) as completely as possible.","The first one relates to the kinetics (rate of reaction) and the second is related to reversibility.","The solution to the problem is a careful choice of reaction conditions.","The best conditions in an industry are known as **compromise conditions**."]},{"type":"h3","text":"The Haber process"},{"type":"p","text":"Ammonia is manufactured in industry by direct synthesis from nitrogen and hydrogen."},{"type":"eq","code":"N₂(g)  +  3H₂(g)    ⇌    2NH₃(g)    ΔH = −92.4 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"type":"list","items":["If the reaction mixture were to reach equilibrium, maximum yield is obtained by low temperature (forward reaction is exothermic) and high pressure (4 mol of gas on the left; 2 mol of gas on the right).","However, the reaction mixture does not reach equilibrium in the reaction chamber."]},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/eq-haber-yield.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/eq-haber-yield-dark.png","alt":"Equilibrium yield of ammonia against pressure at five temperatures from 350 °C to 550 °C — yield rises with pressure and falls with temperature, with 450 °C at 250 atm marked","caption":"The graph shows the equilibrium yield of ammonia at different temperatures and pressures."},{"type":"h3","text":"Why are conditions of 450 °C and 250 atm used?"},{"type":"list","items":["Reactions between nitrogen and hydrogen are very slow at room temperature mainly due to the very strong nitrogen to nitrogen triple bond [E(N≡N) = 945 kJ mol⁻¹] producing a high activation energy for the reaction.","Even at high temperature the reaction is slow in the absence of a suitable catalyst.","Many metals including tungsten and platinum catalyse this reaction. However, they are very expensive, so iron is used in the industrial process.","Catalysts require high temperatures to function efficiently. But high temperatures are not economical because of the extra energy costs involved.","Increasing temperature might also result in a decrease in yield as the forward reaction is exothermic, leading to an increase in the rate of backward reaction."]},{"type":"list","items":["In reality these industrial processes are not in equilibrium because the products are removed from the system as they are formed.","Hence a compromised temperature of 450 °C is used.","The higher the pressure the larger is the yield, but the larger costs for compressing gases makes the process less economical.","Hence a compromise is reached between yield and cost — a pressure of 250 atm is used."]},{"type":"p","text":"Conditions used in the Haber process are;"},{"type":"table","headers":["Temperature","Pressure"],"rows":[["450 °C","250 atm"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"The Contact process"},{"type":"p","text":"Sulphuric acid is manufactured by the Contact process."},{"type":"eq","code":"SO₂(g)  +  ½O₂(g)    ⇌    SO₃(g)    ΔH = −96 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"type":"list","items":["Since forward reaction is exothermic, low temperature would result in a high yield of SO₃(g).","The catalyst vanadium(V) oxide requires high temperature to be effective. So, a moderately high temperature of 450 °C and 1 atm pressure is used.","At 1 atm pressure the yield is already very high at 97%. Higher pressures would increase the yield as there are fewer molecules on the right hand side."]},{"type":"eq","code":"2SO₂(g)  +  O₂(g)    ⇌    2SO₃(g)","caption":"3 moles ⇌ 2 moles"},{"type":"p","text":"Pressures above 2 atm are unnecessary since the yield is already very high."},{"type":"figure","src":"/diagrams/eq-contact-yield.png","srcDark":"/diagrams/eq-contact-yield-dark.png","alt":"Percentage yield of sulphur trioxide against temperature — the curve falls from about 100% at 350 °C to about 78% near 650 °C, with the 97% yield at 450 °C marked","caption":"The yield is already very high — about 97% at 450 °C and 1 atm pressure."},{"type":"p","text":"The mechanism of action of the vanadium(V) oxide is different from iron in the Haber process. The vanadium changes its oxidation state during the reaction but converts back to its original oxidation state. The mechanism for the reaction is"},{"type":"eq","code":"SO₂(g)  +  V₂O₅(s)    ⇌    SO₃(g)  +  V₂O₄(s)"},{"type":"eq","code":"V₂O₄(s)  +  ½O₂(g)    ⇌    V₂O₅(s)"},{"type":"p","text":"Conditions used in the Contact process are;"},{"type":"table","headers":["Temperature","Pressure"],"rows":[["450 °C","2 atm"]]}]}],"cards":[{"id":"cmv1eoi8d00fgjfqk0xfdfa1t","ord":1,"front":"Define activation energy.","back":"The minimum kinetic energy colliding particles must have for a collision to result in a reaction.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8e00fijfqklysrewz1","ord":2,"front":"Why does rate rise with concentration?","back":"More particles per unit volume → more frequent collisions per second → more successful collisions per second. Energy of collisions is unchanged.","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8e00fkjfqkg864r1iw","ord":3,"front":"Why does a 10 °C rise roughly double the rate?","back":"On the Maxwell–Boltzmann curve, many more molecules shift beyond Ea (the curve flattens/flattens out to the right); the fraction of successful collisions rises sharply — frequency of collisions changes only slightly.","tag":"EXPLANATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8f00fmjfqkbpdgmyio","ord":4,"front":"How does a catalyst work?","back":"Provides an alternative reaction route of lower activation energy, so a greater proportion of collisions are successful. Unchanged chemically at the end. On the M–B graph, the Ea line moves left.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8f00fojfqklvpgkw26","ord":5,"front":"Features of a dynamic equilibrium?","back":"Forward and reverse rates are equal; concentrations of reactants/products constant (not equal); occurs in a closed system; both reactions still happening (dynamic).","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8g00fqjfqk7ncxbqj9","ord":6,"front":"State Le Chatelier's principle.","back":"When a change is applied to a system at equilibrium, the system shifts to oppose the change (minimise its effect).","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8g00fsjfqkas2x79l3","ord":7,"front":"What shifts equilibrium left/right for pressure?","back":"Increasing pressure shifts to the side with FEWER moles of gas. Only matters if gas moles differ (e.g. N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ → right at high P).","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8g00fujfqk2gagzbbp","ord":8,"front":"Is Kc affected by concentration, pressure or a catalyst?","back":"No — only TEMPERATURE changes Kc. Catalysts speed both directions equally (faster equilibrium, same position).","tag":"TRAP"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8h00fwjfqkh09a6pch","ord":9,"front":"Write Kc for N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ with units.","back":"Kc = [NH₃]² ÷ ([N₂] × [H₂]³). Units: (mol dm⁻³)² ÷ (mol dm⁻³)⁴ = mol⁻² dm⁶.","tag":"EQUATION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8h00fyjfqki5cegg2d","ord":10,"front":"Why are Haber conditions a compromise? (N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, ΔH negative)","back":"Low temperature favours yield (exothermic, left-shift on heating) but is too slow; high pressure favours yield but is costly. Compromise: ~450 °C, ~200 atm, Fe catalyst — accepting lower yield for a faster rate.","tag":"EXAM"}]},{"slug":"halogenoalkanes-alcohols-spectra","number":10,"unit":2,"unitCode":"WCH12","unitName":"Energetics, Group Chemistry, Halogenoalkanes and Alcohols","title":"Organic Chemistry: Halogenoalkanes, Alcohols and Spectra","subtitle":"Nucleophiles, substitution, alcohols — and reading mass + IR spectra.","summary":"Mechanisms and nucleophiles; halogenoalkane reactions; classifying and oxidising alcohols; mass spectrometry fragmentation; infrared spectroscopy.","estHours":5.0,"sections":[{"id":"cmv1eoi8j00g1jfqkfpnleap0","sectionKey":"mechanism-principles","number":"10.1","title":"Reaction Mechanisms and Nucleophiles","blurb":"How bonds break — and the species that attack.","estMinutes":30,"spec":null,"specText":null,"outline":["Classifying reactions: addition, elimination, substitution, oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis, polymerisation","Homolytic vs heterolytic fission; radicals vs electrophiles/nucleophiles","Definition of a nucleophile; link between bond polarity and mechanism"],"content":null},{"id":"cmv1eoi8j00g3jfqkhcx5myw8","sectionKey":"halogenoalkanes","number":"10.2","title":"Halogenoalkanes","blurb":"Polar C–X bonds and nucleophilic substitution.","estMinutes":35,"spec":null,"specText":null,"outline":["Naming halogenoalkanes; primary, secondary, tertiary classification","Substitution with aqueous KOH to alcohols (OH⁻ as nucleophile)","Elimination with ethanolic KOH to alkenes; conditions decide the outcome","Reaction with ammonia to amines; C–X bond strength and rate trends down the group"],"content":null},{"id":"cmv1eoi8k00g5jfqk0ju7hp84","sectionKey":"alcohols","number":"10.3","title":"Alcohols: Class, Reactions and Oxidation","blurb":"Primary, secondary, tertiary — and what oxidising does to each.","estMinutes":35,"spec":null,"specText":null,"outline":["Classifying alcohols as primary, secondary, tertiary","Oxidation by acidified dichromate: aldehyde → carboxylic acid (distil vs reflux), ketones, no reaction for tertiary","Observing the colour change orange → green; writing oxidation equations","Dehydration/elimination of alcohols to alkenes with hot concentrated acid"],"content":null},{"id":"cmv1eoi8l00g7jfqkemp1cg6c","sectionKey":"mass-spectrometry","number":"10.4","title":"Mass Spectra of Molecules","blurb":"Fragmentation patterns as molecular fingerprints.","estMinutes":30,"spec":null,"specText":null,"outline":["Molecular ion peak M⁺ giving relative molecular mass; the M+1 peak from ¹³C","Interpreting simple fragmentation patterns to identify pieces of the molecule","Predicting spectra for simple compounds; matching spectra to structures"],"content":null},{"id":"cmv1eoi8l00g9jfqkbtlusj9x","sectionKey":"infrared-spectroscopy","number":"10.5","title":"Infrared Spectroscopy","blurb":"Bonds vibrate at characteristic frequencies — and absorb IR.","estMinutes":30,"spec":null,"specText":null,"outline":["How bonds absorb IR radiation; wavenumber as the unit","Characteristic absorptions: O–H, N–H, C=O, C–H; using data sheets","The fingerprint region for identification","IR absorption by greenhouse gases (CO₂, H₂O, CH₄) and the link to global warming"],"content":null}],"cards":[{"id":"cmv1eoi8l00gbjfqkyxrcsluz","ord":1,"front":"Define a nucleophile.","back":"An electron-pair donor that attacks electron-deficient (δ⁺) carbon atoms, e.g. OH⁻, CN⁻, NH₃, :NH₃.","tag":"DEFINITION"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8m00gdjfqkhmmeo4ga","ord":2,"front":"Why do halogenoalkanes undergo substitution?","back":"The C–X bond is polar (X more electronegative → Cδ⁺), so nucleophiles attack the carbon; the C–X bond is also weak enough to break heterolytically.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8m00gfjfqkepz4dfgn","ord":3,"front":"Classify: 1-bromobutane, 2-bromobutane, 2-bromo-2-methylpropane.","back":"Primary (C–Br carbon bonded to 1 alkyl group), secondary (2 alkyl groups), tertiary (3 alkyl groups).","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8n00ghjfqkxydiemry","ord":4,"front":"Aqueous vs ethanolic KOH on a halogenoalkane?","back":"Aqueous (warm): nucleophilic SUBSTITUTION → alcohol. Ethanolic (hot, reflux): ELIMINATION → alkene. Water-dissolved KOH provides OH⁻ nucleophile; ethanol favours elimination.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8n00gjjfqky5jmayuq","ord":5,"front":"Rate of hydrolysis of C–Cl vs C–Br vs C–I halogenoalkanes?","back":"C–I fastest, C–Cl slowest. Bond enthalpy dominates: C–I is weakest. (Not polarity — C–Cl is most polar.)","tag":"TRAP"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8o00gljfqkb7c5fcyo","ord":6,"front":"How do you get an aldehyde vs a carboxylic acid from oxidising a primary alcohol?","back":"Aldehyde: distil off as it forms (acidified K₂Cr₂O₇, heat). Carboxylic acid: reflux with excess oxidant so the aldehyde stays in the mix and oxidises further.","tag":"EXAM"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8o00gnjfqk9b40edn4","ord":7,"front":"What is observed when oxidising alcohols with acidified dichromate?","back":"Orange (Cr₂O₇²⁻) → green (Cr³⁺). Tertiary alcohols: no change — not oxidised under these conditions.","tag":"TEST"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8p00gpjfqk48446fqe","ord":8,"front":"What does the M⁺ peak tell you, and what is the M+1 peak?","back":"M⁺ (molecular ion) m/z = relative molecular mass. M+1 arises from ¹³C (1.1% of carbon atoms) — height ≈ number of carbon atoms.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8p00grjfqkv72olnog","ord":9,"front":"Which IR absorptions should you recognise?","back":"O–H (alcohol) broad ~3200–3550 cm⁻¹; N–H ~3300–3500; C=O sharp ~1630–1820 cm⁻¹ (acids/aldehydes/ketones); C–H ~2850–3300. Fingerprint region <1500 cm⁻¹ identifies the specific molecule.","tag":"CORE"},{"id":"cmv1eoi8q00gtjfqkdgh0xkoj","ord":10,"front":"How do greenhouse gases interact with IR?","back":"C=O and O–H bonds in CO₂, H₂O and CH₄ absorb IR radiation (bond vibrations), trapping outgoing radiation and re-emitting it back — the enhanced greenhouse effect.","tag":"CORE"}]}]